full body anyway.\n\nLet me test: bare curl allowed? Try `curl -s -m 20 -A \"Mozilla/5.0\" \"https://api.duckduckgo.com/?q=test&format=json&no_html=1\"` — that's compact JSON.\n\nAlso try bare pipe: maybe only grep/sed are disallowed and head is allowed? First failure listed sed as the write segment; second listed grep. Possibly the checker scans each pipeline segment and rejects any not on whitelist; `head` might be whitelisted. But curl|head was in both rejected commands too (as first segment curl passed, second head passed?), the rejection names only one \"write: segment\". In attempt 1 it said segment 'sed' is write; attempt 2 said segment 'grep'. So maybe curl and head passed and only sed/grep failed? grep is usually read-only, but the whitelist checker may treat grep oddly... wait, grep IS listed in the allowed examples (\"ls, cat, grep, find...\"). But it flagged grep as write?! Weird — maybe because of the `-oE` flags? Or the pattern? Unclear. Maybe the checker flags grep when it appears after a pipe with certain flags? Honestly the checker seems unreliable here.\n\nLet me try `curl ... | head -c 4000` without grep/sed."}
