己设计\"). I should do a GitHub API search for search API wrappers to ground the design. English ASCII queries — safe with curl. Let me run:\n- curl -s \"https://api.github.com/search/repositories?q=web+search+api+llm&sort=stars\" | head -c N — but no grep/sed allowed; raw JSON will be large. head -c 6000 of JSON is messy but tolerable. Better: use GitHub API with per_page and jq? jq may not be installed. Could use python? `python -c` might be flagged as write? The whitelist rejected `sed`/`grep` (grep is read-only but was flagged — weird). Actually earlier plain `grep` as pipeline segment was rejected; but bare commands worked: `git status && git log`, `ls | head -40` worked (head + pipe worked in `ls .venv/Lib/site-packages | head -40`). And `curl ... | head -c` worked. So pipes to head are fine. grep was rejected though the system prompt claims grep is whitelisted... The checker complained \"segment 'grep' is a write operation\" — maybe because of the `-oE` flags? Unclear. I'll avoid shell grep; use head and my own reading.\n\nFor GitHub API JSON, `python -c` reading is risky under the whitelist; try it — if rejected, fall back to head. Actually simpler: GitHub search API supports returning only what I need? No, but I can limit with per_page=5 and extract titles by eye from head -c 8000. JSON is one line though — head -c 8000 of a single-line JSON gives 8000 chars of dense JSON. With per_page=5 that's manageable (~4-6KB).\n\nPlan for research (quick, 2-3 queries):\n1. `q=search+engine+api+llm` or better targeted: `q=ddgs` (deedy5/duckduckgo_search renamed ddgs), `q=searxng`, `q=tavily`, `q=firecrawl`. I'll do one search: `https://api.github.com/search/repositories?q=web+search+agent+tools&sort=stars&per_page=8` and another `q=searx+api+client&per_page=5`. Plus I know the landscape; the research is to confirm names/stars/URLs to cite.\n\nDesign decision (my own, grounded in research):\n- Add `web_search` + `web_fetch` tools in new module `agent/tools/websearch.py` (stdlib-only: urllib + html.parser + gzip/json) — matching project's self-built ethos and zero new deps (httpx2 exists but urllib is fine and avoids proxy complexities; actually httpx2 is already a dep via openai, so using it is also zero-new-deps and gives cleaner timeout/redirect/gzip handling... but the project's LLM client uses httpx2 via openai only. For consistency and robustness, urllib.request with custom headers is battle-tested; gzip needs manual handling; redirects automatic; charset detection manual. httpx2 handles compression and charset better. Let me check how llm/client.py imports httpx2 to mirror style.)\n\nBackends for web_search (fallback chain, first success wins):\n1. Tavily API (if CLUTCH_TAVILY_API_KEY / env TAVILY_API_KEY set) — LLM-grade results, includes content snippets.\n2. SearXNG JSON (if CLUTCH_SEARXNG_URL set) — self-hosted/aggregator.\n3. Bing RSS (`https://www.bing.com/search?q=...&format=rss&count=N`) — no key, works in CN (proven in this very session), parses XML via xml.etree.\n4. DuckDuckGo HTML (https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?q=...) — no key; parse result__a anchors + uddg= redirect URLs + snippets. (Blocked on this machine but works elsewhere.)\n\nConfig knobs: `web_search_timeout: float = 15.0`, `web_search_max_results: int = 8`, maybe `web_search_backends` override. Env: CLUTCH_TAVILY_API_KEY, CLUTCH_SEARXNG_URL (following CLUTCH_* convention).\n\nweb_fetch: fetch URL, content-type html → extract <title> + text via HTMLParser subclass (script/style/nav stripped), markdown for code blocks not needed; truncate to config.read_max_chars; non-HTML (json/plain) → return raw truncated. Also handle charset from Content-Type header or <meta charset>, gzip/deflate decoding. Add optional `start`/`max_chars` params like read_file. User-Agent header required by many sites. Also Jina fallback? Skip — keep it dependency-free and offline-friendly; document.\n\nPermission: web tools are network reads — no local side effects; `_PATH_TOOLS` doesn't include them; permission gate: check permission.py to see default for tools without path args (allowed, or ask?). Need to read permission.py quickly to be sure they don't require ask by default.\n\nChat mode: web_search/web_fetch are read-only → include in both modes (this directly fixes the user's pain: in chat mode they can now search the web properly).\n\nSystem prompt: mention in mode_work.md/mode_chat.md? Minimal: tool description suffices (descriptions live in prompts/tools/*.md). Maybe add a line to system.md? Keep minimal — no system.md change; the tool schema advertises itself. But discoverability for the model: schemas are always sent, fine.\n\nTests (tests/ dir conventions — check names: local_shell_test.py, remote_path_test.py, perm-args-test.js). Python tests seem to be pytest-style? Need to check one to match (unittest? plain asserts? pytest?). Read tests/local_shell_test.py header.\n\nAlso transport: tools run locally in-process (workspace.run only for shell). web tools are pure functions of (workspace, config) — workspace unused mostly. Follow memory tools signature `func(ws, cfg, **kw)`.\n\nAlso: `agent/prompts/errors/*.md` conventions — web tool errors can be plain strings; registry renders unknown_tool etc. Keep error strings in English like other tools (\"ERROR: ...\").\n\nREADME: add feature mention (tools list section in README \"工作原理\"?). Check README remainder (truncated at 5227 chars; lines 100+). Add a short bullet about web_search/web_fetch and env keys. That's a small doc edit — do after code passes tests. The readme-crafter skill is for README crafting; a small addition is fine without loading, but the guidance says load when the task falls in its domain — the task here is a feature; README touch is incidental. I'll keep the README edit minimal and stylistically consistent.\n\nNow execute:\n1. Read prompts/tools/run_command.md + grep.md + ls prompts dirs; read a test file header; check llm client import of httpx2; read permission.py fully (need to confirm default allow for non-path tools in interactive mode).\n2. GitHub research via API (2 searches).\n3. Implement agent/tools/websearch.py + registry wiring + prompts/tools/web_search.md + web_fetch.md + config knobs.\n4. Tests: tests/websearch_test.py with offline unit tests (XML parsing, HTML→text, DDG HTML parsing from fixture, Bing RSS parsing from fixture, backend fallback) + a --test live mode? The repo has scratch live tests pattern; better: pytest-able offline tests + optional live marker skipped by default (env CLUTCH_TEST_LIVE=1 to run). Run pytest.\n5. Update README + save_memory (durable decision).\n\nBatch next reads."}
