{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789616736.6232812, "id": "a35805e8835b", "content": "调研确认：`deedy5/ddgs`（2.9k★，免 key 聚合搜索）、`tavily-python`（1.4k★，LLM 搜索 API）、`searxng`（自托管元搜索，JSON API）、MCP 参考实现（90k★，brave-search/fetch）。再确认 prompt 目录结构和 README 里的工具清单位置：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_3f7b285e928b48b5978974b4", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"ls agent/prompts agent/prompts/tools agent/prompts/errors\"}"}, {"id": "call_abf0be52ba7849abbe30fc6f", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":60,\"offset\":100,\"path\":\"README.md\"}"}, {"id": "call_3fce2bef2acc410c8346051c", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"grep -n \\\"run_command\\\\|search_memory\\\\|load_skill\\\" ui/app.js | head -20\"}"}], "reasoning": "Excellent! GitHub research results:\n- `deedy5/ddgs` — 2973 stars, actively maintained (pushed 2026-08), metasearch library aggregating web search services (successor of duckduckgo_search). No API key needed.\n- `tavily-ai/tavily-python` — 1399 stars, active. LLM-focused search API (key required, free tier).\n- `firecrawl/firecrawl` — description has emoji, gbk print error (console codepage); repo is well known: web scraping/crawl API for LLM.\n- `modelcontextprotocol/servers` — 90k stars, reference MCP servers (fetch, brave-search).\n\nLet me also quickly confirm searxng stars and maybe brave search API. I have enough though. One more useful query: check ddgs usage — it scrapes multiple backends without keys. Adding ddgs as a dependency would be the easy path, BUT: (1) this project values minimal hand-rolled deps (\"没有使用现成的 agent 框架或 SDK\" ethos — but that's about agent frameworks, not util libs; they do depend on openai SDK); (2) ddgs scrapes engine HTML and breaks often; vendoring it into urllib-based backends gives us control and no dep. Decision: implement backends with stdlib (urllib.request), supporting:\n1. Tavily REST (if key) — POST https://api.tavily.com/search {\"api_key\":..., \"query\":..., \"max_results\":...} — actually current API uses Authorization: Bearer. Response: results[{title,url,content}].\n2. SearXNG JSON (if CLUTCH_SEARXNG_URL) — GET /search?q=..&format=json → results[{title,url,content}].\n3. Bing RSS — GET https://www.bing.com/search?q=..&format=rss&count=.. (works from CN, no key; proven today).\n4. DDG HTML — GET https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?q=.. (no key; blocked on this host but common elsewhere). Parse anchors + uddg redirect + snippets.\n\nFallback chain order: tavily → searxng → bing → ddg. Return formatted text: numbered list \"title — URL\\nsnippet\". Config knobs + env override.\n\nweb_fetch: urllib GET with UA, gzip/deflate decode, charset detect (headers → meta → utf-8 fallback), HTML→text extraction via html.parser (drop script/style/noscript/template/svg; block-level tags → newlines; keep title; cap length with optional offset like read_file). Use httpx2? urllib is stdlib and fine; httpx2 is already a dep and handles gzip/charset... but its response.charset detection is decent. Consistency argument: openai_client uses httpx2 with proxy= via llm/proxy.get_proxy_for_url and trust_env=False. For web tools, corporate proxy support would need the same get_proxy_for_url(url) call. That's a nice reuse: proxy resolution consistent with LLM traffic. Let me read llm/proxy.py quickly to see semantics (it might be a special reverse-proxy for LLM endpoints, not a generic HTTP proxy!).\n\nActually the name get_proxy_for_url(base_url) in LLM context suggests it resolves which local reverse-proxy port to use per LLM endpoint (SSH tunnel forwarding), not corporate proxies. Let me read it to be sure — if it's LLM-specific, web tools should NOT use it; instead honor standard HTTPS_PROXY env via urllib default (urllib does automatically, unlike httpx2 trust_env=False). That favors urllib.request for web tools: standard env-proxy semantics. Decision: stdlib urllib, honoring HTTP(S)_PROXY automatically.\n\nTimeouts/cancel: pass cancel event; urllib blocking IO can't be interrupted mid-read easily, but read in chunks checking cancel between chunks, and socket timeout bounds each read. timeout=config.web_search_timeout (default 15s). \n\nConfig additions:\n```\n# Web access: search backends + page fetch (tools/websearch.py)\nweb_search_timeout: float = 15.0\nweb_search_max_results: int = 8\ntavily_api_key: str | None = field(default_factory=lambda: os.environ.get(\"CLUTCH_TAVILY_API_KEY\") or os.environ.get(\"TAVILY_API_KEY\"))\nsearxng_url: str = field(default_factory=lambda: os.environ.get(\"CLUTCH_SEARXNG_URL\", \"\"))\n```\nHmm, wait: `_SETTING_FIELDS` is only base_url/model/api_key/reasoning_effort — those are the UI settings modal fields. web keys stay env-only (like CLUTCH_API_KEY). Good — no UI changes needed.\n\nRegistry: add web_search (both modes) and web_fetch (both modes). Prompts: prompts/tools/web_search.md, prompts/tools/web_fetch.md.\n\nDescription content for web_search.md: purpose, params, backend chain, when to use (current events, docs lookup), guidance (queries in engine syntax, retries with different words, use web_fetch on a result URL for full text). web_fetch.md: fetch a URL and return readable text; params url, max_chars, offset; non-HTML handled; use relative nothing (network only); errors as data.\n\nAlso `agent/prompts/errors/` has files but I can return plain ERROR strings like some tools do (memory tools return plain \"ERROR: ...\"). Keep plain strings.\n\nMode: web tools in chat mode too — they're read-only. But wait: chat mode contract is \"never touches the workspace\"; network GETs are safe. The run_command.md in chat mode says network available via curl read-only... web_search fits.\n\nSecurity considerations:\n- SSRF-ish: web_fetch could hit http://localhost:8890 (the agent's own server API) — exfiltration surface is minimal (the model already controls the local machine in work mode). For chat mode (read-only promise), fetching localhost could mutate via POST? We only do GET; server endpoints are GET-safe? Probably fine; but to keep the chat-mode promise airtight, block requests to private/loopback/link-local addresses in web_fetch (resolve host, check IP). That's a cheap ipaddress check. Do it for web_fetch only (search backends are fixed hosts anyway). Good poka-yoke, matches project style.\n- Size cap: read at most ~2MB to avoid memory blowups.\n- Redirect to non-public IP: urllib follows redirects internally — the IP check must be applied after redirects... urllib doesn't expose per-hop hooks easily. Simpler: check final response.geturl() host too, but body already fetched by then. Alternative: custom opener with HTTPRedirectHandler subclass validating each redirect target. That's ~10 lines; do it.\n\nTests (offline, no network): tests/websearch_test.py following testsupport.check style:\n- Bing RSS parsing from a canned XML fixture (like the real response from earlier today).\n- DDG HTML parsing from canned fixture (result__a / uddg decode / snippet).\n- Tavily JSON parsing from canned fixture.\n- SearXNG JSON parsing.\n- Backend fallback: fake transport functions (inject fetchers via parameter or monkeypatch module attr) — design module with `_http_get(url, headers, timeout, cancel)` and backend funcs taking an `http_get` param defaulting to real one → tests inject stubs.\n- HTML→text extraction: script/style removal, block newlines, title.\n- Private-IP block in web_fetch.\n- Truncation/offset.\n- Registry wiring: build_default_tools(Config(mode=\"chat\")) includes web_search & web_fetch; work mode too.\n- Optional live check gated behind env CLUTCH_WEBSEARCH_LIVE=1 (runs real Bing RSS query) — skip by default; scratch logs show they run live tests via scratch files. I'll include a `--live` flag: when passed, run a real query (bing) — I can run it myself to verify end-to-end since Bing works here.\n\nStructure of websearch.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Web search + page fetch tools ...\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport gzip, json, re, socket, threading, urllib.parse, urllib.request, xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\nimport zlib\nfrom html.parser import HTMLParser\nfrom ..config import Config\nfrom .workspace import Workspace\n\nUSER_AGENT = \"Mozilla/5.0 ... Clutch/0.1 (coding agent)\"  # hmm, sites block unknown UA less if browser-like. Use a browser-like UA for engines; they may still serve. Bing RSS worked with browser UA. Keep browser-like UA with Clutch suffix.\n\nResult = dict  # {title, url, snippet}\n```\n\n_http_get(url, *, timeout, cancel, headers=None, max_bytes=2_000_000, opener...) -> (status, headers, bytes)\n- build request, urlopen with socket timeout; read chunks (64KB) with cancel checks; decompress by Content-Encoding; return decoded bytes.\n- SSRF guard: validate host IP before connect AND redirect handler validates hops.\n\nBackends each: `(query, limit, cfg, http) -> list[Result] | raises BackendError`.\n\nsearch(ws, cfg, query, max_results=None, backend=None):\n- normalize; iterate chain order (or single backend if requested: \"tavily\"|\"searxng\"|\"bing\"|\"ddg\")\n- on all-fail: error content listing per-backend failure reasons.\n- format results: \"1. title\\n   URL\\n   snippet\" + header line \"N results for '...' via <backend>\"; empty → \"no results\".\n\nfetch(ws, cfg, url, max_chars=None, offset=0):\n- validate scheme http/https; SSRF guard; GET; content-type: html → extract text (title + body); json/plain → raw; \n- truncate: config.read_max_chars default; offset support like read_file (line-numbered? read_file is line-based. For fetch, char-based offset is simpler; description says 1-based char offset? Keep it simple: max_chars only, plus `start` char offset. Hmm consistency with read_file's line-based offset doesn't carry to HTML text; use plain char window. Fine.)\n\nHTML text extraction details:\n- HTMLParser subclass: skip script/style/noscript/template/head? keep <title> separately (capture first title). Convert <br>, <p>, <div>, <li>, <tr>, heading ends to \\n. collapse blank lines. Also capture <meta charset> if headers lacked charset. Decode before parse: get charset from Content-Type header, else sniff bytes for <meta charset=...> in first 2KB, else utf-8 errors=replace.\n- Keep links? Extracting markdown-style links adds value for the model (clickable next targets). That complicates the parser (track href on <a>, emit [text](href)). Mild complexity, big utility. But risk: link text interleaves weirdly when block elements nest inside <a>. Many readability impls skip it. I'll skip inline links — the model gets URLs from web_search; and `web_fetch` is for reading one page. Keep parser simple and predictable.\n\nSnippet cleanup for RSS: description may contain HTML entities (&#183; &lt;) — unescape with html.unescape and strip tags via a tiny regex (RSS descriptions here contain plain text mostly; still strip tags defensively).\n\nEncoding of registry wiring:\n\n```python\ntools.append(Tool(name=\"web_search\", description=render(\"tools/web_search.md\", max_results=config.web_search_max_results), parameters={...}, func=lambda sb, cfg, **kw: websearch.web_search(sb, cfg, **kw)))\ntools.append(Tool(name=\"web_fetch\", ...))\n```\nPlace after run_command, before skills (order = schema order shown to model; put search tools right after grep or after run_command — I'd put them after run_command).\n\nWait — also check `agent/prompts/tools/` file list to confirm naming and look at errors dir. And check that nothing validates tool-name sets elsewhere (permission._PATH_TOOLS only lists path tools; UI? ui/app.js might render tool names — check grep for \"run_command\" in ui/app.js to see if there's a tool list in UI).\n\nAlso supervisor/server? The remote (SSH) case: web tools run client-side in-process (not on remote) — fine (like memory tools). No transport changes.\n\nREADME: add to the tools list — need to read the tail of README (lines 100+) to find the tools section.\n\nLet me batch: ls agent/prompts + agent/prompts/tools + agent/prompts/errors; read README offset 100; grep ui/app.js for run_command."}
