{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789617103.0511985, "id": "40ca5c24838c", "content": "继续：写核心模块 `agent/tools/websearch.py`。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_ab1df820fd0647999107e2a3", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/__init__.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Continue implementing. Next: create `agent/tools/websearch.py` — the core module. Let me write it carefully, stdlib only:\n\n- WebError exception\n- HttpResp dataclass\n- _assert_public_host (SSRF guard)\n- _SafeRedirects\n- _http_get (cancel-aware, gzip, size cap)\n- _decode_body (charset from header/meta/utf-8 fallback)\n- _clean_snippet (strip tags, unescape, collapse whitespace)\n- _TextExtractor (HTML → title + text)\n- _window (char windowing with tail note)\n- backends: _tavily, _searxng, _bing, _ddg (+ _DdgParser)\n- backend chain assembly + web_search tool func\n- web_fetch tool func\n\nStyle conventions in this project: module docstring with \"poka-yoke\" bullet list, `from __future__ import annotations`, type hints, English comments, error-as-data dict returns {\"content\", \"error\"}.\n\nRegistry func signatures: (workspace, config, cancel=None, **kw). Tools get called via tool.func(workspace, config, **args) — args from model.\n\nLet me write the module now.\n\nKey details to get right:\n\n1. `_http_get(url, *, timeout, cancel, method=\"GET\", body=None, headers=None, max_bytes)`:\n```python\ndef _http_get(url, *, timeout, cancel, method=\"GET\", body=None, headers=None, max_bytes=2_000_000) -> HttpResp:\n    if cancel is not None and cancel.is_set():\n        raise WebError(\"aborted by user\")\n    scheme = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).scheme.lower()\n    if scheme not in (\"http\", \"https\"):\n        raise WebError(f\"unsupported URL scheme {scheme!r} (http/https only)\")\n    _assert_public_host(url)\n    req_headers = {\"User-Agent\": USER_AGENT, \"Accept\": \"*/*\", \"Accept-Language\": \"zh-CN,zh;q=0.9,en;q=0.8\", \"Accept-Encoding\": \"gzip\"}\n    if headers: req_headers.update(headers)\n    opener = urllib.request.build_opener(_SafeRedirects())\n    try:\n        resp = opener.open(urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, headers=req_headers, method=method), timeout=timeout)\n    except WebError: raise\n    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e: raise WebError(f\"HTTP {e.code} from {url}\")\n    except urllib.error.URLError as e: raise WebError(_net_error_reason(e, timeout))\n    except (ValueError, OSError) as e: raise WebError(f\"request failed: {e}\")\n    with resp:\n        raw = _drain(resp, cancel, max_bytes)\n        if resp.headers.get(\"Content-Encoding\", \"\").lower() == \"gzip\": raw = gzip.decompress(raw)  # gzip may raise on garbage\n    return HttpResp(url=resp.geturl(), status=resp.status? )\n```\nHmm urllib.response has .status in 3.9+ (getcode()). Use resp.getcode()? addpath. `resp.status` exists for http.client.HTTPResponse (urlopen returns http.client.HTTPResponse for http(s)) — .status available. But after opener.open it's HTTPResponse; fine. I'll store status but backends don't need it (HTTPError handled above; 2xx assumed).\n\n_net_error_reason: URLError.reason could be socket.timeout (\"timed out\") or ConnectionRefusedError etc. Map: `reason = getattr(e, \"reason\", e)`; if isinstance(reason, socket.timeout) → f\"timed out after {timeout:.0f}s\" else str(reason) truncated.\n\n_drain(resp, cancel, max_bytes):\n```python\nchunks = []\ntotal = 0\nwhile True:\n    if cancel is not None and cancel.is_set(): raise WebError(\"aborted by user\")\n    block = resp.read(65536)\n    if not block: break\n    total += len(block)\n    if total > max_bytes: raise WebError(f\"response exceeds {max_bytes} bytes\")\n    chunks.append(block)\nreturn b\"\".join(chunks)\n```\n\n2. `_assert_public_host(url)`:\n```python\nhost = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).hostname  # lowercased, no port, brackets stripped\nif not host: raise WebError(f\"URL has no host: {url!r}\")\nif host.endswith((\".localhost\", \".local\", \".internal\")) or host == \"localhost\":\n    raise WebError(f\"non-public host refused: {host}\")\ntry:\n    infos = socket.getaddrinfo(host, None)\nexcept OSError as e:\n    raise WebError(f\"cannot resolve host {host!r}: {e}\")\nfor info in infos:\n    ip = ipaddress.ip_address(info[4][0])\n    if not ip.is_global:\n        raise WebError(f\"non-public host refused: {host} ({ip})\")\n```\nNote: `is_global` — for 169.254.x is_global False ✓; 10.x False ✓; docs/reserved False ✓. Good.\n\n3. Redirects guard as planned. Also `build_opener(_SafeRedirects())` — default opener already includes HTTPRedirectHandler; adding ours replaces it (same base class chain — build_opener dedupes by subclass priority: custom subclass wins).\n\n4. `_decode_body(raw: bytes, content_type: str) -> str`:\n```python\ncharset = None\nm = re.search(r'charset=[\"\\']?([\\w\\-:+.]+)', content_type or \"\", re.I)\nif m: charset = m.group(1)\nif not charset:\n    head = raw[:4096].decode(\"ascii\", errors=\"ignore\")\n    m = re.search(r'<meta[^>]+charset=[\"\\']?([\\w\\-:+.]+)', head, re.I)\n    if m: charset = m.group(1)\nif charset:\n    try: return raw.decode(charset, errors=\"replace\")\n    except (LookupError, UnicodeDecodeError): pass  # unknown charset name\nif raw.startswith(codecs.BOM_UTF16_LE) or ...: try utf-16\ntry: return raw.decode(\"utf-8\")\nexcept UnicodeDecodeError: return raw.decode(\"latin-1\", errors=\"replace\")  # never fails\n```\nKeep it simple: charset → utf-8 → latin-1. utf-16 via BOM check optional — include, cheap.\n\n5. `_clean_snippet(text)`: `re.sub(r\"<[^>]+>\", \" \", text)` → html.unescape → collapse whitespace (incl \\xa0). Note: unescape AFTER tag-strip (avoid &lt;script&gt; re-parsing concerns — irrelevant, we strip first).\n\n6. `_TextExtractor`:\n```python\n_SKIP = frozenset({\"script\",\"style\",\"noscript\",\"template\",\"svg\",\"iframe\",\"object\",\"embed\",\"canvas\"})\n_BLOCK = frozenset({\"p\",\"div\",\"li\",\"tr\",\"table\",\"ul\",\"ol\",\"section\",\"article\",\"header\",\"footer\",\"main\",\"aside\",\"nav\",\"form\",\"pre\",\"blockquote\",\"h1\",...,\"h6\",\"br\",\"hr\",\"dt\",\"dd\",\"figure\",\"figcaption\"})\n```\nWait — nav inside skip? nav contains links/text; skipping nav helps readability. Add \"nav\",\"head\"? <head> contains title which we want. Handle: don't skip head; skip nav, header, footer, aside? Those sometimes hold article text... standard readability strips them. I'll strip nav/aside; keep header/footer (title extraction unaffected). Hmm keep simple: skip = script/style/noscript/template/svg/iframe/object/embed/head(?) + nav/aside/footer. Title: capture from <title> — if head skipped, must special-case title: I'll NOT skip head; instead track _in_title to capture title text, and head's other children (meta/link/style) produce no visible data except <style>/<script> (skipped) and title (captured, not appended to body since _in_title routes to self.title). <head> has no block text otherwise. OK: skip set = {script, style, noscript, template, svg, iframe, object, embed, canvas, nav, aside, footer, form, button, select, option, input, label}. Reasonable.\n\nParser:\n```python\nclass _TextExtractor(HTMLParser):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__(convert_charrefs=True)\n        self.title = \"\"\n        self._chunks: list[str] = []\n        self._skip_depth = 0\n        self._in_title = False\n\n    def handle_starttag(self, tag, attrs):\n        if tag == \"title\": self._in_title = True; return\n        if tag in _SKIP: self._skip_depth += 1; return\n        if self._skip_depth: return\n        if tag in _BLOCK: self._chunks.append(\"\\n\")\n\n    def handle_endtag(self, tag):\n        if tag == \"title\": self._in_title = False; return\n        if tag in _SKIP and self._skip_depth: self._skip_depth -= 1; return\n        if self._skip_depth: return\n        if tag in _BLOCK: self._chunks.append(\"\\n\")\n\n    def handle_data(self, data):\n        if self._in_title: self.title += data.strip(); return\n        if self._skip_depth: return\n        self._chunks.append(data)\n\n    def text(self) -> str:\n        raw = \"\".join(self._chunks).replace(\"\\xa0\", \" \")\n        lines = [re.sub(r\"[ \\t\\r\\f]+\", \" \", ln).strip() for ln in raw.split(\"\\n\")]\n        return \"\\n\".join(ln for ln in lines if ln)\n```\nVoid tags (br) never fire endtag — handle_startendtag for xhtml br → handle_starttag fires for br in XHTML `<br/>` via handle_startendtag default → calls handle_starttag+handle_endtag? Default handle_startendtag calls handle_starttag then handle_endtag. HTML `<br>` starttag fires once. _BLOCK includes br → \"\\n\" on start; endtag for br may fire spuriously in XHTML (adds second \\n — harmless, collapsed later). OK.\n\n7. Backends. HttpResp dataclass: url:str, content_type:str, body:bytes.\n\n_tavily(query, limit, cfg, get):\n```python\npayload = json.dumps({\"query\": query, \"max_results\": limit}).encode(\"utf-8\")\nresp = get(\"https://api.tavily.com/search\", method=\"POST\", body=payload,\n           headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\", \"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {cfg.tavily_api_key}\"})\ndata = json.loads(resp.body)   # ValueError → BackendError\nresults = data.get(\"results\") or []\nout = [...]\nif not out: raise _BackendError(\"response had no results\")\n```\nget() is a callable the search layer builds: partial(_http_get, timeout=..., cancel=...). Backend raises _BackendError(msg) for \"expected\" failures (parse/empty); WebError propagates from get — web_search catches both, records reason.\n\n_searxng similar with Accept: application/json; fields content.\n\n_bing: as planned. count param: str(limit) plus maybe +5 slack? Bing caps count at 30? limit ≤ 20 fine, request count=limit (RSS returns up to that). Actually earlier I requested count=10 got 10. Use limit.\nmkt param: default market works; add nothing.\n\nHeader for bing: Accept \"application/rss+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8\".\n\n_ddg:\n```python\nresp = get(\"https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?\" + urllib.parse.urlencode({\"q\": query}))\nparser = _DdgParser(); parser.feed(_decode_body(resp.body, resp.content_type)); parser.close()\nout = parser.results[:limit]\nif not out: raise _BackendError(\"no results parsed (page layout changed or blocked)\")\n```\n_DdgParser collects up to say 30.\n\nPost-filter: bing may return irrelevant (as seen with garbage query) — that's query quality, not our problem.\n\nDedup: same URL may appear twice across... within one backend only; dedup by url in web_search anyway (seen set) — cheap.\n\n8. Chain:\n```python\n_BACKENDS = {\"tavily\": _tavily, \"searxng\": _searxng, \"bing\": _bing, \"ddg\": _ddg}\n_DEFAULT_CHAIN = (\"tavily\", \"searxng\", \"bing\", \"ddg\")\n\ndef _available(cfg) -> tuple[str, ...]:\n    chain = []\n    for name in _DEFAULT_CHAIN:\n        if name == \"tavily\" and not cfg.tavily_api_key: continue\n        if name == \"searxng\" and not cfg.searxng_url: continue\n        chain.append(name)\n    return tuple(chain)\n```\n\nweb_search(workspace, config, query, max_results=None, backend=\"\", cancel=None):\n```python\nq = (query or \"\").strip()\nif not q: return error \"query is required\"\nlimit: clamp int(max_results or config.web_search_max_results) to 1..20\nchain = (backend,) if backend else _available(config)\nif backend and backend not in _BACKENDS: error listing available + how to enable\nif not chain: error \"no search backend configured/available\" — can't really happen (bing/ddg always available)\nget = functools.partial(_http_get, timeout=config.web_search_timeout, cancel=cancel)\nfailures = []\nfor name in chain:\n    try:\n        results = _BACKENDS[name](q, limit, config, get)\n    except (_BackendError, WebError) as e:\n        failures.append(f\"- {name}: {_short(e)}\")\n        continue\n    results = dedup[:limit]\n    body = \"\\n\".join(f\"{i}. {r['title']}\\n   {r['url']}\\n   {r['snippet']}\" ...)\n    return {\"content\": f\"{len} results for {q!r} (via {name}):\\n\\n\" + body}\nreturn {\"content\": \"ERROR: all search backends failed\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(failures), \"error\": True}\n```\n_short(e): str(e) first 160 chars single-line.\n\nEmpty-results vs failure: engine returns 0 items → treated as failure → falls to next backend. Good.\n\nsnippet formatting: keep ≤ 300 chars: r[\"snippet\"] = r[\"snippet\"][:300].\n\nweb_fetch(workspace, config, url, max_chars=None, start=0, cancel=None):\n```python\nu = (url or \"\").strip()\nif not u: error\nget = partial(...)\nresp = _http_get(u, timeout=config.web_search_timeout*2, cancel=cancel, max_bytes=2MB)   # fetch page: a bit more headroom\nctype = resp.content_type.split(\";\")[0].strip().lower()\nif ctype.startswith(\"image/\") or ctype in (\"application/pdf\", ...binary...): error \"unsupported content-type\"\ntext = _decode_body(resp.body, resp.content_type)\nif \"html\" in ctype or text.lstrip()[:1] == \"<\":  → extractor\n    ex = _TextExtractor(); ex.feed(text); ex.close()\n    parts = ([f\"# {ex.title}\"] if ex.title else []) + [ex.text()]  → join \"\\n\\n\"\n    if not ex.text(): content note \"(page had no extractable text)\"...\nelse: page = text\nwindow via start/max_chars (default read_max_chars), tail note with next start\n```\nHTML detection also for missing/odd content types (some servers send text/plain html). lstrip(\"<!\") check handles it.\n\nTruncation note: `... [N chars truncated; fetch again with start=K to continue]` — mirrors read_file hint style.\n\nBinary set: image/*, video/*, audio/*, application/{pdf,zip,gzip,x-tar,octet-stream} → error listing ctype.\n\nAlso cap total output via config.truncate? read_max_chars default 20000 → fetch text window uses same; fine.\n\nTimeout for fetch: web_search_timeout applies per backend; fetch = config.web_search_timeout * 2? Simpler: same value; big pages stream chunked with timeout between reads (socket timeout is per-recv). 15s per recv is plenty. Use config.web_search_timeout for both — keep one knob. Actually slow page loads (cold CDN) at 15s connect+first-byte — ok. Keep one knob; document.\n\nUser-Agent: browser-like with product token:\n`Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/124.0 Safari/537.36 ClutchAgent/0.1` — some CDNs (cloudflare botfight) block non-browser UA; mixed UA helps engines. Fine.\n\nWrite it. Then prompts, registry, tests.\n\nOne more check — agent/tools/__init__.py content? It may list modules for package docs. Check quickly with read (small). Also I need functools import."}
