statuses). The README mentions remote sessions forwarding LLM requests through SSH tunnel → local reverse proxy. \"request timed out\" despite good network could be:\n\n1. LLM streaming: httpx2 read timeout during long reasoning pauses (model \"thinking\" without emitting tokens for >60s → read timeout). DeepSeek reasoner can stall >60s.\n2. The timeout error text in agent/llm/client.py LlmError classification.\n3. Or the previous diagnosis: In this session, my own curl commands to DDG got HTTP:000 — network to some hosts is blocked/flaky. Also GitHub API worked, Bing worked.\n\nThe user says \"网络环境良好\" (network is good) yet frequent \"request timed out\". They want me to find where the problem is in the codebase.\n\nI have two tasks now potentially:\nA. Diagnose the timeout issue (the original question, re-asked now).\nB. Finish the websearch feature (write websearch.py, prompts, registry wiring, tests, README).\n\nThe user pressed 继续 5 times and then re-posted the timeout question. I think the immediate priority is answering/diagnosing the timeout question. But the websearch implementation is half-done: config.py was edited (+15 lines), and nothing else yet. Leaving it half-done is bad — but the user's latest message is the timeout question. Hmm, but maybe their re-post is just impatience with the many 继续 presses (the agent kept getting interrupted/stopped, possibly BY the timeout issue itself? The session kept needing \"继续\" — maybe the agent UI kept timing out during my long turns, which is exactly the bug they're asking about!).\n\nInteresting theory: The user's Clutch UI shows \"request timed out\" frequently. They asked to diagnose. During this session, the work kept stalling (my turns got cut off repeatedly, hence 继续 x5). So the user re-asks: why request timed out with good network — diagnose in this codebase.\n\nBest move: Do BOTH — first finish the websearch module quickly (it's nearly done, and it was explicitly requested), then diagnose the timeout. OR diagnose timeout first since it's the re-asked priority, then finish websearch.\n\nActually, let me think about what serves the user: the timeout diagnosis is investigation (grep + read llm client code) and likely a fix. The websearch feature is mostly one big file + small wirings. I can do the timeout diagnosis first (it's the question on the table), find the root cause, fix it, and then finish websearch.py + tests in the same turn if feasible. That's a lot of tokens but doable. Alternatively finish websearch first (quick, I have the design in mind), then diagnose.\n\nThe user's message ordering: they pressed 继续 several times — those were nudges to continue the websearch work. Then they re-typed the timeout question — that's the current priority. I'll diagnose the timeout issue FIRST in this turn, then complete the websearch implementation.\n\nLet me plan the timeout diagnosis:\n\nHypotheses to check in code:\n1. `agent/llm/client.py` LlmError classification: how timeouts are detected and what message \"request timed out\" comes from.\n2. `agent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py`: httpx2.Client(proxy=proxy, trust_env=False, timeout=self.request_timeout) — timeout=60 is a single float → httpx applies it as connect+read+write+pool timeout all 60s. Read timeout 60s: streaming SSE — if no bytes arrive for 60s, ReadTimeout. DeepSeek reasoning models can pause >60s without emitting anything? Usually they emit reasoning deltas continuously. But API-side stalls happen.\n3. The retry loop: attempts up to max_retries; each timeout → retry. UI shows \"request timed out\" maybe from LlmRetryEvent or error.\n4. trust_env=False + proxy=get_proxy_for_url(base_url): if the reverse proxy (SSH tunnel / local 反代) is in play, proxy=localhost port; tunnel congestion → connect fine, but upstream slow → read timeout.\n5. Windows power settings? The UI freeze fix (streaming throttle) was previous. \"request timed out\" also could come from UI-side fetch (app.js calling server API) — check ui for \"timed out\" string.\n6. Also my session today: DDG HTTP:000 (connection failed) but GitHub/Bing fine — this machine's direct international connectivity is spotty (typical CN network: domestic endpoints fine, some foreign blocked). If base_url points to api.deepseek.com (domestic, fine) or foreign (openai etc., flaky) — timeouts despite \"good network\" (good for domestic, bad for the API's route).\n\nSearch the repo for \"timed out\" / \"timeout\" strings to find where the message is produced, then read the classify/retry logic, then reason about root cause. Possibly the real issue: `timeout=self.request_timeout` applies a 60s READ timeout to a streaming response — during long tool-call-free reasoning bursts with zero deltas (some providers buffer reasoning), read timeout fires mid-stream; LlmError.classify marks it retryable; retry restarts from scratch → repeated timeouts on long generations → frequent \"request timed out\" despite good network. Fix options: raise read timeout for streaming (e.g., separate connect vs read: httpx2.Timeout(connect=10, read=300, write=60, pool=10)), and/or treat mid-stream read-timeout as non-retryable (already the case: \"raised immediately\"). Frequent timeouts = read too tight for the provider.\n\nAlso \"request timed out\" exact string might come from httpx2 itself (\"The read operation timed out\") or openai SDK (\"Request timed out\": APITimeoutError message is \"Request timed out.\"). openai.APITimeoutError message: \"Request timed out.\" — YES, that's the exact string! openai SDK raises APITimeoutError when its httpx client times out, message \"Request timed out.\". But the client here passes a custom http_client with timeout=request_timeout — openai wraps httpx timeouts into APITimeoutError. So the message comes from openai SDK when httpx2 TimeoutException occurs (connect or read). 60s read on streaming stalls → APITimeoutError → LlmError classify → retry ×3 → each attempt wastes 60s stall → UI shows request timed out repeatedly. With good network, connect succeeds fast; the stall is provider-side generation gaps or GFW-induced stream stalls on foreign endpoints (TLS RST/throttling on long-lived connections — VERY common: GFW throttles/resets long-lived HTTPS streams to certain hosts; short requests fine, long streams die). That explains \"network good but stream times out\": sustained SSE streams are fragile across GFW; short ones fine.\n\nMitigations in code: retry (exists), but frequent user-visible timeouts. Real fixes:\n1. Distinguish connect timeout (short, 15s) from read timeout (longer, e.g. 120-300s) — streaming gaps legit >60s.\n2. Detect stream stall — already handled by retry (restart).\n3. For CN users on foreign endpoints: nothing code-side fully fixes; but longer read timeout + more retries of the connect phase helps. Also TCP keepalive? GFW RST mid-stream shows as ReadError/RemoteProtocol error not timeout.\n\nLet me actually look at client.py classify and see what surfaces \"timed out\". And check the UI string. Then implement fix: structured timeout in config (llm_connect_timeout?) — minimal: build httpx2.Timeout with connect=min(15, request_timeout), read=request_timeout... but root: request_timeout=60 too small for read on streaming. Change default? Changing llm_request_timeout semantics is risky. Better: split: `llm_request_timeout` stays overall/request timeout for non-stream ops; streaming read stall tolerance becomes separate. Minimal targeted fix in openai_client: `timeout=httpx2.Timeout(request_timeout, connect=min(15.0, request_timeout))` — Timeout(first_arg) sets all four to 60 unless overridden; passing connect separately overrides connect to 15s. That makes CONNECT failures (GFW, dead route) fail fast at 15s instead of hanging 60s — reducing perceived timeouts — while read stays 60s.\n\nHmm wait, but \"频繁出现 request timed out\" during GOOD network — if connect were the problem, network being good contradicts. The stall-then-timeout on read with retry loop is likelier. Also the doom: retry restarts stream; provider stalls again; 3 attempts × 60s → error surfaced. Fix: raise read timeout default (e.g., 300s) for streaming so transient stalls ride through; keep connect at ~15s. Config: keep llm_request_timeout: 60.0 as CONNECT/write budget? Changing semantics confuses. Cleaner: add `llm_read_timeout: float = 300.0` used as read budget; request_timeout keeps default 60 for connect/write/pool. Both feed httpx2.Timeout(request_timeout, connect=..., read=llm_read_timeout). Hmm, Timeout(60) sets read=60 too; overriding read=300 → connect 60? I'd set connect=min(15, request_timeout), write=request_timeout, pool=request_timeout, read=llm_read_timeout explicitly.\n\nBut is 300s read sane for UI? If provider truly hangs, user waits 5min before retry. Tension. Compromise: 120s? DeepSeek reasoner long CoT emits continuously; gaps >60s are rare but happen under load (provider overload → 503 usually). UI shows \"reconnecting…\" on retry — fine.\n\nAlso check llm/client.py classify to ensure timeouts retry BEFORE first token (yes) and error message wording. And whether UI \"request timed out\" comes from llm or from ui fetch to local server (server has its own timeouts? SSE long-poll?). Grep \"timed out\" across agent/ and ui/.\n\nAdditional angle specific to this project: remote SSH sessions — LLM requests forward through tunnel + local reverse proxy. If SSH tunnel hiccups, requests time out despite good internet. get_proxy_for_url maps LLM endpoints → local proxy port. Read proxy.py to see. If the reverse proxy (in ui/) has its own timeout < llm_request_timeout, tunnels could cut long stalls → timeout. Check ui/ssh-tunnel.js / proxy code for timeout constants.\n\nPlan for this turn:\n1. Grep \"timed out|timeout|Timeout\" in agent/llm + ui.\n2. Read llm/client.py classify + openai_client rest + proxy.py.\n3. Diagnose root cause(s), implement targeted fix (structured timeouts: connect 15s, read from new config knob default ~180-300s), update README? config comment. Run existing tests (tests/loop_test? which tests cover llm client — search tests for openai_client usage).\n4. THEN finish websearch.py + prompts + registry + tests + README + run tests.\n\nThat's heavy for one turn but let me push through the diagnosis + fix first, then websearch files. If tokens run low, report status clearly.\n\nAlso memory: save durable facts at end (project conventions + decisions). Earlier I never saved memory; system says save when learning durable facts. I'll save: (a) user prefers 中文, project ethos minimal deps self-built; (b) decision: websearch design multi-backend stdlib; (c) timeout root cause + fix. Do at end of turn.\n\nGo."}
