{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789619473.114243, "id": "3a0f91c60d27", "tool_call_id": "call_57fff69bb26542249492d979", "content": "OK: command succeeded\nstdout:\nagent/llm/client.py:55:        mid-stream stall past the read timeout, a sudden disconnect/reset, a\r\nagent/llm/client.py:66:        if isinstance(e, openai.APITimeoutError):\r\nagent/llm/client.py:67:            return LlmError(code=\"timeout\", retryable=True, message=str(e))\r\nagent/llm/client.py:85:        # httpx2 transport errors raised mid-stream (read timeout / reset / EOF /\r\nagent/llm/client.py:86:        # remote protocol error). TimeoutException is itself a TransportError\r\nagent/llm/client.py:88:        if isinstance(e, httpx2.TimeoutException):\r\nagent/llm/client.py:89:            return LlmError(code=\"timeout\", retryable=True, message=\"Request timed out.\")\r\nagent/llm/factory.py:19:        request_timeout=kwargs.pop(\"request_timeout\", 60),\r\nagent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py:18:        request_timeout: float = 60.0,\r\nagent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py:25:        self.request_timeout = request_timeout\r\nagent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py:31:        http_client = httpx2.Client(proxy=proxy, trust_env=False, timeout=self.request_timeout)\r\nagent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py:43:        # timeout / reset / truncated response) escape the openai SDK unwrapped\r\nBinary file agent/llm/llm_clients/__pycache__/openai_client.cpython-314.pyc matches\nBinary file agent/llm/__pycache__/client.cpython-314.pyc matches\nBinary file agent/llm/__pycache__/factory.cpython-314.pyc matches\nui/app.js:22:      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 800));\nui/app.js:38:async function apiFetch(path, { method = \"GET\", body, base = API_BASE, timeout = 30000 } = {}) {\nui/app.js:43:  const timer = setTimeout(() => ctl.abort(), timeout);\nui/app.js:53:    clearTimeout(timer);\nui/app.js:490:// attempt fails before its first token (network drop / read timeout) and the\nui/app.js:967:    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)); // let the bar repaint between blocks\nui/app.js:1763:  else setTimeout(finish, MODAL_CLOSE_MS);\nui/app.js:2279:  clearTimeout(treeRefreshTimer);\nui/app.js:2280:  treeRefreshTimer = setTimeout(refreshTree, 300);\nui/app.js:2438:    const data = await apiFetch(`/api/history?before=${oldestOffset}&limit=262144`, { timeout: 60000 });\nui/app.js:2469:        await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0)); // repaint between blocks\nui/app.js:2575:        if (++processed % 25 === 0) await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 0));\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789619473.1230097, "id": "761c07b557f6", "tool_call_id": "call_a7ba19f98e2944f8aab7b294", "content": "1: \"\"\"LLM client contract + error normalization.\n2: \n3: - LlmClient is the streaming contract (see stream()); concrete providers live in\n4:   llm_clients/ (OpenaiLlmClient) and are built through the factory (factory.py).\n5: - Errors are normalized to LlmError; the caller decides retry vs abort.\n6: - Proxy is resolved through our own env logic (see proxy.py) so the socks://\n7:   scheme httpx cannot parse never reaches it.\n8: \"\"\"\n9: \n10: from __future__ import annotations\n11: \n12: import ast\n13: from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n14: from dataclasses import dataclass\n15: from typing import Any, Collection, Iterator\n16: \n17: \n18: def _clean_provider_message(raw: str, status: int | None) -> str:\n19:     \"\"\"Tidy an OpenAI-SDK error string. The SDK renders HTTP failures as\n20:     ``Error code: 429 - {'error': {'message': …, 'code': …}}`` (a Python repr\n21:     of the provider's JSON); surface the provider's own message instead of\n22:     that dump. Anything that does not parse stays verbatim.\"\"\"\n23:     text = raw.strip()\n24:     _, sep, body = text.partition(\" - \")\n25:     if not sep:\n26:         return text\n27:     try:\n28:         data = ast.literal_eval(body)\n29:     except (ValueError, SyntaxError):\n30:         return text\n31:     err = data.get(\"error\") if isinstance(data, dict) else None\n32:     if not (isinstance(err, dict) and isinstance(err.get(\"message\"), str) and err[\"message\"].strip()):\n33:         return text\n34:     extra = []\n35:     if err.get(\"code\"):\n36:         extra.append(f\"code {err['code']}\")\n37:     if status is not None:\n38:         extra.append(f\"HTTP {status}\")\n39:     tidied = err[\"message\"].strip()\n40:     return f\"{tidied} ({', '.join(extra)})\" if extra else tidied\n41: \n42: \n43: @dataclass\n44: class LlmError(Exception):\n45:     code: str = \"unknown\"\n46:     status: int | None = None\n47:     retryable: bool = False\n48:     message: str = \"\"\n49: \n50:     @staticmethod\n51:     def classify(e: Exception, retryable_status: Collection[int]) -> LlmError:\n52:         \"\"\"Normalize openai SDK / httpx2 transport exceptions into a structured\n53:         LlmError. The openai SDK only wraps errors raised while the *request* is\n54:         being sent; errors raised while the SSE *body* is being consumed (a\n55:         mid-stream stall past the read timeout, a sudden disconnect/reset, a\n56:         truncated response) escape `Stream.__stream__` unwrapped (its try only\n57:         closes the response), so they arrive here as raw httpx2 exceptions and\n58:         must be mapped explicitly — otherwise they fall into the non-retryable\n59:         \"unknown\" catch-all and a transient network blip kills the whole run.\n60:         \"\"\"\n61:         import httpx2\n62:         import openai\n63: \n64:         if isinstance(e, openai.RateLimitError):\n65:             return LlmError(code=\"rate_limit\", status=429, retryable=True, message=_clean_provider_message(str(e), 429))\n66:         if isinstance(e, openai.APITimeoutError):\n67:             return LlmError(code=\"timeout\", retryable=True, message=str(e))\n68:         if isinstance(e, openai.APIConnectionError):\n69:             return LlmError(code=\"connection\", retryable=True, message=str(e))\n70:         if isinstance(e, openai.APIStatusError):\n71:             status = e.status_code\n72:             if status == 400 and \"context\" in str(e).lower():\n73:                 return LlmError(\n74:                     code=\"context_window_exceeded\",\n75:                     status=400,\n76:                     retryable=False,\n77:                     message=_clean_provider_message(str(e), status),\n78:                 )\n79:             return LlmError(\n80:                 code=\"api_error\",\n81:                 status=status,\n82:                 retryable=status in retryable_status,\n83:                 message=_clean_provider_message(str(e), status),\n84:             )\n85:         # httpx2 transport errors raised mid-stream (read timeout / reset / EOF /\n86:         # remote protocol error). TimeoutException is itself a TransportError\n87:         # subclass, so check it first to keep the more specific code.\n88:         if isinstance(e, httpx2.TimeoutException):\n89:             return LlmError(code=\"timeout\", retryable=True, message=\"Request timed out.\")\n90:         if isinstance(e, httpx2.TransportError):\n91:             return LlmError(\n92:                 code=\"connection\",\n93:                 retryable=True,\n94:                 message=f\"Connection interrupted: {e}\" if str(e) else \"Connection interrupted.\",\n95:             )\n96:         return LlmError(code=\"unknown\", retryable=False, message=str(e))\n97: \n98: \n99: class LlmClient(ABC):\n100:     \"\"\"Streaming chat client contract.\n... (showing lines 1-100 of 111; use offset=101 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789619570.4385643, "id": "53f9a3552e3f", "content": "\"Request timed out.\" 的两个来源都找到了：`openai.APITimeoutError`（SDK 在**发请求/连不上**时抛）和 httpx2 `TimeoutException`（**流式响应中途**停顿超时）。而 `openai_client.py:31` 用的是单一浮点超时 `timeout=60`——httpx 会把它同时套在 connect/read/write 上。先确认 openai SDK 会不会用它自己的默认超时覆盖我们传的值，再看 proxy 和重试循环：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_acbbd63350424b61a6189e7c", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"grep -n \\\"timeout\\\" .venv/Lib/site-packages/openai/_base_client.py | head -30\"}"}, {"id": "call_4757f8f966a34d54a0f1afc2", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/llm/proxy.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now I see the \"Request timed out.\" message source: `httpx2.TimeoutException` mid-stream → LlmError(code=\"timeout\"). Also `openai.APITimeoutError` (\"Request timed out.\" from the SDK).\n\nKey line: `httpx2.Client(proxy=proxy, trust_env=False, timeout=self.request_timeout)` with `request_timeout=60.0` default (config llm_request_timeout: 60.0).\n\nA single float timeout in httpx applies the SAME 60s to connect/read/write/pool. For a STREAMING SSE response, the read timeout means: no bytes for 60s → ReadTimeout → httpx2.TimeoutException → \"Request timed out.\" → retryable → retry → potentially stall again → after 3 attempts, error surfaces to UI. Meanwhile the loop emits LlmRetryEvent (\"reconnecting…\") and finally shows the error.\n\nSo why would the stream stall >60s despite a \"good network\"?\n\nCauses:\n1. Provider-side generation gap: DeepSeek reasoner during heavy load can stall between chunks; >60s gaps happen during provider incidents. Retries make it worse visually (3×60s of waiting).\n2. GFW/ISP throttling of long-lived TLS streams: short requests (search, GitHub) fine; a 2-5 min SSE stream to a foreign endpoint gets its connection throttled/dropped mid-stream → read stalls → 60s timeout. \"网络环境良好\" is measured by browsing/short requests, but long-lived streams are the fragile path. This is extremely common in CN for api.openai.com etc.\n3. Connect timeout: dead route → ConnectTimeout after 60s of SYN retries (Windows TCP connect timeout can exceed 60s? httpx enforces 60s) — for flaky foreign routes, connect sometimes takes 20-60s.\n4. The proxy: get_proxy_for_url(base_url) — if a local socks/http proxy is set via env (LLM_PROXY?), trust_env=False + our own resolution. If the proxy process is slow, pool/connect timeouts. Let me read proxy.py to see when a proxy is applied.\n\nAlso `factory.py` line 19: `request_timeout=kwargs.pop(\"request_timeout\", 60)` — CLI/server passes cfg.llm_request_timeout.\n\nLet me read proxy.py fully and the retry loop part of openai_client (lines 80-118) plus loop.py's retry handling to see what the user actually sees (LlmRetryEvent → UI \"reconnecting\" then \"Request timed out.\" after attempts exhausted).\n\nThen the fix. Options:\n\nA. Split timeouts: `httpx2.Timeout(self.request_timeout, connect=min(15.0, self.request_timeout), read=<read_budget>)` where read budget should be much larger than 60 for streaming. Add config `llm_read_timeout: float = 180.0`. Rationale: read timeout is per-chunk-gap (between SSE bytes), not total — so 180s tolerates provider stalls without hanging forever (a truly dead connection times out at 180s → retry).\n\nB. Reduce visible flakiness: currently EVERY timeout is retryable, good. With read=180, transient stalls ride through without retry storms. Connect failures fail at 15s → faster reconnects, better UX.\n\nAlso consider: `llm_request_timeout` default 60 with connect=min(15, 60)=15 changes behavior: connect fails 4× faster. Good.\n\nBut wait — is the stall really >60s? User says \"频繁出现\". With read=60: any SSE gap ≥60s triggers it. DeepSeek in overload periods emits nothing and then returns 503... During cn incidents, long gaps are common. Also possible: the user's endpoint is a third-party OpenAI-compatible proxy (they set base_url in settings — could be anything, e.g., a relay). Relays buffer streams; some relays hold the whole response before flushing → for long generations, no bytes for >60s → guaranteed timeout EVERY long generation. That matches \"频繁\" perfectly: every long response dies at 60s, short ones pass. The buffering-relay scenario is very plausible given Chinese users commonly use API relays.\n\nWith read=180-300, long buffered generations up to 3-5min ride through. Plus keep retry.\n\nC. Also check loop.py's retry render text and that attempts exhausted error surfaces as \"Request timed out.\" — user sees that string in UI. The fix message could clarify which phase timed out: connect vs read. httpx2 exceptions: ConnectTimeout vs ReadTimeout (both TimeoutException subclasses). Improve classify: distinguish and give hint. E.g.:\n- ConnectTimeout → \"Connection timed out (no route to the API endpoint in 15s)…\"\n- ReadTimeout → \"Request timed out (no data from the API for Xs — provider stall or buffering relay; retrying)…\"\nKeep message short but actionable. Since message shows in UI, this directly answers \"why do I see timeouts with good network\": explain connect vs read.\n\nD. Another real contributor: `llm_request_timeout` is ALSO passed to openai SDK as its own timeout? In openai_client they pass http_client with timeout; OpenAI SDK with custom http_client uses the client's timeout unless per-request timeout given. SDK default timeout not applied since http_client provided (SDK sets per-request timeout = self.timeout unless overridden... Actually with http_client given, openai uses http_client's configuration; the SDK's default timeout applies as request timeout option which OVERRIDES client timeout? In openai-python, if you pass http_client, the SDK still uses its `timeout` (default 600s? DefaultTimeout 600 total...) Hmm: openai SDK: `self.timeout = timeout if timeout is not None else DEFAULT_TIMEOUT` (600s), and per-request it does httpx request(timeout=self.timeout)? In httpx, a request-level timeout overrides client-level. openai SDK passes timeout=options.timeout (its own 600 default or configured max_retries/timeout) to client.send? If so, our httpx2.Client timeout=60 is overridden by SDK's 600! Let me check openai SDK behavior: in openai/base_client.py, `request()` builds `request = self._build_request(...)` where timeout handling: `timeout = httpx.Timeout(timeout) if isinstance...` and `request = self.client.build_request(..., timeout=timeout)`... I recall: when you supply a custom http_client, openai STILL applies its own timeout to requests (default 600s), overriding the client's. Their docs say: \"If you provide your own http client, you'll need to configure the timeout yourself\" — actually they warn the opposite. Hmm. To be accurate, check installed openai package source: .venv/Lib/site-packages/openai/_base_client.py — search \"timeout\". Let me grep it.\n\nThis matters: if SDK's 600s overrides, then observed timeouts wouldn't be 60s... unless mid-stream httpx read timeout uses client-level (request-level timeout param passed to build_request overrides client default entirely — including read). If SDK passes timeout=600 → our 60 is ignored → timeouts would occur at 600s gaps... \"frequent\" 600s stalls = every 10min of stall — less \"frequent\". Or SDK passes its timeout only when not streaming? I need to check the installed source to be sure. Grep openai/_base_client.py for \"timeout\".\n\nThen decide the exact fix point. If SDK overrides with DEFAULT_TIMEOUT(600) for every request, the effective read timeout is 600s already, and \"Request timed out.\" appearing frequently means REAL ≥10min stalls?? Unlikely; more likely the message came from openai.APITimeoutError (classify line 66) — which the SDK raises when ITS timeout (600s? or the http client's?) hits... APITimeoutError is raised by SDK only around the initial request send (its try wraps send/connect), while mid-stream escapes raw (per the docstring here). So a user seeing \"Request timed out.\" could be either.\n\nLet me grep the SDK to pin down timeout precedence. Then craft fix:\n\nIn openai_client.__init__:\n```python\ntimeout = httpx2.Timeout(self.request_timeout, connect=min(15.0, self.request_timeout), read=self.read_timeout)\n```\nand pass `timeout=timeout` into OpenAI(...) too (so SDK's per-request timeout matches ours) — OpenAI accepts timeout param. If SDK default 600 was overriding, passing ours explicitly restores control.\n\nAdd config: `llm_read_timeout: float = 240.0` with comment (per-chunk gap budget for streaming; provider stalls / buffering relays). Factory: pop and pass. base.py._build_llm passes request_timeout already; add read_timeout=cfg.llm_read_timeout.\n\nfactory.py signature: create_llm_client(**kwargs) → OpenaiLlmClient. Update factory to read kwargs.\n\nCheck where else create_llm_client called (supervisor? server? tests). grep create_llm_client.\n\nAlso update classify() to distinguish ConnectTimeout/ReadTimeout for a self-explanatory message:\n```python\nif isinstance(e, httpx2.TimeoutException):\n    if isinstance(e, httpx2.ConnectTimeout): message \"connect timed out ...\"\n    elif ReadTimeout/WriteTimeout...\n```\nhttpx2 exceptions: TimeoutException(base) → ConnectTimeout, ReadTimeout, WriteTimeout, PoolTimeout. PoolTimeout only with limited pool.\n\nMessage wording (user-facing in UI):\n- ConnectTimeout: f\"Connect timed out: no route to the API endpoint within {connect:.0f}s (network to the provider, proxy, or wrong base_url).\"\n- ReadTimeout: f\"Read timed out: no data from the API for {read:.0f}s (provider stall or a buffering relay; the run will retry).\" — but classify shouldn't know budgets... it can accept them? classify(e, retryable_status) static; keep message generic but phase-specific: \"Connect timed out.\" / \"Read timed out (no stream data — provider stall or buffering relay).\"\n- PoolTimeout: \"Timed out waiting for a free connection slot.\"\n\nAlso loop.py surfaces retry events; check wording there quickly (grep \"retry\" in loop.py) — optional.\n\nNow the OTHER timeout surface: ui/app.js apiFetch timeout=30000 for API calls to the local server; long POST /api/run? SSE endpoints use EventSource (no fetch timeout). 30s fetch timeout to LOCAL server could fire if server busy (GIL blocked by a long tool?) — server is Flask/http.server? If a tool call blocks the single-threaded server, UI polls time out → \"request timed out\"?? The user's phrase \"上面\" (in the app) — could be either LLM or UI-server. But string \"Request timed out.\" exact match = LLM path. UI apiFetch abort → error text \"请求超时\"? grep ui for \"timed out\" showed only apiFetch timer and comment. The abort error message would be AbortError → \"Failed to fetch\" typically, not \"request timed out\". So LLM path it is.\n\nAlso supervisor/proxy timeouts: read proxy.py to be thorough (it resolves socks:// etc.). And check ui reverse-proxy (ui/ssh-tunnel.js) for its own timeout on forwarded LLM requests — README: \"LLM 请求经隧道转发回客户端本地反代\". If the local反代 (Node) has a default socket timeout < provider stall, IT would cut the stream → agent sees ConnectionReset/EOF not timeout though. Node http server default has no request timeout by default (server.requestTimeout=300s in Node 18+! headersTimeout 60s). Node 18: server.requestTimeout default 300000ms (5min), headersTimeout 60s. For a proxied streaming response, requestTimeout applies to receiving the request (client→proxy)? It's for parsing the incoming request from the agent — small, fast. The proxy's upstream fetch to provider could have undici default timeouts (headers 300s/300s body?) — undici headersTimeout 300s. If provider stalls >300s... beyond scope.\n\nFocus: primary suspect = 60s uniform timeout too tight for read on streaming + retry storm noise. Check SDK timeout precedence now."}
