{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789619570.8226376, "id": "d3f575ebd13b", "tool_call_id": "call_acbbd63350424b61a6189e7c", "content": "OK: command succeeded\nstdout:\n68:    timeout_exceptions,\n74:    normalize_httpx_timeout,\n75:    normalize_httpx2_timeout,\n79:    normalize_legacy_httpx_timeout,\n388:    timeout: Union[float, Timeout, None]\n400:        timeout: float | Timeout | None = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT,\n407:        self.timeout = timeout\n490:        if \"x-stainless-read-timeout\" not in lower_custom_headers:\n491:            timeout = normalize_httpx_timeout(\n492:                self.timeout if isinstance(options.timeout, NotGiven) else options.timeout\n494:            if isinstance(timeout, Timeout):\n495:                timeout = timeout.read\n496:            if timeout is not None:\n497:                headers[\"x-stainless-read-timeout\"] = str(timeout)\n600:        timeout = self.timeout if isinstance(options.timeout, NotGiven) else options.timeout\n604:            timeout = normalize_legacy_httpx_timeout(timeout)\n606:            timeout = normalize_httpx2_timeout(timeout)\n611:            timeout=timeout,\n791:    def _calculate_retry_timeout(\n812:        timeout = sleep_seconds * jitter\n813:        return timeout if timeout >= 0 else 0\n836:        # Retry on request timeouts.\n841:        # Retry on lock timeouts.\n865:        kwargs.setdefault(\"timeout\", DEFAULT_TIMEOUT)\n904:        timeout: float | Timeout | None | NotGiven = not_given,\n910:        if not is_given(timeout):\n912:            # timeout set then we use that timeout.\n915:            # where they've explicitly set the timeout to match the default timeout\n917:            # pass in a timeout and will ignore it\n918:            client_timeout = normalize_httpx_timeout(http_client.timeout) if http_client else None\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789619570.8325603, "id": "268bb71bb3d7", "tool_call_id": "call_4757f8f966a34d54a0f1afc2", "content": "\"\"\"Environment proxy resolution for the LLM client.\n\nhttpx cannot parse the `socks://` scheme (crashes with \"Unknown scheme for proxy\nURL\") and openai's bundled httpx trusts ambient proxy env vars. So we read only the\nprotocol-specific proxy vars ourselves, skip socks proxies (httpx has no SOCKS\nsupport), and respect no_proxy with a simple host/domain match.\n\nUsage: `get_proxy_for_url(\"https://api.deepseek.com\")` returns a proxy URL string\nfor httpx, or None for \"connect directly\".\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport os\nfrom urllib.parse import urlparse\n\nSOCK_SCHEMES = (\"socks://\", \"socks4://\", \"socks4a://\", \"socks5://\", \"socks5h://\")\n\n\ndef _env(key: str) -> str:\n    return os.environ.get(key.lower(), \"\") or os.environ.get(key.upper(), \"\")\n\n\ndef _no_proxy_match(hostname: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True if hostname is excluded by no_proxy (comma-separated host/domain entries).\"\"\"\n    no_proxy = _env(\"no_proxy\").strip()\n    if not no_proxy or no_proxy == \"*\":\n        return False\n    for entry in no_proxy.split(\",\"):\n        entry = entry.strip().lstrip(\"*\")\n        if not entry:\n            continue\n        if hostname == entry or hostname.endswith(\".\" + entry):\n            return True\n    return False\n\n\ndef get_proxy_for_url(input_url: str | None) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"Return an httpx-compatible proxy URL for the target, or None (direct).\"\"\"\n    if input_url is None:\n        return None\n    parsed = urlparse(input_url)\n    if parsed.scheme not in (\"http\", \"https\"):\n        return None\n    hostname = parsed.hostname or \"\"\n    if not hostname or _no_proxy_match(hostname):\n        return None\n    if hostname in (\"127.0.0.1\", \"localhost\", \"::1\"):\n        # loopback never needs a proxy (e.g. the client-side LLM proxy via SSH -R)\n        return None\n\n    proxy = _env(f\"{parsed.scheme}_proxy\") or _env(\"all_proxy\")\n    if not proxy:\n        return None\n    if any(proxy.lower().startswith(s) for s in SOCK_SCHEMES):\n        # httpx cannot use SOCKS; fall back to a direct connection\n        return None\n    if \"://\" in proxy:\n        return proxy\n    return f\"{parsed.scheme}://{proxy}\"\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789619607.8321595, "id": "b3448ae05733", "content": "SDK 在 `http_client=` 传入且未显式给 `timeout` 时会**沿用 http_client 的超时**（_base_client.py:918）——所以我们传的 60s 就是生效值。确认一下那段构造逻辑，并看 factory 和相关测试：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_b1770c8a32d94c4e85b190c9", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"head -30 .venv/Lib/site-packages/openai/_base_client.py | grep -n \\\"\\\" | head -5; sed -n '895,930p' .venv/Lib/site-packages/openai/_base_client.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_28e883607cc1431ab0af65af", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/llm/factory.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_5b4beb8da5c24fef97b10109", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"grep -rln \\\"create_llm_client\\\\|openai_client\\\\|OpenaiLlmClient\\\" tests/ agent/ --include=\\\"*.py\\\" | head\"}"}], "reasoning": "Key SDK finding at `_base_client.py` line 904-918: when constructing OpenAI with `http_client=` given and no explicit `timeout` passed, the SDK **reads the http_client's timeout** (\"client_timeout = normalize_httpx_timeout(http_client.timeout) if http_client else None ... where they've explicitly set the timeout to match the default timeout / pass in a timeout and will ignore it\"). So the effective per-request timeout comes from our `httpx2.Client(timeout=60)` — the SDK respects it. Good: our 60s IS the effective connect+read+write budget.\n\nSo confirmed root cause chain:\n\n1. `httpx2.Client(timeout=60.0)` — one float → connect=60, read=60, write=60, pool=60.\n2. Streaming SSE: read timeout = max gap between bytes. Any ≥60s gap (provider overload stall, buffering relay/中转站 that flushes only at the end for long generations, GFW-throttled long-lived stream) → `httpx2.ReadTimeout` → `LlmError(code=\"timeout\")` → retry up to 3 → each retry may re-stall → user sees \"Request timed out.\" frequently, while short requests (browsing, search) all work — hence \"网络环境良好\".\n3. Also connect: on flaky routes to foreign endpoints, connect can eat most of the 60s; with our own fix it should fail fast and be retried sooner. But primary read.\n\nAlso relevant: openai SDK's own retry (its `max_retries` param not set → default 2 SDK-level retries on APITimeoutError for the initial send!) — openai default max_retries=2, plus our loop 3 attempts. And SDK line 836: \"Retry on request timeouts\" — SDK retries internally too. Not the bug, but affects latency of surfacing.\n\nNow implement the fix:\n\n1. `agent/config.py`: add `llm_read_timeout: float = 240.0` next to llm_request_timeout, with comment explaining semantics (per-gap streaming budget). Keep llm_request_timeout as connect/write/pool budget (60 → connect override to min(15, x)? I'll set connect=min(15, llm_request_timeout)).\n\nHmm, changing llm_request_timeout semantics: previously it was THE timeout for everything. Now: connect/write/pool = min(15, request_timeout) for connect, request_timeout for write/pool; read = llm_read_timeout. Document in config comments.\n\n2. `agent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py`:\n```python\nrequest_timeout: float = 60.0,\nread_timeout: float = 240.0,\n...\n# One float would give read=connect=60: a streaming SSE response is then\n# killed by any 60s gap between chunks (provider stall / buffering relay),\n# which is the common \"network is fine yet request timed out\" case. Budgets\n# are split instead: fail fast on connect (dead route), tolerate long\n# chunk gaps (read is per-gap, not total).\ntimeout = httpx2.Timeout(\n    self.request_timeout,\n    connect=min(15.0, self.request_timeout),\n    read=self.read_timeout,\n)\nproxy = get_proxy_for_url(base_url)\nhttp_client = httpx2.Client(proxy=proxy, trust_env=False, timeout=timeout)\nself.client = OpenAI(api_key=..., base_url=..., http_client=http_client, timeout=timeout)\n```\nWait — passing timeout to OpenAI(): SDK then uses THAT as self.timeout and per-request timeout (line 600: `timeout = self.timeout if NotGiven`) overriding http_client's. If I pass the same Timeout object, same result. But careful: SDK may convert httpx.Timeout for httpx2? normalize_httpx2_timeout handles. Actually simpler: do NOT pass timeout to OpenAI — SDK picks up http_client.timeout (line 918 logic) and uses it per-request. Hmm, line 600-611: in request(), timeout = self.timeout (DEFAULT_TIMEOUT 600!) if options not given → then normalize → build_request(timeout=timeout). That would override per-request! BUT line 918 area shows constructor logic: when http_client given and no timeout arg → they set self.timeout = client_timeout (from http_client). Need to verify that the constructor actually assigns self.timeout = client_timeout in that branch. Let me read lines 895-930 of _base_client.py to confirm. If yes: not passing timeout → SDK inherits ours.\n\n3. `agent/llm/client.py` classify: split TimeoutException into phase-specific messages:\n```python\nif isinstance(e, httpx2.TimeoutException):\n    phase = \"connect\" if isinstance(e, httpx2.ConnectTimeout) else \"pool\" if PoolTimeout else \"read/write\"\n    messages per phase:\n      ConnectTimeout → \"Connect timed out: no route to the API endpoint (network to provider, proxy, or base_url).\"\n      PoolTimeout → \"Timed out waiting for a connection slot.\"\n      otherwise → \"Read timed out: the API sent no data for a long stretch (provider stall or buffering relay).\"\n```\n4. `agent/llm/factory.py`: pass read_timeout through. Read factory file (19 lines area) first.\n5. `agent/base.py` _build_llm: pass read_timeout=cfg.llm_read_timeout.\n\nThen find tests referencing create_llm_client/openai_client to update (grep tests for \"openai_client\\|create_llm_client\\|request_timeout\").\n\nThen finish websearch module + prompts + registry + tests (websearch_test.py with --live), run all relevant tests, update README (both the web tools and mention timeout knobs?). README minimal: add web_search/web_fetch to tools line in 项目结构 and maybe env keys note. Keep small.\n\nAlso save_memory entries at the end.\n\nLet me read factory.py and the SDK constructor branch now."}
