{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789621037.0880103, "id": "1cab8c6242fe", "content": "两个问题都收到：①每次写这个大 py 文件（websearch.py）连接就被重置——原因和超时同源：**整文件内容作为一次 tool call 的参数流经 LLM API，流越长越容易被链路 RST**，所以我改用「小骨架 + 分段 append」绕开；②先检查我之前哪处改动带了中文提示，改回英文。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_1db3d216726243d589721f00", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"git diff --stat && git diff | grep -nP \\\"[\\\\x{4e00}-\\\\x{9fff}]\\\" | head -20\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user is now reporting:\n1. \"每次写这个py文件时都会这样\" — Every time (the agent) writes \"this py file\", the connection gets interrupted with [WinError 10054] 远程主机强迫关闭了一个现有的连接 (remote host forcibly closed an existing connection).\n2. \"本项目的所有文字都是英文，这里的提示不应该使用中文\" — All text in this project is English, so the prompts/messages should not be in Chinese.\n\nSo two things:\n1. A bug: every time the agent writes a specific .py file, the connection dies with WinError 10054. Which py file? Probably `agent/tools/websearch.py` — the file I'm about to write / was trying to write when the connection interrupts. Actually the pattern \"每次写这个py文件时都会这样\" suggests that when the model attempts to write a large file (websearch.py content is big), the connection drops. Why would writing a file kill the connection?\n\nThink: The UI↔server is HTTP+SSE. WinError 10054 = TCP RST. When does a big write_file kill the connection?\n\nPossible causes:\n- The SSE stream connection to the UI gets RST when the server sends a huge burst of data (the ToolCallDeltaEvent / ToolResultEvent with a large file content). If the UI's read buffer or the server's socket send has an issue... Actually a known Windows issue: writing large payloads to a socket that the peer isn't reading fast enough → RST? Normally it just blocks. RST happens when the peer closes abruptly or the process crashes.\n- More likely: the agent backend process CRASHES when writing this file — e.g., an unhandled exception in the streaming path, or memory. But the run continues (\"每次写这个py文件时都会这样\" — the connection interrupts, then user hits 继续).\n- OR: the file being written is INSIDE the workspace and the UI's file tree refresh watches it... no.\n- Another classic: antivirus (Windows Defender) locks the new file → server's SSE writer fine...\n- Actually 10054 on the UI side: \"远程主机强迫关闭了一个现有的连接\" is the error the UI (or agent?) reports. The message appears as \"[connection] Connection interrupted: [WinError 10054]...\" — that looks like it's rendered in the conversation as a user-side note. Hmm, in this chat the messages labeled \"[connection] Connection interrupted: [WinError 10054] 远程主机强迫关闭了一个现有的连接\" — this is the LLM API connection (agent → provider) being reset! WinError 10054 when the LLM provider endpoint (or the relay) force-closes the TCP connection mid-request.\n\nSo: every time the agent writes \"this py file\" (a big file — websearch.py, several hundred lines), the LLM streaming request gets RST by the remote host. Why? Because writing a large file means:\n1. The model streams a HUGE tool_call arguments payload (the whole file content as one tool call). Long sustained upload+output stream → the connection lives long → relay/GFW kills it mid-stream. This matches my earlier diagnosis: long-lived streams get reset (RST = 10054 on the next read/write). LlmError.classify maps httpx2.TransportError → \"Connection interrupted: ...\" — the exact message format seen: \"Connection interrupted: [WinError 10054] 远程主机强迫关闭了一个现有的连接。\" That's line 94 in client.py: `message=f\"Connection interrupted: {e}\"`. \n\nSo the \"[connection] Connection interrupted: [WinError 10054]\" is a mid-stream TransportError while streaming a big tool call. Each retry restarts the whole stream; the model re-generates the huge file from scratch, and the longer stream increases the odds of another RST → the model may never finish writing the big file. Hence \"每次写这个py文件时都会这样\" — every attempt to write the big websearch.py dies.\n\nThis is the GFW/relay RST-on-long-stream problem. Fixes possible in-code:\n1. Retry exists (pre-token retry). But if the RST happens after many tool-call deltas have been delivered (the huge arguments stream), the retry raises immediately (no duplicate). The user sees the connection die repeatedly.\n2. Mitigations:\n   a. Split large writes: agent could write the file in chunks (multiple smaller edit/write calls) — guidance, not code.\n   b. TCP keepalive / smaller... won't stop RST.\n   c. The real fix is network-level (stable route to provider). But we can reduce stream duration: the model writes the file in several smaller write_file calls. I can design websearch.py smaller, or write it via multiple edit_file appends (each a smaller delta). That's within MY control right now: write the file in 2-3 smaller pieces instead of one giant write_file.\n   \nActually wait — I am the one writing files here through MY tools (the harness), not through Clutch's tool loop. But the user says \"每次写这个py文件时都会这样\" — they're talking about THIS session: every time I attempt to write websearch.py (the big file), the connection between Clutch UI ↔ provider RSTs because MY write_file tool call arguments (the whole file content) stream through the LLM API. The bigger the file, the longer the stream, the higher the RST chance. That's why my previous attempts to emit websearch.py kept dying — and the user had to keep pressing 继续.\n\nSo the practical mitigation: write websearch.py in SMALLER chunks — e.g., write a skeleton file first, then append sections with edit_file (each edit small). Each tool call streams fewer tokens → shorter stream → less RST exposure. I should do that now.\n\n2. The language issue: \"你的修改也不合理，本项目的所有文字都是英文，这里的提示不应该使用中文\" — my modifications used Chinese text where project convention is all-English. Where did I write Chinese? Let me recall my edits:\n- config.py: the llm_read_timeout comment — all English ✓.\n- openai_client.py: comments English ✓.\n- client.py: messages English ✓.\n- factory.py/base.py: English ✓.\n- tests/llm_client_test.py: English ✓.\n\nHmm — wait. Earlier in the session (compacted away?), maybe I added Chinese to somewhere? The first user message in THIS conversation mentioned \"另外你的修改也不合理，本项目的所有文字都是英文，这里的提示不应该使用中文\" — this same complaint appeared in the very first message of this session (the task line). So it refers to something I changed in an earlier session (before compaction). What did I change before? Git status showed modified: agent/core/permission.py, tests/local_shell_test.py, tests/remote_path_test.py, ui/app.js, ui/style.css. Maybe in a previous session I added a Chinese-language hint/error message somewhere — likely in permission.py (the chat-mode read-only rejection?) or ui. The user says \"这里的提示\" (the hint/message here) — \"here\" = wherever the timeout/connection error is displayed? Or the permission prompt?\n\nSince I can't recall, I should check the current diffs for Chinese characters: git diff and grep for CJK in the modified files. Let me search agent/ and ui/ for Chinese characters introduced by my changes: `git diff | grep`... pipeline grep might be blocked? Earlier `grep -rn` worked fine in run_command (the \"timed out\" search). Pipelines with grep got rejected once (\"segment 'grep' is a write operation\") but later `grep -n \"timeout\" file | head` WORKED. Inconsistent checker... earlier failures had curl|grep. Whatever — use grep directly.\n\nPlan:\n1. git diff to find my uncommitted changes; grep them for CJK.\n2. Fix any Chinese text → English (the user's complaint).\n3. Then write websearch.py in SMALL CHUNKS (skeleton via write_file, then 2-4 edit_file appends) to dodge the RST-on-long-stream issue.\n4. Add prompts, registry wiring, tests; run tests.\n5. Update README (project text is Chinese in README! README is Chinese. But \"本项目的所有文字都是英文\" refers to CODE text (tool descriptions, error messages, comments?) — README/UI prose is Chinese in this repo (README.md is Chinese, ui is Chinese?). The user means the text I add for the agent-facing prompts/messages should be English — consistent with all existing tool descriptions and error messages being English. Right: prompts/tools/*.md are all English; error messages English. So websearch prompts must be English. Good.)\n6. Also address the WinError 10054 explanation to the user in my final reply (it's the same long-stream-RST issue as diagnosed; chunked writes are the mitigation; the earlier timeout fix reduces but can't eliminate RST).\n\nLet me start: check the diffs for Chinese."}
