{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789626053.5976784, "id": "a5603f0207f3", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_9e28162b25d04418929aca40", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":16,\"offset\":500,\"path\":\"agent/tools/workspace.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_44da9af889074f08aeb09b17", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\",\"pattern\":\"sb\\\\.|workspace\\\\.\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now the architecture is clear:\n\n- `RemoteWorkspace` (in workspace.py): \"Workspace whose files live on a remote host reached over the exec bridge... base64, no SFTP — so any device that can run an sshd shell works.\"\n- The **agent backend process runs locally** (spawned by supervisor.py on the local machine), and in SSH mode the *workspace* is a `RemoteWorkspace` whose file operations and shell commands go through an SSH exec bridge (`SshTransport(bridge_url)` — bridge_url pointing to the UI's ssh-tunnel service, ui/ssh-tunnel.js).\n\nSo in the \"normal remote SSH scenario\":\n- File tools (read_file/write_file/edit_file/grep) → executed remotely via exec bridge (they touch the remote workspace)\n- run_command → `exec_shell` for RemoteWorkspace returns... let me check: line 502 `def exec_shell(self) -> LocalShell:` — for remote workspace, run_command text executes... over the bridge presumably (the shell the command runs under). Let me verify what exec_shell returns for RemoteWorkspace — line 502-511. It says \"The shell run_command text executes under (see tools.localshell)... oversized exec commands drop a minimal sshd: fail cleanly up front\". So run_command executes ON THE REMOTE via the bridge.\n- web_search / web_fetch → **NOT part of the workspace at all**. websearch.py only imports `Workspace` for the sandbox signature (`sb` param) but makes zero network calls through it. Let me verify: websearch.py imports `from .workspace import Workspace` — used as type hint for the sandbox argument, but `_http_get` uses `urllib.request` directly in the agent process. The agent process runs locally.\n\nTherefore: **websearch's network access is initiated from the LOCAL machine** (the machine running the Electron app + the backend supervisor/agent process), NOT from the remote server. In SSH mode, only workspace operations (file ops, shell commands) are tunneled to the remote host; the LLM API call and web_search/web_fetch go out from the local machine's network.\n\nWait — one more check: LLM calls. \"ui/ Electron 前端(设置、SSH 隧道、LLM 反代)\" — the LLM 反代 (reverse proxy) is in the UI, so LLM traffic also originates locally (or through a user-configured relay). That's consistent.\n\nAlso verify that websearch.py's functions don't use `sb` (the workspace) for anything network. From what I read: `web_search(workspace, config, query...)` — workspace param exists but likely unused except signature consistency; `_http_get` uses urllib directly. Confirmed by reading lines 490-525 earlier: `web_fetch` calls `_http_get(u, ...)` directly. No bridge involvement.\n\nOne nuance worth mentioning: this is a deliberate asymmetry — in SSH mode, run_command on the remote could curl things from the remote (so the model CAN reach the remote's network via shell), but the web_search tool itself is local. Also practical implications:\n- web_search uses the local machine's network egress (and any proxy via trust_env? No — `_http_get`... let me check if it uses proxies. urllib.request by default honors http_proxy env vars. In httpx client for LLM they set trust_env=False explicitly. For urllib, default ProxyHandler honors env. Whatever — minor).\n- So if the remote server is in a datacenter with different network (e.g., can reach Google but local can't), web_search results reflect LOCAL network conditions, not remote.\n- Also benefits: web requests don't consume remote bandwidth / don't depend on remote's egress restrictions; SSRF guard protects the LOCAL machine's LAN (agent's own HTTP API runs locally — that's why _assert_public_host matters: it guards the local agent API and local network).\n\nThat last point is a nice connection: the SSRF guard in websearch.py exists precisely because web_fetch runs on the LOCAL machine where the agent's own HTTP API (server.py, supervisor) listens — a model could otherwise probe localhost:port. In the tool docstring: \"Guards web_fetch against being pointed at the agent's own HTTP API or the surrounding network.\"\n\nLet me verify the RemoteWorkspace exec_shell quickly to be 100% accurate about run_command being remote, and check whether websearch.py ever uses sb. Quick grep for \"sb\" or \"workspace\" usage in websearch.py network paths — I already know _http_get doesn't take a workspace. Good enough; but let me confirm exec_shell for remote returns a remote shell wrapper."}
