{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789626939.1322448, "id": "e7952188e01f", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_472d8794ea6a4da7bf2406b8", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":40,\"offset\":157,\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user asks: \"那websearch/fetch可以浏览任意网页吗?\" (So can websearch/fetch browse any webpage?)\n\nThis is a question about the capabilities and limitations of the web_search/web_fetch tools. Based on my knowledge of the implementation (I wrote it), I should answer thoroughly but honestly. Let me structure the answer around what it CAN and CANNOT do.\n\nLet me recall the implementation details:\n\n**web_fetch limitations:**\n1. Text-only: `_BINARY_TYPES = (\"image/\", \"video/\", \"audio/\")` + `_BINARY_EXACT` (pdf, zip, gzip, tar, octet-stream) → refuses binary content. So no PDFs, no images.\n2. HTML via HTMLParser — no JavaScript execution! JS-rendered pages (SPA) return \"(no extractable text; the page is probably JS-rendered)\". This is the biggest limitation — no headless browser.\n3. 2MB hard cap per response (`_MAX_BYTES = 2_000_000`).\n4. `web_search_timeout: 15.0` — 15 second timeout. Slow pages fail.\n5. 200-char min limit floor on max_chars, default 20000 chars, pagination via start=N.\n6. SSRF guard: refuses non-public hosts (localhost, LAN, link-local, .localhost/.local/.internal, non-global IPs). So it cannot browse the local network, the agent's own API, intranet pages (e.g. 192.168.x.x, 10.x, company intranet). Public IP literals pass.\n7. Only http/https schemes — no ftp, file, etc.\n8. Redirects followed but each hop checked.\n9. Gzip only (Accept-Encoding: gzip) — brotli-only sites? If a site insists on br... we send \"Accept-Encoding: gzip\" so server should respond with gzip or identity. Sites that force brotli regardless would fail to decode (zlib/gzip errors). Rare.\n10. Character decoding: `_decode_body` uses charset from Content-Type, else... probably falls back to utf-8/latin-1. GBK pages without charset declared might be mojibake.\n11. No cookies, no login — pages behind auth return login walls. No browser fingerprint → some sites with Cloudflare bot protection return 403/challenge pages.\n12. No cookies/session — paywalls, etc.\n\n**web_search limitations:**\n1. Backend chain: tavily (key) → searxng (url) → bing RSS → ddg HTML. On this machine Bing RSS works, DDG unreachable.\n2. Bing RSS gives limited results (RSS format returns ~10?) and may be subject to Bing's anti-bot (403s observed with 403 in tests).\n3. BackendError → fall through; all fail → error-as-data with each backend's reason.\n4. max_results cap: min 20.\n5. Search only returns title/url/snippet — need web_fetch for full text.\n6. Snippet cleaning truncates to 300 chars.\n\nSo the answer: \"能浏览大部分公开的、静态/服务端渲染的文本网页;不能渲染 JS、不能下载二进制、不能进内网/登录墙/付费墙,反爬严格的站点会被挡。\"\n\nAlso worth mentioning the *design intent*: this is a \"read pages as text for an LLM\" tool, not a browser. And the escape hatch: for JS-heavy pages, the model can... hmm, actually in work mode run_command could use curl (no JS either). There's no headless browser in the project. So JS-rendered pages are effectively unreachable (unless server-side rendering or an API alternative exists).\n\nAlso mention: any public URL the model constructs is reachable subject to network conditions (local egress, GFW etc. — Bing works here, Google/DDG don't from this network. Actually google.com — untested? DDG was HTTP 000. Probably Google also blocked in this network. Worth noting \"取决于本地网络可达性\").\n\nPractical framing for the user (they're a CS student, Chinese): structure as 能/不能 table, with the mechanism behind each limit. Keep it focused. No need for tools — I know the code. Maybe a quick verification of a couple of facts from the code to be precise: the JS-rendered message, binary types, decode fallback. I have read most of these lines already in this session:\n- Line 506-508: binary check ✓\n- Line 510: html detection, line 514: \"(no extractable text; the page is probably JS-rendered)\" ✓\n- _MAX_BYTES 2MB ✓ line 50\n- timeout 15s config ✓\n- _assert_public_host ✓ lines 71-91\n- scheme check lines 132-134 ✓\n- _BINARY_EXACT ✓ line 487\n\nOne thing I haven't verified: _decode_body fallback for missing charset. Line 195: `return raw.decode(\"latin-1\", errors=\"replace\")` — that's the fallback when no charset! So pages without declared charset (or non-UTF8 like GB2312 without meta) may be mojibake. Let me check the decode function quickly to be accurate — it matters for \"任意网页\" (arbitrary Chinese pages often declare gb2312/gbk in meta but not HTTP header). Let me read lines 160-196.\n\nActually, from line 195 context, the fallback is latin-1 which would mojibake GBK pages lacking HTTP charset. Let me verify whether _decode_body sniffs <meta charset>. Quick read."}
