wall block text?). Add fetch: \n\nres=[(n, ws.web_search(None,cfg,q,max_results=2)) for n,q in [...]]; then for each, extract url, fetch. One line with nested comprehensions and prints gets unreadable but is fine:\n\n[(lambda r, m: (print('='*12,n), print(r['content'][:350]), (lambda f: (print('FETCH',m.group(0)[:80]), print('len:',len(f['content']),'err:',f.get('error')), print(f['content'][:250].replace(chr(10),' | '))))(ws.web_fetch(None,cfg,m.group(0)))) if (r:=ws.web_search(...)) ... \n\nToo complex. Walrus in comprehension + lambda — risky syntax. Keep two commands: (1) search probe one-liner (simple), (2) after seeing URLs, fetch 2-3 specific URLs with a trivial one-liner. Fetch one-liner is trivial: .venv/Scripts/python.exe -c \"import sys; sys.stdout.reconfigure(...); sys.path.insert(0,'.'); from agent.config import Config; from agent.tools import websearch as ws; f=ws.web_fetch(None,Config(),'URL'); print(f.get('error'), len(f['content'])); print(f['content'][:400])\"\n\nURL contains no quotes — fine. Plan: 3 searches in one command, then 3 fetch commands (or one fetch command chaining three fetches with semicolons).\n\nLet me do the search probe first."}
