ne place and \"Current mode: chat (read-only)\" in another — conflicting.\n\nActually, re-reading the system prompt order:\n- \"Current mode: work (full access).\" + \"You are in work mode: ...\" — this appears in the middle\n- \"Current mode: chat (read-only).\" + \"You are in chat mode: ...\" — this appears near the end, after \"Available skills\" section? No wait...\n\nLet me look at the actual order in the system prompt:\n1. Workflow\n2. Tool conventions\n3. Project memory\n4. Mermaid output rules\n5. \"Current mode: work (full access)\" + work mode description\n6. \"Available skills\"\n7. Local environment\n8. \"Current mode: chat (read-only)\" + chat mode description\n\nHmm, both are present. The chat block comes last. But in practice, my first-turn edit_file succeeded — meaning at that point it was work mode. Then mid-conversation commands were rejected as chat mode. Now the user says 开始 after my \"请切到 work mode\" request.\n\nThe pragmatic approach: attempt the work. If the mode is still chat, edit_file will error and I'll tell the user. Given the user explicitly said \"开始\" right after I asked them to switch to work mode, the most reasonable interpretation is they switched. The duplicated mode blocks in the prompt are likely an artifact. I'll proceed with implementation and let tool failures be the arbiter.\n\nNow, implement the plan. First, I need to re-read the files I'll modify (compaction warning: agent/tools/websearch.py, agent/tools/registry.py, tests/websearch_test.py were read in earlier turns of THIS conversation — I've read parts of websearch.py (lines 42-141, 157-197, 195-214, 260-269, 440-525) and registry.py (140-194). I haven't seen: websearch.py lines 1-41, 142-156, 198-259 (partially seen 195-214), 215-259, 270-439 (the backends!), 525-end(no, 525 lines total, seen up to 525). I've seen 270-439? No — I've seen 195-214, 260-269, 440-525. Missing: 1-41, 142-156, 215-259, 270-439. That includes _DdgParser, backends (_tavily, _searxng, _bing, _dd