bsearch_test.py? Design said new file + extend. Keep tests in one new file mcpbackend_test.py plus a regression run of websearch_test.\n- README update\n\nThat's a lot. Let me be methodical but efficient. Start with reads (parallel where independent).\n\nFirst batch of reads:\n- websearch.py offset=1 limit=195 (lines 1-195, missing 1-41 and 142-156... but 42-141 I've seen; reading 1-195 again wastes a bit, fine — actually rule says don't re-read what's in context. Lines 42-141 are in context from this session. I need 1-41, 142-157(seen 157-197), 215-259, 270-439. Let me do offset=1 limit=41? That's precise but multiple calls. Balance: read offset=270 limit=170 (the backends — biggest missing chunk, most critical), read offset=1 limit=41 (imports/consts), read offset=215 limit=55 (extractor internals partially seen... 195-214 seen; 215-259 unseen: _SKIP set etc. — needed? I'm not touching _TextExtractor. Skip unless needed. Actually 260-269 _window seen.)\n\nWait, also 142-157 (_http_get body continuation — headers/POST? seen 119-141 then 157+; the middle 142-156 has resp opening, proxies? Probably fine to read quickly as part of a 1-156 read).\n\nEfficient: read_file offset=1 limit=41, offset=142 limit=17, offset=215 limit=46, offset=270 limit=170. That's 4 calls on one file. Or just read 1-260 and 270-440 in 2 calls (re-reading ~150 known lines, ~8KB — acceptable, simpler). I'll do 2 reads: offset=1 limit=215 (1-215), offset=216 limit=225 (216-440). ~425 lines ≈ 20KB+ might hit max_chars; set max_chars higher (30000). read_file truncates at max_chars default 20000; I can pass max_chars=30000. Two reads with max_chars=30000 should cover.\n\nHmm wait — lines 216-439 includes _TextExtractor (already seen 195-214 = class start), _DdgParser (274+), backends (318+). I mainly need: _BACKENDS dict, _DEFAULT_CHAIN, _available_backends, _tavily/_searxng/_bing/_ddg signatures. Read offset=216 limit=225 covers it.\n\nAlso config.py full read, and the server.py settings region.