model, api_key)) and \"reasoning_effort\" not in body and not mcp_updates:`\n- persist: `raw = load_settings()`, `saved = flatten_settings(raw)`, then `for k in raw: if k.startswith(\"mcp_\"): saved[k] = raw[k]` (preserve through flatten), then apply mcp updates (validated: http(s), '' clears), save_settings.\n- live config: `with self._state.lock:` block — add mcp_urls updates there? The lock block mutates self._cfg. Add after the lock: set/clear entries on self._cfg.mcp_urls.\n\nActually simpler: define module-level helper:\n\n```\ndef _split_mcp_fields(body: dict) -> dict[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"Pull mcp_<provider> URL fields out of a settings POST body.\n\n    Values must be http(s) URLs (or '' to clear); names are free-form so a\n    newer UI can configure providers this build does not know.\n    \"\"\"\n    out = {}\n    for key, value in body.items():\n        if not key.startswith(\"mcp_\") or not isinstance(value, str):\n            continue\n        value = value.strip().rstrip(\"/\")\n        if value and not value.startswith((\"http://\", \"https://\")):\n            raise ValueError(f\"{key} must be an http(s) URL\")\n        out[key] = value\n    return out\n```\n\nThen in _settings_post:\n```\n        try:\n            mcp_updates = _split_mcp_fields(body)\n        except ValueError as e:\n            return self._json({\"error\": str(e)}, status=400)\n        base_url = ...\n        if not any((base_url, model, api_key)) and \"reasoning_effort\" not in body and not mcp_updates:\n            return self._json({\"error\": \"nothing to save\"}, status=400)\n```\nand persist section:\n```\n        raw = load_settings()\n        saved = flatten_settings(raw)\n        for k, v in raw.items():\n            if k.startswith(\"mcp_\"):\n                saved[k] = v  # preserve MCP service URLs flatten knows nothing about\n        ... existing LLM field updates ...\n        for key, value in mcp_updates.items():\n            if value:\n                saved[key] = value\n            else:\n                saved.pop(key, None)\n        save_settings(saved)\n```\nand live config:\n```\n        with self._state.lock:\n            ...\n            for key, value in mcp_updates.items():\n                name = key[4:]\n                if value:\n                    self._cfg.mcp_urls[name] = value\n                else:\n                    self._cfg.mcp_urls.pop(name, None)\n```\nCareful: the lock block currently ends with reasoning_effort. Add the mcp loop inside the `with` block.\n\nWait — one nuance: `rstrip(\"/\")` on empty after strip: \"\" stays \"\". fine. Also value like \"http://localhost:18060/mcp\" — keep.\n\n4c. _settings_get: add mcp_providers metadata from RAW settings:\n```\n        raw = load_settings()\n        ...\n        \"mcp_providers\": [\n            {\n                \"key\": f\"mcp_{p.name}\",\n                \"label\": p.label,\n                \"placeholder\": p.placeholder,\n                \"value\": raw.get(f\"mcp_{p.name}\", \"\"),\n            }\n            for p in mcp_providers()\n        ],\n```\n\n4d. GET route: add `/api/mcp_status` → self._mcp_status(). Add the handler method after _settings_get:\n\n```\n    def _mcp_status(self) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Per-provider MCP reachability + login state, for the settings modal.\n\n        Purely informational and only about services this machine's user chose\n        to configure: unconfigured providers report configured=False and the UI\n        shows a blank row — no suggestions, ever.\n        \"\"\"\n        out = []\n        for p in mcp_providers():\n            url = p.url_for(self._cfg)\n            row = {\"name\": p.name, \"configured\": bool(url), \"reachable\": None, \"login\": None}\n            if url:\n                try:\n                    state = _mcp_status_probe(url, p) if p.status_tool else None\n                    row[\"reachable\"] = True\n                    if isinstance(state, dict):\n                        login = state.get(\"login\", state.get(\"logged_in\", state.get(\"is_logged_in\")))\n                        row[\"login\"] = bool(login) if login is not None else None\n                except Exception as e:  # any failure is a status, not a crash\n                    row[\"reachable\"] = False\n                    row[\"detail\"] = str(e)[:160]\n            out.append(row)\n        self._json({\"providers\": out})\n```\n\nHmm — check_login_status returns something like {\"status\": \"logged_in\"} or bool? Unknown. The summary said row login = state. To be robust: normalize: if isinstance dict → look for common keys (login/logged_in/is_logged_in/status); if status is string \"logged_in\" → True. Else if bool → itself. Else None. Keep it simple and factual: report the raw payload too? Keep: row[\"login\"] normalized + row[\"raw\"]? Raw might be long. I'll normalize with a helper `_normalize_login(state)`:\n\n```\ndef _login_state(state: Any) -> bool | None:\n    \"\"\"Best-effort normalization of a provider's login-status payload.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(state, bool):\n        return state\n    if isinstance(state, dict):\n        for key in (\"login\", \"logged_in\", \"is_logged_in\", \"logged_in_status\"):\n            v = state.get(key)\n            if isinstance(v, bool):\n                return v\n            if isinstance(v, str):\n                return v.lower() in (\"logged_in\", \"logged in\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"ok\", \"success\")\n        return None\n    if isinstance(state, str):\n        return state.strip().lower() in (\"logged_in\", \"logged in\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"ok\", \"success\")\n    return None\n```\n\nFine — best effort, documented as such.\n\n4e. main(): after reasoning_effort resolution (line 735-736):\n```\n    # MCP web-search services (settings.json keys mcp_<provider>); the env-only\n    # CLUTCH_MCP_<NAME>_URL is resolved by the provider at call time.\n    config.mcp_urls.update(\n        {\n            k[4:]: v\n            for k, v in load_settings().items()\n            if k.startswith(\"mcp_\") and isinstance(v, str) and v.strip()\n        }\n    )\n```\n\nLet me write these edits. 4a imports first + helpers after save_settings."}
