py, llm_client_test.py, local_shell_test.py). Those are new work not covered by my earlier verification. Before releasing, I should at least compile everything and run the touched test suites: tests/llm_client_test.py, tests/local_shell_test.py, tests/mcpbackend_test.py, tests/websearch_test.py, plus perm-args-test.js maybe. Quick py_compile across agent/ and run the python test suites.\n\nAlso version bump: current tag v0.1.13 exists and HEAD is \"chore(release): 0.1.13\". So next version: 0.1.14 (assuming v0.1.13 was already released). But wait — was v0.1.13 already pushed/released? The remote is unreachable via SSH right now. ls-remote failed. Hmm. If v0.1.13 was never pushed, then... the summary's earlier session said the user's previous release was \"0.0.13\"?? The summary mentions \"the user's 0.0.13 CD build lacked it\". Odd numbering. The local tag is v0.1.13 though.\n\nI'll go with 0.1.14 unless I find evidence v0.1.14 already exists remotely (can't check remote without auth). Check local: `git tag -l` full list.\n\nAlso need to check whether release.yml's trigger is on tag push `v*`. The summary showed its jobs; I should read the top of the file (on: push: tags: v*).\n\nLet me run batch 1 now.\n\nActually also important: `git fetch` would fail with SSH. I could try `git ls-remote origin` with HTTPS? The remote URL matters. Let's see `git remote -v` first.\n\nOne more consideration: the summary claimed a successful push to github.com:clutch-dev/clutch.git. The current SSH denial might be because the ssh-agent isn't running in this fresh session, or the key has a passphrase and needs agent. On Windows with Git Bash, keys are usually at ~/.ssh. Let me check `ls ~/.ssh` too.\n\nOK, batch 1: 4 commands. Then proceed based on findings."}
