{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789812646.1569808, "id": "83ee90d3f2ee", "content": "真实状态与压缩摘要完全对不上——这是另一个远端（`Ethereal1024/Clutch`，不是摘要里的 `clutch-dev/clutch`），历史也完全不同（`chore(release): 0.1.13` 系列），SSH key 被 GitHub 拒绝，所有改动仍未提交。**摘要里的\"已发布\"没有发生在这个仓库**。现在基于真实现场重新走一遍完整流程。先并行摸清：未推的 10 个 commit、HTTPS 匿名可达性、版本/CI 接线、改动内容，以及项目记忆里的约定：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_de3db022c63e4e86b3be153f", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"git log --oneline origin/master..master\"}"}, {"id": "call_097d7481ff9c41258673aa6e", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 git ls-remote https://github.com/Ethereal1024/Clutch.git 2>&1 | head -12\"}"}, {"id": "call_ab74850e628d43ffb2705ff5", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cat VERSION; echo ====; head -25 .github/workflows/release.yml; echo ====; ls agent/tools/\"}"}, {"id": "call_4694675652dc4baba92a6a84", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"git diff --stat | tail -18; echo ====; git diff agent/llm/llm_clients/openai_client.py | head -50\"}"}, {"id": "call_f5dc4a865d8049ed8b5f323b", "name": "load_memory", "arguments": "{\"name\":\"Project conventions and Windows environment quirks\"}"}, {"id": "call_a3853d5a808a44a9832ae8ca", "name": "load_memory", "arguments": "{\"name\":\"MCP web-search plugin shipped and verified\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now the picture is completely different from the compacted summary. The real state:\n\n1. **Repo**: `git@github.com:Ethereal1024/Clutch.git` — NOT clutch-dev/clutch. The summary's remote was wrong (different repo).\n2. **History**: This is a totally different history! Commits are: 4555509 chore(release): 0.1.13, f43a397 fix(ui) UA-monospace, e9cd4e5 chore(release): 0.1.12, ... This is NOT the f3a9c2e/b7d41e8 history from the summary. So the summary described a DIFFERENT workspace/repo entirely. The compaction summary appears to be from a different session — possibly a different project (clutch-dev/clutch vs Ethereal1024/Clutch).\n\nHmm wait. Interesting. The summary mentioned \"MCP web-search plugin (xiaohongshu)\" etc. And the current untracked files include agent/tools/websearch.py, mcpprovider.py, prompts web_search.md/web_fetch.md, tests mcpbackend_test.py etc. So the WORK is the same, but the repo history is different. The current repo (Ethereal1024/Clutch) has its own history with releases 0.1.9→0.1.13. The local branch is ahead 10 of origin/master.\n\nWait, ahead 10 — which 10? `git log --oneline origin/master..master` would tell. Let me check that.\n\n3. **Tags**: v0.1.6 through v0.1.13 exist locally. HEAD is tagged v0.1.13.\n4. **Auth**: SSH publickey denied for git@github.com. ~/.ssh has id_ed25519 but it's not accepted. No gh CLI. credential.helper = manager (Windows credential manager) — so HTTPS with credential manager might work IF credentials for github.com are stored. The remote is SSH though.\n\nSo the previous session's claims (push succeeded to github.com:clutch-dev/clutch.git) were from... a different repo that no longer matches. Whatever — I deal with reality NOW.\n\nCurrent task: 提交，推送并发布新版本 (commit, push, release new version) for THIS repo.\n\nSteps:\n1. Understand what the 10 unpushed commits are (git log origin/master..master).\n2. Review working tree changes: the MCP/websearch work + openai_client.py + local_shell_test.py changes. Check diffs briefly, run tests.\n3. Handle untracked: websearch.py, webfetch.py?? — wait, webfetch.py is NOT in untracked list now. Let me check if it exists: `ls agent/tools/`. It's not in `git ls-files` output either (the ls-files output I got only showed prompts; it was truncated at 30 lines with head -30). Actually the third command output shows only agent/prompts files up to 30 lines — the head -30 cut it off before showing agent/tools and tests. I need to check whether agent/tools/websearch.py etc. are tracked.\n\nUntracked list from git status: agent/prompts/tools/web_fetch.md, agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md, agent/tools/mcpprovider.py, agent/tools/websearch.py, scratch logs, tests/mcpbackend_test.py, tests/perm-args-test.js, tests/websearch_test.py. NOT webfetch.py. So webfetch.py either doesn't exist or is tracked. Check `ls agent/tools/ | grep web`.\n\nAlso note: `.gitignore` is TRACKED in this repo (it's a real repo with a long-standing .gitignore). But the current .gitignore does NOT ignore scratch_*.log / scratch-*.py! The untracked list shows scratch-launch-tests.py, scratch-relaunch-tests.py, scratch_loop_test.log, scratch_ls.log, etc. — these are NOT ignored by current .gitignore. So I should either add ignore rules or not commit them. The summary's .gitignore (with scratch_* rules) was for the other repo. Here I should NOT commit scratch files. I could add scratch patterns to .gitignore as part of the commit, or just leave them untracked. Better: add ignore rules to keep status clean (matches prior session's intent). Hmm, but careful — modifying .gitignore is a repo change; it's reasonable and low-risk. Actually, let me keep it minimal: just don't stage scratch files. But then git status stays dirty... The user asked to commit/push/release. Cleanliness of status is nice-to-have. The prior memory says .gitignore got scratch rules — but that was the other repo's .gitignore. I'll add a small scratch section to .gitignore — it's in the spirit of the repo's existing \"keep local junk out\" comments. I think that's good and defensible.\n\n4. Version: current 0.1.13 released (tag v0.1.13 exists, HEAD = release commit). New version: **0.1.14**. Files to bump: VERSION (root file? release commit touched VERSION, ui/package-lock.json, ui/package.json). The chore(release) commit pattern bumps VERSION, package.json, package-lock.json. I should follow the same pattern: bump VERSION + ui/package.json + ui/package-lock.json, then commit feature work + release bump either as separate commits (repo convention seems to be separate \"chore(release): x.y.z\" commits) — yes! The repo convention is clear: feature commits then `chore(release): 0.1.14`. I should follow: commit features as feat/fix commit(s), then a `chore(release): 0.1.14` commit bumping versions, then tag v0.1.14 on the release commit, push master + tag.\n\nWait — what does VERSION file contain and what uses it? Check `cat VERSION`. The release.yml probably uses it for the backend bundle. Also check release.yml trigger (on: push: tags: 'v*').\n\n5. Auth problem: SSH denied. Options:\n   a. Test HTTPS remote: `git ls-remote https://github.com/Ethereal1024/Clutch.git` with credential.helper=manager. If the user has credentials in Windows credential manager, this works non-interactively. If not, it may prompt (blocked in non-TTY?). credential-manager might pop a UI window... risky but worth trying `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`.\n   b. Check why SSH fails: maybe the key isn't registered with GitHub anymore, or agent not running. `ssh-add -l` to see agent keys. With BatchMode it used the default key id_ed25519 directly and was denied → the key itself is not accepted by GitHub. Could try `ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 -o IdentitiesOnly=yes -T git@github.com` — same thing. So the key isn't (or no longer) registered on GitHub, OR it's registered to a different account.\n   \n   Also test if the public repo is reachable via HTTPS anonymously: `git ls-remote https://github.com/Ethereal1024/Clutch.git HEAD` — if repo is public, this works without auth and tells me the remote tip. Then I'd know how far behind origin is... wait, local says ahead 10 based on the last fetch. Let me do anonymous ls-remote to see origin/master's current sha. If it matches origin/master in my local refs, then ahead 10 is accurate.\n\n   c. Gitee? credential.https://gitee.com.provider generic exists — maybe there's a gitee mirror. But remote is github only.\n\n   If HTTPS push works via credential manager → use it: push to https://github.com/Ethereal1024/Clutch.git (add as URL to origin or push explicitly: `git push https://github.com/Ethereal1024/Clutch.git master v0.1.14`). Cleaner: temporarily push with explicit URL, leaving origin as-is, OR switch origin to HTTPS. Better to keep SSH remote but push via explicit HTTPS URL if cred helper has a PAT. Hmm, but if it works, maybe just use the URL each time. Actually cleanest for the user long-term: if HTTPS works, I can leave origin untouched and just push via URL; note it to user.\n\n   Let me first test: `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 git ls-remote https://github.com/Ethereal1024/Clutch.git 2>&1 | head`. If credential manager has stored creds, it proceeds silently. If not, it errors (GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 prevents hanging; GUI popup might still appear with GCM — that could hang the command... GCM in non-interactive might fail fast. Add timeout guard? run_command probably has its own timeout. Let's try.)\n\n6. Tests before commit: \n   - py_compile all changed python files + run test suites: tests/llm_client_test.py, tests/local_shell_test.py, tests/mcpbackend_test.py, tests/websearch_test.py, tests/remote_path_test.py, tests/perm-args-test.js (node).\n   - Which python? Summary used .venv/Scripts/python.exe. Check .venv exists here: `ls .venv/Scripts/python.exe`. The summary's env was this same workspace? The summary says workspace C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\临时文件\\Clutch — same path! Interesting: the edit_file result in summary shows \"C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\临时文件\\Clutch\\ui\\package.json\" — same workspace. But the git history differs completely?! That's bizarre. Unless... the summary conversation happened in a DIFFERENT directory also named Clutch, or the repo was reset/re-cloned since. Whatever — current state rules. But the .venv likely exists since prior session used it here.\n\n   Also package.json here shows version 0.1.13 — my 0.1.14 bump from the summary is gone, consistent with a repo reset. And package-lock.json exists (summary repo didn't mention it). Different repo history confirmed.\n\n   Hmm, wait. Actually there's another possibility