{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789813264.381077, "id": "72a99a9c1625", "content": "你说的应该是设置弹窗里新出现的 **Web services** 区块。我先对照实际代码确认一下交互细节（状态文案、保存行为、后端名），再给你准确的用法：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_7c11f7349850426e9a701df4", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/mcpprovider.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_6dc212621fab456c8eeef6ba", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"mcp|initMcpServices\",\"path\":\"ui/app.js\"}"}, {"id": "call_94613241359249868833b9d2", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md\"}"}, {"id": "call_dcd5b64cc1b1498eac96b371", "name": "load_memory", "arguments": "{\"name\":\"Xiaohongshu open-source access landscape\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user says \"我看到对应的ui了，我该如何使用？\" — \"I can see the corresponding UI, how do I use it?\"\n\nContext: We just released v0.1.14 with the MCP web-search plugin (xiaohongshu), web_search/web_fetch tools, and the settings modal Web services section. The user apparently has the UI in front of them now (either they installed the new build or they're running from source — the UI with the new settings rows).\n\nThey're asking how to use it. This is a usage question — no code changes needed. I should explain:\n\n1. The settings modal Web services (Web services) section — how to configure the xiaohongshu MCP service\n2. Prerequisite: xiaohongshu-mcp must be running on their machine (localhost:18060) and logged in (QR code scan)\n3. How to then use web_search with the xiaohongshu backend in a conversation\n\nBut wait — I should ground my answer in the actual code, not memory. The memory \"MCP web-search plugin shipped and verified\" has details, but let me verify the actual UI flow in this repo (this is a different repo than the one in the summary! The memory was about the shipped work though — and indeed this repo has mcpprovider.py, websearch.py, etc.).\n\nKey things to verify in the actual code:\n1. What the UI settings modal shows (ui/app.js initMcpServices block, index.html #mcp-services)\n2. What the xiaohongshu provider spec looks like (mcpprovider.py — name, label, placeholder, status_tool)\n3. How the backend chain works (websearch.py — backend names, how to pin xiaohongshu)\n4. What status feedback the UI gives (connected · logged in / unreachable)\n5. The prerequisite: the user needs xiaohongshu-mcp running — how do they get it? The memory says \"xiaohongshu-mcp service running and QR-logged-in\". Is there any doc? README mentions the MCP services section. The .gitignore memory said \"unconfigured providers are named nowhere and never suggested\" — interesting: the tool deliberately never suggests installing anything. So usage instructions about xiaohongshu-mcp itself must come from me/outside the app.\n\nWait, actually there's an important subtlety: does the placeholder in the UI mention the URL? Let me check mcpprovider.py for the placeholder text. Memory says placeholder field exists. Let me read the actual files to give precise instructions.\n\nAlso how does the user USE it in chat: they just ask \"Search for posts about X on Xiaohongshu\" and the model picks web_search with backend=xiaohongshu, or the chain falls through. Let me verify the backend naming (backend param values: bing | ddg | tavily | searxng | xiaohongshu?).\n\nAlso status check: GET /api/mcp_status reports reachable + login. The UI shows \"connected · logged in\" etc.\n\nAlso important: whether the settings save requires restart or hot-applies. Memory from the other repo said \"mcp_urls hot-applied at runtime\" — this repo's server.py: the settings POST handler sets self._cfg.mcp_urls directly (I saw the diff: `self._cfg.mcp_urls[key[4:]] = value` etc.) — so hot-applied, no restart needed.\n\nAlso the prerequisite: how to run xiaohongshu-mcp. From my memory \"Xiaohongshu open-source access landscape\" — there's a memory titled that! It probably has details about xiaohongshu-mcp projects (how to install/run). Let me load it since the user will likely need those instructions.\n\nAlso which build is the user looking at? They said \"I can see the corresponding UI\" — they see the UI. Possibly they installed v0.1.14 or are running from source. Either way the flow is the same.\n\nLet me read the actual code:\n1. agent/tools/mcpprovider.py (small file, read fully)\n2. The initMcpServices block in ui/app.js (grep for it, read that section)\n3. agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md (what the tool tells the model — backend list)\n4. Maybe load memory \"Xiaohongshu open-source access landscape\" for xiaohongshu-mcp setup instructions\n\nLet me do these in parallel:\n- read mcpprovider.py\n- grep \"mcp-services\\|initMcpServices\" ui/app.js to find line numbers, then read that range — dependent, but I can read app.js section after grep. Actually grep with path ui/app.js pattern \"mcp\" gives me the lines; then read_file offset. Two steps. Or just grep with -A context? The grep tool doesn't have context params. I'll grep first, then read the range.\n- read web_search.md prompt\n- load_memory \"Xiaohongshu open-source access landscape\"\n\nBatch 1: read mcpprovider.py + grep app.js + read web_search.md + load memory. All independent.\n\nThen compose the answer: a practical how-to in Chinese:\n\nStep 0: Prerequisites — xiaohongshu-mcp is a third-party service, it must run locally\nStep 1: Open settings → Web services → fill in URL → Save\nStep 2: Check the status (connected · logged in)\nStep 3: Use web_search in the conversation, usage examples\nStep 4: Brief description of other backends (Tavily/SearXNG optional)\n\nKeep it practical and grounded in what the code actually does (status texts, save behavior, hot apply, how model picks backend).\n\nLet me gather the facts first."}
