 保留）放在代码里比放在用户配置里可靠得多。所以：\n\n- 想加 bilibili / 知乎 / 微博等 MCP 服务？在 `MCP_PROVIDERS` 里加一条即可，搜索链、提示词、设置弹窗、状态探测五处自动跟上——你说一声我就能加\n- 唯一要注意的还是链尾位置：MCP 后端排在 bing/ddg 之后，所以对话里**点名平台**仍然是让模型选对后端的最稳方式"}
{"type": "user_message", "timestamp": 1789813849.8727286, "id": "3d9b489d5762", "content": "那为什么Web services只能填一个单独的url？"}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789813886.9809992, "id": "fec16da09159", "content": "好问题——这是刻意收敛的设计。先确认一下底层 MCP 客户端对 URL 的实际用法，再给你完整的理由：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_e00eefe0087846c0bb211aa8", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":60,\"offset\":417,\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user asks: \"那为什么Web services只能填一个单独的url？\" — \"Why can the Web services (UI) only fill in a single URL?\"\n\nHmm. Let me think about what they're really asking. Possible interpretations:\n\n1. Why does the Web services UI only accept ONE URL per provider (a single text input per row), rather than multiple URLs (e.g., multiple xiaohongshu-mcp instances, load balancing, fallbacks)?\n\n2. Why is there only one row (xiaohongshu) — but I already answered that in the previous turn (registry supports many; only one spec written). So interpretation 1 is more likely — they're asking why the input is a single URL field per service, not e.g. a list of URLs, or a full config (auth headers, path, port, etc.).\n\nSo the question: design rationale for \"one provider = one URL string\" in the settings UI.\n\nLet me ground this in the actual code:\n\n- `McpProvider.url_for()`: reads `urls.get(self.name) or env` → a single string, stripped, rstrip(\"/\").\n- server `_split_mcp_fields`: value must be http(s) URL or '' to clear. One string per key.\n- Config: `mcp_urls: dict[str, str]` — one URL per provider name.\n- UI: one text input per provider row.\n- `_mcp_call(url, tool, args, timeout...)`: the JSON-RPC client takes exactly one endpoint URL.\n\nWhy single URL by design?\n\n1. **MCP transport model**: an MCP server is one HTTP endpoint that multiplexes ALL its tools (`tools/list`, `tools/call` with tool names). You don't address tools by separate URLs — the URL identifies the server, the tool name identifies the capability. So one URL per service is the natural, sufficient addressing unit. A \"xiaohongshu-mcp\" service = one base URL; search_feeds/check_login_status all live behind it.\n\n2. **The underlying client is stateful per-session**: `_mcp_rpc` does initialize → notifications/initialized → tools/call with Mcp-Session-Id, SSE fallback. Each call is a fresh session against ONE endpoint. Multiple URLs would mean multiple sessions/load balancing logic — complexity with no user need.\n\n3. **Identity ↔ backend name 1:1**: the backend name (`backend=xiaohongshu`) must deterministically map to exactly one service. If you allowed multiple URLs for one provider, what would `backend=xiaohongshu` mean? You'd need sub-naming (xiaohongshu#1, xiaohongshu#2), per-URL status lines, fallback ordering — a config surface that duplicates what the registry already does per-platform. If you have two DIFFERENT xiaohongshu-like services, the clean answer in this design is: register two providers (different names, each one URL) — same as same-URL-different-tool case.\n\n4. **Simplicity/security**: `_split_mcp_fields` validates http(s) and nothing else; deliberately bypasses the SSRF guard because \"MCP endpoints are operator-configured loopback\" (memory). Keeping it one plain URL string = trivial to validate, trivial to persist, trivial to display status for. The settings target audience is a desktop-app user pasting one localhost URL from a service's README — not a fleet operator.\n\n5. **Failover exists at the platform level, not the instance level**: the chain already provides fallback across backends (bing/ddg etc.). Per-provider multi-URL failover would be redundant for a local personal service — if your localhost service is down, having a second URL for the same platform is a rare exotic need; and status text (\"unreachable\") tells you immediately.\n\n6. Precedent in the codebase: tavily = one key, searxng = one URL. Config knobs are single-valued throughout. mcp_urls follows the same shape (`dict[str, str]`).\n\nAlso worth noting what the alternatives would look like if a need arose:\n- Multiple instances of the same platform: add another McpProvider entry with a different name (e.g. \"xiaohongshu-remote\") pointing at another URL — zero code beyond the registry line (map_results can be shared).\n- Need for headers/auth? Currently not supported — URL only. That IS a real limitation: if someone's MCP server requires a bearer token, there's no field for it. Honest to mention: auth is assumed to be network-local (loopback, no TLS auth); remote authenticated MCP would need a small extension (e.g. mcp_<name>_headers or URL with token query param).\n\nLet me verify a couple of facts in code before asserting: the `_mcp_rpc` session flow and whether URL query is preserved (rstrip(\"/\") keeps query strings? `\"http://x/mcp?token=abc\".rstrip(\"/\")` → unchanged, yes; and env same). So a token-in-query DOES work today. Good point: `url_for` only strips trailing slashes, so `http://host/mcp?token=...` is legal and works. That partially answers auth needs.\n\nLet me quickly re-check _mcp_call/_mcp_rpc to be accurate about session handling... I have from memory: \"JSON-RPC client (initialize/tools-call, session id, SSE fallback); deliberately bypasses the SSRF guard because MCP endpoints are operator-configured loopback\". I could read lines 417-538 to confirm details for accuracy. Since the question is about design rationale, I mainly need: single endpoint per call, session per call. Let me do one quick read of that range to be safe.\n\nAlso verify `url_for` handles env fallback and rstrip (already read: yes).\n\nPlan: read websearch.py lines 417-538 (the _mcp_rpc/_mcp_call), then answer. One tool call, then final answer.\n\nActually, do I even need it? The user asks a design-rationale question. I can answer mostly from what I've already read (url_for, _split_mcp_fields, UI row, Backend.available, chain). Reading the RPC client would let me state confidently \"每条 URL 一次一建会话\" — worth one read for precision. Let's do it."}
