{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789814570.8212461, "id": "fe4db1ccb1e4", "content": "好，这个问题值得认真调研——正好为上一轮说的“去偏重构”找业界参照。先并行搜 opencode 和 Claude Code 等项目的 MCP 设计：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_6c7cebd72d834c26954936c0", "name": "web_search", "arguments": "{\"query\":\"opencode MCP servers config local remote stdio opencode.json\",\"max_results\":6}"}, {"id": "call_256e3eb1597b42f4bfd747b7", "name": "web_search", "arguments": "{\"query\":\"Claude Code MCP .mcp.json scope user project claude mcp add transport\",\"max_results\":6}"}], "reasoning": "The user asks: \"借鉴一下opencode等开源的agent项目是怎么处理mcp的问题的\" — \"Let's learn from how open-source agent projects like opencode handle MCP.\"\n\nThis is a research task: look at how opencode (and similar open-source agent projects — Claude Code, Cline, Continue, Goose, etc.) handle MCP integration, then draw lessons for Clutch's MCP design (which we just discussed: currently a hardcoded xiaohongshu provider in the registry, user wants no-bias, generic, runtime-extensible design).\n\nThis is chat mode — I should do web research (web_search, web_fetch) and read the actual opencode source (it's on GitHub: sst/opencode — actually opencode is at github.com/sst/opencode, written in TypeScript). Also maybe examine Claude Code's MCP approach (.mcp.json, `claude mcp add`), Cline's, Goose's, etc.\n\nThe goal: produce a comparative analysis of how these projects handle MCP, and what to borrow for Clutch:\n1. Configuration model (where do MCP servers live: project .mcp.json vs global config vs UI)\n2. Transport support (stdio, SSE/HTTP streamable, etc.)\n3. Discovery & tool loading (lazy loading, tool list injection, prompts/resources)\n4. Runtime management (add/remove servers via CLI/UI, OAuth, health checks)\n5. How they avoid bias (no built-in platform servers; everything user-configured)\n6. What Clutch should adopt: e.g., opencode's config-driven servers array in opencode.json, `claude mcp add` UX, lazy tool loading to avoid context bloat, /mcp command showing status, OAuth support, etc.\n\nLet me research:\n- opencode MCP docs: opencode.ai/docs/mcp-servers/ or github sst/opencode\n- opencode config: they have `mcp` field in opencode.json with `type: \"local\" | \"remote\"`, `command[]`, `url`, `enabled`, `environment`\n- Claude Code: .mcp.json (project scope), ~/.claude.json (user scope), `claude mcp add --transport sse/http`, /mcp command, OAuth 2.1 support, tool search / Tool Search Tool (deferred loading), MCP Apps\n- Cline: MCP marketplace, UI-based install, per-server autoApprove\n- Goose: extensions, `goose configure`, 1-click MCP extensions\n- Continue: config.yaml mcpServers, experimental\n- Gemini CLI: settings.json mcpServers, discovery via tools/list, trust\n- Also \"MCP gateway/aggregators\" like mcgravity, 1mcp — maybe out of scope.\n- Tool search / deferred loading in Claude Code v2 (Tool Search Tool, auto @-mentioning) — very relevant to Clutch: don't stuff all MCP tool schemas into context; load lazily.\n\nKey design lessons to map onto Clutch's pending refactor (generic declarative MCP engine):\n1. **No built-in servers, all user-config** (opencode/claude code/goose all ship ZERO servers; config-driven). This aligns with the no-bias principle — strong confirmation.\n2. **Config as the source of truth** — a standard, documented format; both file and CLI/UI manipulate it. Clutch: settings.json custom_mcp_services + UI rows + maybe a server-side add API.\n3. **Transports**: stdio (local process) + streamable HTTP/SSE (remote). Clutch currently only does HTTP JSON-RPC (no stdio). opencode supports both via SDK. Lesson: for a desktop app, stdio support lets users use ANY of the thousands of existing MCP servers (npx ...). This is a big functional gap worth noting — maybe scope it as optional/follow-up. Actually Clutch is a desktop app bundling Python backend; spawning node/npx servers is feasible. But careful about scope creep; mention as roadmap.\n4. **Enable/disable toggles, scopes** (local/project/user), environment variables.\n5. **Lazy tool loading / tool search** to protect context window: don't dump every MCP tool's schema into the system prompt; expose a meta-tool or load on demand. Clutch doesn't even expose arbitrary MCP tools as agent tools — it uses MCP only as search backends. Design decision: keep the \"backend adapter\" concept, or generalize to \"any MCP tool as an agent tool\"? For Clutch's no-bias principle: the web_search-backend mapping is a deliberate curation (returns uniform {title,url,snippet}). A general \"mount any MCP tool\" is what opencode/claude code do. Two different philosophies: Clutch = curated integration (quality control, uniform UX), opencode = generic pipe (any server, all tools, raw). The lesson for Clutch: keep curated backends as the product surface, but make them fully config-driven; optionally later add a generic MCP client mode.\n6. **Health/status UI**: /mcp command in Claude Code shows connection status; opencode logs errors. Clutch has mcp_status already — good, keep.\n7. **OAuth for remote servers** (Claude Code supports OAuth 2.1 with dynamic client registration) — future note for remote authenticated MCP.\n8. **Validation/error surfacing**: connection failures surfaced in UI, not silent.\n\nI should do actual research to ground this in facts with sources. Let me search and fetch:\n\n1. opencode MCP docs — https://opencode.ai/docs/mcp-servers/ (or github.com/sst/opencode README/docs)\n2. Claude Code MCP docs — https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp or docs.anthropic.com\n3. Maybe Cline MCP marketplace docs, Goose.\n\nAlso I could look at the opencode source on GitHub for their MCP config schema (packages/opencode/src/config or mcp.ts). That gives concrete schema shapes. Let me fetch opencode docs first.\n\nPlan:\n- web_search: \"opencode MCP servers config local remote\" \n- web_fetch opencode.ai/docs/mcp-servers/\n- web_fetch Claude Code MCP docs\n- maybe fetch Cline/Goose briefly\n- synthesize into: (a) how each project handles MCP (config model, transports, UX), (b) common patterns / consensus, (c) concrete borrow list for Clutch mapped onto the pending de-bias refactor, (d) what to NOT borrow.\n\nNote the system says network available via curl too, but web_search/web_fetch tools are preferred. Chat mode allows read-only commands; curl is whitelisted (\"curl without -o\").\n\nLet me run searches in parallel: opencode MCP docs, Claude Code MCP, and maybe \"MCP server config format mcpServers stdio remote\" comparison. Then fetch the top 2-3 URLs.\n\nThen produce the final comparative answer in Chinese with a table, mermaid maybe not needed, and concrete \"borrow\" recommendations tied to the refactor we discussed (generic declarative engine, UI add-row, presets as data or none, stdio transport option, lazy tool loading if we ever mount generic tools, status UI, scopes).\n\nAlso remember: memory says web tools web_search/web_fetch shipped — I can use my own tools! But in chat mode, run_command is restricted to whitelist; the tools I have ARE web_search/web_fetch (provided by the harness). Use those.\n\nLet me start with parallel searches."}
