{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789814987.8271604, "id": "a10e01bc4022", "content": "进入执行。这是多文件重构，先把要动的代码区域全部读准（压缩后禁止凭记忆改文件）：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_71c2d0d2f6834fff9dc5d5ef", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"grep -n -i \\\"mcp\\\" agent/server.py | head -40\"}"}, {"id": "call_ec47543f92c54e9c8a77e96e", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"grep -rn \\\"mcp\\\" agent/config.py agent/base.py ui/app.js README.md tests/server_test.py tests/websearch_test.py 2>/dev/null | grep -v \\\"Binary\\\" | head -50\"}"}, {"id": "call_527a6ebe59414065ad2aa1de", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":70,\"offset\":1,\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_c4a2443ac58f46d29ac2f530", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":70,\"offset\":473,\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_44c38a3c50224410a953a784", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":106,\"offset\":584,\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user says \"开始\" (Start) — switch to executing the de-bias refactor (plan ① from my last message):\n\n**Plan ①: 去偏重构**\n- Remove the hardcoded XIAOHONGSHU provider from `mcpprovider.py`\n- Replace with a declarative/generic MCP engine driven by user config (`mcp_services` in settings.json)\n- Fields: name, type (remote only for now), url, enabled, search_tool, args template, fields mapping (title/url/snippet), status_tool\n- Server: settings POST/GET for mcp_services list (validation: name normalization, no builtin conflicts, http(s) URL), `_settings_get` metadata, `/api/mcp_status` per service\n- UI: \"+ add service\" rows in Web services section, editable, removable, per-row status, enabled toggle\n- Compatibility: migrate existing `mcp_xiaohongshu` settings key → mcp_services entry\n- Error text sanitization (no full URL in errors — Claude Code lesson) — maybe include if cheap\n- Tests: update mcpbackend_test.py for the new engine, keep loopback MCP server tests\n- Then: tests all green, commit (no platform names in code!), push, and release v0.1.15? The user said \"开始\" — the plan was ① 去偏重构 → ② stdio → ③ headers/OAuth. So start with ①. Should I also release? The established convention in this project: user asks to commit/push/release explicitly usually. Last time the task was \"提交，推送并发布新版本\". Here \"开始\" means start the refactor work. I'll implement + test + commit + push (since the flow we've established ends with a release each time... but to be safe, I'll implement, test, commit. Then ask or just do release? The previous conversation ended with me saying \"切到 work 模式我就按 ① 开工\" — implying implement plan ①. The user's earlier pattern was explicit about releasing. I think committing and pushing is safe; for release, I could do it too since it's routine — but maybe better to ask? Hmm. The user tends to want the full flow. Let me implement first and decide at the end. Given the previous session pattern (\"帮我提交，推送并发布新版本\" was a one-time task), I'll implement + test + commit + push, and at the end offer/release. Actually — releasing a version bump is cheap and the workflow is established; but it also triggers CI builds. I'd rather do the refactor thoroughly, then commit+push, then ask about release... but the user said \"开始\" referring to implementing ①. I'll do the implementation and the commit+push, and at the end mention release is one command away / or just do it. Let me decide later based on how much budget remains. I'll lean toward: implement + test + commit + push; then say \"要发 v0.1.15 说一声\" — no wait, in the last release session the user explicitly asked for release separately. I'll finish with code shipped to master and offer the release.\n\nHmm, actually let me reconsider: this is a multi-file refactor — the core deliverable. Let me focus most effort there.\n\n**Design details:**\n\nLet me design the new architecture:\n\n### 1. `agent/tools/mcpprovider.py` — becomes the generic engine\n\nNew content:\n- `McpService` frozen dataclass: declarative spec from config\n  - name: str (backend name, normalized [a-z0-9_-])\n  - label: str (display)\n  - url: str\n  - enabled: bool = True\n  - search_tool: str\n  - args: dict[str, Any] — template with {query}/{limit} placeholders\n  - fields: dict[str, str] — mapping title/url/snippet → field path or url template\n  - list_key: str = \"auto\"\n  - status_tool: str = \"\"\n  - login_field: str = \"\" (which key in status result indicates login; probe common ones)\n- Parsing/validation function `parse_service(raw: dict) -> McpService` raising ValueError with clear message\n- Template interpolation: `_interpolate(template: str, values: dict) -> str` — replaces `{key}` placeholders; missing keys → empty? or leave as-is? For URL templates like `https://.../explore/{id}?xsec_token={xsec_token}` — values come from the result row. Missing key → substitute empty string (or drop the param?). Keep simple: replace with \"\" if missing... Hmm, but for args {\"keyword\": \"{query}\", \"limit\": \"{limit}\"} both always present. For url template missing xsec_token → empty token param, probably harmless. Simple approach: str.format-like but safe — use a regex to replace `{name}` with str(values.get(name, \"\")).\n\n  Also fields mapping value like \"desc | description\" — a fallback chain: try each field name separated by `|`. And a full URL (contains `://` or `https://`) → treat as url_template to interpolate with row values. Else treat as field path (maybe dotted path? keep simple: single key or dotted path a.b — dotted is easy: split('.')).\n  \n  title mapping: \"title\" → row[\"title\"], fallback chain allowed \"title | name\".\n  snippet: \"desc | description\".\n  url: either template or field chain.\n\n- Generic row extraction: `_rows(data, list_key)`:\n  - if list_key == \"auto\" or \"\": use `_first_list` (unwrap data/items/feeds/results/notes keys, or bare list)\n  - else: data[list_key] (dotted path?)\n- `map_results(service, data) -> list[dict]` — build {title,url,snippet} rows via fields config\n- `search_args(service, query, limit) -> dict` — interpolate args templates; special placeholders {query}, {limit}\n- `status_check(service, ...) -> bool|None` handled in websearch via _mcp_call(status_tool) — actually status probing lives in websearch.mcp_status already; keep protocol there, just use service.status_tool and login_field.\n\nWait — there's a design tension: mcpprovider.py is \"import-safe from anywhere: stdlib only\". Keep that. The engine needs json? No — mapping is pure dict/list manipulation. url parse via urllib.parse for urlencode of template query? No — url_template is full string, just interpolate. OK stdlib-only, no imports beyond stdlib + os.\n\n### 2. `agent/config.py`\n\nReplace `mcp_urls: dict[str,str]` with... hmm, compatibility. Existing deployments have `mcp_xiaohongshu` key in settings.json and `mcp_urls` config field used by server. New: `mcp_services: list[dict]`? Config dataclass field: `mcp_services: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)` — raw dicts (declarative JSON-ish), parsed lazily by mcpprovider.parse_service at use time (so a broken entry doesn't crash boot; skipped with reason in status).\n\nMigration: keep reading old `mcp_xiaohongshu` key? The plan said: one-time migration using preset. But we're removing ALL platform names from code — including the migration shim?? A migration shim with the xiaohongshu default URL + spec would itself be a hardcoded platform name in code. Options:\n- (a) Ship presets.json data file containing the xiaohongshu record; migration reads preset from data. User said presets must be editable data, shipping zero presets acceptable; \"要不要内置任何预设，由你拍板\". In the last message I suggested \"出厂零预设，最干净\". And user approved starting plan ①. So: zero presets, and migration = convert `mcp_xiaohongshu` URL into a generic service whose OTHER fields are... unknown (tool name search_feeds etc. is platform knowledge!). Hmm.\n\nWithout any platform knowledge, we can't migrate the URL into a working service — we'd know the URL but not search_tool (\"search_feeds\") or the url template. BUT: generic probing could guess the tool name from tools/list? That's fancy. Simpler: migration creates a service entry with name \"xiaohongshu\", url from old key, and search_tool empty → UI marks it \"needs completing\" (show the row, status says needs config). That keeps continuity (URL survives) without baking platform knowledge into code. The user (who knows it's xiaohongshu-mcp) fills in search_tool `search_feeds` and fields — OR we ship a preset...\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider presets-as-data. The user explicitly said in the earlier turn: \"Any built-in platform presets must be editable/deletable DATA (e.g. presets.json), never code; shipping zero presets is acceptable.\" — that's MY saved memory summarizing the agreed direction, and my final message said \"我的建议：出厂零预设，最干净\" and user said \"开始\" (which approves the plan including zero presets).\n\nSo: zero presets in shipped code/data. Migration approach: on settings load, if old `mcp_xiaohongshu` key exists and no service named \"xiaohongshu\" exists, auto-create an entry `{\"name\": \"xiaohongshu\", \"url\": <old url>, \"search_tool\": \"\", ...}` — the name here comes from the OLD KEY NAME itself (not from code!). The key is literally \"mcp_xiaohongshu\" — parsing the suffix gives the name without hardcoding. search_tool empty → the UI shows the row with a \"search tool not set\" state prompting completion. That's fully generic: \"legacy mcp_<name> keys become services with url only\". Any old key migrates generically. \n\nHmm, but a service with empty search_tool is dead weight in the chain — exclude from available backends (like unconfigured), and status shows \"incomplete\" or something. UI edit lets user complete it. Honestly the user of this app is the developer himself; he can re-enter the spec in 20 seconds. Keep migration generic + simple.\n\nWait, actually — should I even keep backward compat with `mcp_urls` in config? Let me check all usages: config.mcp_urls used by server.py (_split_mcp_fields etc.) and websearch (url_for via mcpprovider). I'm rewriting both. Clean break: remove `mcp_urls`, add `mcp_services: list[dict]`. Migration in server settings-load: convert `mcp_<name>` raw keys → service entries (url only). Since v0.1.14 shipped literally hours ago and the user is the only deployment, this is fine.\n\n### 3. `agent/tools/websearch.py`\n\nChanges:\n- Remove `from .mcpprovider import MCP_PROVIDERS...` registry loop building _BACKENDS\n- `_BACKENDS` stays native only (tavily/searxng/bing/ddg)\n- Build MCP backends per-call from config: `_mcp_backends(config) -> dict[str, Backend]` parsing config.mcp_services via mcpprovider, each available if enabled && url && search_tool (and fields for url/title resolvable — validate at parse time)\n- `_available_backends(config)`: native available + service backends (in config order, appended after native — same as before)\n- `web_search` chain: `_BACKENDS[name].search` — needs to handle service backends too; unify: make a function `_backend_by_name(name, config)` or build combined dict per call: `dict(_BACKENDS) | service_backends`? Cleanest: `_all_backends(config) -> dict[str, Backend]` = native + parsed services; used by _available_backends and web_search.\n- `_mcp_backend(provider)` → `_mcp_backend(service: McpService)`: search uses mcpprovider.search_args(service, q, limit), _mcp_call, mcpprovider.map_results(service, data).\n- `mcp_status(url, provider, timeout)` → rework: `probe(service, timeout)` returns dict {configured, complete?, reachable, login} for /api/mcp_status. For services with status_tool: call it; login normalization using login_field (probe common keys if not set — reuse _login_state logic? that's in server.py. Hmm — server._login_state normalizes payload; keep normalization in server or move to mcpprovider? Move generic normalization into mcpprovider (stdlib-only, fine) so server stays thin. Let me move `_login_state` → mcpprovider.normalize_login(state, service.login_field).\n- BackendError/WebError imports stay.\n\nAlso the SSRF note: MCP endpoints bypass the SSRF guard (operator-configured). Keep — the config is still operator-configured (user-entered). Fine.\n\n### 4. `agent/server.py`\n\n- Remove MCP_PROVIDERS import & helpers: `_split_mcp_fields`, `_mcp_keys_from`, `_login_state`, `mcp_providers()`\n- New settings handling:\n  - POST body key `mcp_services`: JSON list of dicts (UI sends JSON string or actual list? apiFetch body is JSON — UI can send actual array). Validate each entry via mcpprovider.parse_service → normalized dict (store canonical dict from dataclasses.asdict minus label? keep label). On invalid → 400 with message.\n  - Legacy: `mcp_<name>` keys → convert to service stub {name, url} (migration), and strip old keys from saved settings? \"one-time migration\": when loading settings at boot (main()) and when saving. Simplest: a `_normalize_settings(raw)` applied everywhere settings are read: pulls `mcp_<name>` keys into `mcp_services` entries (only if name not already present) and drops them from raw. Since save rewrites the file, after the first save the legacy keys disappear naturally.\n  - `flatten_settings` drops non-scalar? mcp_services is a list — need same merge-back trick as before: `saved = {**raw_service_list, **flatten...}`. Before: `saved = {**_mcp_keys_from(load_settings()), **flatten_settings(load_settings())}`. Now: read raw settings; `saved = flatten_settings(raw); saved[\"mcp_services\"] = raw.get(\"mcp_services\", [])` (migrated).\n  - GET /api/settings `mcp_providers` metadata → replace with `mcp_services`: echo current configured services (parsed+normalized) so UI renders rows. Include a shape the UI can render: name, label, url, enabled, search_tool, fields, status_tool, complete flag.\n  - GET /api/mcp_status: iterate services from config (parsed); for each: reachable check — call status_tool if set else a lightweight tools/list? Currently mcp_status(url, provider, timeout) — what does it do? It calls the provider's status_tool? Let me check websearch.mcp_status (line 586-595). I saw def mcp_status at 586 but didn't read the body. It probably does _mcp_call(status_tool). For services without status_tool: reachability check = try tools/list? or just a HEAD/POST initialize? Simplest generic probe: JSON-RPC `tools/list` — generic, no platform knowledge! If status_tool set → call it and normalize login. tools/list also validates search_tool exists? Nice: `has_search_tool` flag → UI can hint \"tool not found\". Hmm keep scope sane: probe = tools/list (reachable + tool names), plus status_tool login if configured. Report {name, configured, reachable, login, has_search_tool}.\n- Keep \"nothing to save\" guard updated (it likely checks which keys present — must recognize mcp_services).\n\nI need to re-read server.py current state around settings (I have the diff from earlier but not the full current code). Also websearch.py current full state (I've seen big chunks). Let me read the relevant sections before editing.\n\n### 5. `ui/app.js`\n\nReplace initMcpServices block with a full editor:\n- Render rows from GET /api/settings `mcp_services` (each: name/label/url/enabled/search_tool/status_tool + completeness)\n- Each row: enabled checkbox, name input (editable for new), url input, search_tool input (advanced?), status span, delete button\n- \"+ add service\" button appends an empty row\n- Save button POSTs the whole list as mcp_services (JSON array), then refreshes status\n- Status per row from /api/mcp_status providers: reachable/login/has_search_tool → text: connected·logged in / connected / unreachable / missing search tool / incomplete\n- Keep it self-contained at end of app.js like before. Size ~150-200 lines. Careful with the \"no double quotes in strings\" — normal JS fine.\n\nUI text language: project convention — all text in code must be ENGLISH (memory). UI labels in English (\"Add service\", \"Save web services\", \"enabled\"). Keep consistent.\n\n### 6. `agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md`\n\nIt uses `$backends` rendered from available set — no platform names hardcoded in the md? Check: memory says \"prompts/tools/web_search.md uses $backends\". The description text says \"Backends available on this machine ($backends)\". Generic. Good — no change needed. But check web_fetch.md — likely generic too.\n\n### 7. README.md\n\nIt currently names xiaohongshu as an example: \"外部 MCP 服务也可以接入为搜索后端（如 xiaohongshu-mcp）：在设置弹窗的 Web services 区填入服务地址即可（存于 ~/.clutch/settings.json 的 mcp_<名字> 键，或环境变量 CLUTCH_MCP_<名字>_URL）。\" — README is CHINESE project doc; the no-bias principle is about CODE. README naming an example is like opencode's docs naming Sentry — acceptable and helpful. But the described mechanism (mcp_<名字> key) CHANGES to mcp_services list → must update README section to document new config shape, keeping xiaohongshu as a documentation example (fine — docs may name examples; code may not).\n\n### 8. Tests\n\n`tests/mcpbackend_test.py` — needs rework: its loopback MCP server tests target the engine (initialize/tools-call/session/SSE/error paths) — those stay; provider-specific parts (XIAOHONGSHU spec mapping, search_args {keyword,limit}, map_results) must become generic-engine tests: parse_service validation, template interpolation, field chains, url_template mapping, list_key, status/login normalization, config-driven backend availability (enabled/complete), end-to-end loopback search through a service entry with a generic mapping. Also migration test: legacy mcp_<name> key → service stub.\n\n`tests/websearch_test.py` — may reference backends/available set with xiaohongshu? It tests native chain mostly + maybe mcp absence. Check it.\n\n`tests/perm-args-test.js` — unrelated. `settings POST` tests? server_test may cover settings roundtrip incl mcp keys — check quickly via grep for \"mcp\" in tests.\n\n### Order of work\n\n1. Re-read current files fully: mcpprovider.py (have it), websearch.py relevant parts (583-689 + _mcp_call body 473-538 + _http_get not needed), server.py settings region, app.js region (have it), config.py region (have diff), websearch_test.py & mcpbackend_test.py (need read).\n2. Write new mcpprovider.py (engine).\n3. Edit config.py (mcp_services field).\n4. Edit websearch.py (config-driven backends, probe).\n5. Edit server.py (settings/status/migration).\n6. Rewrite app.js initMcpServices block.\n7. Update README section.\n8. Update/extend tests.\n9. Run all suites.\n10. Commit (English message, no platform names in code — commit message: avoid naming the platform? The commit message can say \"remove built-in platform provider; generic config-driven MCP services\". Avoid writing the name to respect the principle? Commit messages are history/docs... previous commits named it. Keep message generic-ish: \"refactor(mcp): user-configured declarative services replace built-in provider registry\". Fine.)\n11. Push.\n\nBudget: this is big. Let me be efficient: read the needed regions in 2-3 parallel reads, then write files.\n\nNote the compaction warning: files mcpprovider.py, websearch.py, app.js, web_search.md, ui/package.json, .gitignore \"no longer in context\" — but I re-read mcpprovider.py fully and app.js region this session, websearch regions. websearch.py: I've seen lines 539-583, 417-476, 548+. I still need 473-538 (_mcp_call) and 584-689 (mcp_status, _short, web_search, web_fetch) and the top (1-120: imports, Backend, WebError, SSRF guard) and maybe 323-417 native backends signatures. I don't need to rewrite native parts — just targeted edits. Let me read websearch.py fully-ish: 1-120 and 473-689. Also grep \"mcp\" in server_test.py/websearch_test.py.\n\nserver.py: read the settings-related region. From the diff I know approximate layout: helpers at ~78-125, _settings_post around 203+, _settings_get ~408+, _mcp_status after. Let me grep line numbers first: grep -n \"mcp\\|def _settings\\|def _split\\|flatten_settings\\|def main\" agent/server.py.\n\nLet me also grep config.py current state for mcp fields region.\n\nPlan first batch (parallel):\n- grep -n \"mcp|MCP\" agent/server.py\n- read websearch.py offset 1 limit 120\n- read websearch.py offset 473 limit 216 (473-689)\n- grep -n \"mcp\" tests/websearch_test.py tests/server_test.py tests/mcpbackend_test.py | head -40\n- read config.py offset ~28 limit 70 (the fields region) — from diff I know it; but re-read to be safe for edit anchors: grep -n \"mcp_urls\\|web_search_timeout\\|searxng\" agent/config.py\n\nDo these in one parallel batch. Then design & write.\n\nLet me now think about the engine implementation concretely, writing the new mcpprovider.py in my head:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"User-configured MCP tool servers used as web_search backends.\n\nThe agent core has no opinion about which services exist: everything is a\ndeclarative record from the operator's settings (Config.mcp_services).\nA record says how to CALL a search tool (endpoint, tool name, argument\ntemplate) and how to READ its reply (list location, field mapping, optional\nURL template) — no platform names appear anywhere in this module.\n\nImport-safe from anywhere: stdlib only, no agent-package imports.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport re\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field as dc_field\nfrom typing import Any\n\n_NAME_RE = re.compile(r\"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$\")\n_PLACEHOLDER_RE = re.compile(r\"\\{([a-z_][a-z0-9_]*)\\}\")\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass McpService:\n    name: str\n    url: str\n    search_tool: str = \"\"\n    enabled: bool = True\n    label: str = \"\"\n    args: dict = dc_field(default_factory=dict)          # {\"keyword\": \"{query}\", ...}\n    fields: dict = dc_field(default_factory=dict)        # title/url/snippet -> field chain or URL template\n    list_key: str = \"\"                                    # \"\" = auto\n    status_tool: str = \"\"\n    login_field: str = \"\"\n\n    @property\n    def label_or_name(self) -> str: return self.label or self.name\n\n    def complete(self) -> bool:\n        return bool(self.url and self.search_tool and self.fields.get(\"url\"))\n```\n\nHmm wait — is fields.url required? A generic service might return full urls in rows; default mapping could be fields={\"title\": \"title\", \"url\": \"url\", \"snippet\": \"desc | description | snippet | summary\"}? Provide DEFAULT_FIELDS so a minimal record {name,url,search_tool} works out of the box with standard shapes. That serves the generic-first philosophy: most MCP search tools return title/url/desc-ish. So:\n\nDEFAULT_FIELDS = {\"title\": \"title | name\", \"url\": \"url | link\", \"snippet\": \"desc | description | snippet | summary | content\"}\n\nfields stored = defaults merged with user overrides (user can override url with a template like xiaohongshu needs).\n\ncomplete() = bool(url and search_tool). fields always non-empty via defaults. \n\nparse:\n\n```python\ndef parse_service(raw: Any) -> McpService:\n    if not isinstance(raw, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"service must be an object\")\n    name = str(raw.get(\"name\", \"\")).strip().lower()\n    if not _NAME_RE.match(name):\n        raise ValueError(\"service name must match [a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}\")\n    url = str(raw.get(\"url\", \"\")).strip().rstrip(\"/\")\n    if url and not url.startswith((\"http://\", \"https://\")):\n        raise ValueError(f\"service {name}: url must be an http(s) URL\")\n    search_tool = str(raw.get(\"search_tool\", \"\")).strip()\n    fields_in = raw.get(\"fields\") if isinstance(raw.get(\"fields\"), dict) else {}\n    fields = {**DEFAULT_FIELDS}\n    for key in (\"title\", \"url\", \"snippet\"):\n        v = str(fields_in.get(key, \"\")).strip()\n        if v: fields[key] = v\n    args = raw.get(\"args\") if isinstance(raw.get(\"args\"), dict) else {}\n    # only string templates or scalars allowed; keep simple: pass through, interpolate strings\n    return McpService(name=name, url=url, search_tool=search_tool,\n                      enabled=raw.get(\"enabled\", True) is not False,\n                      label=str(raw.get(\"label\", \"\")).strip(),\n                      args=dict(args), fields=fields,\n                      list_key=str(raw.get(\"list_key\", \"\")).strip(),\n                      status_tool=str(raw.get(\"status_tool\", \"\")).strip(),\n                      login_field=str(raw.get(\"login_field\", \"\")).strip())\n```\n\nenabled parsing: accept True/False/\"true\"/\"false\"? UI sends bool. raw.get(\"enabled\", True) — if it's False → False. `is not False` handles bool False; string \"false\" would be True — normalize: \n\n```python\nen = raw.get(\"enabled\", True)\nenabled = en not in (False, \"false\", \"0\", 0, None)  # None?? raw explicitly None → treat as True? eh\n```\nSimpler: `enabled = str(en).lower() not in (\"false\", \"0\", \"no\")`. OK.\n\nto_dict(service) -> canonical dict for persistence/echo: dict with keys in stable order.\n\nInterpolation:\n\n```python\ndef _interpolate(template: str, values: dict) -> str:\n    return _PLACEHOLDER_RE.sub(lambda m: str(values.get(m.group(1), \"\")), template)\n\ndef search_args(service, query, limit):\n    values = {\"query\": query, \"limit\": limit}\n    out = {}\n    for key, val in (service.args or {}).items():\n        out[str(key)] = _interpolate(str(val), values) if isinstance(val, str) else val\n    return out\n```\n\nHmm: limit as string from template \"{limit}\" → \"8\" string; xhs server expects int? JSON types matter — {\"keyword\": q, \"limit\": limit} previously int. Interpolated becomes \"8\" — might break strict servers. Special-case: if template is exactly \"{limit}\" (single placeholder, nothing else) → return the raw value preserving type. Nice touch:\n\n```python\ndef _interpolate_value(val, values):\n    if not isinstance(val, str): return val\n    m = _PLACEHOLDER_RE.fullmatch(val.strip())\n    if m: return values.get(m.group(1), \"\")\n    return _PLACEHOLDER_RE.sub(lambda mm: str(values.get(mm.group(1), \"\")), val)\n```\n\nfullmatch single placeholder → typed passthrough. Good.\n\nRows:\n\n```python\n_LIST_WRAP_KEYS = (\"data\", \"items\", \"feeds\", \"results\", \"notes\")\n\ndef result_rows(service, data):\n    if not isinstance(data, (list, dict)): return []\n    key = service.list_key\n    if key:\n        node = data\n        for part in key.split(\".\"):\n            node = node.get(part) if isinstance(node, dict) else None\n        rows = node if isinstance(node, list) else []\n    else:  # auto\n        rows = data if isinstance(data, list) else next((data[k] for k in _LIST_WRAP_KEYS if isinstance(data.get(k), list)), [])\n    return [r for r in rows if isinstance(r, dict)]\n```\n\nField reading:\n\n```python\ndef _field(row, chain):\n    for name in (p.strip() for p in chain.split(\"|\")):\n        if not name: continue\n        v = row\n        for part in name.split(\".\"):\n            if isinstance(v, dict) and part in v: v = v[part]\n            else: v = None; break\n        if v not in (None, \"\"):\n            return v\n    return \"\"\n\ndef map_results(service, data) -> list[dict]:\n    rows = []\n    for row in result_rows(service, data):\n        url = _map_url(service.fields[\"url\"], row)\n        title = str(_field(row, service.fields[\"title\"])).strip()\n        snippet = str(_field(row, service.fields[\"snippet\"])).strip()\n        if not url: continue\n        rows.append({\"title\": title, \"url\": url, \"snippet\": snippet})\n    return rows\n\ndef _map_url(spec, row):\n    if \"{\": in spec? -> if it looks like a template (contains \"{\"), interpolate with row values (and keep as-is)\n    else: str(_field(row, spec))\n```\n\nCareful: url template `https://www.xiaohongshu.com/explore/{id}?xsec_token={xsec_token}` — values from row; urlencode token? Old code urlencode'd the token param. Template approach: user can write `?xsec_token={xsec_token}` raw — token may contain chars needing escaping. Old `_xhs_map` used urlencode. For fidelity: if template has query part, quote values with urllib.parse.quote. Simpler: interpolate with urllib.parse.quote(str(v), safe=\"\") for placeholders inside the query string only? Overkill — quote all interpolated values in URL templates with safe=\"\": ids/tokens fine. But path placeholders like {note_id} in path also fine quoted. Do: in URL templates, each placeholder value → urllib.parse.quote(str(v), safe=\"\"). Need urllib.parse import (stdlib, fine).\n\nlogin normalization (moved from server):\n\n```python\ndef normalize_login(state, login_field=\"\"):\n    truthy = (\"logged_in\", \"logged in\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"ok\", \"success\")\n    ... same logic, but if login_field set: check state.get(login_field) first\n```\n\nKeep server's dict-key probing order but prepend login_field.\n\nAlso `parse_services(list) -> tuple[McpService,...], list[str]` helper? websearch/server need: parse each, skip invalid collecting warnings. Add:\n\n```python\ndef parse_services(raw) -> tuple[list[McpService], list[str]]:\n    services, errors = [], []\n    if not isinstance(raw, list): return [], []\n    for i, item in enumerate(raw):\n        try: services.append(parse_service(item))\n        except ValueError as e: errors.append(f\"service[{i}]: {e}\")\n    return services, errors\n```\n\nDedup names? Last wins or first wins — enforce unique in server validation; here keep as parsed.\n\nNow websearch.py edits:\n\nCurrent structure (to verify): imports `from .mcpprovider import McpProvider, MCP_PROVIDERS` presumably; `_BACKENDS` dict + loop; `_mcp_backend(provider)`; `_available_backends`; `mcp_status(url, provider, timeout)`; `web_search` uses `_BACKENDS[name]`.\n\nNew:\n\n```python\nfrom .mcpprovider import McpService, parse_services, search_args as _service_args, map_results as _service_results, normalize_login\n```\n\nWait naming: mcpprovider.search_args(service, query, limit) and map_results(service, data). Fine.\n\n```python\ndef _mcp_backend(service: McpService) -> Backend:\n    def search(q, limit, config, get):\n        cancel = (getattr(get, \"keywords\", None) or {}).get(\"cancel\")\n        data = _mcp_call(service.url, service.search_tool, _service_args(service, q, limit), config.web_search_timeout, cancel=cancel)\n        rows = _service_results(service, data)\n        if not rows: raise BackendError(f\"no results for {q!r} (empty feed or unmapped payload)\")\n        return rows[:limit]\n    return Backend(service.name, search, lambda c, s=service: s.enabled and s.complete())\n```\n\nBackend.available previously lambda c, p=provider: bool(p.url_for(c)). Now service config already holds url. available(config) signature takes config but service doesn't need it. fine.\n\n```python\ndef _service_backends(config) -> dict[str, Backend]:\n    services, _errs = parse_services(getattr(config, \"mcp_services\", None))\n    return {s.name: _mcp_backend(s) for s in services}   # later duplicates overwrite; server validation prevents\n```\n\n`_available_backends`: \n```python\ndef _available_backends(config):\n    backends = {**_BACKENDS, **_service_backends(config)}\n    return tuple(n for n, b in backends.items() if b.available(config))\n```\n\nweb_search: replace `_BACKENDS[name].search(...)` with combined lookup:\n```python\nbackends = {**_BACKENDS, **_service_backends(config)}\n... results = backends[name].search(q, limit, config, get)\n```\nand unknown pinned backend error message lists available — keep existing code shape, just swap dict source.\n\nmcp_status → rewrite:\n\n```python\ndef probe_service(service: McpService, timeout: float = 3.0) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"tools/list reachability + optional login check, for the settings UI.\"\"\"\n    info = {\"name\": service.name, \"configured\": bool(service.url), \"complete\": service.complete(), \"reachable\": None, \"login\": None, \"has_search_tool\": None}\n    if not service.url: return info\n    try:\n        listing = _mcp_call(service.url, \"__tools_list__\"??? \n```\n\nHmm — _mcp_call calls tools/call with a tool name. For tools/list I need a separate JSON-RPC method. Let me look at _mcp_call body (473-538) before writing — it composes initialize → initialized → tools/call. I'll add `_mcp_tools_list(url, timeout, cancel)` sharing the session plumbing, or generalize `_mcp_rpc` usage. Need to read 473-538 first. Probably:\n\n```python\ndef _mcp_call(url, tool, args, timeout, cancel=None):\n    sid = None\n    _mcp_rpc(url, {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{...}}, None, timeout, cancel)  # captures sid\n    _mcp_rpc(url, {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"notifications/initialized\"}, sid, ...)\n    resp, _ = _mcp_rpc(url, {\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":tool,\"arguments\":args}}, sid, ...)\n    return resp.get(\"result\", {})\n```\n\nI'll add tools/list variant reusing that flow. Then:\n\n```python\ndef probe_service(service, timeout=3.0):\n    info = {...}\n    if not service.url: return info\n    try:\n        result = _mcp_tools_list(service.url, timeout)\n        tools = [t.get(\"name\") for t in result.get(\"tools\", []) if isinstance(t, dict)]\n        info[\"reachable\"] = True\n        info[\"has_search_tool\"] = service.search_tool in tools if service.search_tool else None\n    except WebError as e:\n        info[\"reachable\"] = False; info[\"error\"] = str(e)\n        return info\n    if service.status_tool:\n        try:\n            st = _mcp_call(service.url, service.status_tool, {}, timeout)\n            info[\"login\"] = normalize_login(st.get(...) or st, service.login_field)\n        except WebError as e:\n            info[\"login\"] = None; info[\"error\"] = ...\n    return info\n```\n\nWait — old mcp_status extracted structured content? _mcp_call returns result; status tool payload maybe result.structuredContent or content text JSON. normalize_login handles dict/str/bool — pass `result` (the whole result dict) — old server code called `_mcp_status_probe(url, provider...)`? I need to see old mcp_status body + how server used it. Will read.\n\nAlso error sanitization (Claude Code lesson): WebError messages include full url (`f\"MCP server unreachable ({reason}): {url}\"`). Strip url: keep host only? Plan item said \"错误文本脱敏、不带完整 URL\". Implement: `_origin(url)` = scheme://host:port; replace `: {url}` with `: {origin}`. Small edits in _mcp_rpc messages.\n\nserver.py edits:\n\n- imports: `from .tools.mcpprovider import parse_services, parse_service, to_dict?` — need echo metadata. Let me add in mcpprovider: `def service_records(services) -> list[dict]` canonical dicts for GET echo. Or dataclasses.asdict. asdict includes defaults — good for UI prefill.\n- `_split_mcp_fields` deleted; POST handler: body.get(\"mcp_services\") — if key present: validate list; services=[parse_service(r) for r in list] → store canonical `[to_dict(s) for s in services]` in saved[\"mcp_services\"]; errors → 400.\n  Need to see current POST code fully to edit precisely (the \"nothing to save\" guard, mcp_updates loop). Will read.\n- `_settings_get`: replace mcp_providers metadata with `\"mcp_services\": [asdict(s) for s in parsed from config]` — echo enabled/url/tool/fields so UI prefills rows.\n- `_mcp_status` handler: iterate parsed services, call probe_service for configured ones (timeout 3s), return {\"providers\": [...]}? UI previously read st.providers keyed by name. Keep response key \"providers\" for minimal UI churn? I'm rewriting UI anyway — rename to \"services\". Fine either way; use {\"services\": [...]}.\n- migration: in `load_settings` usage sites… Actually put migration inside a helper `_settings_raw()`: \n```python\ndef _load_raw_settings() -> dict:\n    raw = dict(load_settings())\n    legacy = {k: v for k, v in raw.items() if k.startswith(\"mcp_\") and isinstance(v, str) and v.strip()}\n    if legacy:\n        services = list(raw.get(\"mcp_services\") or [])\n        have = {str(s.get(\"name\",\"\")).strip().lower() for s in services if isinstance(s, dict)}\n        for k, url in legacy.items():\n            name = k[4:].strip().lower()\n            if name and name not in have:\n                services.append({\"name\": name, \"url\": url.strip().rstrip(\"/\")})\n        raw[\"mcp_services\"] = services\n        for k in legacy: raw.pop(k, None)\n    return raw\n```\nUse in: main() config load, settings POST save path, settings GET. Config load: main() does cfg.mcp_* loading — the diff showed main() loads mcp_urls at boot; now needs: cfg.mcp_services = raw[\"mcp_services\"]. Config field: `mcp_services: list[dict] = field(default_factory=list)`.\n\nHow does Config get populated from settings? There's flatten_settings + some apply path (base/server boot). Need to read main()/load path. Grep will reveal.\n\nUI app.js: rewrite block ~2813-2908 (~95 lines) with ~170 lines editor. Features:\n- state: services list (from GET), status map (from /api/mcp_status)\n- render rows: each row grid: [enabled checkbox] [name input] [url input] [search_tool input] [status span] [✕ button]\n  - advanced fields (args/fields/list_key/status_tool) — fold into an \"advanced\" toggle per row? Scope control: name/url/search_tool/status_tool/enabled enough for v1? fields mapping is what makes xiaohongshu work (url template). Without editing fields, xiaohongshu service can't be configured! Must include fields.url at least. Put a single \"advanced (JSON)\" textarea? Ugly but pragmatic: row shows core inputs; a small \"advanced\" disclosure per row with fields JSON textarea (validated on save). Hmm — textarea JSON per row: acceptable for power-user config, aligns with \"declarative config\" philosophy. Let me include: title/url/snippet fields as one JSON object textarea labeled \"field mapping (JSON, optional)\".\n\n  Simpler alternative: inputs for the three mapping strings (title / url / snippet) as small text inputs with defaults prefilled — text, not JSON! fields.title is a chain string, fields.url may be template string — all plain strings! So three text inputs. No JSON needed. args is an object though — that's the only non-string. args default {} means server uses... wait if args empty {} then tools/call gets arguments {} — search tool needs keyword! So args must be editable too. Represent args as a single-line JSON input `{\"keyword\":\"{query}\",\"limit\":\"{limit}\"}`. One JSON field, validated on save. OK: core row = enabled/name/url/tool; advanced = args (JSON) + title/url/snippet chains + list_key + status_tool + login_field. To keep UI manageable: two-line row: line1 core inputs + status + delete; line2 (always visible, smaller) args + 3 mapping inputs. Fine — settings modal has space.\n\n- \"＋ Add service\" appends blank row (name empty → user fills; validate client-side: name pattern, url http(s))\n- Save: collect rows → POST {mcp_services: [...]} (objects); on 400 show error; then refresh status.\n- Status text: not configured / incomplete (missing search tool) / unreachable / connected / connected · logged in / connected · not logged in; plus tool-missing hint \"search tool not found on server\" when has_search_tool false.\n- Delete button removes row (DOM); Save persists.\n\nStyle: reuse existing classes (fs-conn-row, dim, primary). Inputs styled by global CSS presumably; grid layout inline styles like existing code does (row.style...). Keep inline styles consistent with previous block.\n\nindex.html: `<div id=\"mcp-services\"></div>` exists — keep unchanged.\n\nREADME: update the MCP paragraph: config lives in settings `mcp_services` list; describe JSON shape briefly; keep xiaohongshu as doc example (docs allowed). Update \"存于 mcp_<名字> 键\" text. Also tests list mentions websearch_test already.\n\nConfig.py: replace mcp_urls field with mcp_services list + comment. Also remove CLUTCH_MCP_<NAME>_URL env support? url_for used env; new engine: url from record only (env per-service adds bias-free complexity — skip; keep simple: config only. Mention in README?). Fine: drop env variant (was undocumented anyway? README mentioned it — remove from README).\n\nWait — careful: base.py/server main() previously applied settings mcp_urls → cfg. Now mcp_services. Also BaseServer boot in agent/base.py? The diff earlier: base.py line ~136 LLM stuff only. Where was mcp_urls applied at boot? Memory said \"main() loads mcp_* at boot\". So server.main() — will read.\n\nTests:\n\ntests/mcpbackend_test.py — rewrite affected parts: keep loopback server fixture (json/SSE/202/error paths); replace provider-specific mapping tests with engine tests:\n- parse_service validation (name pattern, url scheme, enabled parsing, defaults merge)\n- args template typing (single placeholder keeps int; mixed → string)\n- result_rows auto/list_key/dotted\n- field chains + fallbacks\n- url template interpolation + quoting\n- end-to-end search via loopback server with xiaohongshu-shaped payload? Use a NEUTRAL fake payload (notes with id/title/desc + url template \"https://example.com/n/{id}?t={token}\") — no platform names even in tests? Tests are code... principle says code no platform names; tests ideally also clean. Use generic \"notes\" service. \n- probe_service with loopback: tools/list reachable, has_search_tool true/false, status_tool login normalize\n- migration helper: legacy key → service stub (test server._load_raw_settings? it's server module — server_test territory; put migration test here by importing server? mcpbackend_test imports websearch; importing server pulls http server stack — it already imports server? old test tested _split_mcp_fields? Unknown. I'll check test file.)\n\nwebsearch_test.py — check references to mcp/xiaohongshu and available chain; adjust.\n\nserver_test.py — grep mcp; may POST settings with mcp_xiaohongshu — update to mcp_services.\n\nThat's a LOT. Budget-wise: files to read (parallel batch), then write mcpprovider.py (new, full), edit config.py (small), websearch.py (several edits — maybe read fully then targeted edits), server.py (edits), app.js (rewrite block), README (edit), tests (rewrite mcpbackend_test fully — it's mine, ~large; websearch_test/server_test targeted).\n\nGiven 30s command limit and test runtime ~fine (suites are seconds each).\n\nLet me start reading. Batch 1 (parallel):\n1. grep -n \"mcp\\|MCP\" agent/server.py\n2. read websearch.py offset 1 limit 120 (header/imports/Backend/WebError/SSRF)\n3. read websearch.py offset 473 limit 120 (_mcp_call + _BACKENDS region already seen 539+)\n4. read websearch.py offset 584 limit 106 (mcp_status, web_search, web_fetch)\n5. grep -n \"mcp\" tests/websearch_test.py tests/server_test.py tests/mcpbackend_test.py\n6. grep -n \"mcp_urls\\|mcp_services\" agent/config.py agent/server.py agent/base.py ui/app.js README.md\n\nThen batch 2: read server.py settings regions + test files as needed. Then write.\n\nActually the grep tool searches one path; use pattern with multiple files? grep takes single path. Use run_command grep for multi-file (allowed in chat? — I'm in WORK mode now! The user said \"开始\" meaning start implementing → work mode is active? Mode note says \"Current mode: work (full access)\" — yes, system prompt says work mode. Good: run_command unrestricted.)\n\nBatch 1 via run_command combos:\nA: grep -n -i \"mcp\" agent/server.py | head -40\nB: sed/grep multi-file: grep -rn \"mcp\" agent/config.py agent/base.py ui/app.js README.md tests/server_test.py tests/websearch_test.py | head -50\nC: read websearch 1-120\nD: read websearch 473-560 (covers _mcp_call through _BACKENDS)\nE: read websearch 584-689\n\nUse read_file tool for C/D/E and run_command for A/B. Parallel block of 5 calls."}
