{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789815395.1755857, "id": "610e6c5e1716", "content": "`mcpprovider.py` 新引擎已确认纯声明式（`McpService` + 解析/模板/映射，无注册表）。现在精确读取 websearch、config、server、UI 的相关段落。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_423d04239ecc4202823fe64e", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\",\"offset\":400,\"limit\":210}"}, {"id": "call_b7fdb3565ae349f486331332", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/config.py\",\"offset\":60,\"limit\":60}"}], "reasoning": "Now I have a good map of the remaining work. The `mcpprovider.py` engine is the new declarative one (`McpService`, `parse_service(s)`, `search_args`, `map_results`, `normalize_login`). But:\n\n- `websearch.py` still imports `McpProvider, MCP_PROVIDERS` from mcpprovider — which no longer exist (the new module has `McpService` and no registry). So websearch.py is currently BROKEN and needs updating:\n  - Line 42: `from .mcpprovider import McpProvider, MCP_PROVIDERS`\n  - Lines 549-570: `_mcp_backend(provider)` and module-level loop `for _p in MCP_PROVIDERS.values(): _BACKENDS[_p.name] = _mcp_backend(_p)` — this must become dynamic: build backends from `Config.mcp_services` at chain-build time (since services are now config-driven, not registry-driven).\n  - Line 586: `mcp_status(url, provider, timeout)` — signature may stay similar but with `McpService`.\n- `config.py` line 84-88: `mcp_urls: dict[str,str]` → needs to become `mcp_services: list[dict]` (records) with migration from old `mcp_*` keys.\n- `server.py`: imports `MCP_PROVIDERS`, `mcp_providers()`, `_split_mcp_fields`, `_mcp_keys_from`, settings POST handling, `_settings_get` mcp_providers metadata, `_mcp_status`, main() boot loading — all need rework to `mcp_services` list.\n- `ui/app.js` lines 2813-2902: renders from `mcp_providers` metadata with a fixed set of inputs; needs to render rows from `mcp_services` records + \"+ add service\" button + per-record fields.\n- README.md and prompts/web_search.md: check for MCP text and platform names.\n\nAlso memory says: \"Migration: old mcp_xiaohongshu settings key converted one-time via preset\" — hmm, but the no-bias memory says \"Any built-in platform presets must be editable/deletable DATA (e.g. presets.json), never code; shipping zero presets is acceptable.\" The final agreed direction per the compacted summary: \"mcpprovider.py 引擎已落\" — the engine that landed has NO presets (parse_service has no preset merge). So shipping zero presets is acceptable. Migration of the old `mcp_xiaohongshu` settings key: the old key stored a URL string. The new format is `mcp_services` list. A one-time conversion: if settings.json has `mcp_xiaohongshu` URL and no mcp_services, convert to a service record with name=xiaohongshu, url=..., search_tool=search_feeds, args {keyword: {query}, limit: {limit}}, url_template https://www.xiaohongshu.com/explore/{id}?xsec_token={token}... \n\nWait — but the no-bias principle says no platform names in code. A migration that constructs a xiaohongshu record IS naming a platform in code. Hmm. But the memory says \"Migration: old mcp_xiaohongshu settings key converted one-time via preset.\" The \"preset\" here would be data. Hmm, but shipping a preset file with xiaohongshu data... The memory says presets must be editable/deletable DATA, never code; shipping zero presets is acceptable.\n\nActually let me think about what's pragmatic: the user (a xiaohongshu user, per \"Xiaohongshu open-source access landscape\" memory) has an old settings.json with `mcp_xiaohongshu: \"http://localhost:18060/mcp\"`. If we just drop that key, their configured backend silently disappears — a regression. The migration needs the field mappings (search_tool, args, fields url template with xsec_token) which are platform-specific knowledge. Where can that live without polluting code? Options:\n\n1. A data file `agent/tools/mcp_presets.json` (or similar) shipped with the release, containing declarative records (exactly the same shape as settings records). Migration: old `mcp_<name>` string keys are matched against preset names; the preset's record + the operator's URL become an entry in mcp_services. The preset file is DATA — editable/deletable by the operator. Code only knows \"presets are records\", never any name.\n2. No migration at all: old key ignored, backend gone.\n\nThe memory explicitly records the agreed migration approach: \"Migration: old mcp_xiaohongshu settings key converted one-time via preset.\" So option 1 matches. The code contains only generic preset-loading logic; the xiaohongshu record lives in a JSON data file.\n\nHmm wait, but does the engine that landed support presets? No — mcpprovider.py has no preset concept. The migration could be done in config.py or server.py: when loading settings, if legacy `mcp_<name>` string keys exist and `mcp_services` doesn't already contain `<name>`, consult presets data file for a record with that name; if found, append record with the operator's URL. Where to put the preset data file? Let me check what files exist in agent/. Let me look at the structure.\n\nActually, let me first read all the sections I need to edit precisely:\n\n1. websearch.py lines ~1-60 (imports), ~400-600 (MCP client + backends + chain), plus where Backend record and _available_backends are.\n2. config.py lines ~70-100.\n3. server.py lines ~40-120, ~200-215, ~360-470, ~840-850.\n4. ui/app.js lines ~2800-2910, and index.html line 50 area.\n5. README.md MCP section.\n6. prompts/web_search.md ($backends).\n7. tests/mcpbackend_test.py — will need updating too.\n8. main.js writeSettingsFile — memory says no change needed, but with the new mcp_services shape, the UI Save for MCP services might change (memory said app.js has \"own Save button\" for MCP services). Let me check how app.js saves MCP settings — it does its own apiFetch presumably. Let me read.\n\nLet me read websearch.py fully around the MCP parts and the Backend/chain definitions."}
