d the preset come from? A JSON data file shipped in agent (e.g. `agent/mcp_presets.json`) containing the xiaohongshu record (name, search_tool, args, fields with the explore URL template). This is DATA not code. The code only knows the generic mechanics.\n\nOption B: Simpler — migrate legacy `mcp_<name>` keys into `mcp_services` entries with just name+url (no search_tool) — but then `complete()` is False so the backend never joins the chain, and the UI would show a row with a URL but no tool configured. That silently breaks the user's working xiaohongshu setup. Bad.\n\nOption C: Ship presets.json with the xiaohongshu record (data, editable/deletable), migration merges legacy key URL with preset record. This matches the memory's agreed approach: \"old mcp_xiaohongshu settings key converted one-time via preset.\"\n\nLet me check the memory again: \"core keeps ONLY a generic declarative MCP engine — config-driven McpServiceSpec {...}; xiaohongshu is fully expressible as such a record (proven: _xhs_map = list unwrap + url template + desc|description fallback). UI gets '+ add service' rows. Any built-in platform presets must be editable/deletable DATA (e.g. presets.json), never code; shipping zero presets is acceptable. Migration: old mcp_xiaohongshu settings key converted one-time via preset.\"\n\nSo yes: Option C — a preset DATA file. I'll ship `agent/mcp_presets.json` containing declarative records. The xiaohongshu record needs:\n\n- name: \"xiaohongshu\"\n- label: maybe \"Xiaohongshu\" — hmm, label is display name. In DATA file it's fine.\n- search_tool: \"search_feeds\"\n- args: {\"keyword\": \"{query}\", \"limit\": \"{limit}\"}\n- fields: {\"title\": \"title | name\", \"url\": \"https://www.xiaohongshu.com/explore/{id}?xsec_token={xsec_token}\", \"snippet\": \"desc | description\"}\n- list_key: \"\" (auto-unwrap handles \"data\"/\"items\"/\"feeds\"/\"notes\" — xiaohongshu-mcp returns {\"data\": {...}}? Let me think. From the old bespoke code (now gone), the memory says \"_xhs_map = list unwrap + url template + desc|description fallback\". The auto-unwrap list keys include \"data\", \"items\", \"feeds\", \"notes\". Hmm, \"data\" might be a dict containing a list. The old MCP_PROVIDERS xiaohongshu spec mapped search_feeds -> {title,url,snippet} and the map was \"list unwrap + url template + desc|description fallback\". I don't know the exact payload shape of xiaohongshu-mcp's search_feeds. Common shape: {\"data\": {\"feeds\": [...]}} or {\"data\": [...]}. The auto-unwrap only checks top-level keys for a list. If payload is {\"data\": {\"feeds\": [...]}}, auto-unwrap fails and list_key would need \"data.feeds\". Since presets are DATA, I can set list_key explicitly. But I don't know the true shape...\n\nLet me check the test file or scratch logs — maybe tests/mcpbackend_test.py has a fake xiaohongshu payload I can consult. Also check git log / the old version of mcpprovider.py (v0.1.14 shipped). Let me look at tests/mcpbackend_test.py and maybe `git show` the old mcpprovider.py from git history (v0.1.14 tag?). The workspace has .git. `git show HEAD:agent/tools/mcpprovider.py` would show the old (registry-based) version — wait, actually the current working tree has the NEW engine (uncommitted?). Let me check git status and the old file to learn the xiaohongshu payload shape used in tests.\n\nActually the simplest: read tests/mcpbackend_test.py to see the fake payloads used.\n\nAlso the README section and prompt. Let me read:\n1. README.md lines 25-55\n2. agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md (whole)\n3. registry.py 130-175\n4. tests/mcpbackend_test.py (probably long; grep first for structure)"}
