{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789816425.2388363, "id": "91af54079397", "content": "现在我已经完全了解情况了。让我查看一下测试文件和剩余的引用（README 第 45 行，websearch 的 preset 用法）：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_d7f4eaf0c30c48ab9d52b1af", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"tests/mcpbackend_test.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_133c9efa4dc24b468024ca6b", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"preset|mcp_presets\",\"path\":\".\",\"include\":\"*.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now I have the full picture:\n\n1. `agent/mcp_presets.json` exists (untracked, `??`) — needs to move to `agent/tools/mcp_presets.json` (engine: `_PRESETS_FILE = Path(__file__).with_name(\"mcp_presets.json\")`).\n2. The test service name `notes` in `tests/mcpbackend_test.py` (NOTES record, lines 38ff, and all the `backend=\"notes\"` calls) collides with the natural-language \"release notes\" in the prompt file `agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md`. The test at line 301-303 checks `\"notes\" not in tools_plain[\"web_search\"].description` — the description is dynamically rendered from `$backends`. The prompt file contains \"release notes\", so if the backend name \"notes\" appears in the description... wait, let me think.\n\nActually the failure was: test checks that \"notes\" is NOT in the plain description (undeclared service absent). But the natural language \"release notes\" in web_search.md makes the substring \"notes\" appear anyway, so the check `\"notes\" not in ...` fails spuriously. Fix: rename the test service to `notedb`.\n\nSo the fixes:\n1. `git mv agent/mcp_presets.json agent/tools/mcp_presets.json` (or plain mv since it's untracked — `mv` is fine).\n2. In `tests/mcpbackend_test.py`: rename service name \"notes\" → \"notedb\" throughout (the NOTES record name, urls like `https://notes.example/...` — those are fine as URLs? The checks like `check(\"notes\" not in avail...)` would still trip if a URL contains \"notes\"... wait, those checks are against `avail` (list of backend names) and tool descriptions, not URLs. Let me check each line:\n   - Line 42: `\"fields\": {\"url\": \"https://notes.example/n/{id}?t={token}\", ...}` — URL in a data record, fine to keep or rename. But line 301 `check(\"notes\" not in tools_plain[\"web_search\"].description...)` — description contains prompt text. Does the description contain the example URL? Probably not. But to be safe, rename everything consistently: `notes.example` → `notedb.example`, `NOTES` var → `NOTEDB`.\n   - Line 129/133-135: mapped URLs — those are assertions on mapping results, no substring checks on \"notes\". Keeping \"notes.example\" there would be fine, but consistency is nicer. Actually wait — line 301-303: `check(\"notes\" not in tools_plain[\"web_search\"].description, ...)` and `check(\"notes\" in tools_cfg[\"web_search\"].description, ...)`. These check the *description* text. The prompt file web_search.md has \"release notes\" in its text — that's why the first check failed spuriously. Renaming the service to \"notedb\" fixes it since \"notedb\" won't appear in natural language.\n   \n3. Also the docstring in `agent/tools/mcpprovider.py` line 7 uses `{\"name\": \"notes\", ...}` — that's just a docstring example, not part of any rendered description. But for consistency and to avoid future confusion, rename it to \"notedb\" too.\n\n4. Check README.md line 45 mentions `agent/mcp_presets.json` — needs updating to `agent/tools/mcp_presets.json`.\n\nAlso README.md is modified (M) — it already documents the presets file; the path reference there must be updated.\n\nLet me check where the presets path is referenced elsewhere: grep found `agent/tools/mcpprovider.py` (line 26, 140) and README.md line 45. Any tests referencing mcp_presets? Let me grep tests for \"preset\".\n\nAlso check websearch.py to see how presets are used (the legacy mcp_<name> migration). And check whether there's a test for the presets file path.\n\nLet me now:\n1. Read tests/mcpbackend_test.py fully (at least the relevant parts).\n2. grep