{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789817131.4154255, "id": "dbb4a107f118", "tool_call_id": "call_ea21a0312cb644b98eb47073", "content": "(redirected to https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/getting-started/intro)\n\n# What is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)? - Model Context Protocol\n\nDocumentation Index\nFetch the complete documentation index at: /llms.txt\nUse this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nSkip to main content\nModel Context Protocol home page\nDocumentation\nSpecification\nExtensions\nRegistry\nSEPs\nCommunity\nGet started\nWhat is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)?\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open-source standard for connecting AI applications to external systems.\nUsing MCP, AI applications like Claude or ChatGPT can connect to data sources (e.g. local files, databases), tools (e.g. search engines, calculators) and workflows (e.g. specialized prompts)—enabling them to access key information and perform tasks.\nThink of MCP like a USB-C port for AI applications. Just as USB-C provides a standardized way to connect electronic devices, MCP provides a standardized way to connect AI applications to external systems.\n​\nWhat can MCP enable?\nAgents can access your Google Calendar and Notion, acting as a more personalized AI assistant.\nClaude Code can generate an entire web app using a Figma design.\nEnterprise chatbots can connect to multiple databases across an organization, empowering users to analyze data using chat.\nAI models can create 3D designs on Blender and print them out using a 3D printer.\n​\nWhy does MCP matter?\nDepending on where you sit in the ecosystem, MCP can have a range of benefits.\nDevelopers: MCP reduces development time and complexity when building, or integrating with, an AI application or agent.\nAI applications or agents: MCP gives them access to an ecosystem of data sources, tools and apps, which enhances their capabilities and improves the end-user experience.\nEnd-users: MCP results in more capable AI applications or agents that can access user data and take actions on the user’s behalf when necessary.\n​\nBroad ecosystem support\nMCP is an open protocol supported across a wide range of clients and servers. AI assistants like Claude and ChatGPT, development tools like Visual Studio Code, Cursor, MCPJam, and many others all support MCP — making it easy to build once and integrate everywhere.\n​\nStart Building\nBuild servers\nCreate MCP servers to expose your data and tools\nBuild clients\nDevelop applications that connect to MCP servers\nBuild MCP Apps\nBuild interactive apps that run inside AI clients\n​\nLearn more\nUnderstand concepts\nLearn the core concepts and architecture of MCP\nWas this page helpful?\nAssistant\nResponses are generated using AI and may contain mistakes.", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789817131.9617715, "id": "b65fe374fdc9", "tool_call_id": "call_6f087e933c1d404ca567012d", "content": "# Architecture - Model Context Protocol\n\nDocumentation Index\nFetch the complete documentation index at: /llms.txt\nUse this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nSkip to main content\nModel Context Protocol home page\nDocumentation\nSpecification\nExtensions\nRegistry\nSEPs\nCommunity\nArchitecture\nThe Model Context Protocol (MCP) follows a client-host-server architecture where each\nhost can run multiple client instances. This architecture enables users to integrate AI\ncapabilities across applications while maintaining clear security boundaries and\nisolating concerns. Built on JSON-RPC, MCP provides a stateful session protocol focused\non context exchange and sampling coordination between clients and servers.\n​\nCore Components\n​\nHost\nThe host process acts as the container and coordinator:\nCreates and manages multiple client instances\nControls client connection permissions and lifecycle\nEnforces security policies and consent requirements\nHandles user authorization decisions\nCoordinates AI/LLM integration and sampling\nManages context aggregation across clients\n​\nClients\nEach client is created by the host and maintains an isolated server connection:\nEstablishes one stateful session per server\nHandles protocol negotiation and capability exchange\nRoutes protocol messages bidirectionally\nManages subscriptions and notifications\nMaintains security boundaries between servers\nA host application creates and manages multiple clients, with each client having a 1:1\nrelationship with a particular server.\n​\nServers\nServers provide specialized context and capabilities:\nExpose resources, tools and prompts via MCP primitives\nOperate independently with focused responsibilities\nRequest sampling through client interfaces\nMust respect security constraints\nCan be local processes or remote services\n​\nDesign Principles\nMCP is built on several key design principles that inform its architecture and\nimplementation:\nServers should be extremely easy to build\nHost applications handle complex orchestration responsibilities\nServers focus on specific, well-defined capabilities\nSimple interfaces minimize implementation overhead\nClear separation enables maintainable code\nServers should be highly composable\nEach server provides focused functionality in isolation\nMultiple servers can be combined seamlessly\nShared protocol enables interoperability\nModular design supports extensibility\nServers should not be able to read the whole conversation, nor “see into” other\nservers\nServers receive only necessary contextual information\nFull conversation history stays with the host\nEach server connection maintains isolation\nCross-server interactions are controlled by the host\nHost process enforces security boundaries\nFeatures can be added to servers and clients progressively\nCore protocol provides minimal required functionality\nAdditional capabilities can be negotiated as needed\nServers and clients evolve independently\nProtocol designed for future extensibility\nBackwards compatibility is maintained\n​\nCapability Negotiation\nThe Model Context Protocol uses a capability-based negotiation system where clients and\nservers explicitly declare their supported features during initialization. Capabilities\ndetermine which protocol features and primitives are available during a session.\nServers declare capabilities like resource subscriptions, tool support, and prompt\ntemplates\nClients declare capabilities like sampling support and notification handling\nBoth parties must respect declared capabilities throughout the session\nAdditional capabilities can be negotiated through extensions to the protocol\nEach capability unlocks specific protocol features for use during the session. For\nexample:\nImplemented server features must be advertised in the\nserver’s capabilities\nEmitting resource subscription notifications requires the server to declare\nsubscription support\nTool invocation requires the server to declare tool capabilities\nSampling requires the client to declare support in its\ncapabilities\nThis capability negotiation ensures clients and servers have a clear understanding of\nsupported functionality while maintaining protocol extensibility.\nWas this page helpful?\nAssistant\nResponses are generated using AI and may contain mistakes.", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789817171.79845, "id": "c046d979daae", "content": "官方一手定义拿到了（modelcontextprotocol.io，含最新规范）。从正统定义讲：\n\n## 官方定义原文\n\n> **\"MCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open-source standard for connecting AI applications to external systems.\"**\n>\n> \"Think of MCP like a **USB-C port for AI applications**. Just as USB-C provides a standardized way to connect electronic devices, MCP provides a standardized way to connect AI applications to external systems.\"\n\n拆开这句话里的三个关键词：\n\n1. **开放标准** —— Anthropic 于 2024 年 11 月发布并开源的协议，现在 Claude、ChatGPT、VS Code、Cursor 等都支持；目标是“构建一次，到处集成”\n2. **连接** —— 它不执行任何逻辑本身，只是规定 AI 应用与外部系统之间**如何对话**\n3. **外部系统** —— 数据源（文件、数据库）、工具（搜索、计算器）、工作流（专用提示词）\n\n## 正统架构：client–host–server\n\n规范明确是 **客户端–宿主–服务器** 架构，基于 **JSON-RPC 2.0**，是**有状态会话协议**：\n\n| 角色 | 职责 |\n|---|---|\n| **Host**（宿主） | 容器与协调者：Claude Desktop、Clutch 这类 AI 应用。创建并管理多个 client，控制权限与生命周期，执行安全策略与用户授权，聚合上下文 |\n| **Client**（客户端） | 由 host 创建，与某个 server **1:1** 对应：建立一个有状态会话，做协议版本与能力协商，双向路由消息，维护服务器间隔离 |\n| **Server**（服务器） | 通过 MCP 原语暴露特定能力：可以是本地进程（stdio）或远程服务（Streamable HTTP）。**不能读完整对话，也看不到其他 server** |\n\n## 核心原语（按控制方划分）\n\n这是正统定义里最容易被忽略的部分——MCP 不止“工具调用”：\n\n| 原语 | 控制方 | 含义 |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tools** | 模型 | LLM 自己决定何时调用（`tools/call`） |\n| **Resources** | 应用 | 宿主决定把哪些数据放进上下文（类似 GET，无副作用） |\n| **Prompts** | 用户 | 用户主动选择的提示模板 |\n| **Sampling** | 服务器 | 服务器**反向**请求宿主的 LLM 补全（AI 能力反向外露） |\n| **Elicitation** | 服务器 | 服务器请求用户补充输入 |\n| **Roots** | 客户端 | 声明文件系统边界 |\n\n## 生命周期与设计原则\n\n- **生命周期**：`initialize`（版本协商 + **能力声明**）→ 运行（消息/通知/订阅）→ 关闭。双方必须只使用声明过的能力——这就是为什么握手要先于调用\n- **设计原则**（规范原文归纳）：服务器要**极易编写**；服务器**高可组合**、职责单一；服务器**看不见**完整对话、彼此隔离（安全边界在 host）；功能通过能力协商**渐进扩展**，保持向后兼容\n\n## 回扣本项目：Clutch 用的是协议的哪一切片\n\n对照正统定义，Clutch 实现的是 **client 侧的一个薄切片**：\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[\"Host: Clutch<br/>agent 进程\"] --> B[\"Client 切片<br/>_mcp_call\"]\n    B --> C[\"initialize<br/>版本协商 + 会话\"]\n    C --> D[\"notifications/initialized<br/>Mcp-Session-Id\"]\n    D --> E[\"tools/call<br/>仅 Tools 原语\"]\n    E --> F[\"Server: 外部搜索服务\"]\n```\n\n- ✅ 实现了：initialize 握手、session、`tools/call`（**Tools** 原语）——把 MCP server 当作 `web_search` 的一个后端数据源\n- ❌ 有意不用：Resources / Prompts / Sampling / Elicitation——本项目只要“调用搜索工具、读回结果”这一件事，多一个原语都是负担\n\n这恰好符合 MCP 自己的设计哲学：**能力是协商出来的，协议是切片式的**——你只需要实现你声明要用的部分。所以 Clutch 的“声明式 `mcp_services` 记录 + 只走 tools/call”不是对协议的简化偏离，而是协议允许的最小合法子集。", "tool_calls": [], "reasoning": "I have the official intro and architecture pages. Let me also quickly check the core primitives page to be precise about Tools/Resources/Prompts/Sampling/Elicitation/Roots. Actually I have solid knowledge of this; but to be grounded, let me fetch one more page briefly — the concepts page or the spec's \"core primitives\" from llms.txt. One more fetch on concepts would round it out.\n\nActually, I have enough for a faithful \"正统定义\" answer:\n\nOfficial definition (from modelcontextprotocol.io, current docs):\n- \"MCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open-source standard for connecting AI applications to external systems.\"\n- USB-C analogy.\n- Origin: announced by Anthropic Nov 2024, open-sourced; now broadly adopted (Claude, ChatGPT, VS Code, Cursor...).\n- Architecture: client-host-server, JSON-RPC 2.0 based, stateful sessions.\n- Host / Client (1:1 with server) / Server.\n- Primitives: tools, resources, prompts (server→client), plus sampling, elicitation, roots; capability negotiation at initialization.\n- Transports: stdio and Streamable HTTP.\n- Design principles: servers easy to build, composable, isolation (server can't see whole conversation), progressive capability negotiation.\n\nThen connect to the project: what our Clutch implementation covers (a thin client slice of MCP: initialize → tools/call over HTTP, treating a server as a search backend) vs the full protocol (resources/prompts/sampling/elicitation are not implemented — by design, per the project's \"generalized interface + zero footprint\" philosophy, we only need the tool-invocation slice).\n\nMaybe a small table of primitives with control party (who controls: model/user/application). The spec says:\n- Tools: model-controlled (the LLM decides to call)\n- Resources: application-controlled (host decides what context to include)\n- Prompts: user-controlled (user selects a template)\n- Sampling: server requests LLM completion through client\n- Elicitation: server requests additional user input\n- Roots: client declares filesystem boundaries\n\nLet me fetch the concepts page to verify the primitives list quickly? I'm fairly confident. One more fetch for correctness on the 2026 spec revision — the docs redirected to /docs/2026-07-28/ so there's a newer revision. The architecture page I fetched didn't show a revision header but it's the current one. Good enough; I'll note versions carefully without overclaiming: \"2025-06-18 是长期标注的规范版本；docs 目前还列有更新的修订\"。Hmm, the redirect to 2026-07-28 suggests a newer spec date. I should be careful about claiming specifics. I'll mention JSON-RPC 2.0 + stateful session + capability negotiation, which is stable across revisions.\n\nI'll write the answer in Chinese, structured:\n\n1. 官方定义原文（一句话）+ USB-C 类比\n2. 拆解定义里的关键词（开放标准/连接 AI 应用与外部系统/上下文协议）\n3. 架构：host-client-server（mermaid 或表格）\n4. 核心原语（tools/resources/prompts/sampling/elicitation/roots）+ 控制方\n5. 线协议与生命周期（JSON-RPC 2.0, stdio/Streamable HTTP, initialize 能力协商）\n6. 设计原则要点（服务器易写、组合、隔离、渐进能力）\n7. 回扣本项目：Clutch 里实现的是协议的一个薄切片（client 侧的 initialize → tools/call），把 server 当搜索后端用；resources/prompts/sampling 未用 —— 这正好呼应\"能力协商\"思想：声明你需要的部分即可。\n\nKeep it reasonably concise but complete. Cite modelcontextprotocol.io. No more tool calls after this."}
