 D --> E[\"tools/call<br/>仅 Tools 原语\"]\n    E --> F[\"Server: 外部搜索服务\"]\n```\n\n- ✅ 实现了：initialize 握手、session、`tools/call`（**Tools** 原语）——把 MCP server 当作 `web_search` 的一个后端数据源\n- ❌ 有意不用：Resources / Prompts / Sampling / Elicitation——本项目只要“调用搜索工具、读回结果”这一件事，多一个原语都是负担\n\n这恰好符合 MCP 自己的设计哲学：**能力是协商出来的，协议是切片式的**——你只需要实现你声明要用的部分。所以 Clutch 的“声明式 `mcp_services` 记录 + 只走 tools/call”不是对协议的简化偏离，而是协议允许的最小合法子集。"}
{"type": "user_message", "timestamp": 1789817285.2490797, "id": "99ca1a8cc062", "content": "但mcp本身不止是为了实现搜索不是吗？要么不要因为搜索需求强加那么一个累赘，声称实现了mcp而实际上只是阉割版；要么就自己拟定接口和协议，不要用mcp那一套东西，彻底简化运行逻辑和架构"}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789817357.511161, "id": "047ce564ab88", "content": "这是当前架构最重要的一个设计质疑，值得认真对待。让我先量化一下“累赘”到底有多大，再回答：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_06beb5945d8143718fe4c87d", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":62,\"offset\":415,\"path\":\"agent/tools/websearch.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user is making an architectural critique. They're saying: MCP itself isn't just for implementing search, right? So either:\n1. Don't impose such a burden just for search needs — don't claim to \"implement MCP\" when it's actually a castrated/neutered version; OR\n2. Define your own interface and protocol, don't use MCP's stuff at all, thoroughly simplify the running logic and architecture.\n\nThis is a legitimate design challenge. Let me think carefully about this.\n\nThe user's critique has two horns:\n- Horn A: If you claim MCP support, you should implement MCP properly (a real MCP client with full lifecycle, or at least honestly frame what you support). Otherwise claiming \"MCP support\" is misleading — it's a stripped-down version wearing MCP's name.\n- Horn B: Alternatively, abandon MCP branding entirely and define a minimal custom protocol (just \"POST to URL, get JSON rows back\"), which would drastically simplify the code.\n\nLet me think about what's true in the codebase:\n\nCurrent implementation (from what I've seen):\n- `agent/tools/mcpprovider.py`: declarative records (McpService), templates, field mapping engine, presets. Pure data engine.\n- `agent/tools/websearch.py`: `_mcp_rpc` (JSON-RPC over POST, SSE frames), `_mcp_call` (initialize → notifications/initialized → tools/call), `_mcp_backend` adapting to Backend shape, `mcp_status` probe.\n- The test has a real loopback MCP server speaking JSON-RPC.\n\nSo the project DOES implement a genuine MCP client slice: initialize handshake, session id, notifications/initialized, tools/call, SSE and JSON responses, error paths. That's not a fake. It's the Tools primitive only.\n\nIs the user's critique fair? Partially:\n\n1. \"声称实现了mcp而实际上只是阉割版\" — Actually the implementation is an honest MCP client for the Tools primitive. The MCP spec is explicitly capability-negotiated and progressive: a client that declares only tools capability and only calls tools is a legitimate, spec-compliant client. \"阉割版\" is a value judgment — the spec's design philosophy explicitly endorses partial capability adoption. But there IS a real gap worth acknowledging: the client doesn't parse `tools/list` to discover tool schemas, doesn't handle elicitation/sampling (fine), doesn't do the `notifications/cancelled`, doesn't re-handshake on session expiry, etc. For the narrow use case (call one search tool with known name and args template) that's fine.\n\n2. The deeper truth: the real design tension isn't MCP vs no-MCP. The project's core need is: \"operator has some remote service that returns search results in arbitrary JSON shapes; agent needs those results in uniform {title,url,snippet} shape.\" The essential complexity is:\n   - call side: how to invoke (tool name + args template)\n   - read side: how to interpret the reply (field mappings)\n   \n   The MCP handshake is actually a small part — maybe 40 lines (_mcp_rpc + _mcp_call). The field-mapping engine is the bigger, more valuable part and is protocol-independent.\n\nWhat would \"own protocol\" (Horn B) look like? A record like:\n```\n{\"name\": \"notes\", \"url\": \"...\", \"path\": \"/search\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"body\": {...}, \"headers\": {...}, \"read\": {...}}\n```\nThat's basically reinventing a worse MCP: you'd need to spec method, auth headers, response envelopes... For HTTP-JSON services it's simpler, but then you lose:\n- interoperability with the actual MCP ecosystem (thousands of existing MCP servers — filesystem, github, postgres, sqlite, puppeteer, slack, google maps, memory, etc. — the whole point of MCP is \"build once, run everywhere\")\n- the ability to point the same record at an MCP server that the operator already runs.\n\nHmm, but wait — does the user actually want ecosystem interop for search? Their use case: xiaohongshu search via a local MCP server (they have one, mcp-server for xiaohongshu — there's a memory \"Xiaohongshu open-source access landscape\"). The xiaohongshu MCP server exists as an MCP server in the wild. So interop matters: the operator's existing service already speaks MCP. If we invented our own protocol, the operator would have to write an adapter.\n\nSo my recommendation: keep MCP (Horn A resolved honestly) — but there are legitimate improvements to make the claim fully honest and reduce the \"累赘\" feeling:\n\n1. **Frame honestly in docs**: \"MCP-compatible client for the Tools primitive\" — say exactly what's supported (stdio? no — HTTP only; tools only). Actually wait, does it support stdio servers? No — HTTP POST only (streamable HTTP + SSE responses). Many MCP servers are stdio-based (npx-based). The current impl only speaks HTTP. That's a real limitation worth stating. The user's own service is HTTP (localhost:18060/mcp), so it works for them.\n\n2. **Use tools/list**: the current design requires the operator to hand-write `search_tool` name and `args` template in the record. A real MCP client would call `tools/list` at connect time and discover the tool + input schema. This would let the status endpoint show available tools, and could even auto-fill. But — the args template mapping (keyword vs query) is still needed because each server names its parameters differently. Hmm, tools/list would give the input schema; could auto-build args by matching `{query}` into a string param. That's a possible enhancement but adds complexity.\n\nActually — let me reconsider. The user's complaint \"累赘\" (burden/encumbrance). What exactly is the burden in the current architecture?\n\nThe record requires: name, url, search_tool, args, fields. For an MCP server, the args template (`{\"keyword\": \"{query}\", \"limit\": \"{limit}\"}`) exists because MCP tool input schemas vary. For our own protocol, we could fix: `POST url` with `{\"query\": q, \"limit\": n}` and expect `[{title,url,snippet}]` or map fields. Then records shrink to: name, url, fields. And no handshake.\n\nBut then it's not MCP anymore and can't talk to existing MCP servers.\n\nThe honest engineering answer: the two horns aren't symmetric. Horn B (own protocol) only wins if you never talk to existing MCP servers. Since the motivating service (xiaohongshu MCP server, and whatever else the operator runs) already speaks MCP, Horn B costs interop and forces adapter code — while the protocol client cost is already paid (~100 lines, tested). Horn A's \"阉割\" feeling is addressed by honest scoping: support exactly the Tools slice over HTTP, document it as such, and lean on the spec's capability negotiation to justify it.\n\nBut I should also steelman Horn B seriously. Where does Horn B genuinely win? If the project wants \"any HTTP search service\" (not just MCP): e.g., SearXNG, Tavily are already native backends — they're the \"own interface\" version, implemented directly in code. For generic HTTP JSON search services, a `rest` type record could be added later — but that's speculative until a real service demands it (YAGNI).\n\nThere's also a middle path worth mentioning: keep MCP but reduce the operator burden by using `tools/list` discovery + status showing discovered tools, so records shrink to name+url for well-behaved servers. And support stdio via a spawned bridge? No — that adds Python subprocess management, platform issues; not worth it for this project; keep HTTP-only and say so.\n\nNow — importantly — this is CHAT MODE. I can't edit files. The user is raising a design debate. My job: analyze, give a clear recommendation, show the tradeoff concretely, and propose the concrete minimal changes if they want to act. I should NOT just defend the status quo sycophantically, nor cave and say \"you're right, let's rewrite\". I should give an honest architectural verdict.\n\nLet me actually look at the current _mcp_rpc implementation to quantify the \"累赘\" — how many lines is the protocol client vs the mapping engine? Let me check websearch.py lines 421-477 (_mcp_rpc) — I read 477+ earlier. Let me read 415-477 to see _mcp_rpc size. Also check how big mcp_status is. This grounds the \"the protocol slice is ~100 lines, the mapping engine is the real value\" claim.\n\nAlso worth checking: does the record/docstring claim \"MCP\" prominently in README? README line 40 says \"外部 MCP 服务也可以接入为搜索后端\". That's honest enough, but could say \"MCP 兼容（Tools 子集，HTTP 传输）\".\n\nLet me read the _mcp_rpc section quickly. Then compose the answer.\n\nAnswer structure:\n1. 直接回应：你的批评有一半成立 —— \"阉割版\"的说法需要澄清（协议本身支持能力切片，tools-only client 是合法 client），但**文档措辞**确实有夸大空间；\"累赘\"的真实大小需要量化（~100 行，一次性成本）。\n2. 关键论点：第二条路（自拟协议）不是简化，是把协议成本换成适配成本 —— 除非你永远不接现成 MCP 服务器。你的初始需求（xiaohongshu）本身就是现成 MCP 服务器，所以 Horn B 自相矛盾。\n3. 用数字说话：协议客户端占比 vs 映射引擎占比；真正的价值在声明式映射引擎（与协议无关，无论走哪条路都要写）。\n4. 诚实的第三条路（推荐）：不是二选一，而是把话说准 + 把负担降到最小：\n   a. 措辞：README/docs 明确 \"MCP client（Tools 原语 / HTTP 传输）\"，不笼统说\"支持 MCP\"\n   b. 减负：用 tools/list 做发现（status 接口顺带列出可用工具），记录可以缩到 name+url；args 模板保留给参数名不标准的 server\n   c. YAGNI：不要为\"任意 HTTP 服务\"预留 rest 类型，等真实需求出现再加（SearXNG/Tavily 就是直连先例）\n5. 如果仍然选 Horn B，给出自拟协议会长什么样（POST {\"query\",\"limit\"} → rows），并指出你会失去什么 —— 让用户自己权衡。\n\nActually wait — I should be careful about the framing \"声称实现了mcp而实际上只是阉割版\". Let me check what the README actually claims. From earlier read: README line 40-44: \"外部 MCP 服务也可以接入为搜索后端：在设置弹窗的 Web services 区添加服务（名字、地址、搜索工具名），记录存于 ~/.clutch/settings.json 的 mcp_services 列表，每条记录声明如何调用它的搜索工具、如何读取返回结果...\" — it says \"外部 MCP 服务可以接入为搜索后端\" — that's an accurate claim (it does speak MCP). The docstring in mcpprovider.py says \"Operator-configured MCP tool servers that extend web search.\"\n\nSo the claim isn't false. But the user's point about \"强加累赘\" has a real kernel: the record format forces the operator to hand-declare the tool name and args template — things a real MCP client would discover via tools/list. And HTTP-only excludes stdio servers (which are actually the majority of published MCP servers!). That IS a real compatibility gap vs \"支持 MCP\" as commonly understood.\n\nSo my verdict: keep MCP as the protocol (interop with the existing ecosystem is the whole point and your founding use case), but do two concrete things:\n1. honest scoping in wording: \"MCP over HTTP (Streamable), Tools primitive only\" — including stating stdio servers are not supported (that's the honest part of \"阉割版\")\n2. reduce the record burden via tools/list discovery in mcp_status, so a well-behaved server needs only name+url (+search_tool if ambiguous).\n\nAnd explicitly answer the dilemma: \"要么A要么B\" is a false dichotomy — A's honest version costs ~nothing (wording), A's burden-reduction version is a small bounded enhancement, B costs interop and is only justified if the ecosystem never matters.\n\nAlso should mention: the spec itself (capability negotiation + \"servers should be extremely easy to build\" + progressive features) explicitly blesses minimal clients. Quote the design principle.\n\nOne more consideration — the user might be right that a full-featured claim would require more (stdio, tools/list, resources...). If the project someday wants \"any MCP server's tools appear as agent tools\" (generic tool surfacing), that's a much bigger surface (registry per tool, schema → JSON schema translation, security). That's where \"彻底实现 MCP\" would genuinely be a big burden. The search-backend slice deliberately avoids that. Worth noting as the boundary: what would trigger revisiting.\n\nLet me quantify the code sizes first. _mcp_rpc is lines 421-476 (56 lines), _mcp_call 477-527 (51 lines), _mcp_backend 553-570 (18 lines), mcp_status ~598-611. Total MCP-protocol-specific in websearch.py ≈ 125-140 lines. mcpprovider.py total is ~11960 bytes ≈ 330 lines, of which protocol-agnostic engine (records, templates, mapping) is most of it. Tests: 59 checks.\n\nGood. Let me read _mcp_rpc to confirm size and see what it handles (SSE frames etc.)."}
