rver with curl / a command line?\n\nThe xiaohongshu MCP service is at http://localhost:18060/mcp (per the presets file). The question is whether raw command-line tools (curl, python one-liners) can speak to it — i.e., the MCP protocol is just JSON-RPC over HTTP POST, so yes, absolutely. You can:\n\n1. POST initialize → get session id\n2. POST notifications/initialized with the session header\n3. POST tools/call with the search tool name and args\n\nAnd even simpler: many Streamable HTTP servers accept stateless calls without the handshake (depending on server implementation — FastMCP-based servers usually require initialize first; some tolerate stateless mode).\n\nI'm in chat mode. Can I actually test it? run_command whitelist: curl without -o is allowed (read-only GET-ish). But POST via curl — is that allowed? The memory says: \"Chat mode whitelist misjudges piped sed/grep as writes; bare curl|head, python -c, direct grep -rn work.\" curl POST is a write to a remote server technically, but the whitelist is about local state. Hmm, \"curl without -o\" was listed as whitelisted in the tool description. A POST with -d might be flagged as not provably read-only? Actually the whitelist says \"curl without -o\" is fine. POST doesn't require -o. But it does send data to a server. The static classifier might reject `-X POST` or `-d` as a write operation. Also — the xiaohongshu server may not even be running right now (localhost:18060). Let me check if it's up first with a quick probe.\n\nActually, wait. The key question here is more conceptual/architectural: the user is questioning whether MCP-as-integrated is a good solution, and wants to explore whether the \"MCP service\" can be talked to directly via CLI — implying: maybe Clutch's agent could just use its existing shell tool (run_command with curl/python) to talk to the service, instead of having a built-in MCP client layer. That's the \"essence\": the agent already has a shell. If the service is reachable via HTTP JSON-RPC, the agent can call it with curl in 3 commands — no dedicated MCP integration needed.\n\nThis is actually a profound architectural point and it echoes how Claude Code itself works: Claude Code has Bash. It can talk to any API without dedicated integrations. The dedicated MCP layer adds: structured discovery, auth handling, zero-footprint gating, type-safe mapping, UI status... but the essence is: anything an MCP client can do, a shell + curl/python can do (for HTTP transports; for stdio, even easier — just launch the process and speak JSON-RPC on stdin/stdout).\n\nSo my answer should:\n1. Answer directly: yes — MCP is just JSON-RPC; here's the exact curl sequence to talk to the xiaohongshu service right now.\n2. Actually demonstrate it if the server is up (test with curl in chat mode — read-only enough? A POST to a search tool is a read operation semantically. The whitelist concern: \"provably read-only commands\" — curl POST to localhost search is not modifying workspace state. The classifier may or may not allow it. Let me try. If blocked, I'll show the commands for the user to run in work mode.)\n\nLet me first check if the server is running: curl -s -m 2 http://localhost:18060/mcp or check the port. `curl -s -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code}\"` — but -o is not allowed? \"-o\" writes a file... to /dev/null. The whitelist says \"curl without -o\". Use `curl -s -m 2 http://localhost:18060/mcp | head -c 200` or just POST and see.\n\nLet me try the actual sequence:\n1. `curl -s -m 3 http://localhost:18060/mcp -X POST -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -H \"Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\" -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-03-26\",\"capabilities\":{},\"clientInfo\":{\"name\":\"curl\",\"version\":\"0\"}}}' -D -` — include response headers to capture Mcp-Session-Id. -D - dumps headers to stdout... that might count as fine (no file write). Actually `-D -` writes to stdout, fine.\n\n2. Then