P's normal consumer is the LLM — raw payload into context is FINE for a shell-using agent. Clutch's mapping engine exists only because web_search merges rows into a uniform chain. If the agent just needs \"search xiaohongshu and tell me\", shell+curl suffices — the model reads the raw JSON.\n4. So the answer to \"MCP is not a good solution\": distinguish two claims:\n   - For the agent's ad-hoc use: correct — no MCP layer needed; shell is the universal client. The agent can talk to this service today with zero new code.\n   - For web_search's backend chain (merged uniform results): a declarative layer is still the cheapest way to keep the chain uniform — but it doesn't have to be MCP; the record is doing the real work, the MCP envelope is 135 lines of plumbing.\n   - Possible architecture simplification (the user's instinct): drop the built-in MCP client; keep the declarative record but let it describe plain HTTP calls (Clutch-REST), OR keep MCP client but ALSO let the agent use shell freely. Actually simplest: the skill/prompt approach — write a tiny skill \"xiaohongshu-search.md\" documenting the endpoint, tool name, arg names, and a ready curl recipe; the agent runs it via shell. That's zero protocol code, uses existing tools, follows the skill pattern already in the repo. This is arguably the BEST answer to the essence question: the CLI path + a skill = MCP-equivalent capability with 0 lines of protocol code.\n\nLet me verify the server is up and demonstrate. Chat mode: try curl. If the classifier rejects POST, show the commands.\n\nLet me be careful with curl flags: avoid -o, avoid writing files. Use -s -m, -H, -d, -D - (headers to stdout). Piping to head worked per memory (\"bare curl|head\" works).\n\nSequence:\n1. Probe: `curl -s -m 3 -X POST http://localhost:18060/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' -D - -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{\"protocolVersion\":\"2025-03-