{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789818527.9874141, "id": "e3fd30c9e4f4", "content": "对——而且这个不用查外部资料，**你们仓库自己就带着一个活例子**。让我把证据翻出来：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_8583ff27a1e3404eb5f00479", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"ls agent/skills/readme-crafter agent/skills/readme-crafter/scripts agent/skills/readme-crafter/references 2>/dev/null\"}"}, {"id": "call_7b997a8f8ce1478e9c86a09f", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/skills/readme-crafter\",\"pattern\":\"scan-project|scripts/|python\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user is asking: \"据我所知，skill不止包含文本文档，部分专业的skill也自带可调用/可执行的脚本或者现成工具对吧\" — \"As far as I know, skills don't only contain text documents; some professional skills also come with callable/executable scripts or ready-made tools, right?\"\n\nThis is actually the SAME question as the very first task in this conversation (before compaction, the task was exactly this question). Now it comes back at the end, after we've built the full architectural picture: MCP vs skills, bing/ddg internals, failover chains, the reader-is-the-LLM principle.\n\nThe user is right — and this is highly relevant to this project's skill implementation. Let me verify what the project's skills actually contain. Earlier I saw:\n\n- `agent/skills/` contains: readme-crafter/, readme-doctor/, refactor/, web-design/\n- From the grep earlier: `agent\\skills\\readme-crafter\\scripts\\scan-project.py` and `agent\\skills\\readme-crafter\\scripts\\scan_project_support.py` exist! So YES — the repo's own skills already ship executable scripts.\n- `agent/skills.py` loads SKILL.md with frontmatter (name, description, content), and `_load_skill` in registry.py serves SKILL.md \"or a sub-file\" — there's a `file` parameter: \"optional file inside the skill directory to read instead of SKILL.md\". So the skill mechanism already supports referencing files inside the skill directory — scripts included. The model loads the script reference and then runs it via run_command (shell tool).\n\nSo the answer: 对 (correct). Three levels of \"skill carries executable capability\":\n1. Pure text (SKILL.md): knowledge/instructions — the model reads and acts with its general tools.\n2. Text + bundled scripts (the common professional pattern): SKILL.md references scripts in the skill directory; the model executes them via the existing shell tool (`python skills/readme-crafter/scripts/scan-project.py`). The script is deterministic code; the model is the caller/orchestrator. This repo's own readme-crafter does exactly this (scan-project.py + scan_project_support.py).\n3. Text + bundled tools/resources beyond scripts: templates, reference docs (references/quality-checklist.md exists in readme-crafter), fixtures.\n\nIn the broader ecosystem (Claude Code skills, Anthropic's agent skills spec): skills are directories with SKILL.md plus optional supporting files — scripts, templates, references. Anthropic's documentation explicitly describes executable scripts in skills. The model runs them via its Bash tool. The skill doesn't get its own tool-registration; it borrows the shell.\n\nNow connect to this conversation's arc — this is the capstone insight:\n\n**The skill+script pattern is exactly the answer to the \"xiaohongshu integration\" question from the previous turns!** Instead of the MCP layer (135 lines protocol + config UI + records), you can ship a skill whose directory contains a ready-made script `xhs-search.py` (or even document a curl recipe): the model loads the skill, runs the script via shell, gets results. The script IS \"现成工具\" — pre-written by the developer (like _bing/_ddg are), but shipped as data alongside a skill instead of compiled into the backend registry.\n\nThis unifies the whole conversation:\n- bing/ddg = built-in functions (developer-maintained parsers, compiled in, always available)\n- skill scripts = developer-maintained tools, shipped as files, invoked through the shell, on-demand loaded\n- MCP layer = operator-configured external services, generic engine interprets\n- shell ad hoc = model improvises\n\nThe skill+script tier has a unique property: it's the ONLY tier where developer-written tools are loaded on-demand (progressive disclosure — the catalog shows one line; the script costs nothing until relevant) AND yet zero protocol/runtime footprint. It combines bing/ddg's \"pre-written by developer\" quality with the skill's \"loaded only when relevant\" quality.\n\nAnd the security/trust nuance: scripts in skills are code shipped with the repo (reviewed like code), executed by the model via run_command — subject to the same permissions as any shell command (work mode). Not sandboxed beyond that. MCP servers are external processes with their own trust domain. Skills' scripts are in-process trust (well, subprocess, but same user/permissions).\n\nLet me verify the actual facts in the repo before answering — check:\n1. The readme-crafter skill structure (scripts exist — confirmed by earlier grep: agent/skills/readme-crafter/scripts/scan-project.py, scan_project_support.py, references/quality-checklist.md)\n2. How SKILL.md references its scripts (does the instruction tell the model to run them?)\n3. The `_load_skill` file param — confirmed in registry.py (file param, escape check, serves sub-files)\n\nLet me do a quick grep/read to confirm how the skill references its scripts, and whether load_skill serves script files (not just SKILL.md). From registry.py lines 297-318: `_load_skill(_workspace, config, name, file=\"SKILL.md\")` — serves any file inside the skill dir with an escape check. So the model can load scripts (read them), and then run them with the shell tool. The scripts are NOT auto-registered as tools — they're invoked via run_command.\n\nAlso the Anthropic ecosystem fact: Agent Skills (announced Oct 2025) — SKILL.md + optional scripts/, references/, assets/. Claude Code loads them, model runs scripts via Bash. This matches. I can state this from the web-design/readme-crafter skills present in this repo (they follow that pattern) — and I could do a quick web check, but honestly the repo's own skills demonstrate the pattern, and my earlier fetches don't cover it. It's safe to describe the pattern from the repo itself + general knowledge. Actually — to be careful about \"orthodox\" claims, I could quickly check one SKILL.md to show how it references scripts. Let me read the readme-crafter SKILL.md frontmatter/instructions briefly.\n\nLet me grep how scan-project.py is referenced in the readme-crafter SKILL.md.\n\nThen compose the answer:\n\nStructure:\n1. 直答: 对。而且不用查外部——你们仓库自己的 readme-crafter 就是一个带可执行脚本的 skill。\n2. 证据: agent/skills/readme-crafter/scripts/scan-project.py + scan_project_support.py; SKILL.md 指示模型运行它; load_skill 支持 file 参数读子文件; 执行走的是已有的 shell 工具 (run_command)。\n3. 机制解析: skill 没有\"工具注册\"机制 — 脚本不是新 tool, 它们借道 shell. 模型 = 调用者/编排者, 脚本 = 确定性逻辑. 这就是\"渐进披露\": 目录里多 500 行脚本, 常态上下文成本仍是每技能一行.\n4. 生态佐证: Anthropic Agent Skills 官方模式 = SKILL.md + scripts/ + references/ + assets/ (Claude Code 同款). (I should be a bit careful; I can mention it matches the pattern in this repo which follows Claude Code conventions.)\n5. 收束整个对话弧: skill+script 正是\"小红书整合\"问题的第三条路 — 上一轮说 L1(模型多源调用)/L2(fan-out), 其实还有 L1.5: 写一个 xhs-search skill, 内附现成脚本(等价于 _bing/_ddg 的解析逻辑, 但随 skill 发布, 相关时才进上下文, 用 shell 跑). 它同时拿到: 开发者预写(和 bing/ddg 一样的质量) + 按需加载(零足迹) + 无协议/无UI/无record. 四种接入路径最终对比表.\n6. 边界/代价: 脚本没有结构化并入 web_search 链 (same as L1); 信任模型 = 随仓库发布的代码(和 _bing 同级信任), 由模型经 shell 执行; 维护责任在开发者(和 bing/ddg 一样会 rot).\n\nTable comparing four tiers:\n| 路径 | 谁写解析 | 何时加载 | 进链? | 预置代码 |\n| 内置后端 _bing/_ddg | 开发者, 编译进进程 | 恒在 | ✅ | ~30行/家 |\n| skill+脚本 | 开发者, 随 skill 发布 | 相关时(渐进披露) | ❌ 独立调用 | 0行核心, 脚本即数据 |\n| MCP 层 | 通用引擎 + 操作者记录 | 声明后 | ✅ 链尾 | ~465行 |\n| 即席 shell | 模型现场 | 需要时 | ❌ | 0 |\n\nActually skill+script: 脚本可以被 web_search 进链吗? No — unless code calls it. Right.\n\nLet me do the quick verification reads first (SKILL.md of readme-crafter to see how scripts are referenced). Then write the final answer. This is the capstone — make it excellent."}
