{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789869428.6010318, "id": "bec0049678c5", "content": "这个问题最初就是这场对话的开场——现在带着全部积累回来看，正好把它作为终局答案。让我把执行路径的三个核心文件读出来（base.py = 工具的数据结构，loop.py = 循环，registry.py = 注册与包装）：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_3421e1351e7f42d69c1a9bcb", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/base.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_36126e5b701345f7ab20c5c0", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/loop.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user asks: \"现在工具的调用和执行是什么逻辑？\" (What is the current logic of tool invocation and execution?)\n\nWait — this was ALSO the original task at the very start of the conversation (before compaction): \"现在工具的调用和执行是什么逻辑？\" was the first task in this conversation. Hmm, actually looking back at the very first human turn: \"Task: 现在工具的调用和执行是什么逻辑？\" — yes, that was the original task. Then the conversation drifted into MCP config, skills, bing/ddg, everything-is-shell... and now the user returns to the original question with all this context built up.\n\nHmm, but wait. Let me re-read the conversation. The first message after the summary was \"Task: 现在工具的调用和执行是什么逻辑？\" and my response (per the summary) was about fixing the two root causes (presets file location, notedb rename). That seems odd — actually the compaction summary said the task led to finding two root causes about the MCP test failures. So the original question \"现在工具的调用和执行是什么逻辑\" was probably asked in the context of debugging the MCP backend test failures, and the answer involved investigating how tools are invoked/executed (the registry, the loop, etc.).\n\nNow, at the end of this long conversation about architecture, the user asks again: \"现在工具的调用和执行是什么逻辑？\" — they want the full picture of how tool invocation and execution works in Clutch, now grounded in everything we've discussed: the registry, the LLM loop, tool schema rendering, dispatch, execution, permission gating, error-as-data, events.\n\nThis is a \"explain the current implementation\" question. I should investigate the actual code path:\n\n1. **Definition/registration**: `agent/tools/registry.py` — `build_default_tools(config)` builds the Tool objects (name, description, parameters schema, func). I've seen the tool names list. Tools are dataclasses with func.\n\n2. **Schema exposure to the LLM**: How do tools get rendered into the API request? The LLM layer (agent/llm/) — OpenAI-compatible tools parameter. Need to check agent/loop.py and agent/llm/.\n\n3. **The loop**: agent/loop.py (13487 bytes) — the agent loop: LLM responds with tool_calls → dispatch → execute → append tool results as messages → loop until final text. Events emitted (events.py).\n\n4. **Dispatch**: how tool_calls map to Tool.func — argument validation, workspace injection (func signatures have workspace, config params — I saw `_load_skill(_workspace, config, name, file)`).\n\n5. **Execution context**: workspace as cwd boundary, config injection, cancel/event sink.\n\n6. **Special cases**: run_command permission gating (chat mode classifier in shell.py / localshell.py), error-as-data convention ({\"content\": ..., \"error\": True}), parallel tool calls?\n\n7. **Events/UI**: events.py — tool_call/tool_result events streamed to UI.\n\nI haven't actually read loop.py or base.py (Tool definition) yet in this session. I've read registry.py partially (grep only). To answer accurately, I should read:\n- agent/base.py (8118 bytes — probably Tool dataclass, Agent abstract)\n- agent/loop.py (13487 bytes — the loop)\n- parts of registry.py (build_default_tools structure, _ToolFunc signature)\n- maybe agent/core/ directory (what's in there?)\n\nLet me read base.py and loop.py fully, and skim registry.py's top (build_default_tools + Tool building + _run wrapper). Also events.py briefly for the event flow. That's a fair amount of reading but this is the capstone \"explain the system\" question — worth it.\n\nLet me read base.py, loop.py, and the first ~130 lines of registry.py in parallel.\n\nThen compose the answer as a complete walkthrough:\n\n**The full lifecycle of a tool call in Clutch:**\n\n1. **Startup (registration)**: `build_default_tools(config)` — a static list of tool factories; each factory checks config gates (e.g., write_file only in work mode? No — write_file exists but permission mode governs; enable_skills gates load_skill; availability filters like tavily key). Returns list[Tool]. Tool = name + description (+ rendered prompt with dynamic $backends) + JSON schema + callable.\n\n2. **Schema → LLM**: loop sends tools=[{name, description, parameters}] in the OpenAI-compatible API call. The descriptions are dynamically rendered (web_search description includes only real backends — the zero-footprint principle).\n\n3. **Model responds with tool_calls**: assistant message contains one or more tool_call objects (id, name, arguments-JSON-string).\n\n4. **Dispatch**: loop looks up tool by name; unknown → error-as-data message back (so model can recover); arguments parsed from JSON string (parse failure → error-as-data).\n\n5. **Execution**: call the func with injected dependencies — signature convention: (workspace, config, ...params). The Tool dataclass probably has a `_run` wrapper that injects workspace/config and validates required params. Everything happens inside the workspace boundary; run_command additionally passes through the permission gate (chat-mode classifier / work-mode full), which is in shell.py or localshell.py.\n\n6. **Result convention**: every tool returns {\"content\": str, \"error\": bool, ...} — error-as-data: failures are strings the model reads, never exceptions that crash the loop. Timeout/cancel honored.\n\n7. **Feedback**: result appended as role=tool message with tool_call_id; events emitted (tool_call started, tool_result) streamed to UI; loop continues — LLM sees results, may call more tools (max iterations guard?) or answer.\n\n8. **The model is the control flow**: no hardcoded orchestration — the loop is just a while that shuttles messages; the MODEL decides what to call, in what order, when to stop. Tools are the instruction set; the loop is the clock.\n\nAlso worth connecting to the whole conversation arc:\n- This is the \"CPU + instruction set\" frame from the previous turn: registry = instruction set ROM, loop = clock, model = control unit.\n- Dynamic rendering (web_search backends) = how the environment configures the instruction set without changing code.\n- The permission gate = how the OS mediates privileged instructions.\n- error-as-data = exceptions become data because the \"CPU\" (model) can handle them.\n- The parallel to MCP: this internal dispatch IS what MCP standardizes externally — same shape (name, arguments JSON, content result).\n\nLet me read the files now to get the details right. I'll read base.py and loop.py fully, plus registry.py lines 1-60ish and around 130 (run_command build) to see the wrapper mechanics."}
