{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789870369.5783982, "id": "e7743aea32c1", "tool_call_id": "call_c091daf6957144b09eea45e3", "content": "60: \n61: \n62: def _drain(pipe, sink: list[bytes]) -> None:\n63:     \"\"\"Reader thread body: append everything a child pipe emits until EOF.\"\"\"\n64:     try:\n65:         for chunk in iter(lambda: pipe.read(65536), b\"\"):\n66:             sink.append(chunk)\n67:     except (OSError, ValueError):\n68:         pass  # closed under us by the tree kill\n69:     finally:\n70:         try:\n71:             pipe.close()\n72:         except OSError:\n73:             pass\n74: \n75: \n76: def _kill_tree(proc: subprocess.Popen) -> None:\n77:     \"\"\"Kill the whole child tree, not just the direct child: a Stop must not\n78:     leave the command's grandchildren running (the local twin of the\n79:     supervisor's kill guarantee). POSIX: the child got its own process group\n80:     (start_new_session), so a group SIGKILL is exact. Windows: ``taskkill /T``\n81:     walks the tree; plain TerminateProcess (proc.kill) would only reap the\n82:     shell and orphan whatever it spawned.\"\"\"\n83:     if os.name == \"posix\":\n84:         try:\n85:             os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGKILL)\n86:             return\n87:         except OSError:\n88:             pass  # already gone or no group: fall through to the direct kill\n89:     else:\n90:         try:\n91:             r = subprocess.run(\n92:                 [\"taskkill\", \"/PID\", str(proc.pid), \"/T\", \"/F\"],\n93:                 capture_output=True,\n94:                 timeout=10,\n95:             )\n96:             if r.returncode == 0:\n97:                 return\n98:         except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):\n99:             pass  # taskkill missing/denied: still drop the direct child\n100:     try:\n101:         proc.kill()\n102:     except OSError:\n103:         pass  # already dead\n104: \n105: \n106: class LocalTransport(Transport):\n107:     \"\"\"Run commands via subprocess in a fixed cwd (the workspace root).\n108: \n109:     The shell comes from local_shell(): a POSIX host's /bin/sh via shell=True;\n110:     on Windows a detected Git Bash via `bash -c <cmd>` (the agent speaks POSIX)\n111:     or, absent any bash, cmd.exe via shell=True — see localshell's module\n112:     docstring. Text mode is pinned to utf-8/replace: a Chinese Windows box\n113:     would otherwise decode command output as GBK and hand the model mojibake.\n114:     \"\"\"\n115: \n116:     def __init__(self, cwd: str) -> None:\n117:         self.cwd = cwd\n118: \n119:     def run(\n120:         self, command: str, timeout: float, *, binary: bool = False, cancel: threading.Event | None = None\n121:     ) -> CommandResult:\n122:         shell = local_shell()\n123:         try:\n124:             proc = subprocess.Popen(\n125:                 [*shell.argv, command] if shell.argv else command,\n126:                 shell=shell.argv is None,\n127:                 cwd=self.cwd,\n128:                 stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n129:                 stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n130:                 # own session/process group on POSIX: the tree kill below then\n131:                 # cannot take the session server (a group mate) down with it\n132:                 start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\",\n133:             )\n134:         except OSError as e:\n... (showing lines 60-134 of 242; use offset=135 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789870461.1883368, "id": "a373668a314a", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_8163232a0b8b46a48d1a8521", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"def protect|is_protected|class Project|path: Path|path: str\",\"path\":\"agent/tools/workspace.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_c782530276094508b9ea97cf", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"class Project|workdir|memories\",\"include\":\"*.py\",\"path\":\"agent\"}"}], "reasoning": "I now have everything: `agent/tools/transport.py` already has `_kill_tree(proc)` (L76) — the registry subprocess branch can reuse it directly (it's in the same package). The LocalTransport.run pattern (Popen + drain threads + poll loop) is the template.\n\nNow write the answer: plain language, file-by-file, concrete. No invented jargon. Explain each file's role in one sentence since the user doesn't know all details. Structure:\n\n- 先说涉及的文件总览（一个清单表：哪个文件、干什么用的、改什么）\n- 然后逐个给出具体改动（含代码骨架）\n- 最后测试与验证步骤\n- 说明哪些文件明确不用改（loop.py / base.py / permission）以及为什么（各一句）\n\nOrder of implementation. Keep the tone neutral, no opinions, no \"这个方案很优雅\" 之类的感想。\n\nDetails to nail down:\n\n**文件 1：`agent/tools/cli.py`（新建）**\n作用：命令行入口，独立进程里执行一个工具函数。\n- stdin 读一个 JSON 对象：`{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"args\": {...}, \"workspace_root\": \"...\", \"mode\": \"work\", \"read_max_chars\": 20000}`\n- 构建 `Workspace(workspace_root)` 和 `Config(mode=..., read_max_chars=...)`\n- 从 `build_default_tools(config)` 里按 name 找到 Tool，调 `tool.func(workspace, config, **args)`\n- stdout 打印返回 dict 的 JSON（UTF-8 reconfigure）\n- 错误也走 JSON：CLI 自身异常 → `{\"content\": \"...\", \"error\": true}` 打印后 exit 1（但只要 stdout 是合法 JSON，父进程就以它为准）\n\n具体代码骨架给出。注意 memories 参数传 None（memory 工具不在子进程重建——它们不迁移，留在进程内，所以列表里没有也没关系；但如果 name 是 save_memory，CLI 返回 unknown tool —— 在方案里写明：这 3 个 memory 工具不迁移）。\n\n还有个细节：cli.py 里 build_default_tools 会渲染描述（读 prompts/*.md），无害但多余。可以接受，保持简单（复用同一张表，避免维护第二份 name→函数映射）。这是取舍，一句话说清楚即可，不算黑话。\n\nWindows 细节：stdin/stdout 都 reconfigure 为 utf-8（父进程写 stdin 用 json.dumps(..., ensure_ascii=False).encode(\"utf-8\") 或直接 text=True + encoding=\"utf-8\"）。\n\n**文件 2：`agent/tools/registry.py`（修改，3 处）**\n作用：工具注册表，一份 Tool 定义同时生成给模型的 JSON Schema 和本地执行入口；`execute()` 是现在唯一真正调用工具函数的地方。\n- 改动 2.1：`Tool` dataclass（L26-31）加一个字段 `subprocess: bool = False`（默认 False = 现状不变）\n- 改动 2.2：`execute()`（L337 起）在 `_coerce_types` 之后加分支：`if tool.subprocess: result = self._exec_subprocess(...)`\n- 改动 2.3：新增私有方法 `_exec_subprocess(config, name, args, cancel)`：\n  - payload = {\"name\": name, \"args\": args, \"workspace_root\": str(workspace.root), \"mode\": config.mode, \"read_max_chars\": config.read_max_chars}\n  - Popen([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"], cwd=仓库根（Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]）, stdin=PIPE, stdout=PIPE, stderr=PIPE)\n  - 写 stdin（UTF-8 JSON），关闭 stdin\n  - 等待：主线程循环 `proc.wait(timeout=...)`？cancel 轮询需要另一个线程或 proc.wait(0.1) 循环。参照 LocalTransport.run 的做法：wait 循环每 0.1s 醒一次，检查 cancel；cancel set → `_kill_tree(proc)`（从 .transport 导入，现成的 L76）\n  - 超时 config.command_timeout？不对——read_file 一般 <1s，但 web_search 是 15s 超时。exec 工具的超时值：默认 command_timeout=30s 够覆盖 read/grep；对 web 工具以后迁移时按需传。先给 30s 兜底 + cancel 杀。\n  - stdout 按 UTF-8 解码，json.loads；解析失败 → {\"content\": stderr 尾部或 \"subprocess crashed\", \"error\": True}\n  - 返回解析出的 dict\n- 说明：`_cancelable` 的 inspect.signature 不受影响（func 字段还在，cancel 对 subprocess 工具由 _exec_subprocess 自己消费）\n\n**文件 3：`agent/tools/registry.py` 里 read_file 的 Tool 定义（试点）**\n- build_default_tools 里 read_file 那个 Tool(...) 调用加 `subprocess=True` 一个参数，别的都不动。filesystem.py 本身零改动。\n\n**文件 4：`tests/toolcli_test.py`（新建）**\n作用：自运行测试模块（项目测试都是 `python -m tests.X` 形式）。\n- 用例 1：直接子进程跑 `python -m agent.tools.cli`，stdin 喂 read_file 请求（指向一个临时文件），断言 stdout JSON 有 content 且无 error\n- 用例 2：喂不存在的文件，断言 error=true、content 含错误信息\n- 用例 3：中文路径/中文内容 round-trip（编码回归）\n- 用例 4：构造 ToolRegistry，含一个 subprocess=True 的 Tool，调 execute，断言结果与直接 func 调用一致\n- 用例 5：cancel —— 起一个慢函数的工具（exec 模式下其实就是 CLI 假装慢？）——不好造。更直接：用一个假 payload 让 CLI sleep？CLI 没有慢工具。可以在测试里给 CLI 喂一个不存在的工具名前先测 kill？更简单的做法：测试里直接测 `_exec_subprocess`，用一个 name=\"nonexistent\"，或者用 grep 大目录……诚实说：加一个 `--selftest-sleep` 之类的测试钩子？不好。\n  另一种做法：CLI 支持一个仅测试用的 name `\"sleep_test\"`？也是 hack。\n  干脆：用例 5 改为「喂未知工具名，断言 CLI 输出 unknown-tool JSON，exit 码非 0 但 stdout 可解析」；cancel 的树杀逻辑 _kill_tree 已经被 transport 的现有测试覆盖（tests.local_shell_test 有 stop 测试吗？不确定，别断言）。cancel 路径在 _exec_subprocess 里就是「轮询 + _kill_tree」，_kill_tree 是现成已验证的代码，可以在测试里用 monkeypatch/或直接测：Popen 一个 `sleep` 的 python 子进程… 但 run_command whitelist——tests 是自运行模块，测试代码内容不受 chat mode 限制（这是计划，不是我现在写文件）。\n  实际上简单方案：`tests/toolcli_test.py` 里用 threading 起 `_exec_subprocess`（工具名 read_file 指向大文件不够慢）……\n  最直接：CLI 加一个隐藏约定——payload 里 `\"args\": {\"_sleep\": 5}` 不行，会透传给函数报 TypeError。\n  用例 5 最终方案：在测试里注册一个假工具：`Tool(name=\"slow\", ..., subprocess=True, func=lambda...)`，CLI 那边没有 slow → 不行，CLI 用的是 build_default_tools。\n  好吧，最实用：CLI 对未知工具返回 JSON 即可测分发与错误路径；cancel 分支的测试用一个真实的慢工具——`web_search`？需要网络。`run_command`？它 func 有 cancel 参数……在 CLI 里跑 run_command(\"sleep 10\")？shell 命令 sleep 在 Windows Git Bash 有。但 run_command 也是 subprocess=True 的候选。测试：payload name=run_command args={\"command\": \"sleep 30\"}，起线程调 execute，主线程 0.5s 后 set cancel，断言总耗时 < 5s 且结果 error。这能真实覆盖「cancel → 杀进程树」。就这么写。\n- 跑法：`.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m tests.toolcli_test`\n\n**文件 5：回归**\n- `.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m tests.selfcheck`\n- `-m tests.local_shell_test`（transport/_kill_tree 被复用，验证没碰坏）\n- `-m tests.server_test`（full 模式）\n\n**明确不动的文件**（各一句为什么）：\n- `agent/loop.py`：_execute_tool（L277）先 parse_arguments 再 gate.require 再 registry.execute——权限检查、事件流、doom-loop 检测都在这层父进程里，子进程化不影响它们\n- `agent/base.py`：build_tools（L149）只是把 build_default_tools 的列表装进 ToolRegistry，列表形状没变\n- `agent/core/permission.py`（PermissionGate）：守门发生在 registry.execute 之前，与执行介质无关\n- `agent/tools/filesystem.py`：函数本身不写，read_file 照旧被调用，只是调用地点从父进程换成子进程\n- 其余 10 个工具：不动，仍然走 func 直调\n\n**已知代价**（一句）：每个 subprocess=True 的工具调用多一次 Python 解释器启动（Windows 上约 0.1-0.3s）；如果以后觉得慢，再考虑常驻子进程，第一版先不做。\n\n关于 cwd/PYTHONPATH：`python -m agent.tools.cli` 要求仓库根在 sys.path。Popen 里显式 cwd=仓库根就够了（python 会把 cwd 加进 sys.path）。注意：`python -m` 会把当前目录插到 sys.path[0]，所以 cwd=repo root 可行。父进程可能以 venv python 运行（.venv/Scripts/python.exe），用 sys.executable 保证同一解释器。\n\n编码：Popen 用 `text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\"` 或手写 bytes；stdin write UTF-8 bytes。CLI 端 `sys.stdin.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")` + `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")`。Windows 上 Python 3.7+ 支持 reconfigure。\n\nstderr：读回来仅用于诊断信息塞进 error content。\n\nMermaid? 用户要的是改哪里的清单，一张小流程图可能有帮助但用户烦我卖弄——不用图，纯清单最直接。保持朴素。\n\nAlso: user said \"不要预先假设我清楚所有的项目细节\" — so each file gets one sentence \"这个文件是干什么的\". Keep sentences factual.\n\nLine numbers: registry.py Tool dataclass L26-31; execute L337+（我读到 execute 定义在 L337 附近——grep said L353-355 func call in old summary; the file I just read shows execute signature; let me count: from the read, `def execute(` appears after schemas/names. I can cite approximate line numbers: Tool dataclass 约 L26，execute 约 L337，func 调用约 L353。From my full read of registry.py (392 lines? The read wasn't truncated — it showed the whole file, ending with _coerce_types). Let me estimate: the file content I saw… I can count roughly but better to cite \"约 L337\" style with \"约\". Actually I have exact content; execute's `result = tool.func(...)` lines are at approximately L353-355 per prior session. I'll cite both.\n\nStructure of answer:\n\n# 改动清单\n先一句总述：共 2 个文件修改 + 2 个文件新建，改动核心是 registry.py 里调用工具函数的那一处加一个分支。\n\n表格：文件 | 作用 | 改动\n然后逐文件详细：\n1. 新建 agent/tools/cli.py —— 内容骨架\n2. 修改 agent/tools/registry.py —— 三处（dataclass 字段 / execute 分支 / 新方法 _exec_subprocess），骨架\n3. 修改 agent/tools/registry.py 的 read_file Tool 定义 —— 一行\n4. 不动的文件及原因（loop.py、base.py、filesystem.py、permission）\n5. 新建 tests/toolcli_test.py —— 用例列表\n6. 验证命令\n\n最后：落这些改动需要 work mode。\n\nWrite in Chinese, plain. Code comments in English (project convention). Let me write the code skeletons carefully.\n\ncli.py skeleton:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Subprocess entry point for tool execution.\n\nParent process:  registry.execute -> spawn [sys.executable, -m agent.tools.cli]\nThis process:    read one JSON object from stdin, call the tool function,\n                 print its {content, error, diff} dict as JSON on stdout.\n\nstdin JSON: {\"name\": str, \"args\": dict, \"workspace_root\": str,\n             \"mode\": str, \"read_max_chars\": int}\nEverything the tool functions actually read from Config must be listed in\nthe payload and passed to Config(...) below.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom ..config import Config\nfrom .registry import build_default_tools\nfrom .workspace import Workspace\n\n\ndef _run(payload: dict) -> dict:\n    name = payload[\"name\"]\n    args = payload[\"args\"]\n    config = Config(\n        mode=payload.get(\"mode\", \"work\"),\n        read_max_chars=payload.get(\"read_max_chars\", 20000),\n    )\n    workspace = Workspace(payload[\"workspace_root\"])\n    tool = {t.name: t for t in build_default_tools(config)}.get(name)\n    if tool is None:\n        return {\"content\": f\"ERROR: unknown tool {name!r}\", \"error\": True}\n    return tool.func(workspace, config, **args)\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    # Chinese paths/content must survive the pipe on a GBK Windows console\n    sys.stdin.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    try:\n        payload = json.loads(sys.stdin.read())\n        result = _run(payload)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 -- report to parent as data\n        result = {\"content\": f\"ERROR: cli crashed: {e}\", \"error\": True}\n    json.dump(result, sys.stdout, ensure_ascii=False)\n    sys.stdout.flush()\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\nWait — Workspace constructor: `Workspace(root: str | None = None, transport=None)`; LocalWorkspace mkdirs. Which class does base use? build_workspace → probably LocalWorkspace. Workspace at L313 is LocalWorkspace. Use `LocalWorkspace(payload[\"workspace_root\"])` — hmm, but Workspace itself: `class Workspace:` abstract? L266 run is on Workspace (abstract base, delegates to transport) and LocalWorkspace L313. read_file calls workspace.resolve / .list / .read — are those concrete on the base? resolve/list/read at L520 are on the remote one... The base class has abstract read/list. So use LocalWorkspace. Let me verify LocalWorkspace.read exists — the base abstract methods read/list are implemented in LocalWorkspace presumably. Yes, LocalWorkspace implements them. I'll write `LocalWorkspace(root)`.\n\nAlso protected paths: read_file checks `workspace.is_protected(p)` — protection is set by `workspace.protect(project.path)` in start_task. In the subprocess the protection set is lost! write_file/edit_file protect .clc etc. For read_file, protection blocks reading protected files (like .clc memory files?). This is a real detail: `protect()` marks paths that tools must not touch. If the subprocess doesn't call protect, read_file could read files the parent would have refused. So the payload must carry the protected paths, or the CLI must re-apply protection. In start_task: `workspace.protect(project.path)` — project.path is the project dir (`.clc`?). project.path — Project dataclass has path presumably pointing at the project metadata dir. Payload should carry `protected: [paths]` and CLI calls `workspace.protect(p)` for each. Let me check what protect does / what project.path is.\n\nLet me grep Project dataclass and protect."}
