{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789871637.8967528, "id": "e1aef00836e9", "content": "这个提醒是对的——上一版骨架里恰好有一个 POSIX 上的真 bug（取消时会把自己也杀掉）。下面把这条子进程链路上每一个跨平台触点过一遍：哪些已经被仓库现有代码解决，哪三处需要修正或补充。\n\n## 1. 已经由“复用现有代码”解决的触点\n\n`transport.py` 本来就是为三端写的（本地 + SSH 远端），方案里明确复用它的两个部件，这些就不用再发明：\n\n| 触点 | 现成机制 | 位置 |\n|------|---------|------|\n| 杀进程树：Windows 用 `taskkill /PID x /T /F`，POSIX 用 `os.killpg` + `SIGKILL` | `_kill_tree` | transport.py L76-103 |\n| 读管道不卡死（字节流、异常吞掉） | `_drain` | transport.py L62-73 |\n| 输出编码钉死 UTF-8（否则中文 Windows 按 GBK 解码出乱码） | LocalTransport 的注释明确写了这一条 | transport.py L111-113 |\n\n`_kill_tree` 是 `run_command` 一直在用的路径，三端行为已被 `tests.local_shell_test` 覆盖——这正是选它而不是重写的原因。\n\n另外设计本身避开了最容易不跨平台的东西：**全程没有 shell**。`Popen` 用 list 参数、`shell=False`，不经过 cmd.exe / bash，所以引号转义、`/tmp` 指向差异（Git Bash 的 /tmp 是 %TEMP% 挂载）、PATH 差异这些统统不涉及。路径只在同一台机器的父子进程之间传递，两端都用各自的 `Path` 原生处理，不需要像远端 transport 那样做 POSIX 路径翻译。\n\n## 2. 必须修正的一处：POSIX 上取消会误杀自己\n\n我上一版骨架的 `Popen` 少了 `start_new_session`。后果在 macOS/Linux 上是致命的：\n\n`_kill_tree` 的 POSIX 分支是 `os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), SIGKILL)`——杀的是**子进程所在的进程组**。如果不给子进程独立进程组，它的 pgid 就等于父进程（agent 自己）的 pgid，用户一点 Stop，整个 agent 一起被 SIGKILL。\n\ntransport.py L130-132 的注释就是为这个坑写的：\n\n```python\n# own session/process group on POSIX: the tree kill below then\n# cannot take the session server (a group mate) down with it\nstart_new_session=os.name == \"posix\",\n```\n\n修正后的 Popen（Windows 侧不受影响，`taskkill /T` 按 PID 走树，不需要进程组）：\n\n```python\n        proc = subprocess.Popen(\n            [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"],\n            cwd=str(repo_root),\n            stdin=subprocess.PIPE,\n            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n            start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\",\n        )\n```\n\n## 3. 其余触点逐项核对\n\n**编码（三端都要钉死，不能依赖默认值）**\n- 父进程：管道全部用二进制（不给 Popen 传 `text=True`），`payload.encode(\"utf-8\")` 写入，stdout/stderr 收到 bytes 后显式 `decode(\"utf-8\")`。骨架已是这么写的。\n- 子进程：`sys.stdin/stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")`。这一步在三个平台上都必要：Windows 中文系统默认 GBK，西文系统 cp1252；macOS/Linux 在 C locale 下默认 ASCII（Python 3.7+ 的 PEP 538 只是缓解）。显式指定才是确定性行为。\n- 更稳妥的变体：子进程直接读写 `sys.stdin.buffer` / `sys.stdout.buffer`，完全绕开文本层。两者等价，任选。\n\n**换行符（Windows 特有的坑）**\n- 正因为不用 `text=True`，Windows 的文本层不会做 `\\n` ↔ `os.linesep` 翻译。`content` 里的 `\\r\\n` 在 JSON 里被转义成 `\\r\\n` 字面量，round-trip 逐字节保真。如果哪一步用了 `text=True` 且 `newline` 默认值，Windows 写管道时会把 `\\n` 变成 `\\r\\n`，悄悄改坏文件内容——这是要在代码评审里盯死的一条。\n\n**参数传递**\n- stdin JSON 绕开了 Windows 命令行 32K 上限和 argv 的 GBK 编码问题（本会话实测踩过后者）；macOS/Linux 的 argv 虽然没这些问题，统一走 stdin 就没有分叉。\n- `Popen` 的 list 形式 + 项目路径含中文（`...\\临时文件\\Clutch`）也安全：不经过 shell，Windows 走 `CreateProcessW` 宽字符 API。\n\n**解释器与导入定位**\n- `sys.executable` 在三端的 venv 里都指向正确解释器（Windows `.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe`，POSIX `.venv/bin/python`）。\n- `python -m` 把 `cwd` 插进 `sys.path[0]`，`cwd=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]` 指向仓库根，三端一致。\n\n**环境变量**\n- `Config` 的 `CLUTCH_*` 默认值靠环境继承，`Popen` 不传 `env` 即自动继承，三端行为一致。载荷本身不走环境变量。\n\n**子进程秒死（如 import 失败）**\n- 父进程写 stdin 会拿到 BrokenPipeError——三端异常类不同（POSIX `EPIPE`、Windows `ConnectionAbortedError`/`EINVAL`），但都被 `except OSError` 覆盖（server.py L55-60 的注释就是这个结论）。骨架里 `except OSError: pass` + 随后 `wait()` + stderr 兜底，已覆盖。\n\n## 4. 一个部署边界要写明：frozen（PyInstaller）构建\n\nsupervisor.py 里出现了 \"venv/onefile\"（L231），说明项目有打包成单文件可执行的发布形态。frozen 模式下 `sys.executable` 指向 bundle 本体而不是 python，`[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"]` 会把应用自己再启动一遍——错。\n\n处理方式二选一，都便宜：\n- **策略上**：frozen 构建不给任何工具设 `subprocess=True`（字段默认 False，迁移本来就是逐工具 opt-in 的，零代码成本）；\n- **代码上**：`_exec_subprocess` 开头加 `if getattr(sys, \"frozen\", False): return tool.func(workspace, config, **args)` 作为兜底。\n\n建议选代码兜底，防手滑。\n\n## 5. 测试里的一处三端兼容修正\n\n之前取消测试用例写的 `run_command(\"sleep 30\")` 依赖 shell 里有 `sleep`——POSIX 有，Windows 只有装了 Git Bash 才有（裸 cmd.exe 没有）。改成不依赖 shell 命令的写法：\n\n```\npython -c \"import time; time.sleep(30)\"\n```\n\n（用 `sys.executable` 拼进命令串。）三个平台都有这个解释器，测试就不再挑环境。\n\n## 6. 三端矩阵（改后状态）\n\n| 关注点 | Windows | macOS | Linux |\n|--------|---------|-------|-------|\n| 启动方式 | `python.exe -m agent.tools.cli`，CreateProcessW，list 参数无 shell | 同左（`/bin` 系列） | 同左 |\n| 编码 | 管道二进制 + 显式 UTF-8，与 GBK 默认值无关 | 同左（防 C locale ASCII） | 同左 |\n| 换行 | 无文本层翻译，`\\r\\n` 原样进 JSON | 原样 | 原样 |\n| Stop/超时 | `taskkill /T /F` 杀整棵树 | `start_new_session` + `killpg`，只杀 CLI 组 | 同 macOS |\n| 中文/长参数 | stdin JSON，绕开 32K 与 GBK | stdin JSON | stdin JSON |\n| 导入 | cwd=仓库根，`sys.path[0]` 生效 | 同左 | 同左 |\n| 已知例外 | frozen 构建：`sys.executable` 非 python → 走进程内兜底 | 同左 | 同左 |\n\n## 7. 验证方式\n\n- 本机（Windows）跑 `.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m tests.toolcli_test` + `local_shell_test` + `selfcheck`；\n- `toolcli_test` 里所有子进程调用统一用 `sys.executable` 和 UTF-8 bytes，测试代码本身不写任何 `os.name` 分支——这样同一份测试拿到 mac/linux 上就能直接跑，三端差异全部压在 `_kill_tree`/`start_new_session` 这两个已验证点里；\n- 有条件的话在 CI 加 mac/linux 两个 job 跑同一套测试（项目现在似乎只有 Windows 环境验证过，这点我如实说明：本会话只能在 Windows 上实测）。\n\n总结：方案的跨平台性基本是“站在 transport.py 已验证的三端机制上”，真正要动的是上面三处——补 `start_new_session`（修自杀 bug）、frozen 兜底、测试命令去 shell 化。这三处我会在 work mode 落地时一并写进 `registry.py` / `toolcli_test.py`。", "tool_calls": [], "reasoning": "The user repeats the earlier point: \"你还要注意这个实现的跨平台性，它需要能够在win/mac/linux三端可以运行\" — \"You also need to pay attention to the cross-platform nature of this implementation; it needs to run on win/mac/linux.\"\n\nThis is the same message as the original task. So they want me to analyze the subprocess-based tool execution refactor plan specifically for cross-platform correctness on Windows/macOS/Linux.\n\nLet me think carefully about every cross-platform touchpoint in the proposed design (`agent/tools/cli.py` + `_exec_subprocess` in registry.py) and verify each against the actual codebase facts I've read:\n\n1. **Interpreter invocation: `[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"]`**\n   - `sys.executable` is the same interpreter running the parent — works on all three platforms. On Windows: `.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe`; POSIX: `.venv/bin/python`. sys.executable resolves correctly.\n   - Edge case: PyInstaller frozen apps — `sys.executable` points to the bundle executable, not python. The codebase already has a note: shell.py L316 \"compile() in-process: no subprocess, works under PyInstaller too\". And supervisor.py spawns processes with special handling (onefile, Job objects). So if the app is frozen (PyInstaller), `sys.executable -m agent.tools.cli` would re-launch the bundle, not python — broken. Is that a real deployment mode for the tool path? The supervisor has \"venv/onefile\" mentions (L231: \"the kernel terminates the whole session tree — the venv/onefile\"). So the project does ship as a frozen binary at least in some form (the supervisor). A robust guard: check `getattr(sys, \"frozen\", False)` and handle, or fall back to in-process func if no python found. Worth mentioning as a decision point: either (a) require a python and document it, or (b) add a frozen fallback to in-process execution. Honest answer: for the first version, spawn via sys.executable works in dev/venv on all 3 OSes; the frozen case needs a fallback branch.\n\n2. **cwd for `-m` import**: `cwd=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]`\n   - `python -m` inserts cwd (or rather, with `-m`, sys.path[0] = cwd) — yes, `python -m` adds the current working directory to the front of sys.path. Works on all platforms. Path handling via pathlib is cross-platform.\n   - Alternative: env PYTHONPATH — messier on Windows (env var inheritance is fine but cwd is simpler).\n   - Note: spaces in path (e.g., `临时文件` — the project path has non-ASCII!). `C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\临时文件\\Clutch` — the repo path contains Chinese characters. Popen with a list argument handles spaces/Unicode fine on all 3 platforms (no shell involved). cwd as a Unicode path: on Windows, CreateProcessW handles it; Python 3 uses wide APIs. This was already proven in this session: we ran `python -m tests.X` from that path. Also `python -m` with cwd containing Chinese — sys.path[0] becomes that; imports work. Fine. But worth stating explicitly since the user's project path is literally non-ASCII.\n   - macOS edge: `sys.executable` in a venv is the venv python; fine. On macOS with framework Python, no issue for subprocess.\n\n3. **stdin/stdout encoding**: \n   - CLI side: `sys.stdin.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")`, `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")`. \n   - Cross-platform fact: Windows console default is GBK (cp936) on Chinese systems, cp1252 on Western; macOS/Linux default UTF-8 but can be POSIX/C locale → ASCII (PEP 538/540 mitigate; Python 3.7+ enables UTF-8 mode automatically in C/POSIX locale via PEP 538 coercion). Explicit reconfigure makes it deterministic everywhere. This is exactly why transport.py pins \"utf-8/replace\" for its own pipes (L112-113 comment: \"Text mode is pinned to utf-8/replace: a Chinese Windows box would otherwise decode command output as GBK\").\n   - Important subtlety: `sys.stdin.reconfigure` affects the *text* layer. But if the locale is weird, `sys.stdin` itself exists but reconfigure(encoding=) is available Python 3.7+. All three platforms fine.\n   - Even more robust: read `sys.stdin.buffer` and decode explicitly, write to `sys.stdout.buffer`. That bypasses the text layer entirely. Could mention as the bulletproof variant.\n   - Popen side: spawn with default (binary) pipes; parent writes `.encode(\"utf-8\")` bytes, reads bytes, decodes explicitly. That's already in my skeleton. Good — no reliance on locale anywhere.\n\n4. **Line endings**: \n   - `content` may contain `\\r\\n` on Windows files. JSON escapes `\\r\\n` as `\\\\r\\\\n` — safe round-trip on all platforms. No text-mode translation because we use binary pipes (Popen without text=True → no universal newlines translation). If we used text=True on both ends, Windows could translate `\\n`→`\\r\\n` on write or `\\r\\n`→`\\n` on read (Python's text mode does universal newline decoding on read; on write, newline=None translates `\\n` to os.linesep? No — for writing, default newline=None translates '\\n' to os.linesep. That WOULD corrupt content on Windows!). So the design must use binary pipes (no `text=True` in Popen) — my skeleton already does this implicitly (stdout=PIPE without text mode). Worth calling out explicitly as a Windows-specific trap: don't use text=True.\n   - Wait, actually in my skeleton I wrote stdin bytes: `proc.stdin.write(payload)` where payload is bytes — that requires binary stdin (no text=True). Consistent. And `_drain` from transport.py reads bytes (`pipe.read(65536)` returning bytes). Good.\n   - Git autocrlf: files on disk may have CRLF; read returns them; JSON round-trips exactly. The existing in-process path has the same behavior, so no change.\n\n5. **Kill/cancel: `_kill_tree`** (transport.py L76-103):\n   - POSIX: child started with `start_new_session=True` → `os.killpg(os.getpgid(pid), SIGKILL)`.\n   - Windows: `taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F`.\n   - My skeleton's Popen call did NOT include `start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\"`. That matters! Without it, on POSIX the CLI process shares the parent's process group, and `_kill_tree`'s `os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid))` would... getpgid(proc.pid) returns the parent's group → killpg would SIGKILL the ENTIRE app including the agent itself! That's a real cross-platform bug in my skeleton as written. Must add `start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\"` to the Popen call. Critical catch — I should acknowledge and correct this explicitly. This is exactly the kind of platform detail the user is asking about.\n   - Windows side: taskkill exists on all Windows (System32), used already by transport. On macOS/Linux: killpg works with start_new_session. `_kill_tree` is already battle-tested (it's what run_command uses). \n   - `signal.SIGKILL` — POSIX only, but inside `if os.name == \"posix\"` branch. Fine.\n   - Note: on Windows, `proc.wait(timeout=0.1)` loop + taskkill — fine.\n\n6. **`sys.stdout.reconfigure` on the CLI side when stdout is a pipe**: works. On Windows there's no /dev/null weirdness. Also the CLI writes JSON with `ensure_ascii=False` after reconfigure to utf-8 — deterministic.\n\n7. **Paths in payload**: `workspace_root` as absolute str. Child rebuilds `LocalWorkspace(payload[\"workspace_root\"])`. Path flavor differences (Windows backslash vs POSIX slash) don't cross the boundary because each side uses its own Path natively — the string is produced and consumed on the SAME machine/OS. The subprocess never crosses machines, so no path-flavor translation needed. That's worth stating: this design only ever crosses a process boundary, not an OS boundary, unlike the remote transport which does shlex quoting.\n   - Non-ASCII workspace path on Windows: passed via JSON (UTF-8), not env/argv — immune to the GBK argv issues. cwd though is passed as a Python str to Popen → wide-char CreateProcessW, fine.\n\n8. **Protected paths**: `workspace.protected()` returns absolute Path set; serialized `[str(p), ...]` UTF-8 JSON. Same-machine round trip. Fine.\n\n9. **`tempfile.gettempdir()` scratch dirs** — not used by cli path. N/A.\n\n10. **Process startup cost differences**: Windows CreateProcess ~50-150ms; POSIX fork+exec faster but Python interpreter startup dominates (~30-80ms mac, ~20-50ms linux, ~100-300ms Windows). Just a perf note, not correctness.\n\n11. **`-m agent.tools.cli` and module side effects**: agent/__init__.py, agent/tools/__init__.py — do they import heavy stuff? cli.py imports `..config`, `.registry`, `.workspace` — registry imports filesystem, shell, websearch, prompts, skills... These imports run in the child on all platforms identically. One platform-specific gotcha: `skills` or `websearch` might read env vars at import (Config uses env at dataclass field default_factory — env inheritance from parent is automatic on all platforms). Fine.\n\n12. **Exit codes**: CLI returns 0 always (result as data, even errors) — parent treats stdout JSON as authoritative, not exit code. Cross-platform: Windows exit codes are 32-bit ints, POSIX 0-255; since we don't rely on codes except \"crashed\" detection, no issue. My skeleton returns 0 even on error dict — deliberate: exit code only distinguishes \"crashed before printing JSON\" (SystemExit(main()) → 0; unhandled crash would be nonzero via interpreter). Actually if json.dump itself fails (broken pipe), traceback → exit 1. Parent handles parse failure. Fine.\n\n13. **Zombie/orphan processes**: if the PARENT dies while child runs: child is orphaned. On POSIX with start_new_session, no SIGHUP delivery (new session detached from terminal)... parent death doesn't kill child. Edge case: crashed parent leaves a CLI child holding stdin (already EOF'd) — child finishes its tool and writes JSON to a broken pipe → BrokenPipeError → caught by generic handler? In my skeleton, json.dump to closed stdout would raise → traceback, exit 1, child dies. OK. The supervisor's Job-object guarantee (Windows) applies to the server child, not our tool children — out of scope but could mention nothing leaks in practice since children are short-lived. Keep brief.\n\n14. **`os.name` vs `sys.platform`**: use os.name like the codebase does (transport.py L83, L132). Consistent.\n\n15. **Testing on all three**: the test suite uses `sys.executable` too (test 1 in toolcli_test). The project's tests are run on Windows here; mac/linux CI unknown. Just recommend the tests themselves be written platform-neutrally (use `sleep` command? No — test 5 used run_command \"sleep 30\" which needs Git Bash on Windows... but run_command via local_shell() already abstracts: on POSIX /bin/sh has sleep; on Windows Git Bash has sleep; if neither bash nor... cmd.exe lacks sleep! The shell guard notes \"Commands that hang in a non-TTY pipe\" — blocked_prefixes. `sleep` in cmd.exe is not a command (there's `timeout`). The existing localshell detects Git Bash on Windows; the repo's dev environment has Git Bash. Test 5 with \"sleep 30\" would fail on a bash-less Windows box. Alternative: use a Python one-liner `python -c \"import time; time.sleep(30)\"`? But blocked_prefixes... no, that's for interactive commands. Actually run_command's whitelist in chat mode isn't relevant (work mode). Simpler: make the cancel test not go through run_command but through a dedicated slow tool? For the test plan, safer: use `[sys.executable, \"-c\", ...]`-equivalent — but run_command takes a shell string. Could use `python -c \"import time;time.sleep(30)\"` as the command string — works on all 3 platforms since python is on PATH in dev (or use sys.executable's full path interpolated). That's a concrete cross-platform test detail worth fixing in the plan: replace \"sleep 30\" with a python -c sleep so it doesn't depend on Git Bash presence.\n\n16. **macOS specifics**: nothing special beyond the above. Maybe mention Gatekeeper/code-signing irrelevant for `python -m`. Apple Silicon: fine.\n\n17. **One more Windows detail**: `subprocess.Popen` with a list on Windows joins args with list2cmdline — quoting of the module path etc. handled. No shell=True → no cmd.exe involvement → no quoting hell. My skeleton uses a list, shell=False. Good — state it.\n\n18. **`taskkill` output codepage**: irrelevant, we only check returncode.\n\n19. **Unicode in JSON payload vs os.environ**: we deliberately do NOT pass anything through environment variables (except inherited CLUTCH_* for Config) — Windows env vars are UTF-16 internally, Python handles; but our payload doesn't need env at all. Actually Config() in child reads CLUTCH_* env — inherited automatically on all platforms via Popen env=None (inherit). Fine, and values like tavily keys are ASCII-ish; if someone had non-ASCII in env on Windows... os.environ decoding on Windows uses wide API, fine.\n\n20. **The `start_new_session` on POSIX + `_kill_tree` interplay** — the big one, already noted in #5.\n\n21. **What about the CLI being run when repo installed as a package (pip install)?** Project is run from repo (`python -m agent.server` presumably / supervisor). parents[2] is repo root — if installed site-packages, parents[2] would be site-packages — still importable via cwd trick? If installed as package, cwd=site-packages and `-m agent.tools.cli` imports from... sys.path[0]=cwd=site-packages → agent package importable. Works either way actually. But the real deployment is repo checkout. Skip or one line.\n\nSo my answer: walk through each cross-platform touchpoint in the proposed implementation, verify against existing codebase patterns (transport.py already solves most of them), and explicitly CORRECT two things in my earlier skeleton:\n1. Missing `start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\"` — without it, cancel on macOS/Linux would killpg the agent's own process group (suicide bug). \n2. Frozen/PyInstaller caveat: sys.executable isn't python when frozen — the supervisor path has this reality (mentions venv/onefile); fallback needed or documented.\n3. Test 5's \"sleep 30\" → replace with python -c sleep to not depend on Git Bash.\n\nAlso emphasize what's already solved by reusing existing code: `_kill_tree` (POSIX killpg / Windows taskkill), `_drain` (binary pipes), binary Popen (no text=True → no CRLF translation), payload via stdin JSON UTF-8 (no GBK argv issues), no shell=True (no cmd/bash quoting differences).\n\nAnd a summary table: platform risk → mechanism in the design → where it's already proven in the codebase.\n\nStructure:\n1. 开头：结论——方案里所有跨平台点要么复用 transport.py 已验证的机制，要么下面修正 3 处。\n2. 逐项风险清单（编码/引号/换行/杀进程/解释器定位/路径），每项：为什么是问题 → 方案怎么处理 → 代码依据。\n3. 修正我之前骨架里的 1 个真 bug（start_new_session）+ 1 个部署边界（frozen）+ 1 个测试细节（sleep）。\n4. 平台矩阵表。\n5. 建议的验证方式（tests 在三端跑，或至少 CI）。\n\nLength: thorough but not bloated. Use code snippets where precise. Chinese, plain tone.\n\nLet me double-check the start_new_session logic in _kill_tree: \n```python\nif os.name == \"posix\":\n    try:\n        os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGKILL)\n```\ngetpgid(proc.pid) — the PGID of the child. If child was started WITHOUT start_new_session, its pgid = the parent's pgid (unless parent itself is a group leader...). killpg(that) → kills parent (the agent) too. Yes — suicide. With start_new_session=True (setsid), the child becomes session+group leader, pgid == child pid, killpg kills only the CLI tree. transport.py L130-132 comment says exactly this: \"own session/process group on POSIX: the tree kill below then cannot take the session server (a group mate) down with it\". Confirmed.\n\nWindows side without start_new_session: fine, taskkill /T walks by PID tree. But note: on Windows, if CLI spawns its own children (run_command case), taskkill /T handles. Good.\n\nOne more check — CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP in supervisor is for CTRL_BREAK; not needed here since we never send console signals to the CLI (it has no console handler need; it's not interactive). OK.\n\nAlso `proc.stdin.write(payload)` possibly blocking for very large payload while child hasn't started reading? Child starts reading immediately (main→json.loads(sys.stdin.read())). Windows pipe buffer ~4-64KB; for a 1MB write_file payload, parent blocks until child drains — child IS draining, so no deadlock. Both alive, progress guaranteed. On POSIX buffer 64KB, same. Fine — but the drain threads for stdout/stderr are started BEFORE stdin write in my skeleton, so child's stdout writes never block either. Good, keep that ordering point.\n\nEdge: what if child dies instantly (import error)? stdin write gets BrokenPipeError (POSIX EPIPE) / OSError 22 or EINVAL (Windows) — caught by `except OSError: pass`, then wait() returns quickly, stderr shows traceback. Already handled in skeleton. And the comment in server.py L55-60 about EPIPE/ECONNRESET/WSAECONNABORTED variants supports \"catch OSError broadly\" — consistent.\n\nfrozen/PyInstaller: check if there's actually a frozen deployment. supervisor.py L231 mentions \"the venv/onefile\" — suggests PyInstaller onefile mode exists. So the tool-spawn path must handle `getattr(sys, \"frozen\", False)`: in frozen mode, sys.executable = the bundle exe; `[exe, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"]` would re-enter the app — wrong. Options: (a) fall back to in-process func call when frozen; (b) bundle python. Simplest honest recommendation: in frozen builds, keep subprocess=False for all tools (the field defaults False anyway — migration is opt-in per tool, and a frozen build just doesn't set it). That's a one-line policy note: freeze builds don't migrate tools. Or runtime guard: `if getattr(sys, \"frozen\", False): return tool.func(...)`. Either is fine; state it.\n\nNow the matrix:\n\n| 风险点 | Windows | macOS | Linux | 方案处理 | 依据 |\n- 参数传递/长度/中文 → stdin JSON UTF-8 → all fine (本会话实测过 GBK 乱码)\n- 引号/shell 差异 → list Popen, shell=False → 无 shell\n- 换行符 → 二进制管道，JSON 转义 \\r\\n → 无翻译\n- 杀进程树 → taskkill /T /F | killpg → _kill_tree 复用 + start_new_session 修正\n- 解释器 → sys.executable → frozen 例外\n- 导入定位 → cwd=repo root → parents[2]\n- env 继承 → CLUTCH_* 自动继承\n- 临时/scratch 目录差异 → 不涉及（只在同机进程间）\n\nAlso the workspace scratch-dir thing (`/tmp` on Windows is Git Bash's mount) — not touched by CLI design since no shell involved. Can mention: the design deliberately avoids shell semantics entirely, which is why it's *more* portable than run_command.\n\nWrite the answer now. Keep it organized with headers, a table, and the corrected skeleton snippet for the Popen call. Tone: direct, factual."}
{"type": "final", "timestamp": 1789871637.9011898, "id": "a20848d8981d", "status": "completed", "summary": "这个提醒是对的——上一版骨架里恰好有一个 POSIX 上的真 bug（取消时会把自己也杀掉）。下面把这条子进程链路上每一个跨平台触点过一遍：哪些已经被仓库现有代码解决，哪三处需要修正或补充。\n\n## 1. 已经由“复用现有代码”解决的触点\n\n`transport.py` 本来就是为三端写的（本地 + SSH 远端），方案里明确复用它的两个部件，这些就不用再发明：\n\n| 触点 | 现成机制 | 位置 |\n|------|---------|------|\n| 杀进程树：Windows 用 `taskkill /PID x /T /F`，POSIX 用 `os.killpg` + `SIGKILL` | `_kill_tree` | transport.py L76-103 |\n| 读管道不卡死（字节流、异常吞掉） | `_drain` | transport.py L62-73 |\n| 输出编码钉死 UTF-8（否则中文 Windows 按 GBK 解码出乱码） | LocalTransport 的注释明确写了这一条 | transport.py L111-113 |\n\n`_kill_tree` 是 `run_command` 一直在用的路径，三端行为已被 `tests.local_shell_test` 覆盖——这正是选它而不是重写的原因。\n\n另外设计本身避开了最容易不跨平台的东西：**全程没有 shell**。`Popen` 用 list 参数、`shell=False`，不经过 cmd.exe / bash，所以引号转义、`/tmp` 指向差异（Git Bash 的 /tmp 是 %TEMP% 挂载）、PATH 差异这些统统不涉及。路径只在同一台机器的父子进程之间传递，两端都用各自的 `Path` 原生处理，不需要像远端 transport 那样做 POSIX 路径翻译。\n\n## 2. 必须修正的一处：POSIX 上取消会误杀自己\n\n我上一版骨架的 `Popen` 少了 `start_new_session`。后果在 macOS/Linux 上是致命的：\n\n`_kill_tree` 的 POSIX 分支是 `os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), SIGKILL)`——杀的是**子进程所在的进程组**。如果不给子进程独立进程组，它的 pgid 就等于父进程（agent 自己）的 pgid，用户一点 Stop，整个 agent 一起被 SIGKILL。\n\ntransport.py L130-132 的注释就是为这个坑写的：\n\n```python\n# own session/process group on POSIX: the tree kill below then\n# cannot take the session server (a group mate) down with it\nstart_new_session=os.name == \"posix\",\n```\n\n修正后的 Popen（Windows 侧不受影响，`taskkill /T` 按 PID 走树，不需要进程组）：\n\n```python\n        proc = subprocess.Popen(\n            [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"],\n            cwd=str(repo_root),\n            stdin=subprocess.PIPE,\n            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n            start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\",\n        )\n```\n\n## 3. 其余触点逐项核对\n\n**编码（三端都要钉死，不能依赖默认值）**\n- 父进程：管道全部用二进制（不给 Popen 传 `text=True`），`payload.encode(\"utf-8\")` 写入，stdout/stderr 收到 bytes 后显式 `decode(\"utf-8\")`。骨架已是这么写的。\n- 子进程：`sys.stdin/stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")`。这一步在三个平台上都必要：Windows 中文系统默认 GBK，西文系统 cp1252；macOS/Linux 在 C locale 下默认 ASCII（Python 3.7+ 的 PEP 538 只是缓解）。显式指定才是确定性行为。\n- 更稳妥的变体：子进程直接读写 `sys.stdin.buffer` / `sys.stdout.buffer`，完全绕开文本层。两者等价，任选。\n\n**换行符（Windows 特有的坑）**\n- 正因为不用 `text=True`，Windows 的文本层不会做 `\\n` ↔ `os.linesep` 翻译。`content` 里的 `\\r\\n` 在 JSON 里被转义成 `\\r\\n` 字面量，round-trip 逐字节保真。如果哪一步用了 `text=True` 且 `newline` 默认值，Windows 写管道时会把 `\\n` 变成 `\\r\\n`，悄悄改坏文件内容——这是要在代码评审里盯死的一条。\n\n**参数传递**\n- stdin JSON 绕开了 Windows 命令行 32K 上限和 argv 的 GBK 编码问题（本会话实测踩过后者）；macOS/Linux 的 argv 虽然没这些问题，统一走 stdin 就没有分叉。\n- `Popen` 的 list 形式 + 项目路径含中文（`...\\临时文件\\Clutch`）也安全：不经过 shell，Windows 走 `CreateProcessW` 宽字符 API。\n\n**解释器与导入定位**\n- `sys.executable` 在三端的 venv 里都指向正确解释器（Windows `.venv\\Scripts\\python.exe`，POSIX `.venv/bin/python`）。\n- `python -m` 把 `cwd` 插进 `sys.path[0]`，`cwd=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]` 指向仓库根，三端一致。\n\n**环境变量**\n- `Config` 的 `CLUTCH_*` 默认值靠环境继承，`Popen` 不传 `env` 即自动继承，三端行为一致。载荷本身不走环境变量。\n\n**子进程秒死（如 import 失败）**\n- 父进程写 stdin 会拿到 BrokenPipeError——三端异常类不同（POSIX `EPIPE`、Windows `ConnectionAbortedError`/`EINVAL`），但都被 `except OSError` 覆盖（server.py L55-60 的注释就是这个结论）。骨架里 `except OSError: pass` + 随后 `wait()` + stderr 兜底，已覆盖。\n\n## 4. 一个部署边界要写明：frozen（PyInstaller）构建\n\nsupervisor.py 里出现了 \"venv/onefile\"（L231），说明项目有打包成单文件可执行的发布形态。frozen 模式下 `sys.executable` 指向 bundle 本体而不是 python，`[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"]` 会把应用自己再启动一遍——错。\n\n处理方式二选一，都便宜：\n- **策略上**：frozen 构建不给任何工具设 `subprocess=True`（字段默认 False，迁移本来就是逐工具 opt-in 的，零代码成本）；\n- **代码上**：`_exec_subprocess` 开头加 `if getattr(sys, \"frozen\", False): return tool.func(workspace, config, **args)` 作为兜底。\n\n建议选代码兜底，防手滑。\n\n## 5. 测试里的一处三端兼容修正\n\n之前取消测试用例写的 `run_command(\"sleep 30\")` 依赖 shell 里有 `sleep`——POSIX 有，Windows 只有装了 Git Bash 才有（裸 cmd.exe 没有）。改成不依赖 shell 命令的写法：\n\n```\npython -c \"import time; time.sleep(30)\"\n```\n\n（用 `sys.executable` 拼进命令串。）三个平台都有这个解释器，测试就不再挑环境。\n\n## 6. 三端矩阵（改后状态）\n\n| 关注点 | Windows | macOS | Linux |\n|--------|---------|-------|-------|\n| 启动方式 | `python.exe -m agent.tools.cli`，CreateProcessW，list 参数无 shell | 同左（`/bin` 系列） | 同左 |\n| 编码 | 管道二进制 + 显式 UTF-8，与 GBK 默认值无关 | 同左（防 C locale ASCII） | 同左 |\n| 换行 | 无文本层翻译，`\\r\\n` 原样进 JSON | 原样 | 原样 |\n| Stop/超时 | `taskkill /T /F` 杀整棵树 | `start_new_session` + `killpg`，只杀 CLI 组 | 同 macOS |\n| 中文/长参数 | stdin JSON，绕开 32K 与 GBK | stdin JSON | stdin JSON |\n| 导入 | cwd=仓库根，`sys.path[0]` 生效 | 同左 | 同左 |\n| 已知例外 | frozen 构建：`sys.executable` 非 python → 走进程内兜底 | 同左 | 同左 |\n\n## 7. 验证方式\n\n- 本机（Windows）跑 `.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m tests.toolcli_test` + `local_shell_test` + `selfcheck`；\n- `toolcli_test` 里所有子进程调用统一用 `sys.executable` 和 UTF-8 bytes，测试代码本身不写任何 `os.name` 分支——这样同一份测试拿到 mac/linux 上就能直接跑，三端差异全部压在 `_kill_tree`/`start_new_session` 这两个已验证点里；\n- 有条件的话在 CI 加 mac/linux 两个 job 跑同一套测试（项目现在似乎只有 Windows 环境验证过，这点我如实说明：本会话只能在 Windows 上实测）。\n\n总结：方案的跨平台性基本是“站在 transport.py 已验证的三端机制上”，真正要动的是上面三处——补 `start_new_session`（修自杀 bug）、frozen 兜底、测试命令去 shell 化。这三处我会在 work mode 落地时一并写进 `registry.py` / `toolcli_test.py`。"}
