{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789872418.8202884, "id": "0b6a345fcd9c", "content": "开始动手。先把三个还没核实过的细节确认掉（循环导入风险、`_protected` 的初始化、测试的既有写法），然后按清单落地。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_457259b9c59c44e9a65ebf78", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":40,\"offset\":1,\"path\":\"agent/tools/transport.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_79220e72da1b44669053ccaf", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/workspace.py\",\"pattern\":\"^class |self\\\\._protected\"}"}, {"id": "call_3ba780844b52400f9a192afb", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/__init__.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_f0c2c9bd16f14fb28e5f4034", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":60,\"path\":\"tests/local_shell_test.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user says \"开始重构\" (start the refactoring). We're in work mode now (full access). Let me implement the plan:\n\n1. New file `agent/tools/cli.py` — subprocess entry point\n2. Modify `agent/tools/registry.py` — 3 changes:\n   - Tool dataclass: add `subprocess: bool = False`\n   - `execute()`: add branch\n   - New `_exec_subprocess` method\n3. Modify `agent/tools/workspace.py` — add `protected()` method\n4. Pilot migration: read_file gets `subprocess=True`\n5. New `tests/toolcli_test.py`\n6. Run regression tests\n\nLet me be careful about the exact code. First, let me re-check the key files since I've read them already (registry.py fully read this session — it's in context; workspace.py partially; transport.py partially).\n\nKey facts I have in context:\n- registry.py full content (read this session)\n- transport.py `_drain` (L62-73) and `_kill_tree` (L76-103) — read this session\n- workspace.py: `protect` at L147, class Workspace base with `_protected` attribute. I read L143-187 which shows protect/is_protected use `self._protected`. I need to check where `_protected` is initialized — presumably in `__init__` of base Workspace. Let me check that quickly before adding the `protected()` method.\n- loop.py L277-295 `_execute_tool` — read\n- events, context.py — read enough\n\nLet me check workspace.py `__init__` to see `_protected` initialization, and check imports in registry.py (what's already imported: json? subprocess? sys? time? Path?). From my read of registry.py:\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport inspect\nimport threading\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any, Callable\n\nfrom ..config import Config\nfrom ..memory import MemoryStore\nfrom ..prompts import render\nfrom ..skills import cached_library\nfrom . import filesystem, shell, websearch\nfrom .workspace import Workspace\n```\n\nSo I need to add: `json`, `os`, `subprocess`, `sys`, `time`, `Path` (from pathlib), and `_drain`, `_kill_tree` from `.transport`.\n\nWait — careful about circular imports: `agent/tools/registry.py` importing from `.transport` — does transport.py import registry? Let me check transport.py imports. From my earlier read of transport.py L60-134, I didn't see the top of the file. Let me check for circular import risk: transport.py is `agent/tools/transport.py`, and registry is `agent/tools/registry.py`. If transport imports workspace only, no cycle. Let me grep.\n\nAlso `agent/tools/cli.py` imports `..config` (Config), `.registry` (build_default_tools), `.workspace` (LocalWorkspace). registry doesn't import cli (only spawns it as subprocess), so no cycle.\n\nLet me also double check `Workspace.__init__` — need `_protected` set. And `LocalWorkspace.__init__` signature: `def __init__(self, root: str | None = None, transport: Transport | None = None)` and calls `self.root.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` — creates dir if missing. Fine for CLI use with existing root.\n\nHmm wait — for the CLI, should it use `Workspace` base or `LocalWorkspace`? The tools call `workspace.resolve`, `.is_protected`, `.read`, `.list`, `.grep`, `.write` etc. Base Workspace has abstract methods; LocalWorkspace implements. The parent in server mode uses LocalWorkspace (via state.build_workspace). But wait — in eval/remote mode there's a remote workspace (ExecTransport). If the parent's workspace is remote, `workspace.root` is a remote path... spawning a local subprocess CLI with the remote root would read/write LOCAL filesystem, not the remote! That's a real correctness concern.\n\nHmm. Let me think. The refactor plan said: \"子进程需从文件重建 Config/Workspace\". For the first pilot (read_file) with remote workspaces this would be wrong — the subprocess would read the local disk instead of the remote host.\n\nHow to handle: only spawn subprocess when the workspace is local. In `execute()`: `if tool.subprocess and isinstance(workspace, LocalWorkspace)` → subprocess; else fall back to func in-process. That preserves correctness for remote eval runs. Actually `Workspace` base vs LocalWorkspace — check class names: L313 `class LocalWorkspace(Workspace)`. There's presumably a RemoteWorkspace too (L509 run method was on some other class — the one with `_transport.run`, `read_range` with base64 comments at L520-547... that's probably `RemoteWorkspace`). Let me check class names in workspace.py.\n\nAlternatively, safer gate: subprocess only if `type(workspace) is LocalWorkspace` or `isinstance(workspace, LocalWorkspace)`. I'll use isinstance check and fall back to in-process func for remote workspaces — with a comment.\n\nActually wait — is there another workspace type? Let me grep `class.*Workspace` in workspace.py.\n\nAlso — one more design check on `_exec_subprocess` payload: `workspace.protected()` — I'm adding that method to base Workspace (works for both local and remote since `_protected` lives on base).\n\nFor the fallback path with remote workspace: execute() branch becomes:\n\n```python\nif tool.subprocess and isinstance(workspace, LocalWorkspace):\n    result = self._exec_subprocess(workspace, config, name, args, cancel)\nelif self._cancelable.get(name):\n    ...\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow the CLI: `from .workspace import LocalWorkspace` — constructing `LocalWorkspace(root)` mkdirs the root (exist_ok) — fine.\n\nBut protected paths: parent sends `workspace.protected()` — set of Paths (realpath-resolved). CLI does `workspace.protect(Path(p))` — protect does `self._protected.add(self.realpath(path))`. Fine.\n\nNow, CLI `_run`: needs to call the tool func. `build_default_tools(config)` renders descriptions — reads prompts/*.md via `render` — those use Path(__file__) in prompts module presumably, fine in subprocess.\n\nOne subtlety: `build_default_tools(config)` with `mode=\"chat\"` excludes write_file/edit_file. If parent is in work mode, payload carries mode=\"work\" — good. The CLI resolves tool from that list. read_file exists in both modes.\n\nAnother subtlety: CLI's `_run` calls `tool.func(workspace, config, **payload.get(\"args\", {}))` — for read_file the lambda is `lambda sb, cfg, **kw: filesystem.read_file(sb, cfg, **kw)` — fine. For cancel-accepting funcs (run_command lambda has `cancel=None` keyword), CLI doesn't pass cancel — default None. Good — parent kills the tree instead.\n\nTimeout: `config.command_timeout` (30s default). read_file on huge file: workspace.read reads whole file — a 1GB file could exceed 30s locally? Rarely. Keep 30s; the error message tells the model it timed out.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety with timeout for read_file via subprocess: prior behavior had NO timeout for read_file (in-process). Now a 30s cap could turn a slow-but-successful read into an error. 30s local file read is a lot though. Acceptable; note in test maybe. Keep as planned.\n\nNow the JSON parse: what if the tool result dict contains non-JSON-serializable values? All tool results are str/bool per contract. If some tool returns something weird, json.dump in CLI would raise → caught by CLI's generic except → prints error JSON. Good: the CLI's try wraps `_run` — but json.dump is OUTSIDE the try in my skeleton. Let me move serialization inside the try so a non-serializable result becomes an error dict... Actually if json.dump fails midway, stdout has partial JSON — parent parse fails → error path. Acceptable either way; but cleaner to serialize inside try:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    result = _run(json.loads(sys.stdin.read()))\n    payload = json.dumps(result, ensure_ascii=False)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    payload = json.dumps({\"content\": f\"ERROR: cli crashed: {e}\", \"error\": True}, ensure_ascii=False)\nsys.stdout.write(payload)\nsys.stdout.flush()\n```\n\nThat way stdout always contains complete valid JSON (or the interpreter dies before writing anything, which parent handles). \n\nNow registry.py changes. Imports needed: json, os, subprocess, sys, time, Path. And from .transport import _drain, _kill_tree. Check transport imports first to avoid cycles.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should `_exec_subprocess` be a method on ToolRegistry or a module-level function? Method is fine, uses config only. It needs workspace only for root + protected. OK.\n\nTimeout duration: use `config.command_timeout`.\n\nCancel result message: `{\"content\": \"ERROR: tool call canceled\", \"error\": True}` — hmm, check what run_command returns on cancel for consistency... workspace.run with cancel returns CommandResult with code -1 or something; shell.py formats it. The exact text doesn't matter much; loop just feeds content back. I'll use \"ERROR: tool call canceled by user\" — hmm keep \"ERROR: tool call canceled\".\n\nNow workspace.protected() method — add after is_protected (L151-155):\n\n```python\ndef protected(self) -> set[Path]:\n    \"\"\"Snapshot of protected paths (the subprocess CLI re-applies them).\"\"\"\n    return set(self._protected)\n```\n\nNeed to verify `_protected` init exists on base Workspace __init__. Let me read workspace.py top (L1-140 area) to see __init__.\n\nTest file: `tests/toolcli_test.py`. Look at an existing test to match conventions (self-running module with main / assertion style). Let me look at tests/local_shell_test.py or mcpbackend_test.py structure briefly. The summary says tests are self-running modules run via `.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m tests.X`. Let me check one for the pattern (how they report/fail).\n\nAlso tests import from agent package — check how (sys.path manipulation? They run from repo root with `-m tests.x` so repo root is cwd → agent importable).\n\nTest cases:\n1. roundtrip: create tmp dir + file with Chinese content, spawn CLI subprocess with payload, assert content matches, error False.\n2. missing file: error True, content has ERROR.\n3. unknown tool in CLI: parseable JSON with error.\n4. registry branch equivalence: build registry via build_default_tools, set read_file subprocess=True, call execute → same as direct filesystem.read_file call.\n5. cancel: set run_command subprocess=True, execute run_command `python -c \"import time; time.sleep(30)\"` in worker thread, cancel after 0.5s, assert elapsed < 15s and error True.\n\nWait, run_command has blocked_prefixes check — \"python\" is not in blocked list (blocked: vim, vi, emacs, nano, less, more, tail -f, top, htop, make). OK. But run_command in work mode... test builds default tools with work mode config. run_command command string: use `[python_exe, \"-c\", ...]` joined as string: `command = f'\"{sys.executable}\" -c \"import time; time.sleep(30)\"'`. Hmm, quoting on Windows with Git Bash: `bash -c '<command>'` — the command string goes through bash. Quoted exe path with spaces (C:\\Program Files...) — venv path here: `.venv/Scripts/python.exe` under `C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\临时文件\\Clutch\\.venv` — no spaces but non-ASCII. Git Bash handles quoted paths fine. Since the test builds the command via shell quoting... simplest: use `shlex.quote`? On Windows shlex.quote uses POSIX rules — correct since the shell is Git Bash (POSIX). transport local_shell on Windows = Git Bash → POSIX quoting correct. On POSIX hosts /bin/sh → POSIX quoting. So `shlex.quote(sys.executable)` + ` -c ` + shlex.quote(\"import time; time.sleep(30)\")`.\n\nHmm, but wait: does run_command via CLI-in-subprocess even reach the sleep? Yes: CLI calls shell.run_command which spawns bash -c ... The cancel in parent kills CLI process tree (bash + python -c included). \n\nTest 5 timing: worker thread calls registry.execute(..., cancel=evt); main thread sleeps 0.5 then evt.set(); join with generous timeout (30s); assert elapsed < 15s (well under 30) and result error True.\n\nPotential flakiness: taskkill spawn time on Windows ~100-300ms. Fine.\n\nAlso the CLI subprocess path spawns python: startup on Windows 100-300ms; read_file roundtrip test fast enough.\n\nNow — one more consideration for test 1: CLI spawns with cwd=repo_root resolved from registry.py location — the CLI spawns via registry._exec_subprocess for test 4/5; for test 1 I directly spawn `[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"agent.tools.cli\"]` with cwd=repo root — compute repo root in test as `Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]`.\n\nEncoding on Windows for subprocess.run in test: pass bytes input, capture_output=True, then decode utf-8.\n\nLet me also double-check `agent/tools/__init__.py` — does it import anything heavy or have side effects? Let me read it. If `agent/tools/__init__.py` imports registry etc., `-m agent.tools.cli` runs package init first — fine either way, just need no cycle: cli.py imports `.registry` — registry imports `.transport` — transport imports? Let me check.\n\nPlan of tool calls:\n1. Read workspace.py top (init, _protected) + grep class names; read transport.py top (imports); read agent/tools/__init__.py; read one existing test for style (tests/local_shell_test.py head).\n2. Write agent/tools/cli.py.\n3. Edit registry.py (imports, Tool field, execute branch, _exec_subprocess, read_file subprocess=True).\n4. Edit workspace.py (protected method).\n5. Write tests/toolcli_test.py.\n6. Run tests: toolcli_test, local_shell_test, selfcheck. Maybe server_test full (takes longer; run if time permits — selfcheck may cover. The plan said selfcheck + local_shell_test + server_test full. Let me run at least the first three and server_test too.)\n\nAlso README is Chinese — does the refactor need README updates? Not necessarily; internal architecture. Skip.\n\nAlso memory: after done, save a memory entry about the refactor? There's a convention to save durable facts. The existing memories list project conventions. After completion, save \"Tool subprocess execution refactor shipped\" memory. Good practice per instructions.\n\nLet me start with the reads (parallel)."}
