{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789873041.2272923, "id": "eec693018ccb", "content": "`agent/tools/cli.py` 自己**不定义任何工具**——它只是一个分发入口，子进程里现查现用注册表的那张表：\n\n```python\ntool = {t.name: t for t in build_default_tools(config)}.get(name)   # cli.py 的 _run()\n```\n\n所以“它有哪些工具”= `build_default_tools(config)` 在 `memories=None` 时会造出什么，由载荷里的 `mode` 决定：\n\n| 工具 | work 模式 | chat 模式 | 说明 |\n|------|:-:|:-:|------|\n| `read_file` | ✅ | ✅ | 目前唯一打了 `subprocess=True` 的（试点） |\n| `grep` | ✅ | ✅ | |\n| `write_file` | ✅ | ❌ | chat 模式不进表，CLI 会回 unknown tool |\n| `edit_file` | ✅ | ❌ | 同上 |\n| `run_command` | ✅ | ✅ | 描述文本按模式不同，函数同一个 |\n| `web_search` | ✅ | ✅ | |\n| `web_fetch` | ✅ | ✅ | |\n| `load_skill` | ✅ | ✅ | `enable_skills` 默认开；读 `agent/skills/` 目录 |\n| `save_memory` / `load_memory` / `search_memory` | ❌ | ❌ | **永远不在**：它们是绑死父进程 MemoryStore 对象的闭包，`build_default_tools` 只在传入 memories 时才构造，CLI 重建不了 |\n\n载荷里给了 `mode=\"work\"` 就是 8 个，`mode=\"chat\"` 就是 6 个；表里没有的名字一律返回：\n\n```json\n{\"content\": \"ERROR: unknown tool 'xxx'\", \"error\": true}\n```\n\n两个容易混淆的区分：\n\n1. **“CLI 能调” ≠ “已迁移到子进程”**。上面 8 个名字你手喂 payload 都能从 CLI 跑通；但父进程的 `execute()` 目前只把 `read_file` 路由进子进程，其余 7 个在正常 agent 流程里仍走进程内直调。迁移哪个由 `Tool(...)` 上的 `subprocess=True` 决定，与 CLI 能识别哪些名字是两回事。\n2. **表只有一份**。CLI 刻意不维护自己的工具清单，而是复用 `build_default_tools`——将来往注册表加工具或改签名，CLI 自动跟上，不会出现两边清单对不上的情况。", "tool_calls": [], "reasoning": "The user asks again: \"agent.tools.cli有哪些工具？\" — \"What tools does agent.tools.cli have?\"\n\nThis echoes the original task question. The answer: agent.tools.cli itself doesn't define any tools — it's a subprocess entry point/dispatcher that reuses `build_default_tools(config)` from the registry. So the tools available through it are exactly the tools built by `build_default_tools` for the mode given in the payload, minus the memory tools (which are closures and never built in the child since memories=None), minus unknown names.\n\nSo the accurate answer:\n\nIn the child process, `_run` does:\n```python\ntool = {t.name: t for t in build_default_tools(config)}.get(name)\n```\n\n`build_default_tools(config)` with `memories=None` (default) builds:\n- read_file (both modes) — now subprocess=True pilot\n- grep (both modes)\n- write_file (work mode only)\n- edit_file (work mode only)\n- run_command (both modes)\n- web_search (both modes)\n- web_fetch (both modes)\n- load_skill (if config.enable_skills, default True) — reads skills dir\n- NOT save_memory/load_memory/search_memory (only added when memories is not None — the CLI passes nothing, so these three are absent)\n\nSo via the CLI:\n- mode=\"work\": read_file, grep, write_file, edit_file, run_command, web_search, web_fetch, load_skill — 8 tools\n- mode=\"chat\": read_file, grep, run_command, web_search, web_fetch, load_skill — 6 tools (no write/edit)\n\nAnd anything else → \"ERROR: unknown tool ...\" error-as-data JSON.\n\nImportant nuance: the CLI is a *dispatcher*, not a registry of its own — it has zero tool definitions of its own. The authoritative source is `agent/tools/registry.py` `build_default_tools`. That's by design (single source of truth, the child reuses the same table so the name→function mapping never diverges).\n\nAnother nuance worth one line: being callable via CLI ≠ migrated to subprocess. Currently only read_file actually runs through the CLI (subprocess=True). The other 7 are callable in principle through the CLI (the CLI will happily run write_file if you send it directly), but the parent dispatcher only routes read_file there; the others still run in-process. And in practice write/edit via CLI is gated by mode in the payload.\n\nAlso, the running server's actual tool list depends on more conditions (memories passed, enable_skills, chat/work mode). The CLI list = build_default_tools(mode from payload).\n\nShould I verify load_skill presence by checking config.enable_skills default = True (I read config.py L113: enable_skills: bool = True). Yes, default True, and skills dir exists (agent/skills with readme-crafter etc.). In the CLI child, `cached_library(config.skills_dir)` scans agent/skills — the skills listed in the system prompt exist (readme-crafter, readme-doctor, refactor, web-design per this very session's system prompt). So load_skill is built and callable.\n\nAlso chat mode run_command description differs (read-only variant) but the tool exists.\n\nThe answer should be short and precise — it's a simple factual question. A small table or list. Mention: it has no tools of its own; it dispatches to build_default_tools; list per mode; the three memory tools absent and why; only read_file is actually routed by the parent today.\n\nNo tool calls needed — all facts are in context from this session's reads (registry.py full content read this session, config.py read this session). I'm confident. Chat mode, just answer."}
