nd now clearly says delete. The request is explicit and unambiguous: delete MCP-related files, check for leftovers. They said it twice (message duplicated).\n\nHowever, deleting a shipped feature has broad touch points. The safest professional approach in work mode:\n- Inventory all MCP references (grep \"mcp\" case-insensitive across the repo).\n- Delete the standalone files (mcpprovider.py, mcp_presets.json, tests/mcpbackend_test.py).\n- Remove integration points in websearch.py, config.py, server.py, prompts, README, UI.\n- Run tests to make sure everything stays green.\n\nBut wait — should I confirm before deleting? The user's instruction is explicit and they've asked twice (the duplicate is likely accidental). In work mode, mutating work should be intentional and minimal — but this IS the intentional request. Deleting files is what they want. However there's real risk of misunderstanding scope: \"原来添加的mcp相关的文件\" — the files added for MCP. That includes integration lines in shared files. \"删除干净\" = delete cleanly (no leftover references). \"检查是否有多余的文件\" = check for redundant files (leftovers/orphans).\n\nGiven the explicit double request, I'll proceed with the full removal. But it's a big change. Let me first inventory everything MCP-related, then plan the removal, then execute, then run tests.\n\nActually — one consideration: is there a git repo? If so, the deletion is recoverable via git. Let me check git status. The conventions memory says \"Clutch push/release conventions + auth reality\" — there's a git repo presumably. If files are tracked and committed, deletion is safe/recoverable. If uncommitted... the memories say v0.1.14 released, so MCP stuff is committed. Still, never use git checkout per instructions; but deletion via rm is fine.\n\nLet me start by inventorying:\n1. `grep -i mcp` across the workspace (excluding .venv).\n2. Look at README mentions.\n3. Check websearch.py integration extent — this is the big one: the MCP client lives inside websearch.py? The memory says \"MCP web-search plugin shipped and verified\" and \"MCP plugin backend design (approved, pending work mode)\" and the summary says \"web_search 是 failover 链（首个成功者胜出）而非多源融合，MCP 服务排在链尾\" — so websearch.py contains an MCP client component. Also \"bing/ddg 为进程内 urllib 函数\".\n\nAlso settings UI: `agent/server.py` `_services_from` (L86-94, read this session) handles `mcp_services` in settings POST. And `agent/config.py` L84-88 `mcp_services` field. And the frontend — is there a UI directory? Let me check. There might be `web/` or static files served by server.py. The system prompt mentions \"the settings UI\" — need to find where the frontend lives and remove MCP settings UI parts.\n\nAlso memory says \"No-bias principle: no hardcoded platform names in code\" — relevant: presets file (mcp_presets.json) was moved from agent/ to agent/tools/. \n\nHmm wait, actually — careful. Deleting MCP support is a FEATURE REMOVAL, not just file cleanup. The touch points:\n\n1. `agent/tools/mcpprovider.py` — delete entire file\n2. `agent/tools/mcp_presets.json` — delete entire file\n3. `tests/mcpbackend_test.py` — delete entire file\n4. `agent/tools/websearch.py` — remove MCP backend code (import of mcpprovider, the MCP client in the failover chain, mcp services resolution)\n5. `agent/config.py` — remove `mcp_services` field\n6. `agent/server.py` — remove `mcp_services` settings handling (`_services_from`), validation, maybe settings GET exposing them\n7. Frontend (if exists) — remove MCP services settings UI\n8. `agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md` — mentions of backends/MCP?\n9. `README.md` — mentions of MCP feature\n10. tests that reference mcp_services (websearch_test? server_test settings tests?)\n11. `.clc` / memory files? No, those are project memory, not code. My own saved memories mention MCP but those are agent memories, not project files.\n\nAlso \"检查是否有多余的文件\" — after deletion, check for orphans: e.g., files only referenced by deleted code, stale presets,