{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789873578.3518717, "id": "fc8aabc2f419", "tool_call_id": "call_6bbb84fdc59e4b478e6f78ec", "content": "1: \"\"\"Web access tools: web_search (multi-backend search) + web_fetch (page -> text).\n2: \n3: Built on the stdlib only (urllib + html.parser + ElementTree) — the LLM loop is\n4: hand-rolled here, and so is web access: no scraping SDK that rots when an\n5: engine tweaks its markup. Backends, tried in order, first success wins:\n6: \n7:   tavily   LLM-first search API, best quality   (CLUTCH_TAVILY_API_KEY)\n8:   searxng  self-hosted metasearch, JSON API     (CLUTCH_SEARXNG_URL)\n9:   bing     the undocumented RSS endpoint of cn.bing.com — keyless, CN-friendly\n10:   ddg      duckduckgo html endpoint — keyless\n11: \n12: Operator-declared MCP services (Config.mcp_services, see tools/mcpprovider.py)\n13: join the chain after the native backends, in declared order; a service that is\n14: not declared simply does not exist on this machine.\n15: \n16: Poka-yoke (make it hard for the model to misuse):\n17: - timeout: every request is bounded (Config.web_search_timeout); Stop is\n18:   honored between chunks so a hung fetch never outlives the user's patience\n19: - size cap: responses larger than max_bytes are rejected, not swallowed\n20: - private-network guard: web_fetch refuses loopback/LAN hosts (and follows\n21:   redirects only to public hosts) — a GET into the agent's own API or the\n22:   LAN stays impossible even when the model is talked into trying\n23: - error-as-data: backend failures list each reason; a truncating fetch says\n24:   how to fetch the next slice, mirroring read_file's offset hints\n25: \"\"\"\n26: \n27: from __future__ import annotations\n28: \n29: import functools\n30: import gzip\n31: import ipaddress\n32: import json\n33: import re\n34: import socket\n35: import urllib.error\n36: import urllib.parse\n37: import urllib.request\n38: import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\n39: import zlib\n40: from dataclasses import dataclass\n41: from html import unescape\n42: from html.parser import HTMLParser\n43: from typing import Any, Callable\n44: \n45: from ..config import Config\n46: from .mcpprovider import McpService, map_results, parse_services, search_args\n47: from .workspace import Workspace\n48: \n49: # browser-shaped but honest about the product; several CDNs 403 blank UAs\n50: USER_AGENT = (\n51:     \"Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) \"\n52:     \"Chrome/124.0 Safari/537.36 ClutchAgent/0.1\"\n53: )\n54: \n55: _MAX_BYTES = 2_000_000  # hard cap per response: pages are for reading, not hoarding\n56: \n57: \n58: class WebError(Exception):\n59:     \"\"\"A request-level failure (DNS, timeout, size cap, private host).\"\"\"\n60: \n... (showing lines 1-60 of 702; use offset=61 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789873578.369678, "id": "bb7c26d645e3", "tool_call_id": "call_d790ed2a08154af99a637b05", "content": "405:     resp = get(url)\n406:     parser = _DdgParser()\n407:     parser.feed(_decode_body(resp.body, resp.content_type))\n408:     parser.close()\n409:     out = [r for r in parser.results if r[\"url\"]][:limit]\n410:     if not out:\n411:         raise BackendError(\"no results parsed (bot-checked, blocked, or layout changed)\")\n412:     return out\n413: \n414: \n415: # ---- HTTP MCP client (JSON-RPC over POST) ------------------------------------\n416: \n417: \n418: _MCP_PROTO = \"2025-03-26\"  # protocol version announced in the initialize call\n419: \n420: \n421: def _mcp_rpc(\n422:     url: str,\n423:     payload: dict,\n424:     session: str | None,\n425:     timeout: float,\n426:     cancel: Any = None,\n427: ) -> tuple[dict, str | None]:\n428:     \"\"\"One JSON-RPC POST to an HTTP MCP server; returns (response, session id).\n429: \n430:     Deliberately NOT routed through _http_get: the private-network guard exists\n431:     for model-controlled URLs, while this endpoint comes from operator config\n432:     and is typically loopback. Every failure surfaces as WebError so callers\n433:     treat MCP problems like any other backend failure.\n434:     \"\"\"\n435:     if cancel is not None and cancel.is_set():\n436:         raise WebError(\"aborted by user\")\n437:     body = json.dumps(payload).encode(\"utf-8\")\n438:     headers = {\n439:         \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n440:         \"Accept\": \"application/json, text/event-stream\",\n441:     }\n442:     if session:\n443:         headers[\"Mcp-Session-Id\"] = session\n444:     request = urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, headers=headers, method=\"POST\")\n445:     try:\n446:         resp = urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=timeout)\n447:     except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:\n448:         raise WebError(f\"HTTP {e.code} from MCP server {url}\") from e\n449:     except urllib.error.URLError as e:\n450:         reason = getattr(e, \"reason\", e)\n451:         if isinstance(reason, (socket.timeout, TimeoutError)):\n452:             raise WebError(f\"MCP server timed out after {timeout:.0f}s: {url}\")\n453:         raise WebError(f\"MCP server unreachable ({reason}): {url}\") from e\n454:     except (ValueError, OSError) as e:\n455:         raise WebError(f\"MCP request failed ({e}): {url}\") from e\n456:     with resp:\n457:         raw = _drain(resp, cancel, _MAX_BYTES)\n458:     sid = resp.headers.get(\"Mcp-Session-Id\") or session\n459:     text = _decode_body(raw, resp.headers.get(\"Content-Type\", \"\"))\n460:     if text.startswith(\"event:\") or text.startswith(\"data:\") or \"\\ndata:\" in text[:2048]:\n461:         # SSE-framed reply: keep the last data frame (the JSON-RPC response)\n462:         frames = [ln[5:].strip() for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"data:\")]\n463:         text = frames[-1] if frames else \"\"\n464:     if not text.strip():\n465:         return {}, sid  # e.g. 202 Accepted for a notification\n466:     try:\n467:         data = json.loads(text)\n468:     except ValueError as e:\n469:         raise WebError(f\"MCP server returned non-JSON: {_short(e)}\") from e\n470:     if isinstance(data, dict) and data.get(\"error\") is not None:\n471:         err = data[\"error\"]\n472:         msg = err.get(\"message\", str(err)) if isinstance(err, dict) else str(err)\n473:         raise WebError(f\"MCP error: {_short(msg)}\")\n474:     return (data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}), sid\n475: \n476: \n477: def _mcp_call(\n478:     url: str,\n479:     tool: str,\n480:     args: dict,\n481:     timeout: float,\n482:     cancel: Any = None,\n483: ) -> Any:\n484:     \"\"\"initialize -> tools/call against an HTTP MCP server; returns the parsed\n485:     tool output (JSON when it parses, else the plain text).\"\"\"\n486:     hello = {\n487:         \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n488:         \"id\": 1,\n489:         \"method\": \"initialize\",\n490:         \"params\": {\n491:             \"protocolVersion\": _MCP_PROTO,\n492:             \"capabilities\": {},\n493:             \"clientInfo\": {\"name\": \"clutch\", \"version\": \"0.1\"},\n494:         },\n495:     }\n496:     _, session = _mcp_rpc(url, hello, None, timeout, cancel)\n497:     if session:\n498:         _mcp_rpc(\n499:             url,\n500:             {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"method\": \"notifications/initialized\"},\n501:             session,\n502:             timeout,\n503:             cancel,\n504:         )\n505:     resp, _ = _mcp_rpc(\n506:         url,\n507:         {\n508:             \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n509:             \"id\": 2,\n510:             \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n511:             \"params\": {\"name\": tool, \"arguments\": args},\n512:         },\n513:         session,\n514:         timeout,\n515:         cancel,\n516:     )\n517:     result = resp.get(\"result\") or {}\n518:     parts = result.get(\"content\") or []\n519:     text = \"\\n\".join(\n520:         p.get(\"text\", \"\") for p in parts if isinstance(p, dict) and p.get(\"type\") == \"text\"\n521:     ).strip()\n522:     if result.get(\"isError\") and text:\n523:         raise WebError(f\"MCP tool error: {_short(text)}\")\n524:     try:\n525:         return json.loads(text)\n526:     except ValueError:\n527:         return text\n528: \n529: \n530: # ---- registry ---------------------------------------------------------------\n531: # One protocol for every source: a Backend knows how to search and whether it\n532: # exists on this machine. web_search only ever sees this shape, so adding a\n533: # source (e.g. an MCP service) touches nothing else in this module.\n534: \n535: \n536: @dataclass(frozen=True)\n537: class Backend:\n538:     name: str\n539:     search: Callable[[str, int, Config, Callable[..., HttpResp]], list[dict[str, str]]]\n540:     available: Callable[[Config], bool] = lambda config: True\n541: \n542: \n543: _BACKENDS: dict[str, Backend] = {\n544:     \"tavily\": Backend(\"tavily\", _tavily, lambda c: bool(c.tavily_api_key)),\n545:     \"searxng\": Backend(\"searxng\", _searxng, lambda c: bool(c.searxng_url)),\n546:     \"bing\": Backend(\"bing\", _bing),\n547:     \"ddg\": Backend(\"ddg\", _ddg),\n548: }\n549: # Base try order; the live chain is this filtered through Backend.available.\n550: _DEFAULT_CHAIN = tuple(_BACKENDS)\n551: \n552: \n553: def _mcp_backend(service: McpService) -> Backend:\n554:     \"\"\"Adapt one operator-declared MCP service to the uniform Backend shape.\"\"\"\n555: \n556:     def search(q: str, limit: int, config: Config, get: Callable[..., HttpResp]) -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n557:         cancel = (getattr(get, \"keywords\", None) or {}).get(\"cancel\")\n558:         data = _mcp_call(\n559:             service.url,\n560:             service.search_tool,\n561:             search_args(service, q, limit),\n562:             config.web_search_timeout,\n563:             cancel=cancel,\n564:         )\n565:         rows = map_results(service, data)\n566:         if not rows:\n567:             raise BackendError(f\"no results for {q!r} (empty feed or unmapped payload)\")\n568:         return rows[:limit]\n569: \n570:     return Backend(service.name, search)\n571: \n572: \n573: def _all_backends(config: Config) -> dict[str, Backend]:\n574:     \"\"\"Native backends plus this machine's configured MCP services, in chain\n575:     order (services last, as declared). A service that is disabled or missing\n576:     its endpoint/tool is not built at all: it does not exist here.\"\"\"\n577:     out = dict(_BACKENDS)\n578:     services, _errors = parse_services(config.mcp_services)\n579:     for service in services:\n580:         if service.enabled and service.complete():\n581:             out[service.name] = _mcp_backend(service)\n582:     return out\n583: \n584: \n585: def _available_backends(config: Config) -> tuple[str, ...]:\n586:     \"\"\"The usable subset of the chain: registered AND configured on this machine.\n587: \n588:     Rendered into prompts and error texts, so an unconfigured service simply\n589:     does not exist here — no name, no install advice.\n590:     \"\"\"\n591:     return tuple(name for name, backend in _all_backends(config).items() if backend.available(config))\n592: \n593: \n594: # Public alias: server and registry render UI/prompt text from this too.\n595: available_backends = _available_backends\n596: \n597: \n598: def mcp_status(url: str, service: McpService, timeout: float = 3.0) -> Any:\n599:     \"\"\"Query a service's status tool (e.g. login state); raw parsed payload.\n600: \n601:     Raises WebError on any failure — the caller turns that into a status row,\n602:     never into user-facing advice.\n603:     \"\"\"\n604:     if not service.status_tool:\n605:         return None\n606:     return _mcp_call(url, service.status_tool, {}, timeout)\n607: \n608: \n609: def _short(reason: object) -> str:\n610:     return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", str(reason)).strip()[:160]\n611: \n612: \n613: def web_search(\n614:     workspace: Workspace,\n615:     config: Config,\n616:     query: str,\n617:     max_results: int | None = None,\n618:     backend: str = \"\",\n619:     cancel: Any = None,\n... (showing lines 405-619 of 702; use offset=620 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789873607.404334, "id": "7358e4b2c421", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_a1b6bc392a3f436486c482df", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":175,\"offset\":2805,\"path\":\"ui/app.js\"}"}, {"id": "call_de1b2acb03eb43e08f91cd83", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":18,\"offset\":42,\"path\":\"ui/index.html\"}"}, {"id": "call_f465f02af7e3478794f4bf86", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":14,\"offset\":36,\"path\":\"README.md\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now websearch.py is clear. The removal plan for websearch.py:\n- L12-14 docstring paragraph (MCP services join the chain...) — remove; adjust \"Backends, tried in order\" list (keep tavily/searxng/bing/ddg).\n- L46 import line — remove.\n- L415-528: `_MCP_PROTO`, `_mcp_rpc`, `_mcp_call` — remove the whole section (keep the `# ---- registry ----` comment from L530, but fix L533 comment \"(e.g. an MCP service)\").\n- L553-570: `_mcp_backend` — remove.\n- L573-582: `_all_backends` — replace with native-only: rename usage — `_available_backends` uses `_all_backends(config)`; simplest: change `_all_backends` to return `dict(_BACKENDS)` — or delete `_all_backends` and let `_available_backends` iterate `_BACKENDS`. Cleaner: delete `_all_backends` and `_mcp_backend`, change `_available_backends` to `tuple(name for name, b in _BACKENDS.items() if b.available(config))`.\n- L598-606: `mcp_status` — remove.\n- Check L609 `_short` still used elsewhere (yes — used by other backends? `_short` used in bing/ddg error paths presumably; grep later. It was used by _mcp_rpc/_mcp_call; also maybe elsewhere. Check usage count after removal.)\n\nDoes websearch use `parse_services`, `search_args`, `map_results`, `McpService` elsewhere? Only in the removed regions. `socket` import — used by `_mcp_rpc` (socket.timeout) and maybe elsewhere (private-network guard probably uses socket). Check after edit via py_compile + grep.\n\nserver.py removal plan:\n- L46-50 imports: remove 4 lines (keep the comment L46? \"login-state normalization is the engine's\" — tied to mcpprovider import; remove it too).\n- `_services_from` L86-102: remove. But careful: `_settings_post` uses it (L351) and `services` var (L354-358, L371-372, L379-382). Remove all services handling from _settings_post: \n  - L350-353 try/except → remove\n  - L354-358 \"nothing to save\" condition drops `and services is None`\n  - L371-372 → remove\n  - L373-382: comment + `if services is None: saved.update(_mcp_keys_from(...))` / `else: saved[\"mcp_services\"] = services` → remove entirely, keep `saved = flatten_settings(load_settings())`.\n- L400, L407-409 `_settings_get`: remove services line + mcp_services field in the JSON.\n- `_mcp_status` method L413-438: remove whole method.\n- Route L189-190: remove.\n- L818-820: remove boot wiring.\n- `_mcp_keys_from` L104-107: remove.\n\nconfig.py: remove L84-88 (comment + field).\n\nui/index.html L50: `<div id=\"mcp-services\"></div>` — check surroundings (maybe a section header/label to remove too). Read L40-60.\n\nui/app.js L2813-2965ish: read the full block to remove (2813 to where initMcpServices IIFE ends). Read L2800-2980.\n\nREADME L38-47: read to edit.\n\nAlso check `flatten_settings` — does it know about mcp_services? L105 comment says \"flatten drops them\" for legacy keys. flatten_settings in server.py presumably handles LLM fields only; the mcp_services handling was in _settings_post/_settings_get. But flatten_settings may also reference mcp? grep showed no other mcp hits in server.py beyond those listed. Good.\n\nAlso `tests/settings-mirror.test.js` — greps found no mcp there. But it might POST mcp_services? No hits. OK.\n\nAlso `eval/` — grep found nothing (the earlier command grep -ril over eval scripts dist pyproject.toml .github returned nothing before `---`). Good.\n\nAlso check `agent/prompts/tools/web_search.md` — prompts grep found no matches. But it has `{backends}` interpolation — fine, dynamic.\n\nNow, the fix.clc / net_fix.clc / scratch files: report only.\n\nAlso VERSION file — is there a release convention? VERSION file contains 0.1.14. Removing a feature would warrant a version bump, but that's the user's call — mention it, don't do it unilaterally? The release conventions memory says releases are tagged/committed. I'll leave VERSION alone and mention.\n\nLet me read the remaining regions: app.js block, index.html, README."}
