ads above. Let me assemble it carefully:\n\n```\n    if not out:\n        raise BackendError(\"no results parsed (bot-checked, blocked, or layout changed)\")\n    return out\n\n\n# ---- HTTP MCP client (JSON-RPC over POST) ------------------------------------\n\n\n_MCP_PROTO = \"2025-03-26\"  # protocol version announced in the initialize call\n\n\ndef _mcp_rpc(\n    url: str,\n    payload: dict,\n    session: str | None,\n    timeout: float,\n    cancel: Any = None,\n) -> tuple[dict, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"One JSON-RPC POST to an HTTP MCP server; returns (response, session id).\n\n    Deliberately NOT routed through _http_get: the private-network guard exists\n    for model-controlled URLs, while this endpoint comes from operator config\n    and is typically loopback. Every failure surfaces as WebError so callers\n    treat MCP problems like any other backend failure.\n    \"\"\"\n    if cancel is not None and cancel.is_set():\n        raise WebError(\"aborted by user\")\n    body = json.dumps(payload).encode(\"utf-8\")\n    headers = {\n        \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n        \"Accept\": \"application/json, text/event-stream\",\n    }\n    if session:\n        headers[\"Mcp-Session-Id\"] = session\n    request = urllib.request.Request(url, data=body, headers=headers, method=\"POST\")\n    try:\n        resp = urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=timeout)\n    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:\n        raise WebError(f\"HTTP {e.code} from MCP server {url}\") from e\n    except urllib.error.URLError as e:\n        reason = getattr(e, \"reason\", e)\n        if isinstance(reason, (socket.timeout, TimeoutError)):\n            raise WebError(f\"MCP server timed out after {timeout:.0f}s: {url}\")\n        raise WebError(f\"MCP server unreachable ({reason}): {url}\") from e\n    except (ValueError, OSError) as e:\n        raise WebError(f\"MCP request failed ({e}): {url}\") from e\n    with resp:\n        raw = _drain(resp, cancel, _MAX_BYTES)\n    sid = resp.headers.get(\"Mcp-Session-Id\") or session\n    text = _decode_body(raw, resp.headers.get(\"Content-Type\", \"\"))\n    if text.startswith(\"event:\") or text.startswith(\"data:\") or \"\\ndata:\" in text[:2048]:\n        # SSE-framed reply: keep the last data frame (the JSON-RPC response)\n        frames = [ln[5:].strip() for ln in text.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"data:\")]\n        text = frames[-1] if frames else \"\"\n    if not text.strip():\n        return {}, sid  # e.g. 202 Accepted for a notification\n    try:\n        data = json.loads(text)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        raise WebError(f\"MCP server returned non-JSON: {_short(e)}\") from e\n    if isinstance(data, dict) and data.get(\"error\") is not None:\n        err = data[\"error\"]\n        msg = err.get(\"message\", str(err)) if isinstance(err, dict) else str(err)\n        raise WebError(f\"MCP error: {_short(msg)}\")\n    return (data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}), sid\n\n\ndef _mcp_call(\n    url: str,\n    tool: str,\n    args: dict,\n    timeout: float,\n    cancel: Any = None,\n) -> Any:\n    \"\"\"initialize -> tools/call against an HTTP MCP server; returns the parsed\n    tool output (JSON when it parses, else the plain text).\"\"\"\n    hello = {\n        \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n        \"id\": 1,\n        \"method\": \"initialize\",\n        \"params\": {\n            \"protocolVersion\": _MCP_PROTO,\n            \"capabilities\": {},\n            \"clientInfo\": {\"name\": \"clutch\", \"version\": \"0.1\"},\n        },\n    }\n    _, session = _mcp_rpc(url, hello, None, timeout, cancel)\n    if session:\n        _mcp_rpc(\n            url,\n            {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"method\": \"notifications/initialized\"},\n            session,\n            timeout,\n            cancel,\n        )\n    resp, _ = _mcp_rpc(\n        url,\n        {\n            \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n            \"id\": 2,\n            \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n            \"params\": {\"name\": tool, \"arguments\": args},\n        },\n        session,\n        timeout,\n        cancel,\n    )\n    result = resp.get(\"result\") or {}\n    parts = result.get(\"content\") or []\n    text = \"\\n\".join(\n        p.get(\"text\", \"\") for p in parts if isinstance(p, dict) and p.get(\"type\") == \"text\"\n    ).strip()\n    if result.get(\"isError\") and text:\n        raise WebError(f\"MCP tool error: {_short(text)}\")\n    try:\n        return json.loads(text)\n    except ValueError:\n        return text\n\n\n# ---- registry ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# One protocol for every source: a Backend knows how to search and whether it\n# exists on this machine. web_search only ever sees this shape, so adding a\n# source (e.g. an MCP service) touches nothing else in this module.\n```\n\nnew_string:\n```\n    if not out:\n        raise BackendError(\"no results parsed (bot-checked, blocked, or layout changed)\")\n    return out\n\n\n# ---- registry ---------------------------------------------------------------\n# One protocol for every source: a Backend knows how to search and whether it\n# exists on this machine. web_search only ever sees this shape.\n```\n\n**Edit 4 — registry comment MCP mention**: included in Edit 3's new_string (I rewrite the comment's last line to drop the MCP parenthetical). Good — one less edit.\n\n**Edit 5 — _mcp_backend + _all_backends** (L553-582): \nold:\n```\ndef _mcp_backend(service: McpService) -> Backend:\n    \"\"\"Adapt one operator-declared MCP service to the uniform Backend shape.\"\"\"\n\n    def search(q: str, limit: int, config: Config, get: Callable[..., HttpResp]) -> list[dict[str, str]]:\n        cancel = (getattr(get, \"keywords\", None) or {}).get(\"cancel\")\n        data = _mcp_call(\n            service.url,\n            service.search_tool,\n            search_args(service, q, limit),\n            config.web_search_timeout,\n            cancel=cancel,\n        )\n        rows = map_results(service, data)\n        if not rows:\n            raise BackendError(f\"no results for {q!r} (empty feed or unmapped payload)\")\n        return rows[:limit]\n\n    return Backend(service.name, search)\n\n\ndef _all_backends(config: Config) -> dict[str, Backend]:\n    \"\"\"Native backends plus this machine's configured MCP services, in chain\n    order (services last, as declared). A service that is disabled or missing\n    its endpoint/tool is not built at all: it does not exist here.\"\"\"\n    out = dict(_BACKENDS)\n    services, _errors = parse_services(config.mcp_services)\n    for service in services:\n        if service.enabled and service.complete():\n            out[service.name] = _mcp_backend(service)\n    return out\n```\nnew:\n```\ndef _all_backends(config: Config) -> dict[str, Backend]:\n    \"\"\"Every registered backend in chain order.\"\"\"\n    return dict(_BACKENDS)\n```\n\n**Edit 6 — mcp_status** (L598-607):\nold:\n```\n# Public alias: server and registry render UI/prompt text from this too.\navailable_backends = _available_backends\n\n\ndef mcp_status(url: str, service: McpService, timeout: float = 3.0) -> Any:\n    \"\"\"Query a service's status tool (e.g. login state); raw parsed payload.\n\n    Raises WebError on any failure — the caller turns that into a status row,\n    never into user-facing advice.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not service.status_tool:\n        return None\n    return _mcp_call(url, service.status_tool, {}, timeout)\n\n\ndef _short(reason: object) -> str:\n```\nnew:\n```\n# Public alias: server and registry render UI/prompt text from this too.\navailable_backends = _available_backends\n\n\ndef _short(reason: object) -> str:\n```\n\nWait — also check `_available_backends` docstring mentions \"unconfigured service\" — that's generic enough, fine to keep. Line 586-590 docstring: \"Rendered into prompts and error texts, so an unconfigured service simply does not exist here — no name, no install advice.\" Generic backend wording, acceptable. Could tweak but not necessary. Actually \"unconfigured service\" without MCP is still meaningful (tavily without key). Keep.\n\nAlso `Callable` import: still used by Backend dataclass + _available_backends. `Any` — used elsewhere probably. `json` — used by websearch elsewhere (json in other backends?) — after removing the MCP block, `json.loads`/`json.dumps` usages: were they ONLY in the MCP block? The module has `import json` at top... need to check remaining usage. Similarly `socket`, `urllib.error`, `threading`, `time` — those are used by _http_get/_drain presumably. Let me check usage after edits with a quick grep for `json\\.` in websearch.py. If unused imports remain, flake would complain but it's minor — still, clean removal means removing now-unused imports. I'll check after the edits.\n\nAfter websearch edits: server.py still imports `from .tools.websearch import mcp_status as _mcp_status_probe` — must remove too.\n\nNow execute websearch edits 1, 2, 3 in one message? Edit 3 is huge; do 1+2 first (small), then 3 alone, then 5+6 together. Actually edits are independent regions — can batch all. But a huge old_string in a batch with others is fine; each edit_file is a separate tool call. Risk: if edit 3's old_string has any mismatch, it errors and I retry that one. Batch 1+2 first to validate the channel, then 3, then 5+6. Let me do 1+2+5+6 (all small, verified text) in one message, then the big one separately.\n\nOrder matters? They're independent text regions — no overlap. Batch them."}
