put\n5. L408-426: _mcp_status method\n6. L813-815: boot wiring\n\nPlus imports — but wait, git grep -i mcp didn't show the import lines (L47-49 from earlier session grep)! The earlier session's grep showed:\n```\nLine 47: from .tools.mcpprovider import parse_services as _parse_mcp_services\nLine 48: from .tools.mcpprovider import service_records as _mcp_service_records\nLine 49: from .tools.websearch import mcp_status as _mcp_status_probe\n```\nBut today's `git grep -n -i mcp -- agent/server.py` does NOT list those lines! And `git grep -c -i mcp` counted 26. The import lines contain \"mcpprovider\" and \"mcp_status\" — they'd match -i mcp. They're NOT in the output. Conclusion: the import edit DID apply (from the first session), removing L47-49. The import block was replaced. Let me verify what the imports look like now — need to read around L44-50 of server.py.\n\nAlso \"Line 187: elif /api/settings\" etc. from the corrupted earlier grep was garbage; today's clean output shows route at 184-185.\n\nAnd `_parse_mcp_services` at L93, L395, L416 — references the imported alias... if imports were removed but usages remain, server.py currently has NameErrors at runtime (but imports fine until those paths execute — actually `_parse_mcp_services` is referenced at module level? No, only inside functions — module imports fine, breaks on call).\n\nWait, but the import probe earlier today failed only on websearch.py's mcpprovider import. server.py imports websearch (via registry → websearch). So server.py module-level imports are otherwise fine — meaning NO server.py imports reference mcpprovider anymore. Confirms import cleanup applied.\n\nHmm, but earlier `git status` showed server.py modified — consistent with the applied import edit (-5 lines: 3 mcpprovider imports + adjustments).\n\nNow do the remaining server.py cuts. I need exact current text for each region. Read the regions:\n- L78-103 (_services_from + _mcp_keys_from + surroundings)\n- L180-190 (route)\n- L355-385 (s