ubprocess=True` flag in the Tool dataclass)\n8. `registry.py` — not a tool module itself: the Tool dataclass, `build_default_tools`, the executor (`execute`, `_coerce_types`, `_exec_subprocess`, cancel handling)\n\nLayering diagram:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    R[\"registry.py<br/>Tool 表 + execute 执行器\"] --> F[\"filesystem.py<br/>read/grep/write/edit\"]\n    R --> S[\"shell.py<br/>run_command\"]\n    R --> W[\"websearch.py<br/>web_search/web_fetch\"]\n    F --> WS[\"workspace.py<br/>Workspace ABC / Local / Remote\"]\n    S --> WS\n    S --> LS[\"localshell.py<br/>POSIX shell 探测\"]\n    S --> T[\"transport.py<br/>Local/Ssh Transport\"]\n    WS --> T\n    R --> C[\"cli.py<br/>子进程执行器(试点)\"]\n```\n\nHmm wait — mermaid: need to be careful with the rules: no double quotes inside labels, use <br/> for line breaks — that's fine. Chinese OK in quoted labels.\n\nActually, let me double check which modules workspace.py depends on transport — does workspace use Transport? RemoteWorkspace probably wraps a Transport. Let me not overclaim; I verified earlier grep: registry imports `_drain, _kill_tree` from transport. workspace.py imports? I didn't check. RemoteWorkspace likely takes a Transport. It's plausible but let me verify quickly to avoid a wrong edge in the diagram. Actually I can just not draw that edge, or check. One quick grep: `grep Transport agent/tools/workspace.py`.\n\nAlso broader context: user asked \"除了workspace外，其他的部分还有哪些\" — could also mean the broader agent/ parts. The tools' `func` signature takes `(workspace, config, ...)` — workspace is the first param. The user probably saw in earlier discussions that tools receive `sb` (sandbox/workspace). I'll answer the tools-module question thoroughly and add a one-line note about where memory/skill backends live (agent/memory.py, agent/skills.py) since those 4 tools' real logic is there.\n\nLet me verify workspace↔transport quickly, then write the final answer."}
