ators, scratch dirs). But its main axis is sandbox/local-vs-remote.\n- `shell.py` — builds commands assuming POSIX sh (`bash -c`); on Windows it runs through Git Bash (per localshell detection). So shell.py itself mostly assumes POSIX, relying on localshell to provide the bash.\n- `filesystem.py` — mostly platform-neutral through Workspace primitives.\n\nThe cleanest answer to the user's question:\n\n**管跨平台的层（平台差异的适配层）**：\n1. `localshell.py` — the dedicated cross-platform shim: detects which POSIX-capable shell exists (Git Bash, WSL, /bin/bash), decides posix vs windows argv splitting (`split_command` → `_split_windows`). Without it, run_command wouldn't work on Windows.\n2. `transport.py` partially — `_kill_tree` differs per OS (taskkill /T on Windows vs killpg on POSIX). But its main axis is local-vs-remote, not platform.\n\n**不管平台（平台无关）的层**：\n- `registry.py`, `filesystem.py`, `websearch.py`, and most of `workspace.py`'s logic — they deal in logical operations (read/grep/edit, URLs) and call Workspace/Transport primitives; they don't care where or on what OS they run. `shell.py` too — it just composes a POSIX command string and hands it to the workspace/transport; it assumes \"there is a POSIX sh\" rather than adapting.\n\nActually let me verify _kill_tree and split_command details before asserting. Let me read transport._kill_tree and localshell functions quickly. Also check shell.py's run_command to see how it uses localshell/posix_shell_argv.\n\nLet me look at:\n1. transport.py L76-105 (_kill_tree)\n2. localshell.py _detect/_split_windows (already saw names; peek at logic briefly)\n3. shell.py run_command head — how it invokes (does it use posix_shell_argv / LocalShell?)\n\nQuick greps/reads to ground the answer."}
