{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789876827.6743627, "id": "a8ff0f6395be", "tool_call_id": "call_a508b4a32c86443684cd7357", "content": "179: class SshTransport(Transport):\n180:     \"\"\"Run commands on a remote host through the Electron exec bridge.\n181: \n182:     The remote only needs an sshd (no python, no SFTP, no base64): the client-side\n183:     bridge turns POST /exec into an ssh exec channel, and for binary=True returns\n184:     the raw stdout base64-encoded CLIENT-side. timeout is sent in ms (the\n185:     bridge's remoteExec deadline); a timed-out remote exec comes back as code -1,\n186:     surfaced here as TransportError(timeout=True) like LocalTransport.\n187:     \"\"\"\n188: \n189:     def __init__(self, bridge_url: str) -> None:\n190:         self.bridge_url = bridge_url.rstrip(\"/\")\n191: \n192:     def run(\n193:         self, command: str, timeout: float, *, binary: bool = False, cancel: threading.Event | None = None\n194:     ) -> CommandResult:\n195:         body = json.dumps({\"command\": command, \"timeout\": int(timeout * 1000), \"binary\": binary}).encode(\n196:             \"utf-8\"\n197:         )\n198:         req = urllib.request.Request(\n199:             self.bridge_url + \"/exec\",\n200:             data=body,\n201:             headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n202:             method=\"POST\",\n203:         )\n204:         # urlopen parks in a socket read until the bridge answers, and a socket\n205:         # timeout would cut off a legitimately slow command — so the call runs\n206:         # in a helper thread and Stop abandons the wait. The bridge's own\n207:         # deadline still reaps the remote exec; only the agent's wait ends.\n208:         result: dict[str, Any] = {}\n209: \n210:         def _call() -> None:\n211:             try:\n212:                 with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout + 5) as r:\n213:                     result[\"payload\"] = json.loads(r.read().decode(\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\"))\n214:             except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 -- re-raised by kind below\n... (showing lines 179-214 of 242; use offset=215 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789876857.0488868, "id": "b90572375c2e", "content": "`SshTransport` 的文档串已经透露关键事实：**远程只需要 sshd，不装任何东西**。再核实 RemoteWorkspace 把文件操作翻译成了什么、以及状态层怎么接线：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_eba67d9fa45d44e3a4fef884", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":42,\"offset\":449,\"path\":\"agent/tools/workspace.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_c042187ca0334455b2c63aca", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":34,\"offset\":60,\"path\":\"agent/base.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_ed46773f65c14ae982175492", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent/tools/workspace.py\",\"pattern\":\"def exec_shell|ExecShell\"}"}], "reasoning": "Very interesting! The architecture is becoming clear:\n\n1. **No agent software runs on the remote machine** — \"The remote only needs an sshd (no python, no SFTP, no base64)\". The remote is just a plain SSH server!\n2. The \"exec bridge\" is CLIENT-side (Electron app): \"the client-side bridge turns POST /exec into an ssh exec channel\". So the Electron shell (desktop app) exposes a local HTTP endpoint (bridge_url, e.g. localhost:PORT) that translates POST /exec {\"command\": ...} into `ssh host command`.\n3. `SshTransport` POSTs to the bridge; the bridge runs ssh; remote just executes via sshd's exec channel.\n4. `RemoteWorkspace` maps read/write/list operations to `sh` commands over the bridge (workspace.py L10: \"RemoteWorkspace (SSH degradation layer) maps read/write/list to sh operations\").\n\nSo to the user's question \"安装在远程的服务器哪些部分并不知道自己作为一个远程工具运行\" — the answer is profound: **NOTHING is installed on the remote**. The remote machine is completely unaware — it's stock sshd executing shell commands. Every part of Clutch runs on the local machine; the remote doesn't even have Python.\n\nWait — but let me double check: is there also a \"remote server\" mode where agent/server.py runs remotely? The memory said \"Clutch push/release conventions\" and app.js has \"reconciledBackendUrl\" — that's about the UI connecting to a backend, which is the local agent server. Let me check base.py L64-93 and how tools get their workspace, plus supervisor/server wiring (\"build the workspace first (ssh mode -> RemoteWorkspace over the bridge)\").\n\nAlso which parts KNOW about remote:\n- `base.py` (AgentBase? state) — backend_mode/bridge_url/remote_root, workspace factory: KNOWS\n- `server.py` — /api/backend POST endpoint set_backend(\"ssh\", bridge, root): KNOWS\n- `browsing.py` — explicitly branches `isinstance(ws, RemoteWorkspace)`, uses SshTransport directly for one-level ls: KNOWS\n- `core/lazy.py` — \"Range reader for a .clc over local or remote (exec-bridge) storage\": KNOWS\n- `core/persist.py` — LineWriter via bridge or local append: KNOWS\n- `memory.py` — index_offset / remote append: KNOWS\n- `project.py` — .clc LineWriter for remote bridge: KNOWS\n- `workspace.py` RemoteWorkspace + base class path normalization (PurePosixPath): KNOWS\n- `transport.py` SshTransport: KNOWS (it's the boundary)\n- UI app.js (switchBackend, reconciledBackendUrl): KNOWS\n\nWhich DON'T know:\n- `filesystem.py`, `shell.py`, `websearch.py` tool implementations: receive a Workspace, call its methods — DON'T know\n- `registry.py` executor: passes workspace to tools — DON'T know\n- `localshell.py`: hmm — local platform detection. Does remote care? RemoteWorkspace.exec_shell() — what does it return? workspace.py:337 `if os.name != \"nt\" or not local_shell().posix:` — that's LocalWorkspace probably. For remote, exec_shell presumably returns a POSIX shell record (remote is always POSIX). Let me verify.\n- The remote machine itself: NOTHING installed, doesn't know at all — stock sshd.\n\nKey insight for the user: the remote server doesn't run ANY part of Clutch. All ~10 tools, the registry, the LLM client, the loop — everything runs locally on the user's machine. The remote only ever sees ordinary shell commands (`sh -c 'cat ...'`, `ls ...`) arriving via sshd's exec channel. The \"degradation layer\" name (SSH degradation) means: remote support is achieved by DEGRADING all file operations to shell primitives instead of requiring a companion daemon.\n\nLet me verify a few specifics:\n1. What commands does RemoteWorkspace generate (read_file → `cat`? write → `printf`/`tee`?) — workspace.py L449-530\n2. base.py L60-95 — the state/factory\n3. exec_shell for remote — check workspace.py around L337 and RemoteWorkspace.exec_shell override if any\n4. How supervisor/server builds workspace (server.py L571) and where tools get ws per call — the loop passes workspace to executor?\n\nLet me read those regions. Also check how `shell.py`'s run_command works over remote: it calls workspace.exec_shell() — for RemoteWorkspace what does it return? Let me grep exec_shell."}
