{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789876986.474727, "id": "3072bc1909ca", "tool_call_id": "call_584fb05dfaf74210a61a5294", "content": "1: \"\"\"Per-machine supervisor: spawns and manages session subprocesses.\n2: \n3: Every UI window is a SESSION: the supervisor spawns one agent.server child\n4: (--port 0, random port parsed from its stdout banner) per session and reports\n5: the port back. The window then talks to its child DIRECTLY (locally, or over\n6: its own tunnel forward) — the supervisor never proxies traffic, it only owns\n7: the lifecycle:\n8: \n9:     POST /api/session/start      -> {session_id, port}\n10:     POST /api/session/stop       -> kill the session child\n11:     POST /api/session/heartbeat  -> keep the session alive (stale ones die)\n12:     GET  /api/health             -> ok\n13: \n14: Lifecycle (per product decision):\n15:   - the FIRST window starts the supervisor (Electron probes /api/health and\n16:     spawns it when down); the LAST window's exit ends it — each window stops\n17:     its session on close, and the supervisor self-exits after an idle grace\n18:     once no sessions remain. A stale-session reaper also clears sessions whose\n19:     window crashed (heartbeat stops).\n20:   - on Windows each session child is assigned to a kill-on-close Job\n21:     (_job_assign): the stale reaper needs THIS process alive, so a supervisor\n22:     that dies without reaping (crash, TerminateProcess, Electron's tree going\n23:     down with it) would orphan the session — the Job makes the kernel kill the\n24:     whole session tree the moment this process's Job handles die with it. An\n25:     orphaned session is not cosmetic: it keeps holding its .clc write lock,\n26:     and reopening that project then lands read-only with no visible other\n27:     window.\n28: \n29: Locking: each session child is an independent process, so the .clc write lock\n30: is a kernel file lock (flock / LockFileEx) on the machine's tmp dir —\n31: process-level mutual exclusion, released by the OS on exit, no TTL needed.\n32: (noclobber+TTL remains only for\n33: the execBridge-degraded path, where the agent process and the project files\n34: live on different machines.)\n35: \"\"\"\n36: \n37: from __future__ import annotations\n38: \n39: import argparse\n40: import ctypes\n... (showing lines 1-40 of 786; use offset=41 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789876986.4807212, "id": "a467e881603f", "tool_call_id": "call_ad0bd1d4df074aeb9f7e339e", "content": "258:             # missing API key: an anticipated, user-facing condition\n259:             return self._json({\"error\": f\"LLM init failed: {e}\"}, status=500)\n260:         if agent is None:\n261:             return self._json({\"error\": \"a run is already active\"}, status=409)\n262:         self._state.gate = agent.gate\n263:         # tag the active run with its project so SSE subscribers on other\n264:         # projects can filter it out of their live stream\n265:         self._state.run_project = str(project.path)\n266: \n267:         def _worker() -> None:\n268:             # run() emits a graceful error final for anticipated AgentError\n269:             # failures; anything else (e.g. the SSH tunnel dying under a degraded\n270:             # backend) must surface as an error final instead of a silent idle.\n271:             try:\n272:                 agent.run(task)\n273:             except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 -- last-resort user-facing final\n274:                 print(f\"[clutch] run crashed: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n275:                 try:\n276:                     self._broadcaster.publish(FinalEvent(status=\"error\", summary=f\"run crashed: {e}\"))\n277:                     self._broadcaster.publish(StateUpdateEvent(value=\"error\"))\n278:                 except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001\n279:                     pass\n280:             finally:\n281:                 self._state.finish()\n... (showing lines 258-281 of 773; use offset=282 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789877012.593337, "id": "bd40a9c66e32", "content": "两条远程方案的轮廓出来了。`ui/main.js` 里有 `restartRemoteServer()`——**supervisor 本身也能跑在远程**。再核实隧道实现和 LLM 反向代理，同时调相关记忆：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_1b481ede62164ef2981786ca", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"ui/ssh-tunnel.js\",\"pattern\":\"^(function|const|async function|  function) |forward|deploy|rsync|scp|LLM_PROXY|REMOTE\"}"}, {"id": "call_972a830e661342b49a818665", "name": "load_memory", "arguments": "{\"name\":\"Clutch push/release conventions + auth reality\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now the second remote scheme is becoming clear. Key findings:\n\n1. `ui/main.js:20`: \"remote sessions: LLM via the tunnel's reverse forward (matches LLM_PROXY_REMOTE_PORT)\" — there's a reverse forward (-R)\n2. `ui/main.js:75-77`: \"the remote supervisor may have idle-exited: restart it through the tunnel and retry once... restarting remote supervisor\" via `tunnel.restartRemoteServer()` — **the supervisor RUNS ON THE REMOTE too!**\n3. `ui/main.js:53`: sessions have `kind: \"tunnel\"` vs local\n4. `llm-proxy.js:4`: \"reaches it via the SSH -R tunnel (no remote internet/key needed)\"\n5. supervisor.py docstring: \"Every UI window is a SESSION: the supervisor spawns one agent.server child... per session\" — and sessions can be local OR remote (\"locally, or over its own tunnel forward\")\n6. `ui/exec-bridge.js`: \"Loopback HTTP bridge exposing the ssh2 tunnel's remoteExec to the local Python agent (SshTransport)\"\n\nSo the TWO remote schemes are:\n\n**方案 A：SSH 降级层（exec bridge + RemoteWorkspace）** — the agent server runs LOCALLY on the user's machine; only file ops/commands are shipped to the remote via the exec bridge; remote needs nothing but sshd. \"degraded mode\" / \"degrade\" in UI terms.\n\n**方案 B：远程部署服务器（remote server / tunnel mode）** — the FULL agent server (supervisor + session children) is deployed ON the remote machine; the UI connects through the SSH tunnel; the supervisor on the remote spawns session children; the LLM API is reached from the remote via a REVERSE tunnel (-R) to the local llm-proxy (no remote internet/key needed).\n\nEvidence:\n- ui/main.js: `reconcileSession` tries `tunnel session start` → supervisor on remote (via tunnel) spawns a session; if remote supervisor idle-exited → `tunnel.restartRemoteServer()`; fallback to local session.\n- llm-proxy.js: remote reaches the LLM via SSH -R reverse forward (LLM_PROXY_REMOTE_PORT) — so the API key stays local.\n- workspace.py:58: \"Single source for the remote-wire limits, shared with ui/ssh-tunnel.js\"\n- supervisor.py:4-6: \"spawns one agent.server child (--port 0...) per session... The window then talks to its child DIRECTLY (locally, or over its own tunnel forward) — the supervisor never proxies traffic\"\n- supervisor.py:32-34: \"noclobber+TTL remains only for the execBridge-degraded path, where the agent process and the project files live on different machines.\" — this confirms: in the DEGRADED path (scheme A), agent process and files live on DIFFERENT machines; in the tunnel/remote-server path (scheme B), the agent process lives WITH the files.\n\nSo in scheme B, the whole python agent (server, tools, workspace, LLM client) runs remotely as a normal LOCAL setup from its own perspective — `LocalWorkspace`, local `localshell` (Linux), everything is local to the remote box. The UI (Electron) just reaches it over the tunnel.\n\nThat perfectly answers the user's earlier question too: in scheme B, the parts running on the remote server DON'T KNOW they're remote — they run as a completely ordinary local deployment (LocalWorkspace, posix shell), and only the Electron main process + ssh-tunnel.js + llm-proxy.js know about the tunneling.\n\nLet me verify a few more specifics:\n1. `ui/ssh-tunnel.js` — the tunnel implementation (connectTunnel, remoteExec, restartRemoteServer, openSessionForward, reverse forward for LLM)\n2. `ui/llm-proxy.js` — the LLM proxy and LLM_PROXY_REMOTE_PORT\n3. How the remote supervisor/server gets deployed — is there a deploy script? grep for restartRemoteServer implementation and any rsync/scp/push of the agent code to the remote.\n\nAlso check the memory \"Clutch push/release conventions + auth reality\" — that's about how releases get pushed, possibly related to deploying the server remotely. Let me load it since it's directly relevant (\"push/release conventions\" + \"auth reality\" sounds exactly like the remote-server deploy story).\n\nLet me read ssh-tunnel.js key functions and llm-proxy.js header, and load the memory."}
