in grep, but not the exact code)\n- parse_arguments import in loop.py / where ToolCallEvent is defined (events.py L69: `tool_calls: list[dict] # [{id,name,arguments}]`)\n- A concrete tool func signature: shell.run_command signature (L209) I have: `def run_command(workspace: Workspace, config: Config, command: str, cancel: threading.Event | None = None) -> dict:` and filesystem.read_file L21 (partial — multi-line signature)\n- The lambda adapters: `func=lambda sb, cfg, **kw: filesystem.read_file(sb, cfg, **kw)` — exact, have it\n\nSo I need to read loop.py around L200-230 to get the exact tool_calls → ToolCallEvent code. Let me read that. Also events.py ToolCallEvent definition (L69 area) to show the event shape.\n\nLet me read:\n1. loop.py offset 199 limit 32 (the tool_calls iteration + ToolCallEvent emit)\n2. events.py offset 60 limit 15 (ToolCallEvent dataclass)\n3. filesystem.py offset 21 limit 14 (read_file full signature)\n\nThen compose the answer as a numbered walk with exact code blocks, minimal commentary — the user wants the code trail. Keep prose tight. The answer should be a sequence of exact snippets with file:line references, showing:\n\n```\n模型输出 (finish_reason=tool_calls)\n  → loop.py: content, tool_calls, finish_reason = self._llm_call(msgs)\n  → for tc in tool_calls: emit ToolCallEvent(name=tc[\"name\"], arguments=tc[\"arguments\"])\n  → _execute_tool(ev): args = parse_arguments(ev.arguments)   # json.loads\n  → gate.require(ev.name, args_repr, workspace)\n  → registry.execute(workspace, config, ev.name, args, cancel)\n      → tool = self._tools[name]\n      → args = _coerce_types(tool, args)\n      → tool.func(workspace, config, **args)   # or cancel=cancel, **args\n          → lambda sb, cfg, **kw: filesystem.read_file(sb, cfg, **kw)\n              → def read_file(workspace, config, path, offset=None, limit=None, ...)\n```\n\nShow exactly where workspace/config come from (loop holds them; execute receives them from loop as self.workspace/self.con