{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789879058.9893084, "id": "77e3de3134b4", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_f0c651793c7f409eb511c67f", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"limit\":36,\"offset\":70,\"path\":\"agent/tools/transport.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_43bd97a83fd049afaf40e344", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"include\":\"*.py\",\"path\":\"agent/tools\",\"pattern\":\"def shell_path\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user is asking again: \"但是由于我们的适配，所以现在我们在win上使用的也是同一套shell逻辑是吗\" — \"But because of our adaptation, on Windows we're now using the same shell logic, right?\"\n\nThis was answered in previous sessions (per the compacted summary): platform decision is at localshell.py:77; Windows uses detected Git Bash with `bash -c <cmd>` (posix=True), so commands run under bash with POSIX dialect — same logic as Linux. But there are nuances:\n\n1. YES for the command text dialect: on Windows with Git Bash detected, commands are executed via `bash -c` and judged as POSIX (posix=True). So the model writes POSIX shell syntax and it works the same.\n2. BUT it's not 100% identical:\n   - The spawn mechanism differs: Linux = `shell=True` → /bin/sh (sh, not bash!); Windows = `bash.exe -c` (Git Bash, real bash). On Linux it's actually /bin/sh (POSIX sh) via shell=True. So the shell binary differs, but both speak POSIX.\n   - No bash on Windows → fallback to cmd.exe with posix=False — then it's NOT the same logic at all (cmd dialect, CommandLineToArgvW splitting for judging).\n   - Path translation: `/tmp` → `%TEMP%` via MSYS mapping (bash does this itself — MSYS path conversion), `/dev/null` → NUL. Actually the workspace.shell_path(tok) maps /tmp for the path-escape guard.\n   - WSL bash deliberately excluded (System32 bash.exe) because its filesystem is the Linux subsystem, not the workspace drive.\n   - Process tree killing: Windows uses taskkill /T /F vs POSIX start_new_session + kill process group.\n   - Text decoding pinned utf-8/replace because a Chinese Windows would default GBK.\n   - Frozen builds: subprocess execution of tools disabled, etc.\n\nLet me verify the current code state to answer precisely. From the compacted summary and what I read this session:\n\n- localshell.py:76-85 `_detect()`: POSIX host → `LocalShell(argv=None, posix=True, name=\"posix-sh\")`; Windows + probed bash → `LocalShell(argv=(cand, \"-c\"), posix=True, name=\"bash\")`; else → `LocalShell(argv=None, posix=False, name=\"cmd\")`.\n- transport.py:124-126: `Popen([*shell.argv, command] if shell.argv else command, shell=shell.argv is None, ...)`.\n- shell.py:212: `posix = workspace.exec_shell().posix` — \"the flavor of the shell that will PARSE this text: local host's decision for local workspaces, always POSIX for a remote (SSH) workspace\".\n- transport.py:110: \"on Windows a detected Git Bash via `bash -c <cmd>` (the agent speaks POSIX)\".\n- localshell.py:49-57: bash candidates; WSL excluded (88-96).\n- transport.py:132: `start_new_session=os.name == \"posix\"`; _kill_tree at transport.py:76 — on Windows taskkill /T /F.\n- transport.py:112-113: utf-8/replace pinning.\n- workspace.py:326 mentions `/tmp` mapping in a comment about shell_path — \"writes (`> /tmp/out.log` 2>&1 ...)\" — and shell.py:237-239 comment: \"on a Git-Bash host /tmp and /dev/null mean %TEMP% and the NUL device\".\n\nSo the answer structure:\n\n**对，命令语言层面是同一套（POSIX 方言），但底层执行机制有三层差异。**\n\nTable:\n- 判定/编写方言: Linux posix=True (sh) / Win+bash posix=True (bash) — SAME: model always writes POSIX text\n- 实际解析器 binary: /bin/sh vs Git Bash bash.exe — DIFFERENT binary, same dialect\n- spawn 方式: shell=True (string→CreateProcess→cmd.exe→sh -c... actually on POSIX shell=True → /bin/sh -c) vs argv=[bash, -c] — different mechanism, same effect\n- 兜底: Windows 无 bash → cmd.exe, posix=False — NOT the same; that's the degradation path\n- Remote (SSH): always posix=True regardless\n\nSo \"同一套\" = the *authoring* logic (what the model writes) and the *judging* logic (classify_command, path escape guard with shlex, /tmp mapping) is unified under POSIX. The *execution* plumbing differs (bash -c prefix, taskkill, utf-8 pinning, start_new_session posix-only).\n\nShould I verify anything else? I have localshell.py, transport.py, shell.py read this session. The key facts are verified. Maybe check _kill_tree at transport.py:76 to quote exact code? The summary said taskkill /T /F. Let me read transport.py:70-105 to have exact code for the kill difference, and maybe workspace.py shell_path for /tmp mapping. That would strengthen the answer with file:line. Let me do one quick parallel read: transport.py:70-105 and workspace.py shell_path (need to grep for def shell_path).\n\nActually the question is a yes/no confirmation question. I can answer from what I have, but adding exact kill-tree and shell_path evidence makes it concrete. Let me do a quick grep for shell_path def and read transport.py:70-105."}
