skill 不进** — same.\n10. **不自带 run/sh** — no built-in shell subcommand.\n11. **截断进 CLI** — truncation (read_max_chars) goes INTO the CLI (--max-chars flag). Wait, Q11 was \"截断职责：read_max_chars 这类截断进 CLI（--max-chars，默认值从配置文件读）还是留给调用方？\" — answer \"进\" = goes into CLI. But Q27 answer asks what config items exist... and Q26 强制utf-8. Hmm, so --max-chars flag exists; default value — from Q27 the user asks what the config items are, meaning they haven't decided config yet. Default can be a built-in constant for now (e.g. current host default 20000). Actually since it's C++ zero-dep, config file parsing would need to be hand-rolled JSON... Q27 user asks \"配置项有哪些?\" — they want me to enumerate. I should answer that in my response: propose the minimal config item set.\n12. **--path x** — space-separated flag style.\n13. **--json** — global --json flag switches to envelope output.\n14. **按照unix标准细分** — exit codes follow Unix standards. I need to define this: sysexits.h conventions? 0 success, 1 generic error, 2 usage error (like getopt/EX_USAGE), 64 EX_USAGE, 66 EX_NOINPUT, 69 EX_IOERR, etc. I should explain BSD sysexits in my reply and propose the mapping. The user asked \"按照unix标准细分\" so they want e.g. EX_USAGE=64, EX_NOINPUT=66, EX_UNAVAILABLE=69, EX_SOFTWARE=70, EX_IOERR=74 etc.\n15. **你来决定** (diff contract) — user delegates the diff contract decision to me. I'll decide: unified diff in stdout (human mode) / diff field in JSON envelope, with --diff flag? Let me keep it simple: write/edit print unified diff to stdout in human mode by default? Or to keep \"content channel\" clean... Previous design discussion: envelope {content, error, diff}. I decide: --json envelope includes \"diff\" field; human mode prints unified diff after a header line. And no separate --diff flag in v1 (keep minimal). Actually let me think about what's most Unix-y: tools like `patch` output diffs to stdout. write_file's result to the model should contain a compact summary + diff. I'll decide: human mode prints diff to stdout; JSON mode envelope has content (summary) + diff (unified diff string). Also --no-diff? Keep it minimal: no flag; always produce diff on write/edit.\n16. **前者** (error message attribution) — \"CLI 出结构化错误码+简讯（host 负责渲染成模型/人类文案）\". So CLI outputs structured error code + brief message; host renders model-facing text. \n17. **Question about error code table** — user asks: what is an error code table? Is it currently custom? Is there a universal standard? I need to answer this. Current state: no error code table in clutch — errors are rendered prose from errors/*.md templates; exit codes: cli.py exits 0 whenever JSON produced, non-zero marks crash. There IS a universal standard: BSD sysexits.h (EX_USAGE=64, EX_DATAERR=65, EX_NOINPUT=66, EX_NOUSER=67, EX_NOHOST=68, EX_UNAVAILABLE=69, EX_SOFTWARE=70, EX_OSERR=71, EX_OSFILE=72, EX_CANTCREAT=73, EX_IOERR=74, EX_TEMPFAIL=75, EX_PROTOCOL=76, EX_NOPERM=77, EX_CONFIG=78) and POSIX shells reserve 2 for misuse (bash: 2 = misuse of shell builtins), 126 = command found but not executable, 127 = command not found, 128+n = killed by signal n. So I can propose using sysexits for clutch-workspace.\n18. **Question about drift, schema/argparse** — user asks what drift means, why it exists, what schema/argparse are. I need to explain: \n    - schema = JSON Schema in the Tool.parameters (the contract given to the model describing what arguments exist)\n    - argparse = the CLI's argument parsing definitions (what flags the binary accepts)\n    - Two implementations of the same truth in two codebases; when one changes (e.g. rename --offset to --start) and the other doesn't, they diverge = drift. \n    - Solution options: single spec file generating both; or `workspace schema read_file` self-description; or since Q18 asks, just explain and note the risk is accepted/mitigated by tests. Given C++ zero-dep and Q18 being a question, I should explain and then propose how to handle: for now, hand-maintained both sides + a contract test? Or `workspace schema` subcommand? Since user delegated nothing here (they asked questions), I explain and offer options, letting them decide later — but I shouldn't block implementation on it. Actually since they said \"先实现\" for the host-connection stuff, I can note: the schema drift issue is a host-side concern for later (Q33: 不用管host，先实现). So for M1 I just build the CLI; the JSON Schema for the model stays in clutch host and must be updated manually to match. I'll note that.\n19. **Install independent from clutch** — despite Q1 being C++, installation should be independent from clutch (standalone binary, not bundled into clutch... or at least installable separately). For host integration later (Q31/Q32/Q33: don't consider now).\n20. **什么叫可配置?** — user asks what \"configurable binary name\" means. I explain: when host generates the command text, the first token is the program name (`clutch-workspace ...`); \"configurable\" meant whether the host should look it up from env/settings instead of hardcoding. Since Q31/Q33 say don't worry about host connection now, this is deferred. I just explain the term.\n21. **直接全切，做好版本回溯准备就行** — switch over entirely at once (no dual-track period), keep version rollback readiness (git tags / release rollback).\n22. **保持实现独立** — keep implementations independent: new project carries its own copies of shared utilities (no shared package). So split_command etc. duplicated where needed.\n23. **可以上 pytest** — pytest allowed in new repo. Note: current host repo has no pytest; the new independent repo CAN use it (its own tooling). On this Windows machine, is pytest available? The host repo's .venv may not have pytest. New repo would need its own venv. I'll note that.\n24. **留host** — chat-mode whitelist classification stays in host. Confirmed.\n25. **独立** — independent Python version baseline (new repo defines its own... but it's C++ now! So .python-version doesn't apply. Instead: C++ standard version, e.g. C++17/20, compiler