, error brief messages (structured-ish, host renders later — but standalone humans need reasonable messages too: brief messages on stderr).\n\nBUT: I'm in chat mode. Read-only. I cannot create the new repository or write files. The system prompt says: if task requires writing files, tell the user clearly that it needs work mode, and they can switch with the mode button next to Run.\n\nWait — but there's a huge practical question: can I even build C++ here? The environment is Windows host with Git Bash. Is there a C++ compiler available? Need to check: g++/clang++/MSVC (cl). Git Bash doesn't ship a compiler. MinGW might be installed. Let me check availability in chat mode (read-only commands are fine: which g++, clang++, cl, gcc, cmake, make, ninja). That's important for the plan: the build toolchain. If no compiler, we need to install one (work mode) or use another approach.\n\nAlso C++ + zero deps means I must implement JSON output by hand (simple string building/escaping), file I/O (std::filesystem since C++17), and UTF-8 handling (bytes pass-through mostly; line splitting careful). grep = regex: std::regex is in the standard library (zero extra deps) — OK though std::regex is slow and has quirks; ECMAScript grammar by default. Current host grep tool uses Python re syntax. Drift between Python regex and std::regex ECMAScript — flag this as a known semantic difference. For v1 maybe acceptable; note it.\n\nedit_file semantics: current filesystem.py edit_file — I haven't read it this session. It does find/replace with expected_count? I should read it to replicate behavior. Since we're implementing in the new repo anyway, and user said \"全部转移\", I should faithfully port the four tools' semantics. Let me read filesystem.py fully in this session to extract exact semantics (I've only read lines 18-35). Also read the grep part. That's needed for the port spec. But implementation happens in work mode (new repo — wait, where would the new repo live? \"独立新仓库\" — but within this workspace I can only create files under the current workspace root. The new repo would need to be a sibling directory... The workspace root is the clutch repo itself. Creating a new directory `clutch-workspace/` inside the clutch repo would nest a repo inside a repo — messy but workable as \"独立新仓库\" staged inside the workspace then moved out, OR the user opens a new workspace for it. Hmm. I should ask/flag: where do I create it? Options: (a) subdirectory `clutch-workspace/` inside current workspace (can later be split out), (b) user opens new workspace pointing at the new repo. Given Q2 独立新仓库 and my constraint (tools operate within workspace root), I should flag this as the one remaining logistical question: I can scaffold it as a folder in the current workspace now, and it can be pushed as its own repo later; or they give me a separate workspace. I'll flag it but propose default: scaffold inside current workspace as clutch-workspace/ (git init inside it for independence), since that's executable now.\n\nSo my response should:\n1. Answer their three questions (Q17 error codes/sysexits, Q18 schema/argparse drift, Q20 configurable name meaning, Q27 config items) — these were genuine questions from them.\n2. Restate the frozen decision table briefly.\n3. Flag the C++-specific decisions I need or propose defaults: C++ standard (C++17 needed for filesystem; C++20 if available), build system (CMake vs plain Makefile vs single build script — zero-dep suggests minimal: a Makefile or build.sh/build.bat; CMake is standard but adds a dep-ish; user said zero deps — for build system, propose CMake or plain make; ask), compiler on this machine (check availability now), regex dialect difference (Python re → std::regex ECMAScript), JSON hand-rolled.\n4. Ask the logistical question: where to create the repo (inside current workspace as subfolder vs new workspace).\n5. Tell them: to implement, switch to work mode.\n\nActually the instruction says don't make unilateral decisions — but they delegated Q15 to me (diff contract — I decide and state it). For build system and C++ standard, I should present options but these are critical-path; I can check the environment first to inform. Let me check compilers now with run_command (read-only: `which`).\n\nLet me run: which g++ gcc clang++ cc cl cmake make ninja mingw32-make python; g++ --version if found.\n\nAlso check whether edit_file semantics need reading: read filesystem.py fully now (188 lines for read_file portion... the file — actually filesystem.py is 188 lines? That was for read_file's view earlier: \"showing lines 18-35 of 188\" — so filesystem.py is 188 lines total containing all four tools? That seems short for 4 tools but plausible. Let me read the whole filesystem.py in this session to have exact semantics for the port. It's important for writing the spec. 188 lines is cheap. Let me do it.\n\nPlan for tool calls (parallel):\n- run_command: check toolchain (which + versions) — one command string with && chaining? Chat mode whitelist allows which; chained might trip the write-detector? It's all read-only; `which g++ gcc clang++ cmake make ninja; g++ --version 2>/dev/null | head -1` — semicolons should be fine (not writes). The earlier notes said composite commands get rejected in chat mode (\"复合 shell 命令会被拒（写段检测）\"). Hmm — memory says: \"chat 模式：复合 shell 命令会被拒（写段检测），改用 grep 工具\". So avoid chaining. Single command per call. I can make multiple run_command calls in one block (independent): `which g++`, `which cmake`, `which make`, `which clang++`, `which ninja`. That's 5 calls... or one `which g++ gcc clang++ cmake make ninja cc` — `which` with multiple args is a single command, not composite. That should pass.\n- read_file filesystem.py (full, 188 lines)\n\nDo these in one parallel block. Then compose the response.\n\nAlso maybe check pyproject for anything relevant — not needed.\n\nLet me also think about what the spec for each subcommand looks like (from filesystem.py) so the plan is concrete:\n\nread_file(path, max_chars=0, offset=0, limit=0):\n- directory → entry listing (dirs end with '/')\n- offset/limit line-based reads\n- max_chars truncation (default 20000 from host config → CLI default constant 20000, --max-chars)\n- output hints \"(showing lines X-Y of N; use offset=Y+1 to continue)\"\n- returns content; errors: not found, protected (host concern now — pure capability so no protected), etc.\n\ngrep(pattern, path=\".\", include glob): recursive regex search, output \"path:  Line N: text\", capped 100 hits, skip hidden/binary.\n\nwrite_file(path, content, create_parents?): writes, returns diff (old→new), protected check (host concern → dropped per Q7 pure capability).\n\nedit_file: exact block replacement — I recall from earlier sessions it has old_string/new_string with uniqueness/occurrence count (expected occurrences). Need to read to confirm.\n\nI'll read filesystem.py now. Note filesystem.py earlier read showed lines 18-35 of 188 — that included read_file. The whole file is 188 lines. Let me read it all (offset 1 limit 188) — one call.\n\nAlso, do I need shell.py semantics? No — run_command stays in host (Q10).\n\ngrep.py doesn't exist as separate file — grep is in filesystem.py? From the registry: Tool(name=\"grep\"...) func probably lambda to filesystem.grep. Yes likely. Reading filesystem.py confirms.\n\nResponse structure (Chinese):\n\n1. 三个问题的解答：\n   - Q17 错误码表: explain; current = no table, prose errors + cli.py 0/non0; universal standard = BSD sysexits + POSIX shell conventions (126/127/128+n); propose the mapping table for clutch-workspace.\n   - Q18 schema/argparse/漂移: explain JSON Schema vs argparse, drift = two sources of truth diverging; in our new split the schema lives in host (registry.py parameters), argparse in C++ CLI; mitigation options; per Q33 defer, manual sync + contract test note.\n   - Q20 可配置二进制名: explain (host-side concern, deferred anyway).\n   - Q27 配置项: propose v1 = zero config files, flags only; enumerate knobs: --max-chars (default 20000), that's basically it. JSON/paths are per-invocation.\n2. 冻结的决策表 (compact).\n3. 我替你定的 (Q15 diff contract) + C++ 细节需要拍板的少量新题:\n   - C++ 标准: C++17 (std::filesystem) 最低; C++20 可选\n   - 构建系统: CMake vs 裸 Makefile vs build 脚本 (zero-dep 精神 → 我可以给建议但让你拍板; since they delegate... they didn't delegate this. Ask: propose default CMake (industry standard, works with MSVC/MinGW) or single Makefile. Actually for a single-target small binary, I'd suggest a plain Makefile + compile_flags; but Windows + MSVC... which compiler exists? Check env result first.)\n   - regex 方言漂移: Python re vs std::regex(ECMAScript) — acknowledge; v1 options: std::regex (zero-dep) with documented dialect; \n   - JSON: 手写转义器 (trivial, zero-dep fine)\n   - 新仓库落点: inside current workspace as clutch-workspace/ (git init) vs separate workspace\n4. 环境检查结果 (compiler availability) → affects whether I can build/test here.\n5. 下一步: switch to work mode; M1 file tree sketch.\n\nLength: substantial but organized. Let me do the reads/commands now."}
