{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789891270.176319, "id": "f1b1bd422f2f", "content": "", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_61b01c5b985644c18d2166c1", "name": "save_memory", "arguments": "{\"title\":\"clutch-workspace C++ spin-off: frozen decisions\",\"content\":\"User decided (34-question round) to spin tools out as independent C++ tool:\\n\\n- Name/repo: `clutch-workspace`, independent new repo (repo physical location TBD — likely scaffolded as subfolder of clutch workspace, own git; confirm before M1).\\n- Language/toolchain: C++ rewrite, zero dependencies (STL/OS APIs only). Machine probe result: cmake+make+ninja present, NO compiler (no g++/gcc/clang++/cl; VS/18 dir is empty leftover; only arm-none-eabi cross tools). Toolchain install = open critical-path question (options: MSYS2 mingw-w64 / VS Build Tools / w64devkit / zig cc).\\n- Scope: exactly 4 subcommands = read_file, grep, write_file, edit_file (faithful port of agent/tools/filesystem.py semantics). web_search/web_fetch/memory×3/load_skill stay in host. No built-in run/sh subcommand.\\n- Philosophy: pure capability (no containment/protected-path logic inside CLI; security entirely caller's side; chat-mode whitelist stays in host). Internal component, no docs, \\\"相对独立但还是clutch的一部分\\\".\\n- CLI contract: flags `--path x` (space-separated); global `--json` → envelope {content, error, diff}; exit codes per Unix/BSD sysexits (my proposal: 0 ok / 64 EX_USAGE / 65 EX_DATAERR(edit not found or ambiguous) / 66 EX_NOINPUT / 70 EX_SOFTWARE / 74 EX_IOERR / 130=128+SIGINT reserved); CLI emits structured error code + brief message, host renders model-facing prose (errors/*.md stay in host).\\n- My delegated decision (Q15): human mode prints summary + unified diff to stdout; --json envelope carries diff field.\\n- read truncation lives in CLI (--max-chars, default 20000; no config file in v1 — all knobs are flags).\\n- Integration with host: explicitly deferred (Q21 direct full cut with git-tag rollback readiness, no dual-track; Q31/32/33 don't touch clutch now, connection later). Remote: remote servers will install workspace binary (and future tool binaries) — remote service part stays in clutch; connection design deferred.\\n- Shared utilities (e.g. split_command): implementations stay independent (duplication accepted).\\n- New repo may use pytest (host repo stays runner-less). Python 3.14 at /c/Python314 available for test harness.\\n- Known C++ port risks flagged to user: std::regex ECMAScript dialect vs Python re (no lookbehind!); hand-written unified diff (LCS/Myers, n=3); forced UTF-8 on Windows (binary file I/O, CP_UTF8 argv/console); hand-rolled JSON writer; big args (write --content) vs Windows 32K cmdline limit — proposal: value `-` = read stdin, pending user confirm.\\n- Exit-code-table & schema/argparse drift explained to user; drift mitigation (workspace schema subcmd / codegen / contract tests) deferred to integration phase.\"}"}], "reasoning": "The `18` directory is empty (or ls showed nothing because the dir listing was empty). Actually `ls` of `/c/Program Files (x86)/Microsoft Visual Studio/18` returned no output — directory exists but empty (or only hidden). And find found no cl.exe. So no usable MSVC either. The \"18\" folder is probably a leftover from a partial VS 2026 install/update.\n\nSo the environment has: cmake, make, ninja — but NO C++ compiler (no g++, gcc, clang++, cl, no cygwin g++, no arm native; only arm-none-eabi cross compilers which can't build a Windows host CLI).\n\nConclusion: toolchain must be installed. Options to present to the user:\n(a) MSYS2 + mingw-w64 gcc (pacman install; produces native windows GNU binary, static libgcc/libstdc++ possible: -static) — the classic choice; needs MSYS2 setup (~400MB) or standalone w64devkit/niXman mingw builds.\n(b) Visual Studio Build Tools (winget install Microsoft.VisualStudio.2022.BuildTools + VCTools workload; several GB; produces MSVC binary, /MT static CRT) — heavyweight but native.\n(c) zig cc (download single tarball ~45MB, no admin; zig c++ acts as clang++ cross compiler targeting windows-gnu or windows-msvc; can produce static binaries; zero-install into PATH) — lightest path; unusual but excellent for this purpose.\n(d) w64devkit — single 7z ~100MB, includes gcc, make; portable, no admin. Very aligned with \"零依赖/独立\" ethos.\n\nI'll present these with a recommendation... but the user said don't make decisions unilaterally — for toolchain they haven't delegated, so present options + my lean (they can say \"你来定\"). I'll mark it as the ONE remaining critical-path question blocking M1, along with repo location.\n\nRepo location: current workspace root is the clutch repo. Options: (a) create `clutch-workspace/` subfolder inside current workspace, git init inside for independence, later push as own remote; (b) user opens a separate workspace for the new repo. I'll propose (a) as default since I can act immediately, flag the nested-repo caveat (clutch's .gitignore should ignore it or it just stays untracked... actually a nested git repo appears as untracked dir; harmless; can add to .gitignore).\n\nNow the response. Answer their questions:\n\n**Q17 什么是错误码表？目前是自定义的吗？有没有通用标准？**\n- 错误码表 = a documented mapping of exit codes → error classes, so callers can react mechanically.\n- 现状: clutch has none — errors are prose (errors/*.md rendered text fed to model); cli.py convention: exit 0 whenever a result was produced, non-zero only = crash before JSON. So \"errorness\" travels in the JSON `error` field, not the exit code. 自定义的、且只有一个约定。\n- 通用标准 exists: BSD sysexits.h (EX_USAGE 64 ... EX_CONFIG 78), plus POSIX shell conventions (0 ok; 1 generic fail; 2 misuse of shell builtin; 126 not executable; 127 not found; 128+N killed by signal N). GNU coreutils mostly use 1/2 loosely. propose sysexits-based mapping table for clutch-workspace:\n  - 0 EX_OK success\n  - 64 EX_USAGE wrong flags/unknown subcommand (host maps → invalid_arguments, feed back to model for self-correction)\n  - 65 EX_DATAERR (edit_file: old_string not found/ambiguous — data-level failure)\n  - 66 EX_NOINPUT (file not found)\n  - 70 EX_SOFTWARE (internal invariant broken)\n  - 74 EX_IOERR (read/write failures)\n  - 130 = 128+SIGINT (killed) — reserved for future cancellation\n  Unless objection, implement this.\n\n**Q18 什么漂移？schema/argparse 是什么？**\n- schema = Tool.parameters JSON Schema in registry.py — the contract the LLM sees (names/types/descriptions of args).\n- argparse = the C++ CLI's own flag definitions (what `clutch-workspace read_file` accepts).\n- 漂移 = same truth maintained twice in two codebases; rename/add a flag on one side, forget the other → model emits args the CLI rejects (or vice versa). It exists because the two sides live in different repos/languages with no shared source.\n- 缓解选项: (a) `workspace schema <subcmd>` self-description + host fetch; (b) single spec file, codegen both sides; (c) 手动同步 + contract tests. Per Q31/33 defer host connection; note for later. Not blocking M1.\n\n**Q20 什么叫可配置（二进制名）？**\n- Host generates command text whose first token is the program name; \"可配置\" = whether that name comes from a host setting/env (so users can rename or point at a specific install) instead of being hardcoded. Host-side concern; deferred per Q31/33.\n\n**Q27 配置项有哪些？**\n- My proposal: v1 = ZERO config files (fits zero-dep/pure-capability/internal). All behavior via flags; the only knobs:\n  - read_file: --path (required), --max-chars (default 20000), --offset, --limit\n  - grep: --pattern (required), --path (default \".\"), --include\n  - write_file: --path, --content\n  - edit_file: --path, --old-string, --new-string  — hmm wait: old_string/new_string can be huge multi-line blocks. Passing via argv has the 32K limit (registry.py:403 mentioned argv 32K limit!) and quoting pain. The current host passes them via **args in-process / stdin JSON to subprocess pilot. For CLI: multi-KB content as argv value is legal-ish on POSIX (ARG_MAX is MBs on Linux; Windows CreateProcess 32K total command line!) — Windows 32K limit is real for the whole command line. write_file --content with a big file (10KB+) → risk. Mitigation: read values from stdin or a temp file when they'd be huge; or always allow `--content -` meaning stdin. This is a REAL design point to flag: **大参数通道** — propose: flags accept `-` value = read from stdin; or `@file` syntax; decide at M1. I'll flag as question W1 with default proposal (value `-` = stdin; host may pipe). Actually keep it simple: I propose `--content -`/`--new-string -` read from stdin, and host-free usage can heredoc. Present it, let user confirm.\n- If later a config file is wanted: single key = default max-chars; defer.\n\nScope confirmation: 4 subcommands = read_file, grep, write_file, edit_file (per Q6 \"全部转移\" + Q8/9 exclusions). Directory listing rides read_file (no separate list tool).\n\nQ15 (delegated) my decision: unified diff.\n- Human mode: write/edit print summary line + unified diff to stdout.\n- --json: {\"content\": summary, \"error\": false, \"diff\": \"<unified>\"}.\n- read/grep: diff=\"\".\n- read hints preserved (\"use offset=N to continue\") — they're part of content.\n\nNow the port-fidelity table (semantics to replicate from filesystem.py):\n- read_file: dir→sorted listing \"(empty directory)\" / line-range errors on dir / truncation marker with next_offset / line numbers \"N: line\" / range-over-budget → ERROR not silent / continuation hint\n- grep: 100-hit cap + footer, group per file \"path:\", \"  Line N: text[:300]\", skip hidden+binary, include glob, \"(no matches)\"\n- write: diff + adds/dels summary or chars for create\n- edit: 0 occurrences / >1 occurrences distinct errors, single replace, diff\n- Dropped per Q7: resolve containment, is_protected, snapshot, external-path absolute header (just print given path).\n\nC++ zero-dep implications (3 risks):\n1. regex dialect: std::regex ECMAScript vs Python re — no lookbehind in ECMAScript (!), no (?P<>), different escape quirks; performance. Document dialect; tests lock common cases. (Alternative: hand-rolled matcher — no.)\n2. unified diff: hand-write LCS/Myers (~100-150 lines), context n=3 to match difflib.\n3. Windows UTF-8: force UTF-8 in/out (SetConsoleOutputCP/CP_UTF8, wmain or Convertargv with CP_UTF8; std::filesystem paths via u8 conversion). Byte-faithful file I/O (no CRLF translation: open binary).\n4. JSON: hand-escaped writer only (no parser needed in v1).\n\nToolchain question (new critical path): present 4 options with sizes/traits + note env findings (cmake/make/ninja present; zero compilers; VS/18 empty leftover). Lean: w64devkit or zig cc for lightness, MSYS2 mingw for mainstream, MSVC Build Tools for mainstream-native. Let user pick; note CMake generator implications.\n\nRepo location question: propose scaffold at clutch-workspace/ inside current workspace w/ own git; or separate workspace. \n\npytest: new repo its own venv (`python -m venv .venv; pip install pytest`) — Python 3.14 present. Q23 said 可以上.\n\nEnd: after toolchain + repo-location answered (or delegated), switch to work mode and I start M1 (scaffold + read_file + json + exit codes + tests).\n\nAlso worth noting explicitly: Q21 全切 + Q31/32/33 → clutch repo untouched in this effort; version rollback = git tag on host before any future integration (none now since nothing changes).\n\nStructure the reply with headers, tables, keep tight. No more tool calls after this. Also save memory? The system says save durable facts. This is a major project decision set (C++ rewrite, clutch-workspace, zero-dep, pure capability, etc.). There are existing memories like \"No-bias principle\", \"MCP removed\". This decision set is durable across sessions — worth saving. Memory titles exist already; I can add a new one: \"clutch-workspace C++ spin-off decisions\". Yes — save_memory with the full decision table + open questions. That's a legitimate durable fact for future sessions. Do it before final answer (tool call), then final text.\n\nLet me write the memory content compactly but completely."}
