ative to CWD.\n- Directory detection: host used workspace.list which raised NotADirectoryError for files. In pure Python: `if p.is_dir():` → entries = sorted children; dirs end with '/'; empty → \"(empty directory)\". Note host's workspace.list EXCLUDED protected files (host concern — dropped). sorted(entries): host workspace.list returns names (strings) relative? Let me check workspace.list signature: `def list(self, path: str) -> list[str]:` — returns entry names (dirs end with '/'). Sorted in read_file: `\"\\n\".join(sorted(entries))`. For CLI: `[e.name + ('/' if e.is_dir() else '') for e in p.iterdir()]` sorted by name. Host sorted the strings — with '/' suffixes mixed in, sorting may differ slightly (e.g. \"foo/\" vs \"foo.txt\": '/' (0x2F) < '.' (0x2E)? No: '.' is 0x2E, '/' is 0x2F, so \"foo.\" < \"foo/\"). Keep same approach: append suffix then sort — matches host behavior since host workspace.list also returned suffixed names then read_file sorted them. \n\nWait, actually did host workspace.list return suffixed names? From memory of workspace.py summary: \"Directory entries (dirs end with '/')\" (abstract list docstring at workspace.py:298). Yes — suffixed, then sorted. Mirror it.\n\n- offset/limit on directory → CommandError(64? which code?) — host message: \"ERROR: cannot read a line range of a directory; list it without offset/limit\". Which sysexits? This is a data/usage issue... The user provided conflicting args semantics. I'd classify EX_USAGE (64) — the request itself is invalid. Hmm, or EX_DATAERR. Usage = command line wrong; this is semantically invalid combination — closer to usage. I'll pick EX_USAGE and note it. Actually wait — this is a decision point! The user said: surface decision points, don't silently execute. Is exit-code classification for each error a decision point? The overall mapping was approved (\"不反对就按此实现\" — user replied \"按照unix标准细分\" earlier and then \"就这样吧\"). The fine-grained classification of individual errors (which error gets which code) is implementation detail within the approved frame. But there are a few genuinely ambiguous ones: \n  - dir + offset/limit → 64 vs 65\n  - range exceeds budget → host ERROR message; classify 65 EX_DATAERR (the requested range can't be served)? It's the data being too big vs request invalid... I'd say 65.\n  - invalid regex (grep) → 64 usage? or 65 data? Pattern is data from model → 65? Hmm. GNU grep uses 2 for bad regex... but our standard: I'd use EX_USAGE? Actually a bad regex is a data error in the pattern argument → 65.\n  - I need to not over-ask. The mapping frame was approved; individual assignments are part of implementation. But the user explicitly said \"碰到需要决策的技术点一定让我决策\". Classification of ambiguous errors is borderline. Strategy: present a complete error-code assignment table in my final report and flag the 2-3 genuinely ambiguous ones for the user to confirm — implementing with my best-guess while flagging. That respects \"don't silently execute\" for real decisions while not stalling on trivia. Actually — better: since user emphasized strongly, I'll implement M1 with the table and EXPLICITLY list \"分类待你复核\" items in the report. The mapping frame itself was pre-approved.\n\n- Read file text: host workspace.read read TEXT (utf-8?). Presumably utf-8 with replacement? Need to check workspace.read implementation... LocalWorkspace.read — I haven't read its body. It likely reads text with utf-8/replace or strict? Important for fidelity: binary files → host? grep skips binary; read_file on a .pyc? The host read probably does Path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=?) — unknown. I should check quickly with grep/read in work mode. Let me read workspace.py read implementation.\n\n- Truncation: char-based (Python str len). `if len(text) > limit_chars:` head = text[:limit_chars]; next_offset = head.count(\"\\n\") + 1; append `\\n... [truncated, file is N chars; use offset=X to continue]`. Port directly.\n\n- _read_range: splitlines; 1-based; body