{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789892740.7654784, "id": "4a8b901bd3b0", "tool_call_id": "call_3ec0e06dbfa54015ba3a65db", "content": "349:     def read(self, path: str) -> str:\n350:         p = self.resolve(path)\n351:         if not p.is_file():\n352:             raise FileNotFoundError(path)\n353:         return p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n354: \n355:     def read_range(self, path: str, lo: int, hi: int) -> bytes:\n356:         p = self.resolve(path)\n357:         with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n358:             f.seek(lo)\n359:             return f.read(hi - lo)\n360: \n361:     def size(self, path: str) -> int:\n362:         return os.path.getsize(self.resolve(path))\n363: \n364:     def write(self, path: str, content: str) -> None:\n365:         p = self.resolve(path)\n366:         p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n367:         # newline=\"\\n\": byte-exact on every host. The .clc project file is\n368:         # byte-addressed (offsets + in-place header updates), and the SSH bridge\n369:         # writes LF, so the local backend must not let Windows text mode expand\n370:         # every \\n into \\r\\n (that desynchronizes every stored offset).\n371:         p.write_text(content, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")\n372: \n373:     def list(self, path: str) -> list[str]:\n374:         p = self.resolve(path)\n375:         if not p.is_dir():\n376:             raise NotADirectoryError(path)\n377:         return sorted(f.name + (\"/\" if f.is_dir() else \"\") for f in self.visible_entries(p))\n378: \n379:     def append_line(self, path: str, line: str) -> None:\n380:         p = self.resolve(path)\n381:         # one LF terminator per line (the remote bridge's `printf '%s\\n'` shape):\n382:         # the .clc JSONL offsets count exactly one newline byte per event\n383:         with open(p, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as f:\n384:             f.write(line + \"\\n\")\n385: \n386:     def write_at(self, path: str, offset: int, data: bytes) -> None:\n387:         p = self.resolve(path)\n388:         with open(p, \"r+b\") as f:\n389:             f.seek(offset)\n390:             f.write(data)\n391: \n392:     def grep(self, pattern: str, path: str = \".\", include: str | None = None) -> list[tuple[str, int, str]]:\n393:         rx = re.compile(pattern)\n394:         root = self.resolve(path)\n395:         files = [root] if root.is_file() else self._grep_files(root)\n396:         out: list[tuple[str, int, str]] = []\n397:         for f in files:\n398:             if self.is_protected(f):\n399:                 continue\n400:             rel = str(f.relative_to(self.root))\n401:             if include and not (fnmatch.fnmatch(f.name, include) or fnmatch.fnmatch(rel, include)):\n402:                 continue\n403:             if self._is_binary(f):\n404:                 continue\n405:             try:\n406:                 with open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") as fh:\n407:                     for i, line in enumerate(fh, 1):\n408:                         if rx.search(line):\n409:                             out.append((rel, i, line.rstrip(\"\\n\")))\n410:                             if len(out) >= 100:\n411:                                 return out\n412:             except OSError:\n413:                 continue\n414:         return out\n415: \n416:     def _grep_files(self, dirpath: Path) -> list[Path]:\n417:         files: list[Path] = []\n418:         for ent in sorted(dirpath.iterdir()):\n419:             if ent.name.startswith(\".\") or ent.name == \"__pycache__\":\n420:                 continue\n421:             if ent.is_dir():\n422:                 files.extend(self._grep_files(ent))\n423:             elif ent.is_file():\n424:                 files.append(ent)\n425:         return files\n426: \n427:     @staticmethod\n428:     def _is_binary(p: Path) -> bool:\n429:         try:\n430:             with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n431:                 return b\"\\x00\" in f.read(1024)\n432:         except OSError:\n433:             return True\n434: \n435: \n436: # Minimal sshd drops a single exec over ~8KB: writes are chunked below it;\n437: # limits come from transport_defaults.json (shared with ui/ssh-tunnel.js).\n438: _EXEC_CHUNK_BYTES = int(_DEFAULTS[\"exec_chunk_bytes\"])\n439: _EXEC_MAX_COMMAND_BYTES = int(_DEFAULTS[\"exec_max_command_bytes\"])\n440: \n441: # generous per-op timeout for internal remote file ops (read/write/list/append)\n442: _REMOTE_IO_TIMEOUT = 60.0\n443: # how much of a failed exec's stderr/stdout to show in the raised error\n444: _ERR_SNIPPET = 300\n445: # undo stack depth per file (snapshots before each overwrite)\n446: _MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE = 5\n447: \n448: \n449: class RemoteWorkspace(Workspace):\n450:     \"\"\"Workspace whose files live on a remote host reached over the exec bridge.\n451: \n452:     Every exec starts a fresh shell, so read/write/list use absolute paths and\n453:     run() prefixes `cd '<root>' &&` for the same cwd semantics as LocalWorkspace.\n454:     File operations are plain POSIX sh — cat, ls, and `printf '%s'` appends (no\n455:     base64, no SFTP) — so any device that can run an sshd shell works. Writes and\n456:     appends are chunked so no single exec command exceeds the sshd's limit.\n457:     \"\"\"\n458: \n459:     def __init__(self, root: str | None, bridge_url: str) -> None:\n460:         super().__init__(root, transport=SshTransport(bridge_url))\n461:         self._remote_home: str | None = None\n462: \n463:     def _root_path(self, root: str) -> PurePath:\n... (showing lines 349-463 of 715; use offset=464 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789893010.7957752, "id": "204ac8ecb34a", "content": "工具链与语义基准都齐了（hatchling + ruff 120 + py310 镜像 host 约定；`read`/`write`/`list`/`grep` 四个实现体已到手）。开始 M1：先建骨架，检查 pytest。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_935063d90bc1408a9a48e5b2", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"mkdir -p clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace clutch-workspace/tests && python -m pytest --version\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now I have everything for a faithful port:\n\n**LocalWorkspace.read**: `p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")` — universal newlines ON (read_text default), utf-8/replace. FileNotFoundError if not is_file.\n\n**LocalWorkspace.write**: mkdir parents; `p.write_text(content, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")` — LF byte-exact.\n\n**LocalWorkspace.list**: NotADirectoryError if not dir; `sorted(f.name + (\"/\" if f.is_dir() else \"\") for f in visible_entries(p))` — note `visible_entries` excludes protected (host concern). For CLI: all entries (pure capability), but hidden files? visible_entries — probably also filters protected only. Hmm — does list show hidden dotfiles? grep skips dot-entries but list? visible_entries likely only filters protected. So list shows hidden files. For CLI: show ALL entries (protected concept dropped). sorted with suffix.\n\n**LocalWorkspace.grep**: \n- `re.compile(pattern)` — Python re\n- files = [root] if file else recursive walk: sorted(iterdir), skip `.`-prefixed names AND `__pycache__`, recurse dirs, collect files\n- per file: rel = path relative to self.root (!!) — for CLI: relative to the SEARCH ROOT (path arg), since no workspace.root. Host used workspace.root as base even when grep path=\"sub\" — rel was relative to workspace root. For CLI: relative to the search root passed (natural for a standalone tool: `grep --path sub` shows `sub/x.py:  Line N:`... wait host showed rel from workspace root so it'd show \"sub/x.py\"). CLI: rel from the given path arg → \"sub/x.py\" too if path=\"sub\" is