ch new fpath: \"\" blank line between groups (not before first), f\"{fpath}:\", then f\"  Line {lineno}: {text[:300]}\". No matches → \"(no matches)\". Cap footer: \"\\n\\n(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)\".\n\nNote filesystem.grep calls workspace.grep(pattern, path=path, include=include or None); re.error → \"ERROR: invalid regex: {e}\". Which exit code? 65 EX_DATAERR (bad pattern data)? or 64? I'll flag: choose 64 EX_USAGE? Hmm. The pattern comes from the model as an argument — a bad regex is like invalid arguments → usage-ish. But EX_DATAERR = \"data other than caller's was unusable\"... The model's pattern IS caller data. GNU grep exits 2 on bad regex. I'll assign 64 EX_USAGE (bad argument value) and flag for user review. Actually wait — let me think about the frame: 64 = \"bad flags/arguments\" (host → invalid_arguments feed-back-to-model), 65 = \"data error during execution\" (host → model self-correct). Both feed back. Bad regex = bad argument value → 64. dir+offset/limit = contradictory arguments → 64. range over budget = data too large → 65? Host message says \"Use a smaller limit, or search with grep instead\" → model self-corrects → 65 or 64 both plausible; I'll use 65 EX_DATAERR... hmm, actually over-budget range is a property of the FILE (data) vs request — 65. edit not-found/ambiguous = 65 (data state). old_string empty = missing required arg → 64. file not found = 66. IO errors = 74. argparse failures = 64. Internal unexpected = 70.\n\nI'll implement this table and list it in the final report for user review (user can adjust — single dict constant, trivially editable).\n\n**Encoding/newlines for read**: host read_text default → universal newlines (\\r\\n → \\n) and errors=\"replace\". Mirror exactly: `open(p, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")` (universal newlines default in text mode). \n\n**Write**: `open(p, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")` → write(content). Mirror.\n\n**Console/stdout encoding**: