 args = materialize(ns)         # '-' values → stdin (only for the declared stdin-able fields)\n        res = ns.func(ns)\n        emit(res, ns.json)\n        return 0\n    except CommandError as e:\n        emit_error(e)\n        return e.code\n    except BrokenPipeError:\n        return 74  # downstream closed the pipe (head etc.)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        return 130\n    except Exception as e:  # unexpected → EX_SOFTWARE\n        emit_error(CommandError(EX_SOFTWARE, f\"internal error: {e!r}\"))\n        return 70\n```\n\nHmm BrokenPipeError → 74? Unix convention for SIGPIPE... exit 141 (128+13). Python raises BrokenPipeError. Common practice: exit 74 or re-raise signal. Keep 74 EX_IOERR + suppress the \"Exception ignored\" noise (devnull dup2 trick). Keep simple: catch, return 74. Minor.\n\nargparse setup:\n```python\nclass Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n    def error(self, message):\n        self.exit(EX_USAGE, f\"ERROR: {message}\\n\")  # exit() writes message to stderr? \n```\nargparse's exit(status, message) writes message to stderr. But message doesn't include usage unless error() prepends. Standard error() prints usage + message. Our override: print usage + \"ERROR: msg\" to stderr, exit 64. And for no-subcommand: set required=True on subparsers (Python 3.7+ subparsers required attr) → argparse error → 64. `--version`? add it (harmless, useful). Version prints to stdout exit 0.\n\nSubcommand definitions:\n\nread_file:\n```\np = sub.add_parser(\"read_file\")\np.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\np.add_argument(\"--max-chars\", type=int, default=0, dest=\"max_chars\")\np.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=int, default=0)\np.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=0)\n```\nflag naming: host args were max_chars/offset/limit (JSON schema names max_chars etc.). CLI flags: --max-chars (argparse converts to max_chars). Model-side schema stays host's concern later. OK.\n\ngrep:\n```\n--pattern (required), --path default \".\", --include default \"\"\n```\n\nwrite_file:\n```\n--path required, --content required (allow '-')\n```\n\nedit_file:\n```\n--path required, --old-string required dest old_string, --new-string required dest new_string (allow '-')\n```\n\nNote: argparse's `type=str` with values starting with '-' — e.g. --content \"-something\": argparse treats leading-dash values as options unless they look like negative numbers... Known argparse wart. Value \"-\" alone is fine (matched as value? Actually argparse treats a bare \"-\" as a positional-ish value... Let me think: argparse handles \"-\" specially: a single dash is treated as a value (convention for stdin). Values like \"-abc\" as option values: argparse will misparse `--content -abc` as missing value/error. Workaround for the model/host side: use `--content=-abc` form. Document. In W3 the '-' value reads stdin — implement materialize: if value == \"-\": read sys.stdin (bytes→utf-8 strict; on UnicodeDecodeError → CommandError(EX_DATAERR, \"stdin is not valid UTF-8\")).\n\nBut careful: argparse with `--content -` — is \"-\" consumed as the option's value? Yes: argparse accepts \"-\" as a value for an option that requires one (it's in the special `_negative_number_matcher`/`_has_negative_number_optionals` logic... actually the rule: arguments that look like negative numbers are treated as values only if no option strings look like negative numbers; a lone \"-\" is treated as a value/positional since it's a common stdin convention — argparse source: `'-' in arg` special-cased? In `_parse_optional`: `if arg_string == '-': return None  # '-' is a positional/value`. Yes — lone '-' is treated as a value. Good.\n\nMulti-line values via argv work fine (args can contain newlines).\n\nread_file port — exact:\n\n```python\ndef read_file(path, max_chars=0, offset=0, limit=0) -> Result:\n    p = Path(path)\n    limit_chars = max_chars or DEFAULT_MAX_CHARS  # 20000\n    try:\n        if p.is_dir():\n            if offset > 0 or limit > 0:\n                raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"cannot read a line range of a directory; list it without offset/limit\")\n            entries = sorted(e.name + (\"/\" if e.is_dir() else \"\") for e in p.iterdir())\n            return Result(\"\\n\".join(entries) if entries else \"(empty directory)\")\n        try:\n            text = p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            raise CommandError(EX_NOINPUT, f\"file not found: {path}\")\n        except IsADirectoryError:  # race/dir-like special\n            ... treat as dir listing? host checked is_dir first; keep simple: rare; map to EX_IOERR? Actually read_text on a dir raises IsADirectoryError on POSIX, PermissionError(?) on Windows. p.is_dir() guard covers; skip extra handling — fall through to generic OSError → EX_IOERR.\n        except OSError as e:\n            raise CommandError(EX_IOERR, f\"read failed: {e}\")\n        if offset > 0 or limit > 0:\n            return _read_range(text, offset, limit, limit_chars)\n        if len(text) > limit_chars:\n            head = text[:limit_chars]\n            next_offset = head.count(\"\\n\") + 1\n            text = head + f\"\\n... [truncated, file is {len(text)} chars; use offset={next_offset} to continue]\"\n        return Result(text)\n    except ValueError as e:  # host mapped ValueError (resolve escapes) — CLI has no resolve; keep for uniform? Drop. But null bytes in path → ValueError from OS. Map to EX_USAGE? Rare. I'll map ValueError → EX_USAGE \"invalid path\". Hmm — keep: except ValueError → CommandError(EX_USAGE, f\"invalid path: {e}\")? Simple.\n```\n\n_read_range port exactly (messages preserved verbatim).\n\nWait — messages: host used render(\"errors/file_missing.md\", path=path) — I don't know that template's exact text! For fidelity of host messages I'd need the templates. But decision Q16 says: CLI emits brief message, host renders model-facing prose later. So brief messages in CLI are NEW text (not the templates). The messages that live in the CONTENT channel of results (like range-over-budget error, dir-range error, edit not-found — these were content of the error result) — host will re-render? Q16 decision: CLI emits structured code + brief message; host renders. So ALL error text in the CLI is new brief text; host mapping maps codes → templates. But some errors' text was informative to the model with specifics (e.g., \"old_string appears N times... include more context\"). The brief message should still carry the specifics (N, path) since host prose will wrap it. I'll write concise, specific, self-contained messages. For exact-message fidelity I'd need the templates — they're host-side prose now (Q16). So writing fresh brief messages is CORRECT per decisions. The SUCCESS/continuation texts (like \"... (showing lines X-Y of N; use offset=Y+1 to continue)\", truncation marker, \"(no matches)\", \"(empty directory)\", group format) are CONTENT (not error prose) — those I port VERBATIM since they're the tool's output contract the model relies on. Important distinction:\n- content-channel formats: verbatim port ✓\n- error-channel texts: brief fresh messages + codes ✓\n\nThe dir+range error was content-channel error in host... now becomes code+brief. Message text I'll keep close to original (\"cannot read a line range of a directory; list it without offset/limit\").\n\ngrep port:\n\n```python\ndef grep(pattern, path=\".\", include=\"\") -> Result:\n    try:\n        rx = re.compile(pattern)\n    except re.error as e:\n        raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, f\"invalid regex: {e}\")\n    root = Path(path)\n    files = [root] if root.is_file() else _walk(root)\n    out = []\n    for f in files:\n        rel = relpath_of(f, root)\n        if include and not (fnmatch.fnmatch(f.name, include) or fnmatch.fnmatch(rel, include)):\n            continue\n        if _is_binary(f):\n            continue\n        try:\n            with open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") as fh:\n                for i, line in enumerate(fh, 1):\n                    if rx.search(line):\n                        out.append((rel, i, line.rstrip(\"\\n\")))\n                        if len(out) >= 100:\n                            ... build and return with footer\n        except OSError:\n            continue\n    build output\n```\n\nCareful: host checked protected BEFORE rel/include/binary — dropped. Order in host: include filter, then binary, then read. Mine same. Host rel uses workspace.root — CLI uses search root. For root.is_file() case rel = str(f.relative_to(root)) — f==root → relative_to gives \".\"? Path(\"a.txt\").relative_to(Path(\"a.txt\")) = Path(\".\"). Host: rel = f.relative_to(self.root) — for a direct file arg outside root it was ValueError→? host: `rel = str(f.relative_to(self.root))` would RAISE for outside-root file! Host bug or root==workspace root always contains. For CLI: guard with try/except → fallback str(f). I'll do relpath fallback.\n\nAlso `_walk`: sorted iterdir, skip name.startswith(\".\") or name == \"__pycache__\", dirs recurse, files append. Path.sort: `sorted(dirpath.iterdir())` sorts Path objects (by string parts). Mirror.\n\nOutput building:\n```python\nif not out: return Result(\"(no matches)\")\nlines = []\ncurrent = None\nfor fpath, lineno, text in out:\n    if current != fpath:\n        if current is not None: lines.append(\"\")\n        current = fpath\n        lines.append(f\"{fpath}:\")\n    lines.append(f\"  Line {lineno}: {text[:300]}\")\ncontent = \"\\n\".join(lines)\nif len(out) >= 100: content += \"\\n\\n(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)\"\nreturn Result(content)\n```\nVerbatim. \n\nwrite_file port:\n\n```python\ndef write_file(path, content) -> Result:\n    p = Path(path)\n    old = \"\"\n    try:\n        old = p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        pass\n    except OSError as e:  # IsADirectoryError etc → write will fail anyway; let write raise\n        pass? No — host: try read, except FileNotFoundError: pass. IsADirectoryError would propagate to generic → write_failed EX_IOERR. Mirror: only swallow FileNotFoundError; other OSErrors propagate to outer → EX_IOERR.\n    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    try:\n        with open(p, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as f:\n            f.write(content)\n    except OSError as e:\n        raise CommandError(EX_IOERR, f\"write failed: {e}\")\n    diff = _unified_diff(old, content, rel=path)\n    adds/dels count\n    summary = f\"OK: wrote {path} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\" if old else f\"OK: wrote {path} ({len(content)} chars)\"\n    return Result(summary, diff=diff)\n```\n\nHmm — one subtlety: host read old via workspace.read (FileNotFoundError → old=\"\"). But a directory at p: read raises IsADirectoryError → host outer catch → write_failed. Mirror: let it propagate to generic OSError handler → EX_IOERR. OK.\n\n_unified_diff: difflib.unified_diff(old.splitlines(keepends=True), new.splitlines(keepends=True), fromfile=f\"a/{rel}\", tofile=f\"b/{rel}\", n=3) → \"\".join. rel = path as given.\n\nedit_file port:\n\n```python\ndef edit_file(path, old_string, new_string) -> Result:\n    p = Path(path)\n    try:\n        text = p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        raise CommandError(EX_NOINPUT, f\"file not found: {path} — use write_file to create it\")\n    except OSError as e:\n        raise CommandError(EX_IOERR, f\"read failed: {e}\")\n    if not old_string:\n        raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"old_string is required\")\n    count = text.count(old_string)\n    if count == 0:\n        raise CommandError(EX_DATAERR, f\"old_string not found in {path}; re-read the file and provide a unique block of context\")\n    if count > 1:\n        raise CommandError(EX_DATAERR, f\"old_string appears {count} times in {path}; include more surrounding lines so it matches exactly once\")\n    new = text.replace(old_string, new_string, 1)\n    diff = _unified_diff(text, new, rel=path)\n    adds, dels\n    try:\n        with open(p, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as f: f.write(new)\n    except OSError as e:\n        raise CommandError(EX_IOERR, f\"write failed: {e}\")\n    return Result(f\"OK: edited {path} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\", diff=diff)\n```\n\nHost order: validate old_string empty AFTER read; not-found/multi BEFORE write; snapshot before write (dropped). Mirror order: read → empty check → count checks → compute → write. ✓.\n\nEdge: text.count with empty old handled before. \n\n**tests** (black-box via subprocess):\n\nconftest.py:\n```python\nimport subprocess, sys, os, pathlib\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\n\ndef run(args, cwd=None, input=None):\n    env = {**os.environ, \"PYTHONPATH\": str(ROOT), \"PYTHONIOENCODING\": \"utf-8\"}\n    return subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace\", *args], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\", input=input, env=env)\n```\nFixture `run` in conftest as a fixture returning the function. tmp workspace fixture uses tmp_path with chdir (pass cwd=tmp_path).\n\ntest_read.py:\n- read a file exact content (write file in tmp, read_file --path, expect stdout == content+\"\\n\")\n- --json envelope shape: json.loads → keys/content/error False/diff \"\"\n- missing file → exit 66, json error True, content contains \"not found\"\n- directory listing: create dir with sub/ file.txt → listing \"file.txt\\nsub/\"; empty dir → \"(empty directory)\"\n- dir with --offset → exit 64\n- line range: file 10 lines, --offset 3 --limit 4 → \"3: ...\\n4: ...\" with continuation hint \"... (showing lines 3-6 of 10; use offset=7 to continue)\"\n- range over budget: --max-chars small → exit 65, message mentions \"exceeds the read limit\"\n- truncation: long file > max_chars → marker \"[truncated, file is N chars; use offset=X to continue]\"\n- stdout encoding: non-ascii content round-trips (中文)\n\ntest_grep.py:\n- basic hit format grouping \"path:\" / \"  Line N: text\"\n- (no matches) → content \"(no matches)\", exit 0\n- cap: 100+ hits → footer, exactly 100 lines? (generate 120 hits in one file → 100 results + footer)\n- hidden dirs skipped; __pycache__ skipped; binary skipped (file with \\x00)\n- --include glob filter\n- bad regex → exit 64\n\ntest_write.py:\n- create new file: content on argv, file bytes exact (LF, no trailing newline added), summary \"OK: wrote ... (N chars)\", diff field has a/{rel}/b/{rel} headers\n- overwrite: diff has -old/+new lines, adds/dels in summary\n- parents auto-created\n- stdin: --content - with piped input\n- utf-8 中文 content byte-exact\n\ntest_edit.py:\n- edit unique → file replaced once, summary ±, diff\n- not found → 65\n- ambiguous (2 occurrences) → 65 with \"appears 2 times\"\n- missing file → 66\n- empty old_string → 64\n- multi-line old_string across lines works\n\nAlso cli basics: --help exit 0; no args → 64; unknown subcommand → 64; --version.\n\nExit code check: argparse override in Parser.error and subparsers required.\n\nNow — pyproject:\n\n```toml\n[project]\nname = \"clutch-workspace\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Standalone file-inspection CLI: read_file/grep/write_file/edit_file as plain flags-in, text-out commands.\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\nlicense = \"MIT\"\ndependencies = []\n\n[project.scripts]\nclutch-workspace = \"clutch_workspace.cli:main\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"hatchling\"]\nbuild-backend = \"hatchling.build\"\n\n[tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel]\npackages = [\"clutch_workspace\"]\n\n[tool.ruff]\nline-length = 120\ntarget-version = \"py310\"\n\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\"E\", \"F\", \"W\", \"B\", \"I\"]\n\n[dependency-groups]\ndev = [\"pytest>=8.0\"]\n```\n\nMirror host style (hatchling, ruff 120, py310). description: keep neutral, no agent/LLM words (Q29 no-bias).\n\n.gitignore: __pycache__/, .venv/, *.pyc, dist/, .pytest_cache/\n\ngit init in clutch-workspace/, initial commit? Clutch push conventions memory exists — but this is a NEW local repo; user said independent repo. git init + initial commit is reasonable and expected for \"独立新仓库\". I'll git init and commit at milestones. (No remote — user will add.)\n\nvenv: create .venv inside clutch-workspace, pip install pytest using tsinghua mirror? Host uv config uses tsinghua mirror. For pip: `python -m venv .venv && .venv/Scripts/python -m pip install pytest -i https://pypi.tuna.tsinghua.edu.cn/simple`. Mirror host convention.\n\nWait — do I even need the venv? Global python 3.14 might have pytest already? Check `python -m pytest --version`. If missing, venv+install. Simpler alternative: since zero-dep CLI, I could run tests with plain unittest (stdlib) — but user approved pytest and pytest gives better assertions. Check global pytest first; if absent, venv.\n\nSteps now:\n1. `python -m pytest --version` check. (If fail → venv + pip install pytest)\n2. mkdir + write all files (M1 scope: all four commands? I planned M1=read_file, M1b=rest. Since I'm on a roll and the port is mechanical + verified by tests, do: scaffold + read_file + tests → run; then grep/write/edit + tests → run. Incremental verification.)\n3. git init + commit.\n\nAlso note: package dir name with underscore module; repo dir \"clutch-workspace\".\n\nLet me be careful about one Windows detail: subprocess in tests with `sys.executable -m clutch_workspace` requires cwd OR PYTHONPATH set to repo root; set env PYTHONPATH. Also the CLI's reconfigure: `sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")` — Python 3.7+ OK.\n\nAnother subtlety — `read_text` default newline handling: read_text uses universal newlines → \\r\\n becomes \\n. Host same. Fidelity ✓. write newline=\"\\n\" → exact LF. ✓\n\n`--max-chars` type=int; negative? host: max_chars or default — negative truthy → limit_chars negative → text[:negative] weird. Host had same behavior; models pass positive. Mirror (no extra validation) — or add guard? Mirror host exactly: no guard. (Any difference from host behavior = infidelity.)\n\noffset/limit negative: host: `start = max(0, offset-1) if offset>0 else 0` — offset negative → treated as 0 → full read with line numbers. Mirror code exactly.\n\nDirectory detection: host listed via workspace.list → raised NotADirectoryError if path doesn't exist or is file → then read. CLI: `if p.is_dir(): list` else read (read raises FileNotFoundError → 66). Broken symlink: is_dir False → read fails FileNotFoundError → 66. Host: resolve+list raises NotADirectoryError → read FileNotFoundError → file_missing. Same outcome.\n\nSymlinked dirs: is_dir() follows links → lists. Host resolve() also followed. ✓\n\nNow write the files. Order:\n1. bash: check pytest\n2. write pyproject.toml, .gitignore\n3. write clutch_workspace/__init__.py, exitcodes.py, envelope.py, filesystem.py, cli.py, __main__.py\n4. write tests/conftest.py, test_read.py, test_cli.py\n5. run pytest → fix → green\n6. write grep/write/edit + tests → run → green\n7. git init, add, commit\n8. final report with decision table for user review\n\nGiven response-length practicalities, batch writes efficiently. Let me start with the pytest check + mkdir in one command batch (two run_commands or one with &&). Composite: `mkdir -p clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace clutch-workspace/tests && python -m pytest --version` — && of read-only + mkdir... mkdir is a write op; work mode allows. The chat-mode write-detector is gone in work mode. OK single command.\n\nThen file writes.\n\nfilesystem.py content — let me write it fully now (all four commands in one file, since I have exact bodies; then tests staged: I'll still write ALL tests at once and run everything together — simpler than two-stage, and verification is per-test-suite anyway. Hmm, plan said M1 read_file first then M1b. Practically: write everything, run full suite, iterate. The incremental discipline is served by the test suite granularity. I'll do it in one pass but if failures cluster, fix per file.)\n\nexitcodes.py:\n```python\n\"\"\"BSD sysexits.h exit codes (the CLI's error contract; see README decision table).\"\"\"\nEX_OK = 0\nEX_USAGE = 64       # bad flags / unknown subcommand / contradictory arguments\nEX_DATAERR = 65     # data-level failure (edit target not found/ambiguous, over-budget range)\nEX_NOINPUT = 66     # input file missing\nEX_SOFTWARE = 70    # internal invariant broken\nEX_IOERR = 74       # read/write failure\nEX_SIGINT = 130     # 128 + SIGINT(2): aborted by the caller\n```\nNo README (Q29) — docstring only.\n\nenvelope.py:\n```python\n\"\"\"Result/CommandError + the two output modes (human text, --json envelope).\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport json, sys\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Result:\n    content: str\n    diff: str = \"\"\n\nclass CommandError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, code: int, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.code = code\n        self.message = message\n\ndef emit_result(res, as_json):\n    if as_json:\n        _write_line(json.dumps({\"content\": res.content, \"error\": False, \"diff\": res.diff}, ensure_ascii=False))\n    else:\n        sys.stdout.write(res.content + \"\\n\")\n        if res.diff:\n            sys.stdout.write(res.diff)\n            if not res.diff.endswith(\"\\n\"): sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\")\n\ndef emit_error(err, as_json):\n    if as_json:\n        _write_line(json.dumps({\"content\": err.message, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\"}, ensure_ascii=False))\n    else:\n        sys.stderr.write(f\"ERROR: {err.message}\\n\")\n```\nWait — human error mode: stderr + no stdout content. Host consumed only --json anyway. Fine.\n\nwrite errors to stderr in human mode; in --json mode envelope goes to stdout (host reads stdout). ✓\n\n`_write_line`: sys.stdout.write(s + \"\\n\"); flush? print flushes at exit; subprocess capture fine. Add flush for safety on pipe death? OK plain.\n\ncli.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Command-line entry: argparse wiring, stdin ('-') materialization, exit codes.\n\nUsage shape (stable contract):\n    clutch-workspace [--json] <subcommand> [flags]\nA flag value of exactly \"-\" reads that value from stdin (UTF-8).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\nimport argparse, sys\nfrom . import exitcodes as ex\nfrom .envelope import CommandError, emit_error, emit_result\nfrom .filesystem import edit_file, grep, read_file, write_file\n\nDEFAULT_MAX_CHARS = 20000   # mirrors host config.read_max_chars default\n\nclass _Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n    def error(self, message):  # usage errors exit 64, not argparse's 2\n        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)\n        sys.stderr.write(f\"ERROR: {message}\\n\")\n        raise SystemExit(ex.EX_USAGE)\n\ndef _stdin_value(value: str, field: str) -> str:\n    if value != \"-\":\n        return value\n    raw = sys.stdin.buffer.read()\n    try:\n        return raw.decode(\"utf-8\")\n    except UnicodeDecodeError as e:\n        raise CommandError(ex.EX_DATAERR, f\"--{field} -: stdin is not valid UTF-8 ({e})\")\n\ndef build_parser():\n    p = _Parser(prog=\"clutch-workspace\", description=\"...\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--version\", action=\"version\", version=f\"clutch-workspace {_version()}\")\n    p.add_argument(\"--json\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"machine envelope {content,error,diff} on stdout\")\n    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True, parser_class=_Parser)\n\n    r = sub.add_parser(\"read_file\", help=\"print a file (or a directory listing)\")\n    r.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    r.add_argument(\"--max-chars\", dest=\"max_chars\", type=int, default=0)\n    r.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=int, default=0)\n    r.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=0)\n    r.set_defaults(func=lambda ns: read_file(ns.path, ns.max_chars, ns.offset, ns.limit))\n\n    g = sub.add_parser(\"grep\", ...)\n    ...\n\n    w = sub.add_parser(\"write_file\", ...)\n    w.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    w.add_argument(\"--content\", required=True)\n    w.set_defaults(func=lambda ns: write_file(ns.path, _stdin_value(ns.content, \"content\")))\n\n    e_ = sub.add_parser(\"edit_file\", ...)\n    e_.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    e_.add_argument(\"--old-string\", dest=\"old_string\", required=True)\n    e_.add_argument(\"--new-string\", dest=\"new_string\", required=True)\n    e_.set_defaults(func=lambda ns: edit_file(ns.path, _stdin_value(ns.old_string, \"old-string\"), _stdin_value(ns.new_string, \"new-string\")))\n    return p\n\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n        try: stream.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n        except ... pass\n    ns = build_parser().parse_args(argv)\n    try:\n        res = ns.func(ns)\n        emit_result(res, ns.json)\n        return 0\n    except CommandError as e:\n        emit_error(e, ns.json)\n        return e.code\n    except BrokenPipeError:\n        return ex.EX_IOERR\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        return ex.EX_SIGINT\n    except Exception as e:\n        err = CommandError(ex.EX_SOFTWARE, f\"internal error: {e!r}\")\n        emit_error(err, ns.json)\n        return ex.EX_SOFTWARE\n```\n\nWait: emit_error in human mode writes to stderr; but ns.json — parse_args before try; if parse_args raises SystemExit(64), main propagates SystemExit → `python -m` exit code = 64 ✓ (SystemExit with int code sets process exit). main() returns int → __main__.py: `raise SystemExit(main())`.\n\nargparse `--version` action prints to stdout exit 0. Also `--json` AFTER subcommand? argparse: global flags must come before subcommand (subparser doesn't know --json). Host inst would generate `clutch-workspace --json read_file ...` — put --json before subcommand. Also allow after? Each subparser would need its own --json. Keep: global before subcommand only. Document in help. Hmm — risk: host generates `... --json` at end. Design the contract explicitly: `clutch-workspace --json <cmd> ...` (global first). Flag in report as frozen contract detail.\n\nversion import: from . import __version__.\n\nfs module docstring: note pure capability (paths as given, relative to CWD; no protection concepts).\n\nNow __init__.py: `__version__ = \"0.1.0\"`.\n\nTests conftest:\n\n```python\nimport os, subprocess, sys, pathlib\nimport pytest\nROOT = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef run():\n    def _run(args, cwd=None, stdin=None):\n        env = dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH=str(ROOT), PYTHONIOENCODING=\"utf-8\")\n        return subprocess.run(\n            [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace\", *args],\n            cwd=str(cwd) if cwd else None,\n            input=stdin, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\", env=env,\n        )\n    return _run\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef ws(tmp_path):\n    return tmp_path\n```\n\ntest_cli.py:\n- test_no_args_exit_64: run([]) → rc 64, \"usage\" in stderr.lower()\n- test_unknown_command_64\n- test_version_0: run([\"--version\"]) rc 0\n- test_json_envelope_read: covered in read tests\n- test_stdin_content: write_file --content - with stdin → file written\n- test_stdin_bad_utf8: input can't be invalid in text mode... run with input bytes? subprocess.run(input=str, text=True) encodes with encoding param. For invalid utf-8 test, use input bytes w/o text mode: separate helper — skip this test (edge; the host always sends valid utf-8). Keep suite focused; note in report? Skip silently — it's implemented but untested edge. Better: test via binary input using subprocess.run(input=b\"\\xff\", text=False) — doable: add helper param binary. I'll include: `def _run(..., data: bytes|None)` variant... Keep simple: one test with data=b'\\xff\\xfe' using text=False run. Implement helper `_run_raw`. OK.\n\ntest_read.py as planned. Let me write everything now.\n\nOne more check — `sorted(f.name + (\"/\"...) for ...)` — my dir listing: entries sorted by suffixed name (host identical). iterdir on Windows: includes hidden? Yes. fine.\n\ngrep walk: `sorted(dirpath.iterdir())` — Path sorting: by path string. Host identical code. skip startswith(\".\") or \"__pycache__\". ✓ binary: read(1024) contains b\"\\x00\" → skip. ✓ include: fnmatch name OR rel. ✓ cap: append then check >=100 return. My structure: build full output after loop but need early-stop at 100: mirror host — break out with flag:\n\n```python\nout = []\nfor f in files:\n    ...\n    for i, line in enumerate(fh, 1):\n        if rx.search(line):\n            out.append((rel, i, line.rstrip(\"\\n\")))\n            if len(out) >= 100:\n                break   # inner\n    if len(out) >= 100:\n        break   # outer\n```\nHost returned immediately from nested loop (return out inside). Same effect. ✓ footer if len(out) >= 100. ✓\n\nEdge: files = [root] when root.is_file() — but is_file on missing → False → walk path: iterdir on missing dir raises OSError → host: `self._grep_files(root)` → iterdir raises FileNotFoundError → propagates to filesystem.grep generic except → \"ERROR: {e}\" error=True. CLI: catch OSError around walk → CommandError(EX_NOINPUT, f\"path not found: {path}\")? Host mapped generic → error. Exit code for missing grep root: 66 EX_NOINPUT ✓ reasonable.\n\nAlso grep root as special file that isn't dir/file (broken symlink): is_file() False → walk → iterdir raises OSError (Windows: FileNotFoundError) → 66 ✓.\n\nWalk permission errors (iterdir OSError mid-walk): host would raise → generic. CLI: let OSError propagate → wrap in EX_IOERR at top-level? filesystem.grep: wrap whole body except already-raised CommandError: \n\n```python\ntry:\n    ...core...\nexcept CommandError:\n    raise\nexcept OSError as e:\n    raise CommandError(ex.EX_IOERR, f\"search failed: {e}\")\n```\nHmm but per-file open OSError caught inside (continue) like host. iterdir OSError inside walk — host didn't catch → generic. Mirror: let walk OSErrors bubble to the wrapper. OK.\n\nread_file outer: wrap ValueError → EX_USAGE \"invalid path\" (null byte path etc.).\n\nNow write files. After tests pass: git init + commit (\"M1: read_file/grep/write_file/edit_file + envelope + sysexits + tests\").\n\nAlso ruff check? dev group has ruff in host; new repo: pip install ruff in venv too? Optional; run if available. Host mirror includes ruff config; installing ruff keeps hygiene. I'll add pytest only (keep light) — skip ruff for now, note it. Actually line-length 120 discipline I'll respect manually.\n\nLet me also double check argparse subparsers required=True: `add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True)` — Python 3.7+ ✓. With required, no-subcommand → Parser.error → our 64 override (parser_class for subparsers: _Parser passed via parser_class kwarg on add_subparsers ✓).\n\n`--json` before subcommand: `clutch-workspace --json read_file --path x`. But what if user puts --json after subcommand → subparser errors \"unrecognized arguments\"? Actually global option after subcommand: parse_args top-level parses until subcommand; the subparser gets remaining args; --json unknown to subparser → error 64. Acceptable (documented contract). Hmm — argparse with subparsers: unrecognized extra args → subparser.error → 64 ✓.\n\nAlso `--` separator support: argparse built-in ✓ (paths starting with dashes: `--path -- -weird` — argparse: --path then value \"-weird\"... \"--path -weird\" → argparse treats -weird as option-ish → error. Contract: use `--path=-weird` form. Document in help epilog briefly.)\n\nTime to execute. Command 1: mkdir + pytest check."}
