r EX_USAGE or EX_DATAERR — decision), cap 100 hits, output \"path:  Line N: text\", truncate line to 300 chars? Summary says \"300 字符截行\". Footer when truncated: \"Output is capped at 100 hits as \\\"path:  Line N: text\\\"; narrow the pattern or path if the result is truncated.\" — from the tool description here. Plus \"(no matches)\" when zero hits.\n\n**write_file(path, content)**: parents mkdir, write_text utf-8 newline \\n, diff via difflib.unified_diff(old, new), summary \"+N -M lines\"? Need host format. Host write returns something like \"OK\" + diff. Read from source.\n\n**edit_file(path, old_string, new_string, replace_all?)**: Host edit_file replaces ONE exact occurrence, errors if missing/ambiguous: \"old_string must appear exactly once in the file; if it is missing or ambiguous the tool errors\". Errors: not found → \"old_string not found in file\", multiple → \"old_string is not unique (N occurrences); provide more surrounding lines\". Exit codes 65? These are data errors. Hmm, decision: not-found could be 66 (no input)? Summary said edit 的 0 次与 >1 次分码报错 — \"分码\" = different codes? Maybe 0 occurrences → 66 and >1 → 65? Or both 65? The frozen list includes 66 EX_NOINPUT which reads as \"no input (file)\". For edit on missing old_string, 65 EX_DATAERR seems right. But \"分码报错\" implies distinct codes for 0 vs >1... Hmm, re-reading: \"edit 的 0 次与 >1 次分码报错\" — could mean \"edit's 0-occurrence and >1-occurrence are separately coded errors\". If so, what codes? Maybe 66 for not-found and 65 for ambiguous? That wasn't in the pending review list though. The pending list: \"dir+range/坏 regex/超预算 range 的 64 vs 65 归类\". So the edit 分码 must already be decided... but I don't have the exact codes in context. I need to check the memory \"clutch-workspace C++ spin-off: frozen decisions\" — it's in my memory list! Let me load it. It should contain the frozen contract details including error code assignments.\n\nYes — load_memory(\"clutch-workspace C++ spin-off: frozen decisions\") first. That's essential to avoid re-deciding frozen things or contradicting them.\n\nSo first batch: load_memory + the 6 reads. All independent. \n\nThen write code. Let me do it.\n\n### cli.py design\n\n```python\nimport argparse, json, sys\nfrom . import __version__?\nfrom .envelope import emit_result, emit_error, CommandError\nfrom . import filesystem\n\ndef build_parser():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog=\"clutch-workspace\", description=...)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--json\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"emit machine envelope {content,error,diff} as single-line JSON on stdout\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--version\", action=\"version\", version=...)\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", required=True)\n    p_read = sub.add_parser(\"read_file\", help=...)\n    p_read.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    p_read.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=int, default=None)\n    p_read.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=None)\n    p_read.add_argument(\"--max-chars\", type=int, default=20000)\n    p_grep = sub.add_parser(\"grep\")\n    p_grep.add_argument(\"--pattern\", required=True)\n    p_grep.add_argument(\"--path\", default=\".\"?)\n    p_grep.add_argument(\"--include\")\n    p_write = sub.add_parser(\"write_file\")\n    p_write.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    p_write.add_argument(\"--content\", required=True)\n    p_edit = sub.add_parser(\"edit_file\")\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--old-string\", required=True, dest=\"old_string\")\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--new-string\", required=True, dest=\"new_string\")\n    p_edit.add_argument(\"--replace-all\", action=\"store_true\")?  # host has no replace_all; tool replaces one occurrence. Skip.\n```\n\nWait — host edit_file has no replace_all (it replaces exactly one occurrence). Keep faithful: no replace-all flag. Hmm, but is that a decision? The frozen contract said 4 subcommands read/grep/write/edit mirroring host; host edit has no replace-all. Keep it out. If user wants it later, add.\n\n`--path` for grep: host grep default path = \".\" (whole workspace). Tool description: \"path limits the search to a subdirectory or file (default: whole workspace)\". In CLI terms with cwd-as-workspace, default `--path` = \".\" i.e. cwd. Fine, default \".\".\n\nError handling in main():\n- argparse errors: override parser.error to raise SystemExit(EX_USAGE) — actually argparse calls parser.error(msg) which prints usage to stderr and exits 2. Override: subclass ArgumentParser with error() that prints to stderr and sys.exit(64). Decision item #2 (argparse 2→64 coverage) — the frozen contract says exit 64 for usage errors, so mapping argparse → 64 is implied, but the user wants to confirm coverage. I'll implement 64 and list it.\n- no command / missing subcommand → argparse required=True → error → 64.\n- CommandError → human: print \"clutch-workspace: {message}\" to stderr? Envelope: {\"error\": message} single-line JSON stdout, exit code.\n- BrokenPipeError → 74 (and devnull dup2 to avoid Python printing \"Exception ignored\").\n- KeyboardInterrupt → 130.\n- Unexpected Exception → 70 with message. In --json mode, error envelope on stdout; human mode stderr.\n\nShould the traceback be printed for 70? Human mode: yes, print to stderr for debuggability? Host cli precedent: crashes go to stderr. For a CLI tool, printing traceback on internal error is helpful in dev but noisy. Decide: print short message + traceback to stderr in human mode; in json mode, error field gets \"internal error: {type}: {msg}\" and traceback suppressed? Or always traceback to stderr even in json mode (keeps stdout clean for json). That's nice: json mode → envelope on stdout, traceback on stderr (diagnostic), exit 70. Human mode → message+traceback stderr. Hmm, decide: always emit traceback to stderr for EX_INTERNAL. Keep stdout clean. I'll implement that; minor, not worth a decision question... but \"silent decisions\" — the user emphasized. The decision list will have 4 items; this is really an implementation detail with an obvious answer. I'll fold it into the report as \"implementation details I chose (speak up if you disagree)\" vs \"decision items needing your pick\". Good separation.\n\n- stdin `-` handling: `_resolve_stdin(value)` helper: if value == \"-\": return sys.stdin.read(). Flags: write_file --content, edit_file --old-string/--new-string. (Decision item: scope.)\n\n- stdout/stderr reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\"): wrap in try/except (AttributeError for weird streams, ValueError if closed).\n\n```python\ndef main(argv=None):\n    argv = list(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv)\n    parser = build_parser()\n    try:\n        args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    except SystemExit as e:\n        # argparse already printed; keep its code but map 2 → 64\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, cleaner: subclass ArgumentParser:\n\n```python\nclass _Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n    def error(self, message):\n        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)\n        sys.stderr.write(f\"{self.prog}: error: {message}\\n\")\n        raise SystemExit(EX_USAGE)\n    def exit(self, status=0, message=None):\n        # --version and -h call exit(0)\n        if message: sys.stderr.write(message)\n        raise SystemExit(status if status != 2 else EX_USAGE)\n```\n\nActually argparse's exit() is called by -h/--version with status 0 after printing. Only error() needs override; required-subparser-missing goes through error(). parse_args with `required=True` on subparsers → error(\"the following arguments are required: command\"). Good.\n\nMain flow:\n\n```python\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n        try: stream.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        except (AttributeError, ValueError): pass\n    parser = build_parser()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)  # may SystemExit(64)\n    try:\n        result = _run(args)  # dispatch\n    except CommandError as exc:\n        return _fail(args.json?, ...)\n```\n\nWait, args.json exists only on main parser — fine, it's namespace attr.\n\nDispatch:\n\n```python\ndef _dispatch(args):\n    if args.command == \"read_file\":\n        return filesystem.read_file(path=args.path, offset=args.offset, limit=args.limit, max_chars=args.max_chars)\n    if args.command == \"grep\":\n        return filesystem.grep(pattern=args.pattern, path=args.path, include=args.include)\n    if args.command == \"write_file\":\n        return filesystem.write_file(path=args.path, content=_payload(args.content))\n    if args.command == \"edit_file\":\n        return filesystem.edit_file(path=args.path, old_string=_payload(args.old_string), new_string=_payload(args.new_string))\n    raise CommandError(\"unknown command\", EX_USAGE)  # unreachable\n```\n\n_fail: if --json: emit_error(json) to stdout; else stderr message. return code.\n\nSuccess: emit_result(content, diff): --json → single-line JSON {\"content\":..., \"error\": null, \"diff\":...} on stdout; human → content to stdout, diff appended? Human mode for write/edit: what does it print? Probably content (the summary \"OK +N -M\" + diff). Keep: human prints content to stdout; if diff present, print diff too (to stdout). Envelope keeps them separate. Host precedent: --json → single JSON object stdout. Human: content stdout. For write/edit the \"content\" is the summary text and diff is separate. Human mode: print content then diff (diff only if non-empty). Fine — implementation detail, will state in report.\n\nexit 0.\n\nNow envelope.py already exists with Result/CommandError/emit_result/emit_error. I need to read it to see exact API and adapt (or it may already do everything and cli.py just calls it). Read first.\n\n### filesystem.py detailed port\n\nI'll write after reading the sources. Key: byte-exact channel formats. Let me now make the calls.\n\nOne more thing: tests. Plan (from summary):\n- tests/conftest.py: helper running subprocess `[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace\", ...]` with cwd=tmp_path, env PYTHONPATH=repo root. Provide `run(*args, cwd)` fixture returning (code, stdout, stderr).\n- test_read.py: file read (line-numbered, hint), offset/limit, max-chars truncation hint, directory listing, missing file → 66, dir + range → decision-dependent code (test uses the implemented constant — but if user flips, test flips; I'll reference exitcodes constants in tests to make flipping trivial: `from clutch_workspace.exitcodes import EX_DATAERR` etc. Then flipping a constant updates both sides. And if user picks the other code, I edit exitcodes.py only... wait no, the classification is at raise sites. Better: tests assert against exitcodes module constants; classification lives in filesystem.py raise sites. Flip = change raise site + maybe test import. Fine.)\n- test_grep.py: hit format, 100 cap + footer, hidden skip, __pycache__ skip, binary skip, include filter, bad regex (decision-dependent), no matches.\n- test_write.py: create file, parents auto-create, newline normalization, diff output, overwrite existing.\n- test_edit.py: unique replace, 0 occurrences → 66?, >1 → 65?, missing file.\n- test_cli.py: --json envelope shape (keys content/error/diff, single line), --json before subcommand enforcement, usage error → 64, unknown command → 64, --version, stdin '-' payload, UTF-8 roundtrip, broken pipe → 74 (hard to test portably... can test with `| head -c 1`? In subprocess on Windows Git Bash... tricky. Maybe simulate by calling main() with stdout replaced by a mock that raises BrokenPipeError. Unit-test style: monkeypatch. I can test via direct main() invocation with patched sys.stdout. OK.)\n- 130: KeyboardInterrupt monkeypatched similarly.\n\nTests must be TTY-free, subprocess-based for black box + a few in-process for exotic paths.\n\nEnv for subprocess: PYTHONPATH=repo_root so `python -m clutch_workspace` works without install. Or better: `pip install -e .` into venv? Simpler: PYTHONPATH. Repo root = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1].\n\nAlso `--max-chars` default 20000.\n\nWindows console encoding for subprocess: capture text with encoding=\"utf-8\". And the child's stdout when not a console → Python uses locale encoding (cp1252?) unless PYTHONUTF8. We reconfigure stdout to utf-8 in main(), so output bytes are utf-8 regardless. Good. Also set env PYTHONIOENCODING? reconfigure covers it.\n\nLet me also make sure: `python -m clutch_workspace` needs __main__.py with `from .cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())`. Wait main returns int; SystemExit(main()). And console script `clutch-workspace = clutch_workspace.cli:main` — hatchling creates wrapper calling main() and sys.exit(return)? Entry points: return value is passed to SystemExit by the console script wrapper? Actually console scripts do `sys.exit(main())`. Yes, the generated wrapper is `sys.exit(main())`. Good — returning int works.\n\nEdge: BrokenPipeError handling — on Windows, `sys.stdout` write errors surface as OSError EINVAL sometimes at flush during interpreter shutdown. Standard recipe:\n\n```python\nexcept BrokenPipeError:\n    try:\n        devnull = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY)\n        os.dup2(devnull, sys.stdout.fileno())\n    except Exception: pass\n    return EX_IOERR  # 74\n```\n\nBut wait: 74 is EX_IOERR in sysexits (I/O error). BrokenPipe → 74 was already frozen per summary. Good.\n\nAlso flush stdout explicitly before returning, catching BrokenPipeError at that point too. Simplest: wrap the whole emit in try.\n\nLet me structure cli.main:\n\n```python\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    _force_utf8()\n    parser = build_parser()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    try:\n        result = _dispatch(args)\n        emit_result(result, as_json=args.json)   # writes stdout\n        return 0\n    except CommandError as exc:\n        emit_error(exc, as_json=args.json)\n        return exc.exit_code\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        print(... to stderr ...)\n        return EX_SOFTWARE? no — 130\n    except BrokenPipeError:\n        _silence_stdout()\n        return EX_IOERR\n    except Exception as exc:\n        traceback to stderr; emit internal error envelope if json\n        return EX_SOFTWARE  # 70\n```\n\nHmm, but BrokenPipeError can occur during emit_result inside try — caught. But also the stderr writes in except path could break; acceptable.\n\nOrder: KeyboardInterrupt is BaseException, BrokenPipeError is OSError(Exception). Careful: on Ctrl+C during stdout write, could get both; fine.\n\nemit_error for KeyboardInterrupt/Exception in json mode: envelope {\"content\": \"\", \"error\": \"interrupted\"/\"internal error: ...\", \"diff\": \"\"}? envelope.py's emit_error signature — read it.\n\nWait, actually — the frozen envelope is {content, error, diff}. For errors, error=message. For internal errors in json mode, error=\"internal error: ValueError: ...\". OK.\n\nNow, filesystem.py functions signature — return what? envelope.Result dataclass with content + diff fields. Read envelope.py first.\n\nLet me also handle: read_file on a path that is a directory WITH offset/limit requested → decision item #1 (dir+range → 64 vs 65). Implement: raise CommandError(\"cannot use --offset/--limit on a directory\", EX_USAGE) — pending user pick.\n\nBad regex → EX_USAGE (it's a malformed argument — usage) vs EX_DATAERR (bad input data). My rec: 65 EX_DATAERR? Hmm. sysexits: EX_DATAERR = \"The input data was incorrect in some way... or in the format of a file\". A regex is a program-ish input... argparse-level would be 64. GNU grep uses exit 2 for bad regex. sysexits EX_USAGE = \"command was used incorrectly, e.g., wrong number of args, bad flag, bad syntax in a parameter\". \"bad syntax in a parameter\" — bad regex fits EX_USAGE honestly. Over-budget range: read_file with offset beyond EOF / limit too large? \"超预算 range\" — offset past end → empty result or error? Host: if offset beyond total lines → returns \"(showing lines...)\"? Actually the host hint logic: if start > total, output is empty with hint \"use offset=1 to continue\"? Hmm, need to see host code. The decision item says 超预算 range 的 64 vs 65. So host may error when offset > total lines? I'll see in the code. My rec pending read.\n\nI'll present recommendations in the final ask:\n1. dir+range → 64 (usage: flags don't apply to this input kind) — recommend 64? or 65? Let me think: 64 EX_USAGE \"command was used incorrectly\". Passing --offset with a directory is using the command incorrectly. 65 EX_DATAERR is about data content being wrong. I'd say dir+range → 64, bad regex → 64 (\"bad syntax in a parameter\"), offset-past-EOF → 65 (the data/file doesn't support the requested range)? Hmm, actually offset past EOF isn't \"data incorrect\", it's a request that can't be served... could argue 66 EX_NOINPUT (\"no input could be found... file not accessible\")? No — file exists. I'd do: offset beyond EOF → return empty-with-hint (graceful, no error)? If host errors, port the error with code 65? Let me read the host code first, then form recommendations.\n\nOK, enough planning. Make the calls.\n\nBatch 1 (independent):\n- load_memory(\"clutch-workspace C++ spin-off: frozen decisions\")\n- read_file clutch-workspace (dir listing)\n- read_file clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/envelope.py\n- read_file clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/exitcodes.py\n- read_file agent/tools/filesystem.py\n- read_file agent/tools/workspace.py offset=349 limit=112\n\nThen batch 2 if needed: agent/tools/cli.py, pyproject.toml, __init__.py.\n\nActually let me include pyproject.toml and __init__.py in batch 1 too — they're small and independent. And agent/tools/cli.py might be long; grep for --json part later if needed. Include it; if too long, truncated read is fine (20000 chars default).\n\nLet me go."}
