{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789893361.0574996, "id": "e855f0a300a6", "tool_call_id": "call_9e6564277ead4cb7bef4b2da", "content": "\"\"\"Output contract: human text vs the --json machine envelope.\n\nTwo modes, one Result shape:\n- human:  content (+ diff) on stdout; errors go to stderr as `ERROR: <brief>`\n- --json: ONE compact JSON line on stdout, always\n          {\"content\": str, \"error\": bool, \"diff\": str}\n          errors use the same envelope with error=true, diff=\"\", and a\n          non-zero exit code — callers parse stdout, never prose.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport sys\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Result:\n    \"\"\"A successful command outcome: content plus an optional unified diff.\"\"\"\n\n    content: str\n    diff: str = \"\"\n\n\nclass CommandError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"A failed command: sysexits code (see exitcodes) plus a brief message.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, code: int, message: str) -> None:\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.code = code\n        self.message = message\n\n\ndef _write_line(text: str) -> None:\n    sys.stdout.write(text + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef emit_result(res: Result, as_json: bool) -> None:\n    if as_json:\n        _write_line(json.dumps({\"content\": res.content, \"error\": False, \"diff\": res.diff}, ensure_ascii=False))\n        return\n    sys.stdout.write(res.content + \"\\n\")\n    if res.diff:\n        sys.stdout.write(res.diff if res.diff.endswith(\"\\n\") else res.diff + \"\\n\")\n\n\ndef emit_error(err: CommandError, as_json: bool) -> None:\n    if as_json:\n        _write_line(json.dumps({\"content\": err.message, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\"}, ensure_ascii=False))\n        return\n    sys.stderr.write(f\"ERROR: {err.message}\\n\")\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789893361.0635116, "id": "4a0ec03c8c64", "tool_call_id": "call_a49a943fbed14e58bbaedad6", "content": "\"\"\"Exit codes: BSD sysexits.h plus POSIX signal convention.\n\nThis table IS the CLI's error contract — the caller classifies failures from\nthese codes (and from stderr briefs), never from parsing prose.\n\n  0   ok\n  64  EX_USAGE    bad flags / unknown subcommand / contradictory arguments\n  65  EX_DATAERR  data-level failure (edit target not found or ambiguous,\n                  requested line range exceeds the read budget, bad stdin bytes)\n  66  EX_NOINPUT  input file (or search root) missing\n  70  EX_SOFTWARE internal invariant broken (bug — report it)\n  74  EX_IOERR    read/write I/O failure\n  130 128+SIGINT  aborted by the caller\n\"\"\"\n\nEX_OK = 0\nEX_USAGE = 64\nEX_DATAERR = 65\nEX_NOINPUT = 66\nEX_SOFTWARE = 70\nEX_IOERR = 74\nEX_SIGINT = 130\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789893361.069512, "id": "b7a6643a8ea3", "tool_call_id": "call_dd976d2f125648c3adc34aa8", "content": "[project]\nname = \"clutch-workspace\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Standalone file-inspection CLI: read_file / grep / write_file / edit_file as plain flags-in, text-out commands.\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\nlicense = \"MIT\"\ndependencies = []\n\n[project.scripts]\nclutch-workspace = \"clutch_workspace.cli:main\"\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"hatchling\"]\nbuild-backend = \"hatchling.build\"\n\n[tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel]\npackages = [\"clutch_workspace\"]\n\n[dependency-groups]\ndev = [\"pytest>=8.0\"]\n\n[tool.ruff]\nline-length = 120\ntarget-version = \"py310\"\n\n[tool.ruff.lint]\nselect = [\"E\", \"F\", \"W\", \"B\", \"I\"]\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789893361.0735092, "id": "f8c3538502c3", "tool_call_id": "call_41ddf86862364889bf69a00e", "content": "\"\"\"Filesystem tools: read_file (file or directory) / write_file / edit_file / grep.\n\nEach tool returns a structured dict; the registry formats it for the model.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport difflib\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom ..config import Config\nfrom ..prompts import render\nfrom .workspace import Workspace\n\n\ndef _result(content: str, error: bool = False, diff: str = \"\") -> dict:\n    return {\"content\": content, \"error\": error, \"diff\": diff}\n\n\ndef read_file(\n    workspace: Workspace,\n    config: Config,\n    path: str,\n    max_chars: int = 0,\n    offset: int = 0,\n    limit: int = 0,\n) -> dict:\n    limit_chars = max_chars or config.read_max_chars\n    try:\n        p: Path = workspace.resolve(path)\n        if workspace.is_protected(p):\n            return _result(render(\"errors/protected_read.md\", path=path), error=True)\n        # a directory reads as its entry listing; workspace.list raises\n        # NotADirectoryError for a file or missing path\n        try:\n            entries = workspace.list(str(p))\n        except NotADirectoryError:\n            entries = None\n        if entries is not None:\n            if offset > 0 or limit > 0:\n                msg = \"ERROR: cannot read a line range of a directory; list it without offset/limit\"\n                return _result(msg, error=True)\n            return _result(\"\\n\".join(sorted(entries)) if entries else \"(empty directory)\")\n        try:\n            text = workspace.read(str(p))\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            return _result(render(\"errors/file_missing.md\", path=path), error=True)\n        if offset > 0 or limit > 0:\n            return _read_range(text, offset, limit, limit_chars)\n        if len(text) > limit_chars:\n            # point the model at the first unread line instead of re-serving the same head\n            head = text[:limit_chars]\n            next_offset = head.count(\"\\n\") + 1\n            text = head + f\"\\n... [truncated, file is {len(text)} chars; use offset={next_offset} to continue]\"\n        return _result(text)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return _result(f\"ERROR: {e}\", error=True)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 -- tool boundary: report to model\n        return _result(render(\"errors/read_failed.md\", error=e), error=True)\n\n\ndef _read_range(text: str, offset: int, limit: int, limit_chars: int) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Read lines [offset, offset+limit) (1-based) with line numbers; a range\n    that cannot fit the char budget is an ERROR, not a silent truncation.\"\"\"\n    lines = text.splitlines()\n    total = len(lines)\n    start = max(0, offset - 1) if offset > 0 else 0\n    end = start + limit if limit > 0 else total\n    selected = lines[start:end]\n    body = \"\\n\".join(f\"{i + 1}: {ln}\" for i, ln in enumerate(selected, start=start))\n    if len(body) > limit_chars:\n        return _result(\n            f\"ERROR: the requested range (lines {start + 1}-{end}) exceeds the read limit of \"\n            f\"{limit_chars} chars. Use a smaller limit, or search with grep instead.\",\n            error=True,\n        )\n    shown = start + len(selected)\n    if shown < total:\n        body += f\"\\n... (showing lines {start + 1}-{shown} of {total}; use offset={shown + 1} to continue)\"\n    return _result(body)\n\n\ndef write_file(workspace: Workspace, config: Config, path: str, content: str) -> dict:\n    try:\n        p: Path = workspace.resolve(path)\n        if workspace.is_protected(p):\n            return _result(render(\"errors/protected_write.md\", path=path), error=True)\n        # capture the previous content (if any) to build a unified diff\n        old = \"\"\n        try:\n            old = workspace.read(str(p))\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            pass\n        if old:\n            workspace.snapshot(p, old)\n        workspace.write(str(p), content)\n        # external paths (approved escapes): show the absolute path in the diff header\n        try:\n            rel = p.relative_to(workspace.root)\n        except ValueError:\n            rel = p\n        diff = _unified_diff(old, content, rel=rel)\n        adds = sum(1 for ln in diff.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"+\") and not ln.startswith(\"+++\"))\n        dels = sum(1 for ln in diff.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"-\") and not ln.startswith(\"---\"))\n        summary = f\"OK: wrote {p} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\" if old else f\"OK: wrote {p} ({len(content)} chars)\"\n        return _result(summary, diff=diff)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return _result(f\"ERROR: {e}\", error=True)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001\n        return _result(render(\"errors/write_failed.md\", error=e), error=True)\n\n\ndef edit_file(workspace: Workspace, config: Config, path: str, old_string: str, new_string: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Targeted string replacement: one occurrence of old_string becomes new_string\n    (tiny diffs keep the context small instead of re-emitting the whole file).\"\"\"\n    try:\n        p: Path = workspace.resolve(path)\n        if workspace.is_protected(p):\n            return _result(render(\"errors/protected_write.md\", path=path), error=True)\n        try:\n            text = workspace.read(str(p))\n        except FileNotFoundError:\n            return _result(f\"ERROR: file not found: {path} — use write_file to create it\", error=True)\n        if not old_string:\n            return _result(\"ERROR: old_string is required\", error=True)\n        count = text.count(old_string)\n        if count == 0:\n            return _result(\n                f\"ERROR: old_string not found in {path}. The file's current content is in an \"\n                f\"earlier read; re-read it if needed and provide a unique block of context.\",\n                error=True,\n            )\n        if count > 1:\n            return _result(\n                f\"ERROR: old_string appears {count} times in {path}. Include more surrounding \"\n                \"lines so it matches exactly once.\",\n                error=True,\n            )\n        new = text.replace(old_string, new_string, 1)\n        workspace.snapshot(p, text)\n        workspace.write(str(p), new)\n        try:\n            rel = p.relative_to(workspace.root)\n        except ValueError:\n            rel = p\n        diff = _unified_diff(text, new, rel=rel)\n        adds = sum(1 for ln in diff.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"+\") and not ln.startswith(\"+++\"))\n        dels = sum(1 for ln in diff.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"-\") and not ln.startswith(\"---\"))\n        return _result(f\"OK: edited {p} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\", diff=diff)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return _result(f\"ERROR: {e}\", error=True)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001\n        return _result(render(\"errors/write_failed.md\", error=e), error=True)\n\n\ndef _unified_diff(old: str, new: str, rel: str | Path) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return a unified diff string between old and new file contents.\"\"\"\n    old_lines = old.splitlines(keepends=True)\n    new_lines = new.splitlines(keepends=True)\n    diff_lines = list(difflib.unified_diff(old_lines, new_lines, fromfile=f\"a/{rel}\", tofile=f\"b/{rel}\", n=3))\n    return \"\".join(diff_lines)\n\n\ndef grep(workspace: Workspace, config: Config, pattern: str, path: str = \".\", include: str = \"\") -> dict:\n    try:\n        hits = workspace.grep(pattern, path=path, include=include or None)\n    except re.error as e:\n        return _result(f\"ERROR: invalid regex: {e}\", error=True)\n    except ValueError as e:\n        return _result(f\"ERROR: {e}\", error=True)\n    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001\n        return _result(f\"ERROR: {e}\", error=True)\n    if not hits:\n        return _result(\"(no matches)\")\n    lines: list[str] = []\n    current: str | None = None\n    for fpath, lineno, text in hits:\n        if current != fpath:\n            if current is not None:\n                lines.append(\"\")\n            current = fpath\n            lines.append(f\"{fpath}:\")\n        lines.append(f\"  Line {lineno}: {text[:300]}\")\n    content = \"\\n\".join(lines)\n    if len(hits) >= 100:\n        content += \"\\n\\n(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)\"\n    return _result(content)\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789893361.0800502, "id": "24a1a085c46a", "tool_call_id": "call_75c122f70d0745f7bebc70b2", "content": "349:     def read(self, path: str) -> str:\n350:         p = self.resolve(path)\n351:         if not p.is_file():\n352:             raise FileNotFoundError(path)\n353:         return p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n354: \n355:     def read_range(self, path: str, lo: int, hi: int) -> bytes:\n356:         p = self.resolve(path)\n357:         with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n358:             f.seek(lo)\n359:             return f.read(hi - lo)\n360: \n361:     def size(self, path: str) -> int:\n362:         return os.path.getsize(self.resolve(path))\n363: \n364:     def write(self, path: str, content: str) -> None:\n365:         p = self.resolve(path)\n366:         p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n367:         # newline=\"\\n\": byte-exact on every host. The .clc project file is\n368:         # byte-addressed (offsets + in-place header updates), and the SSH bridge\n369:         # writes LF, so the local backend must not let Windows text mode expand\n370:         # every \\n into \\r\\n (that desynchronizes every stored offset).\n371:         p.write_text(content, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")\n372: \n373:     def list(self, path: str) -> list[str]:\n374:         p = self.resolve(path)\n375:         if not p.is_dir():\n376:             raise NotADirectoryError(path)\n377:         return sorted(f.name + (\"/\" if f.is_dir() else \"\") for f in self.visible_entries(p))\n378: \n379:     def append_line(self, path: str, line: str) -> None:\n380:         p = self.resolve(path)\n381:         # one LF terminator per line (the remote bridge's `printf '%s\\n'` shape):\n382:         # the .clc JSONL offsets count exactly one newline byte per event\n383:         with open(p, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\") as f:\n384:             f.write(line + \"\\n\")\n385: \n386:     def write_at(self, path: str, offset: int, data: bytes) -> None:\n387:         p = self.resolve(path)\n388:         with open(p, \"r+b\") as f:\n389:             f.seek(offset)\n390:             f.write(data)\n391: \n392:     def grep(self, pattern: str, path: str = \".\", include: str | None = None) -> list[tuple[str, int, str]]:\n393:         rx = re.compile(pattern)\n394:         root = self.resolve(path)\n395:         files = [root] if root.is_file() else self._grep_files(root)\n396:         out: list[tuple[str, int, str]] = []\n397:         for f in files:\n398:             if self.is_protected(f):\n399:                 continue\n400:             rel = str(f.relative_to(self.root))\n401:             if include and not (fnmatch.fnmatch(f.name, include) or fnmatch.fnmatch(rel, include)):\n402:                 continue\n403:             if self._is_binary(f):\n404:                 continue\n405:             try:\n406:                 with open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") as fh:\n407:                     for i, line in enumerate(fh, 1):\n408:                         if rx.search(line):\n409:                             out.append((rel, i, line.rstrip(\"\\n\")))\n410:                             if len(out) >= 100:\n411:                                 return out\n412:             except OSError:\n413:                 continue\n414:         return out\n415: \n416:     def _grep_files(self, dirpath: Path) -> list[Path]:\n417:         files: list[Path] = []\n418:         for ent in sorted(dirpath.iterdir()):\n419:             if ent.name.startswith(\".\") or ent.name == \"__pycache__\":\n420:                 continue\n421:             if ent.is_dir():\n422:                 files.extend(self._grep_files(ent))\n423:             elif ent.is_file():\n424:                 files.append(ent)\n425:         return files\n426: \n427:     @staticmethod\n428:     def _is_binary(p: Path) -> bool:\n429:         try:\n430:             with open(p, \"rb\") as f:\n431:                 return b\"\\x00\" in f.read(1024)\n432:         except OSError:\n433:             return True\n434: \n435: \n436: # Minimal sshd drops a single exec over ~8KB: writes are chunked below it;\n437: # limits come from transport_defaults.json (shared with ui/ssh-tunnel.js).\n438: _EXEC_CHUNK_BYTES = int(_DEFAULTS[\"exec_chunk_bytes\"])\n439: _EXEC_MAX_COMMAND_BYTES = int(_DEFAULTS[\"exec_max_command_bytes\"])\n440: \n441: # generous per-op timeout for internal remote file ops (read/write/list/append)\n442: _REMOTE_IO_TIMEOUT = 60.0\n443: # how much of a failed exec's stderr/stdout to show in the raised error\n444: _ERR_SNIPPET = 300\n445: # undo stack depth per file (snapshots before each overwrite)\n446: _MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE = 5\n447: \n448: \n449: class RemoteWorkspace(Workspace):\n450:     \"\"\"Workspace whose files live on a remote host reached over the exec bridge.\n451: \n452:     Every exec starts a fresh shell, so read/write/list use absolute paths and\n453:     run() prefixes `cd '<root>' &&` for the same cwd semantics as LocalWorkspace.\n454:     File operations are plain POSIX sh — cat, ls, and `printf '%s'` appends (no\n455:     base64, no SFTP) — so any device that can run an sshd shell works. Writes and\n456:     appends are chunked so no single exec command exceeds the sshd's limit.\n457:     \"\"\"\n458: \n459:     def __init__(self, root: str | None, bridge_url: str) -> None:\n460:         super().__init__(root, transport=SshTransport(bridge_url))\n... (showing lines 349-460 of 715; use offset=461 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789893495.919778, "id": "6df22944074f", "content": "契约与源码都在了。还差两个语义细节：`LocalWorkspace.resolve` / `visible_entries` 的确切行为（决定 CLI 里 cwd 语义的路径解析和目录列表过滤）。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_c94e27e13441490897298a4b", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"def (resolve|visible_entries|is_protected|__init__|_is_binary)\",\"path\":\"agent/tools/workspace.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "I have everything I need except a couple of details:\n\n1. `workspace.resolve` and `workspace.list` visible_entries — list uses `self.visible_entries(p)` not plain iterdir. Let me check what visible_entries does — probably filters hidden. Also `resolve` semantics (absolute-ization against root). For our CLI, paths resolve against process CWD. Let me grep workspace.py for `def resolve` and `def visible_entries`.\n\n2. The host grep output format differs from what I planned! Looking at filesystem.py grep:\n```\nlines.append(f\"{fpath}:\")\nlines.append(f\"  Line {lineno}: {text[:300]}\")\ncontent += \"\\n\\n(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)\"\n```\nSo the actual channel format is:\n- per file: `path:` then for each hit `  Line N: text` (2-space indent), blank line between files\n- cap footer: `(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)` when hits >= 100\n- `(no matches)` when empty.\n\nThat's the \"逐字节平移\" target. Note the current system prompt's grep description (path:  Line N:) differs from the host code — I port the HOST CODE since that's the frozen semantic baseline (\"grep 走 100 上限... 逐组空行分隔、300 字符截行\" — matches the code).\n\n3. read_file format from host:\n- directory: `\"\\n\".join(sorted(entries))` or `\"(empty directory)\"` — entries from workspace.list = sorted(name + \"/\" for dirs). And offset/limit on dir → error \"cannot read a line range of a directory; list it without offset/limit\".\n- file, no range: text, if len > limit_chars: head + `\\n... [truncated, file is N chars; use offset=K to continue]` where next_offset = head.count(\"\\n\") + 1.\n- file, with range: `_read_range` — lines `\"{i+1}: {ln}\"` (1-based numbering via enumerate start=start → so f\"{i + 1}\" where i starts at start... wait: `enumerate(selected, start=start)` gives i starting from start, then prints i+1. start = offset-1, so line numbers = start+1 = offset. Correct.) Over-budget → ERROR \"the requested range (lines A-B) exceeds the read limit of N chars. Use a smaller limit, or search with grep instead.\" Hint when not fully shown: `\\n... (showing lines X-Y of N; use offset=Y+1 to continue)`.\n\nSo the \"showing lines\" hint exists in range mode; the \"[truncated...use offset=K]\" hint in whole-file mode. Matches the tool descriptions.\n\n4. write_file summary: `f\"OK: wrote {p} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\"` if old else `f\"OK: wrote {p} ({len(content)} chars)\"`. Note `{p}` is the resolved Path — ABSOLUTE path in host (workspace.resolve). The pending decision: CLI summary shows path as-given (relative) instead of absolute. In host, p is absolute because workspace.root is fixed. For CLI with cwd semantics, resolve() against cwd would give absolute too. Decision item #3: as-given vs absolute. My rec: as-given (keeps output stable/portable, matches relative diff headers when path is relative... wait diff header uses rel = p.relative_to(root) which in CLI = the relative path if under cwd).\n\nHmm wait, for the CLI: host diff header `a/{rel}` where rel is path relative to workspace root. CLI equivalent: path relative to CWD. If user passes relative path, rel = as-given (normalized?). If absolute path under cwd → relativize. If absolute outside cwd → as-is (host does the same: rel = p).\n\nDecision: implement resolve = Path(path).absolute()? Host resolve probably does root/path with some normalization. Let me check `def resolve` and `visible_entries` in workspace.py.\n\n5. edit_file host:\n- missing file → `ERROR: file not found: {path} — use write_file to create it`\n- empty old_string → `ERROR: old_string is required` (code? 64? 65? Hmm. Frozen table says 65 = \"edit old_string not found or ambiguous\". Empty old_string is a usage/parameter problem → could be 64. But argparse can enforce non-empty? required=True only ensures presence; `--old-string \"\"` gives empty. I'd make it 64 usage or... let me check what I flagged before. The summary said \"edit 的 0 次与 >1 次分码报错\" — 0次=not found, >1次=ambiguous. Both 65 per frozen memory table (\"edit old_string not found or ambiguous\"). And missing file → 66 EX_NOINPUT. Empty old_string → 64 (contradictory/invalid arguments)? The exitcodes.py docstring I already wrote says: 65 = \"data-level failure (edit target not found or ambiguous, requested line range exceeds the read budget, bad stdin bytes)\". Interesting — so in the previous session I already classified range-over-budget → 65 and \"bad stdin bytes\" → 65. That docstring is already committed to disk and reviewed by user? It was written by me last session. The user's instruction now: decision points must go to them. The pending review items remain: (a) dir+range code, (b) bad regex code, (c) summary path display. Those three are explicitly listed in the summary as \"向用户汇报「错误码分配细目」待复核项\".\n\nGiven exitcodes.py docstring already pins: range over budget → 65. dir+range and bad regex still open. I'll implement with recommendations: dir+range → 64 (EX_USAGE: flags contradict the input kind — matches exitcodes 64 docstring \"contradictory arguments\"!) — yes! exitcodes.py 64 line says \"contradictory arguments\". dir+range = contradictory args → 64. Bad regex → hmm, \"bad syntax in a parameter\" is EX_USAGE's canonical text, but a regex is data... The 65 docstring says \"data-level failure\". GNU grep → 2. My rec: 64 for bad regex (bad flag/parameter syntax), since the caller constructs the pattern, and 65 is reserved for data-content issues. Actually hold on — for a host integration, the distinction matters little; the host will map to prose. But let me give