rectories end with '/')\". No mention of hiding dotfiles. And earlier session summary said grep skips `.` 开头 but said nothing about list skipping dotfiles. Let me trust the code: visible_entries = sorted(root.iterdir()) minus protected, sort key (is_file, name.lower()). So dotfiles INCLUDED in read_file listings. Port: no dotfile filter in read_file dir listing. (The CLI has no protected set, so no filter at all.)\n\nSo CLI read_file dir listing:\n```python\nentries = sorted(p.iterdir(), key=lambda e: (e.is_file(), e.name.lower()))\nnames = sorted(e.name + (\"/\" if e.is_dir() else \"\") for e in entries)\ncontent = \"\\n\".join(names) if names else \"(empty directory)\"\n```\nWait — host: `\"\\n\".join(sorted(entries)) if entries else \"(empty directory)\"` where entries = list of suffixed strings from visible_entries. sorted(strings) — plain str sort. ✓ my code above matches (build suffixed names, plain-sort, join).\n\n2. `resolve`: root = realpath(root); base = root/rel; realpath parent + final component. CLI equivalent: root = Path.cwd().resolve(); base = root / path (Path handles absolute path input: root / absolute → absolute ✓); p = realpath(parent)/name. Then NO containment check (pure capability) → return p. This gives: relative paths anchored at cwd, symlinks in the middle resolved, final component not followed, `..` normalized by realpath. And `~` NOT expanded (host resolve doesn't expanduser for tool paths? It does `root / rel_path` — `~` stays literal. Correct: tool paths don't expand ~. Fidelity ✓ keep as-is.)\n\nEmpty path \"\"? base = root / \"\" = root → directory. read_file(\"\") → listing of cwd. Fine.\n\n3. is_protected — CLI drops (no protected set). visible_entries without protected filter = sorted iterdir.\n\nNow — one more check: workspace.read: `if not p.is_file(): raise FileNotFoundError` — read on a DIRECTORY → FileNotFoundError → host renders file_missing... wait no: read_file flow: first tries workspace.list (raises NotADirectoryError for file/missing), then workspace.read raises FileNotFoundError → file_missing.md. For a DIRECTORY list succeeds → listing. ✓ CLI same flow.\n\nEdge: read_file on missing path: list → NotADirectoryError; read → FileNotFoundError → \"file not found: {path}\" 66. ✓\n\nNow write_file to a path whose PARENT is a file: mkdir(parents=True) raises NotADirectoryError/FileExistsError → OSError → 74. Fine (host: generic error).\n\nOK. Now I also want to double-check the `__init__.py` content (has __version__ 0.1.0 presumably) — for `--version` flag. I'll just use \"0.1.0\" literal? Better: `from . import __version__`. Let me quickly read __init__.py — it's tiny. Also check tests/ dir (exists — is there anything in it?). Summary said tests 待写. Check.\n\nThen write:\n- clutch_workspace/filesystem.py\n- clutch_workspace/cli.py\n- clutch_workspace/__main__.py\n- tests/conftest.py, test_cli.py, test_read.py, test_grep.py, test_write.py, test_edit.py\n\nLet me draft filesystem.py now.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"The four command implementations: a faithful port of the host's\nagent/tools/filesystem.py semantics, minus everything caller-shaped.\n\nDifferences by design (frozen contract):\n- no Workspace object: paths resolve against the process CWD (\"workspace\"\n  = whatever cwd the caller picks); no protected set, no snapshots, no\n  containment/escape verdicts\n- no error prose templates: failures raise CommandError(code, brief); the\n  caller owns the prose\n- output-channel formats (hit layout, continuation hints, footers) are\n  byte-identical to the host's tool results\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nPort notes:\n\n```python\nREAD_MAX_CHARS = 20_000\nGREP_MAX_HITS = 100\nGREP_LINE_MAX = 300\n```\n\nHmm wait: 300 truncation — host `text[:300]`. Name it. ✓\n\nread_file(path, offset=0, limit=0, max_chars=0)? CLI passes max_chars default 20000 from argparse. Host: `limit_chars = max_chars or config.read_max_chars`. CLI: argparse default = 20000, so limit_chars = max_chars. But mirror the `or` guard: if 0 passed → fall to default const. I'll do `limit_chars = max_chars or READ_MAX_CHARS` and validate max_chars >= 0 in cli (negative → usage error). \n\n```python\ndef read_file(path, offset=0, limit=0, max_chars=READ_MAX_CHARS) -> Result:\n    p = _resolve(path)\n    limit_chars = max_chars or READ_MAX_CHARS\n    # a directory reads as its entry listing\n    if p.is_dir():\n        if offset > 0 or limit > 0:\n            raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"cannot read a line range of a directory; list it without offset/limit\")\n        entries = sorted(e.name + (\"/\" if e.is_dir() else \"\") for e in p.iterdir())\n        return Result(\"\\n\".join(entries) if entries else \"(empty directory)\")\n    try:\n        text = _read_text(p)\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        raise CommandError(EX_NOINPUT, f\"file not found: {path}\") from None\n    if offset > 0 or limit > 0:\n        return _read_range(text, offset, limit, limit_chars)\n    if len(text) > limit_chars:\n        head = text[:limit_chars]\n        next_offset = head.count(\"\\n\") + 1\n        text = head + f\"\\n... [truncated, file is {len(text)} chars; use offset={next_offset} to continue]\"\n    return Result(text)\n```\n\nWait — host flow: `try: entries = workspace.list(str(p)) except NotADirectoryError: entries = None`. workspace.list raises NotADirectoryError when not is_dir. So host treats \"p.is_dir()\" → listing; else read. My is_dir() check equivalent. But OSError from iterdir (permission)? Host: workspace.list generic OSError propagates → caught by outer generic except → read_failed prose. CLI: let OSError propagate to cli.py catch-all → 70? Hmm, PermissionError on listing = I/O failure → 74 nicer than 70. I'll catch OSError in read_file dir branch → 74 brief str(e)? Let me handle OSError centrally in cli.py as 74 EX_IOERR with brief str(e). That covers read/write/list OSErrors uniformly. But careful: FileNotFoundError IS an OSError subclass — must catch it FIRST (already done in each site). Order in filesystem: catch FileNotFoundError → 66; let others bubble → cli: OSError → 74. And ValueError from resolve — resolve can't raise ValueError now (no containment) except... realpath can raise OSError on Windows weirdness; resolve() has try/except internally in host. My CLI _resolve:\n\n```python\ndef _resolve(path: str) -> Path:\n    root = Path.cwd().resolve()   # wait — host: root = realpath(root); base = root/rel; realpath(base.parent)/base.name\n    base = root / path\n    try:\n        if base.name:\n            return base.parent.resolve() / base.name\n        return base.resolve()\n    except OSError:\n        return base.resolve()  # hmm host falls back to realpath(base)\n```\n\nHost resolve: try realpath(parent)/name, on (OSError, ValueError) → realpath(base). realpath = Path(path).resolve(). Mirror:\n\n```python\ndef _resolve(path: str) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"cwd-flavored mirror of LocalWorkspace.resolve: anchor relative paths at\n    the process CWD, resolve symlinks in the parent chain, keep the final\n    component unresolved. No containment: whatever this returns is the file.\"\"\"\n    base = Path.cwd().resolve() / path\n    try:\n        if base.name:\n            return base.parent.resolve() / base.name\n    except OSError:\n        pass\n    return base.resolve()\n```\n\nHmm wait, host: else branch (no name) → p = realpath(base). Mine: falls through to base.resolve() ✓. If base.name and parent.resolve() raises → pass → base.resolve() ✓ matches host fallback.\n\nNote: Path.cwd() already absolute; .resolve() handles symlinked cwd. On Windows, Path(\"C:/x\")/ \"D:/abs\" → \"D:/abs\" ✓. /-style Git Bash paths? Path(\"/c/x\") on Windows = \"\\c\\x\" on current drive — same as host Python behavior. Fidelity fine.\n\n_read_range port:\n\n```python\ndef _read_range(text, offset, limit, limit_chars) -> Result:\n    \"\"\"Lines [offset, offset+limit) (1-based) with line numbers; a range that\n    cannot fit the char budget is an error, not a silent truncation.\"\"\"\n    lines = text.splitlines()\n    total = len(lines)\n    start = max(0, offset - 1) if offset > 0 else 0\n    end = start + limit if limit > 0 else total\n    selected = lines[start:end]\n    body = \"\\n\".join(f\"{i + 1}: {ln}\" for i, ln in enumerate(selected, start=start))\n    if len(body) > limit_chars:\n        raise CommandError(\n            EX_DATAERR,\n            f\"the requested range (lines {start + 1}-{end}) exceeds the read limit of \"\n            f\"{limit_chars} chars. Use a smaller limit, or search with grep instead.\",\n        )\n    shown = start + len(selected)\n    if shown < total:\n        body += f\"\\n... (showing lines {start + 1}-{shown} of {total}; use offset={shown + 1} to continue)\"\n    return Result(body)\n```\n\nByte-identical to host including the guidance sentence (it's part of the channel message the model/caller consumes; frozen \"内容通道格式逐字节平移\" applies to content channel; this error is an ERROR in host though — error=true content. Hmm: host returns this as error result. CLI: CommandError(65, same text). Human stderr: \"ERROR: the requested range...\" — byte-similar to host's content. Good.)\n\nwrite_file port:\n\n```python\ndef write_file(path, content) -> Result:\n    p = _resolve(path)\n    old = \"\"\n    try:\n        old = _read_text(p)\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        pass\n    try:\n        p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        p.write_text(content, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")\n    except OSError → let bubble to cli (74)\n    rel = _rel_display(p)\n    diff = _unified_diff(old, content, rel)\n    adds = ...; dels = ...\n    summary = f\"OK: wrote {_display(path)} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\" if old else f\"OK: wrote {_display(path)} ({len(content)} chars)\"\n    return Result(summary, diff=diff)\n```\n\n_display(path): DECISION #3. Host shows absolute p. I'll implement `_display(path) = path` (as-given) per my recommendation, flagged in report. And _rel_display for diff header: relativize vs cwd else absolute:\n\n```python\ndef _rel(p: Path) -> str:\n    try:\n        return str(p.relative_to(Path.cwd()))\n    except ValueError:\n        return str(p)\n```\n\nHmm host: rel = p.relative_to(workspace.root) where root realpath'd... cwd may be symlinked; p was built from resolved root. relative_to(cwd unresolved) might fail where relative_to(cwd.resolve()) works. Use Path.cwd().resolve()? p's parent chain is resolved except final component; p.relative_to(resolved_cwd) works when p under cwd. Use resolve()d cwd for the try. Actually simpler: `p.relative_to(Path.cwd().resolve())` hmm but Path.cwd() on Windows may be \"C:\\Users\\...\\foo\" and resolve() keeps it; p = resolved root/... → matches. Fine.\n\nAlso: str(p) on Windows uses backslashes; host rel = same convention. Diff headers \"a/sub/file.py\" vs \"a\\sub\\file.py\" — host on Windows also produces backslashes (Path relative_to). Fidelity: keep (bug-for-bug). Hmm — but as-given path in summary: user typed forward slashes → summary shows forward slashes while diff header shows backslashes on Windows. Host: both from p (backslashes). Minor cosmetic divergence — flag in the minor-choices list? It's part of decision #3 discussion: \"as-given\" means as-given. OK.\n\nedit_file port:\n\n```python\ndef edit_file(path, old_string, new_string) -> Result:\n    p = _resolve(path)\n    try:\n        text = _read_text(p)\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        raise CommandError(EX_NOINPUT, f\"file not found: {path} — use write_file to create it\") from None\n    if not old_string:\n        raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"old_string is required\")\n    count = text.count(old_string)\n    if count == 0:\n        raise CommandError(EX_DATAERR, f\"old_string not found in {path}\")\n    if count > 1:\n        raise CommandError(EX_DATAERR, f\"old_string appears {count} times in {path}\")\n    new = text.replace(old_string, new_string, 1)\n    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    p.write_text(new, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")\n    diff = _unified_diff(text, new, _rel(p))\n    adds/dels...\n    return Result(f\"OK: edited {_display(path)} (+{adds} -{dels} lines)\", diff=diff)\n```\n\nHost edit doesn't mkdir before write (file must exist — we just read it). Skip mkdir. ✓ (host: workspace.write does mkdir internally anyway; harmless either way; keep faithful-ish: write_text directly.)\n\nBrief for 0次: host text: \"old_string not found in {path}. The file's current content is in an earlier read; re-read it...\" — drop tail (prose) → \"old_string not found in {path}\". For >1: \"old_string appears {count} times in {path}\" (drop \"Include more surrounding lines...\"). List in report.\n\ngrep port:\n\n```python\ndef grep(pattern, path=\".\", include=\"\") -> Result:\n    try:\n        rx = re.compile(pattern)\n    except re.error as e:\n        raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, f\"invalid regex: {e}\") from None   # DECISION: 64 vs 65\n    root = _resolve(path)\n    files = [root] if root.is_file() else _grep_files(root)\n    out = []\n    for f in files:\n        rel = _rel(f)\n        if include and not (fnmatch.fnmatch(f.name, include) or fnmatch.fnmatch(rel, include)):\n            continue\n        if _is_binary(f):\n            continue\n        try:\n            with open(f, encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\") as fh:\n                for i, line in enumerate(fh, 1):\n                    if rx.search(line):\n                        out.append((rel, i, line.rstrip(\"\\n\")))\n                        if len(out) >= GREP_MAX_HITS:\n                            break\n        except OSError:\n            continue\n        if len(out) >= GREP_MAX_HITS:\n            break\n    if not out:\n        return Result(\"(no matches)\")\n    lines = []\n    current = None\n    for fpath, lineno, text in out:\n        if current != fpath:\n            if current is not None:\n                lines.append(\"\")\n            current = fpath\n            lines.append(f\"{fpath}:\")\n        lines.append(f\"  Line {lineno}: {text[:GREP_LINE_MAX]}\")\n    content = \"\\n\".join(lines)\n    if len(out) >= GREP_MAX_HITS:\n        content += \"\\n\\n(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)\"\n    return Result(content)\n```\n\nCareful: host `return out` inside inner loop when reaching 100 — breaks both loops (returns immediately). My break-break: inner break then check after outer body — need `if len(out) >= MAX: break` after inner loop too. ✓ above. Edge: host returns the moment 100th hit appended — same set of hits. ✓\n\nMissing root: root.is_file() False; _grep_files(root): root.iterdir() raises FileNotFoundError (OSError) — host catches generic → \"ERROR: {e}\". CLI: raise 66 explicitly BEFORE walking:\n\n```python\n    if not root.is_file() and not root.is_dir():\n        raise CommandError(EX_NOINPUT, f\"file not found: {path}\")\n```\n\nHmm, but then _grep_files may still hit OSError mid-walk (permission) → host: iterdir inside sorted(...) raises → propagates to generic handler. CLI: let it bubble → cli OSError → 74. Host shows error prose; CLI 74. Acceptable, list in report? Mid-walk permission errors: host ABORTS whole grep with error. CLI: also abort (74). Same shape. OK.\n\nAlso `rel` for a file root that is the search root itself: rel = _rel(f) — f == root; if path=\".\" → rel=\".\". Fine.\n\nis_binary: OSError → True (skip). ✓\n\nWait, subtle: host grep include check happens BEFORE binary check and per-file. Also is_protected skip — dropped. ✓\n\nAlso note: host grep output per hit uses `rel` (relative to workspace ROOT), so even when path=\"sub\", hits display \"sub/file.py:  ...\"? No wait — display format: `lines.append(f\"{fpath}:\")` where fpath=rel=\"sub/file.py\", then `f\"  Line {lineno}: {text}\"`. So file header line is `sub/file.py:` and hit lines `  Line N: text` (2-space indent, colon after N). NOT \"path:  Line N:\". ✓ my port matches host exactly.\n\n_grep_files:\n\n```python\ndef _grep_files(dirpath: Path) -> list[Path]:\n    files = []\n    for ent in sorted(dirpath.iterdir()):\n        if ent.name.startswith(\".\") or ent.name == \"__pycache__\":\n            continue\n        if ent.is_dir():\n            files.extend(_grep_files(ent))\n        elif ent.is_file():\n            files.append(ent)\n    return files\n```\n✓ byte-faithful (sorted by full path — host sorted(dirpath.iterdir()) same).\n\n_unified_diff + counts:\n\n```python\ndef _unified_diff(old: str, new: str, rel: str) -> str:\n    diff_lines = difflib.unified_diff(\n        old.splitlines(keepends=True), new.splitlines(keepends=True),\n        fromfile=f\"a/{rel}\", tofile=f\"b/{rel}\", n=3,\n    )\n    return \"\".join(diff_lines)\n\ndef _count_changes(diff: str) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    adds = sum(1 for ln in diff.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"+\") and not ln.startswith(\"+++\"))\n    dels = sum(1 for ln in diff.splitlines() if ln.startswith(\"-\") and not ln.startswith(\"---\"))\n    return adds, dels\n```\n\n✓ identical logic.\n\n_read_text:\n\n```python\ndef _read_text(p: Path) -> str:\n    if not p.is_file():\n        raise FileNotFoundError(str(p))\n    return p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\")\n```\n✓ mirror workspace.read. (is_file check → missing/dir both FileNotFoundError → 66.)\n\nCLI reads text via read()... note: read_text default newline translation: \\r\\n→\\n ✓ same as host read_text.\n\nNow cli.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Argument wiring: flags in -> filesystem commands -> envelope out.\n\n  clutch-workspace [--json] <command> [flags]\n\n- human mode: content (plus the unified diff for writes/edits) on stdout,\n  `ERROR: <brief>` on stderr, sysexits code as the exit status\n- --json: ONE compact JSON line {\"content\", \"error\", \"diff\"} on stdout for\n  both outcomes; the exit status still carries the sysexits code\n`--json` must come BEFORE the subcommand (global flag, argparse ordering).\nA flag value of `-` reads the value from stdin (write/edit payloads), so\ncallers can push multi-KB bodies past the Windows 32K argv limit.\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nargparse:\n\n```python\nclass _Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n    def error(self, message):  # usage errors are EX_USAGE(64), not argparse's 2\n        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)\n        self.exit(EX_USAGE, f\"{self.prog}: error: {message}\\n\")\n```\n\nCareful: self.exit(status, message) writes message to stderr then SystemExit(status). argparse's default exit() prints message via print_usage... Actually ArgumentParser.exit(status=0, message=None): if message: self._print_message(message, _sys.stderr); then _sys.exit(status). So `self.exit(EX_USAGE, \"...\")` prints to stderr and raises SystemExit(64). ✓ Decision item #2: argparse 2→64 mapping — implemented, confirm with user.\n\nVersion: `parser.add_argument(\"--version\", action=\"version\", version=f\"clutch-workspace {__version__}\")` — version action calls parser.exit(0, message) → prints to stdout. ✓ default exit(0) fine (only error() overridden).\n\nBuild:\n\n```python\ndef build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:\n    parser = _Parser(prog=\"clutch-workspace\", description=\"...\", allow_abbrev=False)\n    parser.add_argument(\"--json\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"emit one JSON envelope line on stdout instead of human text\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--version\", action=\"version\", version=f\"clutch-workspace {__version__}\")\n    sub = parser.add_subparsers(dest=\"command\", metavar=\"<command>\", required=True)\n\n    read = sub.add_parser(\"read_file\", help=\"read a file (or list a directory), line-numbered slices with continuation hints\")\n    read.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True, help=\"file or directory, relative to the process CWD\")\n    read.add_argument(\"--offset\", type=int, default=0, metavar=\"N\", help=\"1-based first line to read\")\n    read.add_argument(\"--limit\", type=int, default=0, metavar=\"N\", help=\"max lines to return\")\n    read.add_argument(\"--max-chars\", type=int, default=READ_MAX_CHARS, metavar=\"N\", help=\"char budget before truncation (default 20000)\")\n\n    grep = sub.add_parser(\"grep\", help=\"regex search across text files, capped at 100 hits\")\n    grep.add_argument(\"--pattern\", required=True, help=\"Python regex\")\n    grep.add_argument(\"--path\", default=\".\", help=\"file or directory to search (default: the CWD)\")\n    grep.add_argument(\"--include\", default=\"\", metavar=\"GLOB\", help=\"fnmatch filter on file name or relative path\")\n\n    write = sub.add_parser(\"write_file\", help=\"create or overwrite a file (parents auto-created); prints a unified diff\")\n    write.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    write.add_argument(\"--content\", required=True, help=\"new file body; `-` reads stdin\")\n\n    edit = sub.add_parser(\"edit_file\", help=\"replace exactly one occurrence of old_string; prints a unified diff\")\n    edit.add_argument(\"--path\", required=True)\n    edit.add_argument(\"--old-string\", required=True, dest=\"old_string\", help=\"exact text to replace; `-` reads stdin\")\n    edit.add_argument(\"--new-string\", required=True, dest=\"new_string\", help=\"replacement; `-` reads stdin\")\n    return parser\n```\n\nallow_abbrev=False: --json before subcommand is naturally handled; prefix abbreviations could confuse (`--js`). Host style? Frozen: `--path x` space style. allow_abbrev False = stricter, better for machine callers. Minor choice, list it.\n\nNote: `--json` AFTER subcommand → argparse: read parser doesn't define --json → error → 64 ✓ (\"unrecognized arguments\"). Actually parse_args errors on unknown → 64 ✓. Good: enforced ordering naturally.\n\n_validate: negative ints:\n\n```python\ndef _check_args(args):\n    if args.command == \"read_file\":\n        if args.offset < 0: raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"--offset must be >= 0\")\n        if args.limit < 0: ...\n        if args.max_chars < 0: ...\n```\n\nHmm offset negative → host max(0, offset-1) clamps; strictness vs fidelity? Host never receives negative (model tool). CLI flag negative = usage error → 64. List in minor choices. OK.\n\npayload stdin:\n\n```python\ndef _payload(value: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"`-` means: the value arrives on stdin (Windows argv caps at 32K).\"\"\"\n    return sys.stdin.read() if value == \"-\" else value\n```\n\nApplied to --content, --old-string, --new-string ONLY (frozen list). grep --pattern NOT stdin-able (frozen memory lists exactly those three) ✓ good, no decision needed.\n\nDispatch + main:\n\n```python\ndef main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:\n    for stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n        try:\n            stream.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        except (AttributeError, ValueError, OSError):\n            pass\n    parser = build_parser()\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    try:\n        _check_args(args)\n        result = _dispatch(args)\n    except CommandError as err:\n        emit_error(err, as_json=args.json)\n        return err.code\n    except BrokenPipeError:\n        return _on_broken_pipe(args.json)\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        if args.json: _write_error_envelope(\"interrupted\")? \n        hmm — envelope needs a CommandError; make one: emit_error(CommandError(EX_SIGINT, \"interrupted\"), as_json=args.json)\n        return EX_SIGINT\n    except OSError as err:\n        emit_error(CommandError(EX_IOERR, str(err)), as_json=args.json)\n        return EX_IOERR\n    except Exception as err:  # noqa: BLE001 — a bug must say so (EX_SOFTWARE), not masquerade as bad input\n        traceback.print_exc()  → stderr\n        emit_error(CommandError(EX_SOFTWARE, f\"internal error: {err}\"), as_json=args.json)\n        return EX_SOFTWARE\n    emit_result(result, as_json=args.json)\n    return 0\n```\n\nWait order: BrokenPipeError is OSError subclass → must precede OSError ✓. KeyboardInterrupt BaseException — separate except ✓. Also flush: emit_result writes via sys.stdout.write — buffered; interpreter flushes at exit; BrokenPipeError may surface at THAT point (after main returned) → \"Exception ignored\" message. Mitigate in _on_broken_pipe: dup2 devnull onto stdout fd. Standard:\n\n```python\ndef _on_broken_pipe(as_json: bool) -> int:\n    # silence the interpreter's shutdown flush on the dead pipe\n    try:\n        os.dup2(os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY), sys.stdout.fileno())\n    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):\n        pass\n    return EX_IOERR\n```\n\nHmm but if BrokenPipe happens in emit_error (stderr dead)? Rare; fine.\n\nAlso: in json mode when BrokenPipe — stdout is dead, can't emit envelope. Just code 74. OK.\n\nKeyboardInterrupt json envelope: emit to stdout {\"content\":\"interrupted\",\"error\":true,\"diff\":\"\"} code 130. Fine.\n\nTraceback for EX_SOFTWARE: traceback.print_exc(file=sys.stderr) ✓ human+json both (json keeps stdout clean).\n\nWait — Exception during _dispatch includes OSError from filesystem writes: caught → 74 with str(err). e.g. \"[Errno 13] Permission denied: 'x'\". OK.\n\nBut ALSO: FileNotFoundError leaking from grep walk? I pre-check root; _grep_files could raise FileNotFoundError only if root vanishes mid-run → 74 via OSError. fine.\n\n_dispatch:\n\n```python\ndef _dispatch(args: argparse.Namespace) -> Result:\n    if args.command == \"read_file\":\n        return filesystem.read_file(args.path, offset=args.offset, limit=args.limit, max_chars=args.max_chars)\n    if args.command == \"grep\":\n        return filesystem.grep(args.pattern, path=args.path, include=args.include)\n    if args.command == \"write_file\":\n        return filesystem.write_file(args.path, content=_payload(args.content))\n    if args.command == \"edit_file\":\n        return filesystem.edit_file(args.path, old_string=_payload(args.old_string), new_string=_payload(args.new_string))\n    raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, f\"unknown command: {args.command}\")\n```\n\n(last line unreachable — required subparsers.)\n\n__main__.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"`python -m clutch_workspace` — same entry the console script wraps.\"\"\"\nfrom .cli import main\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\nNow __init__.py — read it (tiny) to see if __version__ defined. And tests/ dir contents. Quick check both via read (tests dir listing). Then write everything.\n\nTests design (black-box subprocess):\n\nconftest.py:\n\n```python\nimport os, subprocess, sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport pytest\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\n\nclass Run:\n    def __init__(self, tmp_path): self.tmp = tmp_path\n    def __call__(self, *args, cwd=None, stdin=None, env=None):\n        env = dict(os.environ, **(env or {}))\n        env[\"PYTHONPATH\"] = str(ROOT)  # run from source, no install needed\n        env.setdefault(\"PYTHONUTF8\", \"1\")  # hmm — our reconfigure handles it; keep default encoding? Actually child stdout is a pipe; python uses locale encoding (cp1252 on Windows) unless PYTHONUTF8 — BUT cli.main reconfigures stdout/stderr to utf-8 FIRST thing. So fine without. Still set PYTHONUTF8=1? It changes stdin encoding too! stdin.read() when payload via `-` → stdin pipe decoded with locale encoding unless PYTHONUTF8. reconfigure stdin too in main! I reconfigure only stdout/stderr currently. Add stdin to the loop? stdin.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\") — safe guard same way. OR set PYTHONUTF8 in tests env only — but real callers won't... Real callers on Windows with UTF-8 payload via stdin: without PYTHONUTF8, stdin decodes cp1252 → mojibake. So CLI MUST reconfigure stdin as well. Do it. (And I'll note it.)\n        proc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace\", *args], cwd=cwd or self.tmp, input=stdin, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\", env=env)\n        return proc\n```\n\nReturn proc; tests use .returncode/.stdout/.stderr.\n\nWait: `text=True, encoding=\"utf-8\"` decodes child output as utf-8 ✓ (child reconfigured to utf-8).\n\nAlso PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE? Child writes __pycache__ into package dir — harmless. But grep tests create tmp dirs with __pycache__ name deliberately — fine.\n\nutf-8 mode: also pass `-X utf8`? Simpler: env PYTHONUTF8=1 in tests, AND reconfigure stdin in cli for real-world. Both.\n\ntest_cli.py:\n- --version → 0, stdout startswith \"clutch-workspace 0.1.0\"\n- no args → 64, stderr contains \"usage\"\n- unknown command → 64\n- missing required flag → 64\n- --json after subcommand → 64 (enforced ordering)\n- --json envelope success: single line, keys exactly {content,error,diff}, error is False, json.loads works; human vs json same content\n- --json error envelope: error true, content = message, exit 66 etc.\n- stdin payload: write_file --content - with stdin=\"data\\n\" → file written\n- UTF-8: write 中文 content via --json, read back, ensure_ascii=False (json output contains raw 中文)\n- human error goes to stderr with \"ERROR:\" prefix, stdout empty\n- broken pipe: in-process test — monkeypatch sys.stdout with object raising BrokenPipeError on write, call cli.main([\"read_file\",\"--path\",\"f\"]) → returns 74. Use monkeypatch on clutch_workspace.cli.sys.stdout.\n- KeyboardInterrupt: raise inside _dispatch via monkeypatch → 130.\n- OSError → 74: write_file to path whose parent is a file: e.g. tmp/file (a file) then write_file --path file/child.txt → mkdir parents raises NotADirectoryError (WinError) → 74. Cross-platform ✓ (on Linux also NotADirectoryError). Good black-box test.\n- internal error → 70: monkeypatch filesystem.read_file to raise RuntimeError in-process → main returns 70, stderr has traceback. (In-process via monkeypatch.setattr(cli.filesystem, \"read_file\", boom)).\n\ntest_read.py:\n- basic read: content printed as-is (no line numbers without range) — wait: whole-file read = RAW text (host: returns text unchanged, no numbering). Only RANGE mode numbers lines. ✓ test that.\n- offset/limit numbering + continuation hint: file with 5 lines, --offset 2 --limit 2 → \"2: l2\\n3: l3\\n... (showing lines 2-3 of 5; use offset=4 to continue)\"\n- exact EOF: no hint when shown==total\n- over-budget range → 65, message contains \"exceeds the read limit\"\n- whole-file truncation: 30000-char single-line file, default max-chars → head 20000 + \"... [truncated, file is 30000 chars; use offset=2 to continue]\" — single line: head.count(\"\\n\")+1 = 1 → offset=1?? hmm: head = text[:20000] no \\n → next_offset = 1. Host math identical. Hmm, offset=1 would re-serve same head... host acknowledges this (\"point the model at the first unread line instead of re-serving the same head\") but for a single-line file there IS no next line. Fidelity: keep. Test the exact string.\n  With multi-line: \"line\\n\"*15000 → each 5 chars; head 20000 chars = 4000 lines; next_offset = 4001? head.count(\"\\n\")=4000 → +1 = 4001 ✓ test hint.\n- --max-chars custom: --max-chars 10\n- directory listing: dirs get \"/\", sorted, dotfiles INCLUDED (host behavior!) — test .hidden present. Hmm wait, is that right? visible_entries: no dot filter → yes included. Test locks it. In report, note \"dir listing shows dotfiles (host-identical)\".\n- (empty directory)\n- dir + offset → 64 (DECISION item — test asserts 64 per my implemented rec; if user flips, change one raise + this test)\n- missing file → 66, \"file not found: {path}\" (as-given path in message)\n- read a directory as ... read missing path with --offset also → 66 (file check first? host: dir check via is_dir → false → read → FileNotFoundError → 66 regardless of offset ✓)\n- CRLF file: read normalizes to \\n (universal newlines) ✓\n- bad-encoding file (latin-1 bytes 0xE9) → replacement char U+FFFD, exit 0\n- offset beyond EOF: --offset 99 on 5-line file → selected=[] → body=\"\" → shown=0 < 5 → hint \"(showing lines 1-0 of 5...\"? Let's compute host: start = 98; end = total=5 (limit=0 → end=total=5! wait end = start + limit if limit > 0 else total → end=5; selected = lines[98:5] = [] ✓. body=\"\". shown = 98+0 = 98 < 5? NO 98<5 false → no hint. Result: empty string content, exit 0. Host: same → _result(\"\") → empty. OK: test returns empty output (stdout \"\\n\"). Fidelity ✓.\n\ntest_grep.py:\n- hit format: two files, pattern matches both → grouped: \"f1.txt:\\n  Line 2: x\\n\\nf2.txt:\\n  Line 1: y\"\n- no matches → \"(no matches)\" exit 0\n- include filter by name \"*.py\" and by rel \"sub/*.py\" — note fnmatch: \"*\" matches \"/\" in python fnmatch (it's not glob) → \"sub/*.py\" matches \"sub/a/b.py\" too. fine.\n- hidden skipped: .secret not searched; __pycache__ skipped; but explicit --path .secret (file root) IS searched? host: files=[root] directly — root file bypasses name filter ✓ test explicit dotfile search works when path points at it.\n- binary skip: file with \\x00 in first 1024 bytes skipped; binary PAST 1024 bytes with match later → still searched? host reads 1024 only; put \\x00 at offset 2000, match \"needle\" at line 1 → hits ✓ test.\n- 100 cap: 120 matching lines in one file → 100 hits + footer \"(Results capped at 100; use a more specific pattern or path.)\" ✓ also rel group header once.\n- 300-char line truncation: line of 400 chars matching → \"  Line 1: \" + first 300 chars.\n- bad regex \"(\" → DECISION code (implement 64) + \"invalid regex:\"\n- missing search root → 66\n- search a single file as path → hits with rel\n- regex features: \"^def \\w+\" works (Python dialect — frozen \"Python re IS the reference\")\n- rel paths use forward...on Windows str(path) backslash — DON'T assert separator! Assert with os.sep or just check basename present. Tests must be Windows-safe: use os.sep joins or check \"Line 2:\" presence. I'll build expected with os.sep. Hmm: tmp_path on Windows → rel like \"sub\\\\f.txt\". json/human output contains that. Assert f\"f1.txt\" pieces... simpler: create files directly in tmp root (no subdir) for format tests; subdir tests only assert counts/footer. Good.\n\ntest_write.py:\n- create new: summary \"OK: wrote {path} (N chars)\", diff has +++ a/{rel} +lines; file exists with exact bytes LF; parents auto-created (sub/dir/new.txt)\n- overwrite: summary \"(+A -D lines)\", diff -old +new\n- no change overwrite: old=\"x\" write \"x\" → diff empty; summary: old truthy → \"(+0 -0 lines)\". Host: old non-empty → +0 -0. ✓ test.\n- content \"\" on new file: summary \"(0 chars)\"; write empty to EXISTING file → old=\"x\" → summary \"(+0 -0 lines)\"? diff between \"x\" and \"\" → -x. adds=0 dels=1 ✓.\n- newline normalization: write \"a\\r\\nb\" → file bytes \"a\\nb\" ✓ and read back shows \"a\\nb\".\n- trailing newline preserved: \"x\" without \\n stays \"x\" (write_text no auto-append) ✓.\n- json envelope: diff field carries the unified diff, content = summary.\n\ntest_edit.py:\n- unique replace: content change, diff, summary \"OK: edited {path} (+1 -1 lines)\"\n- not found → 65 + \"old_string not found in {path}\" (as-given path in message; file unchanged)\n- ambiguous → 65 + \"appears 2 times\"\n- replace count 1 semantics: \"aa\" in \"aaa\"? count(\"aa\") in \"aaa\" = 1 (non-overlapping) → replaces first → \"a\"+\"new\"... test old=\"aa\" new=\"X\" on \"aaa\" → \"aX\"? replace(\"aa\",\"X\",1) → \"Xa\"? \"aaa\".replace(\"aa\",\"X\",1) = \"Xa\" ✓ eh, skip exotic.\n- missing file → 66 \"file not found: {path} — use write_file to create it\"\n- empty old_string → 64 \"old_string is required\"\n- multiline old/new with - separator... old/new via flags with embedded \\n from shell: tests pass python list args so \\n in arg fine.\n- stdin `-` for old/new.\n\nAlso test_read: read with BOTH offset+limit, offset only, limit only.\n- offset only: --offset 3 → lines 3..5 + hint if cut? end=total → to EOF, hint when shown<total never (end=total). Wait: offset only → start=2, end=total, selected to EOF, shown=total → no hint ✓.\n\nDirectory listing order test: create \"B.txt\", \"a.txt\", \"dir\" → sorted plain: \"B.txt\" < \"a.txt\" < \"dir\" (uppercase first). assert that order with \"/\" suffix on dir. And .hidden included.\n\nEdge: read_file --path \"\" → listing of cwd? Path.cwd()/\"\", base.name == \"\"... Path(\"C:/x\") / \"\" → \"C:/x\", .name = \"x\" hmm actually Path(\"C:/x\")/\"\": PurePath join with \"\" → same path; .name = \"x\". So read cwd listing ✓ fine, skip test.\n\nNow — Windows console: child stdout is pipe → reconfigure(utf-8) works (io.TextIOWrapper). In --version test, output \"clutch-workspace 0.1.0\\n\" ✓.\n\nHuman mode success prints content + \"\\n\" (even empty content → \"\\n\"). ✓ envelope emit_result.\n\nOne more check on emit_result human mode: prints content then diff if non-empty. diff ends with \"\\n\" normally (difflib lines keepends) — emit handles missing trailing newline ✓ already written last session.\n\nCheck emit for human diff + content: for write new file: content summary line + \"\\n\" + diff. Good.\n\ngrep include with empty string default → include=None → host: include param \"\" → passes include or None → None → no filter ✓ mine: `if include and ...` ✓.\n\nNow write the test for \"--json before subcommand\": [\"grep\", \"--pattern\", \"x\", \"--json\"] → 64 ✓. And [\"--json\",\"grep\",...] works.\n\nAlso black-box empty-directory listing: mkdir empty → \"(empty directory)\".\n\nI think we're ready to write files. Also read __init__.py + tests dir first (batch), then write 3 source files + 6 test files.\n\nActually — one design point I should double check: emit_error human mode writes ONLY stderr; but frozen says human mode errors → stderr \"ERROR: <brief>\" ✓ already in envelope.py.\n\nBrokenPipe + json: can't write envelope; return 74 ✓.\n\nLet me also decide grep --pattern \"-\"?? Frozen: stdin only for the three payload flags → pattern \"-\" is literal regex ✓ consistent.\n\ntest for stdin with --json: write via stdin, json envelope content summary ✓ covered in test_cli stdin test (human) + maybe json variant.\n\nAlright — batch: read __init__.py + list tests/."}
