matches the house convention of focused commits and is honest. Two commits is nice. Or one initial commit is also defensible for a new repo. The memory says the release flow uses focused commits — for a brand-new repo, splitting feat/test is a clean nod to the convention. I'll do two.\n\nCommands:\n```\ncd clutch-workspace\ngit init -b main\ngit add .gitignore pyproject.toml clutch_workspace/\ngit commit -m \"feat: ...\"\ngit add tests/\ngit commit -m \"test: ...\"\ngit log --oneline\ngit status --short\n```\n\ngit init -b main: git version on Windows should support -b (git 2.28+). Fallback: git init then git branch -m main. Use `git init -b main 2>/dev/null || (git init && git branch -m main)` to be safe.\n\nCommit message content — bilingual? House convention seems English prefixes; the Clutch repo commits... I don't know the language of past messages, but memory shows prefix style only. I'll write English summary lines (safe, conventional), maybe with a short body. For the feat commit, a body listing the contract essentials is valuable for a first commit. Keep heredoc quoting safe in sh: use -m with single quotes; avoid apostrophes in message text. Avoid backticks in -m within single quotes is fine, but keep it simple. No apostrophes like \"don't\".\n\nfeat message:\n\"feat: read_file/grep/write_file/edit_file CLI over a --json envelope and BSD sysexits\n\nStandalone zero-dependency port of the host tool semantics: cwd is the\nworkspace, paths resolve against the process CWD with no containment;\n--json must precede the subcommand; usage=64 data=65 noinput=66 io=74;\npayload flags accept - to read stdin; UTF-8 forced on all streams.\"\n\nHmm multi-line with -m \"...\" containing newlines is fine in sh with quotes.\n\ntest message:\n\"test: 64 black-box subprocess cases across formats, filters and exit codes\"\n\nThen verify. Also confirm nothing unexpected got committed (git status clean, log shows 2 commits).\n\nThen update memory: the 4 decisions are now ratified (no code change), repo initialized with 2 commits, next steps = host integration deferred, ruff optional, PyPI/push later (W2 open: physical location/repo remote). Actually W2 was repo physical location — stays as subfolder for now.\n\nAlso mention in final report: committed as two focused commits, hashes, what's next (host wiring per Q21/31-33, release = optional pip build later).\n\nSave updated memory: overwrite \"clutch-workspace M1 实现+测试全绿，4 个决策待用户拍板\" — the title says pending decisions; now ratified. save_memory overwrites same title; but title itself would be stale (\"待用户拍板\"). Better: write a NEW memory with updated title reflecting the new state, and... the old one remains as a stale duplicate. The instructions say \"Saving the same title again overwrites it.\" I can't delete memories. Best: overwrite the old title's content to point to the new state? That leaves a misleading title. Alternative: create new memory \"clutch-workspace M1 committed: 4 decisions ratified\" and ALSO overwrite the old one's content with a one-liner \"superseded — see 'clutch-workspace M1 committed: 4 decisions ratified'\". That keeps search results accurate. Do both saves.\n\nThen final report. Let me run the git commands. One command chain, but per the 30s timeout convention memory says run test suites one per command — git ops are fast, chain them."}
