{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789896031.9528573, "id": "5c488c66fff7", "content": "好问题——工具本体已经能用，我先把「三种用法 + 速查 + 实跑效果」给你。先实际跑几个只读命令演示（chat 模式只读，write/edit 只给命令样例不真执行）：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_05dee1b0d9614507a3d668e4", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cd clutch-workspace && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace --version && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace read_file --path clutch_workspace/exitcodes.py --limit 8 && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace read_file --path tests && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace grep --pattern \\\"^def read_file\\\" --path clutch_workspace && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace --json read_file --path pyproject.toml --limit 3 && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace read_file --path ghost.txt; echo \\\"exit=$?\\\"\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user asks: \"现在我该怎么使用workspace?\" (How do I use the workspace now?) followed by \"继续\" (continue).\n\nSo they want to know how to actually USE the clutch-workspace CLI tool. This is a usage documentation question. Let me give them a practical guide:\n\n1. Where the tool lives: `clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/` — it's not installed globally. Two ways to run:\n   - `python -m clutch_workspace ...` from the repo root (with PYTHONPATH or cwd inside the repo)\n   - Install it: `pip install -e clutch-workspace` into some environment, then the `clutch-workspace` console script becomes available anywhere\n   - The venv has pytest but the package isn't installed into the venv either (tests run via PYTHONPATH)\n\nLet me verify how to actually invoke it right now without install. From the workspace root (C:/Users/12741/Desktop/临时文件/Clutch):\n\n```\nclutch-workspace/.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace --help\n```\n\nWait — does that work? `python -m clutch_workspace` requires clutch_workspace package importable. Cwd is `clutch-workspace/` (repo root) so the package dir `clutch_workspace/` is in cwd → Python adds cwd to sys.path for `-m`. Yes, works.\n\nOr with any Python (e.g. /c/Python314 python.exe) from the repo root.\n\nOr after `pip install -e .` → `clutch-workspace` command available globally (in that env's Scripts).\n\n2. Show concrete usage examples for each subcommand, with real commands the user can copy-paste:\n   - read a file\n   - read with offset/limit\n   - list a directory\n   - grep\n   - write_file\n   - edit_file\n   - --json mode\n   - stdin `-` trick\n\n3. Key design point: \"workspace = wherever you run it from\" (cwd). That's the mental model: cd to a project folder, then run the tool — it can only see that folder's stuff (well, relative paths resolve there; it's pure capability so absolute paths escape, but the intended usage is cd + relative).\n\n4. The intended real consumer is the clutch host later, but for now the user can use it manually.\n\nShould I actually demo it? A quick live demo would be excellent — show real output. I'm in chat mode, read-only. Running `python -m clutch_workspace read_file --path ...` is read-only — allowed. But write_file demos would write files — NOT allowed in chat mode (mutating). Hmm: run_command whitelist rejects writes. Writing demo files into a temp dir via the tool = a write to disk. The whitelist may reject... the tool itself writes via subprocess. The whitelist checks the command string probably; running the CLI tool that writes a file — would that be caught? The command itself is `./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace write_file ...` — no obvious write keyword. But ethically/instruction-wise: chat mode must not modify the workspace. Writing demo files into the repo would be a workspace modification. I could demo write into the system temp dir (%TEMP%) which is outside the workspace... The rule says \"you must never modify it [the project files/workspace]\". %TEMP% is outside the workspace. Git Bash /tmp = %TEMP%. Writing dem