raft:\n\n```bash\ncd clutch-workspace && PY=.venv/Scripts/python.exe && $PY -m clutch_workspace --version && echo --- && $PY -m clutch_workspace read_file --path clutch_workspace/exitcodes.py --limit 8 && echo --- && $PY -m clutch_workspace read_file --path tests && echo --- && $PY -m clutch_workspace grep --pattern \"def read_file\" --path clutch_workspace && echo --- && $PY -m clutch_workspace read_file --path 不存在.txt; echo \"exit=$?\"\n```\n\nHmm, variables in the command — fine for sh. But whitelist analysis: `$PY -m ...` might confuse a static analyzer; safer to spell full path each time. Long but safe.\n\nAlso note: read_file on tests dir lists entries. Nice demo.\n\nOne concern: the tool's stdout reconfigured utf-8; Git Bash console shows fine. The repo path has 临时文件 (Chinese) — cwd display might garble in output but that's cosmetic (we saw mojibake earlier in pytest output — that was Git Bash console codepage; tool output itself is utf-8).\n\nWait — earlier pytest output showed 临时文件 as garbled ( temporadas file chars). That's the console encoding of the tool harness output. The clutch-workspace CLI reconfigures its streams to utf-8 and our capture shows... in the demo, content is ASCII anyway. Directory listing shows filenames — ASCII. OK.\n\nAlso worth demoing --json on a success:\n\n`$PY -m clutch_workspace --json read_file --path pyproject.toml --limit 3`\n\nThat shows the envelope line. Include it.\n\nLet me finalize the command (full paths, ; only before echo exit):\n\ncd clutch-workspace && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace --version && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace read_file --path clutch_workspace/exitcodes.py --limit 8 && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace read_file --path tests && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace grep --pattern \"^def read_file\" --path clutch_workspace && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace --json read_file --path pyproject.toml --limit 3 && echo --- && ./.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m clutch_workspace read_file --path ghost.txt; echo \"exit=$?\"\n\nread_file --limit 8 on exitcodes.py shows the numbered... wait no — without offset, limit alone triggers RANGE mode (offset>0 or limit>0) → numbered lines \"1: ...\" — good demo of numbering + hint (exitcodes.py has ~20 lines → hint appears). \n\ngrep \"^def read_file\" → filesystem.py Line 29 hit \"def read_file(...)\" — but line is long; 300-char cap fine.\n\n--json demo: pyproject.toml first 3 lines as content — single JSON line with \\n escaped — shows envelope.\n\nThen the final answer assembles: how to run (3 ways), command cheat sheet, demo outputs annotated, scripting contract (exit codes + --json), stdin trick, and note that write/edit demos are shown as commands (not run now — chat mode read-only; also honestly they'd write real files).\n\nActually wait — should I demo write_file into /tmp? It would make the answer more complete (\"show the diff output\"). But chat mode: \"run_command rejects anything that could write files\" — a tool invocation that writes a file under /tmp... The whitelist is static; `python -m clutch_workspace write_file` contains no flagged token, might pass. But the SPIRIT: don't modify... /tmp is outside the project. Still, I'd rather not gamble on whitelist semantics; the read demos + clear command examples suffice. User can try writes themselves in one paste.\n\nLet me also double-check: is there anything about `pip install -e` path spelling for Git Bash on Windows: `clutch-workspace/.venv/Scripts/pip.exe install -e .` — venv has pip? venv created with --seed? They installed pytest via pip (memory says 清华镜像 install), so pip exists in venv. So: `cd clutch-workspace && .venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -e .` → then `.venv/Scripts/clutch-workspace.exe` exists → add to PATH or call by full path. Also could install into system Python: `python -m pip install -e \"C:/Users/12741/Desktop/临时文件/Clutch/clutch-workspace\"` → `clutch-workspace` lands in Python314/Scripts; PATH? memory says /c/Python314 is on PATH; Scripts subdir may or may not be. I'll present the venv route as primary (self-contained), system route as option, and note how to verify `which clutch-workspace`.\n\nAlright, run the demo now, then compose the final answer."}
