er session + security toggles. The UI → host app → transport → CLI. So CLI stays config-agnostic (receives flags); the UI config UI + persistence live in host (ui + config storage). Confirm this division: clutch-workspace does NOT read the UI's config store; host transport translates UI config into CLI flags. That respects \"tool封装没有配置入口\" — wait, but if host transport assembles CLI args from config, then the transport/tool wrapper IS the configuration conduit. The user says tool封装 has NO configuration entry — meaning the tool wrapper cannot *set* the workspace/security itself (no per-call override, no tool-level knob); it only relays what UI configured. So: 配置源头唯一 = UI; tool 封装只是传递，不能新增/覆盖。\n\nI think that's the right reading. Let me also consider: maybe the user means the UI configures it DIRECTLY on the workspace component (e.g., the UI talks to clutch-workspace or writes its config), and the host tool layer is completely unaware (no flags pass through tool wrappers at all — the CLI reads config from a known location). Hmm. \"客户端ui对其有配置权，但tool封装没有配置入口\" — both readings satisfy \"UI yes, tools no\". The difference: does configuration flow UI→host→CLI flags, or UI→config-file→CLI direct?\n\nThis is exactly a decision point to ask rather than guess. Given the standing instruction (\"必须让我决策\"), I should present my understanding + the decision questions.\n\nGiven the user asked \"你清楚我的意思吗\", the answer format:\n1. 是的，复述一遍我的理解（简洁、带一张小图）\n2. 指出这推翻了哪两条冻结决策（纯能力/cwd 语义；v1 零配置文件）\n3. 列出需要拍板的具体问题（4-6 个，带推荐项），让他逐条裁决\n\nKeep it accessible (the user earlier appreciated plain explanations with concrete examples). Use a small diagram (mermaid or ASCII). Mermaid rules: careful with quotes in labels; use subgraph id + quoted title.\n\nLet me make a simple mermaid flow:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    UI[\"客户端 UI（人）\"] -- \"配置：任务目录 + 安全限制\" --> WS[\"clutch-workspace（CLI）\"]\n    WS --> FENCE[\"围栏内执行：read/grep/write/edit\"]\n    T[\"tool 封装（host 的 4 个工具函数）\"] -- \"只能发起调用，无任何配置入口\" --> WS\n```\n\nHmm wait — actually the tool封装 calls the workspace to DO work; the UI configures it. Both touch the workspace but with different rights. Let me refine:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph host[\"clutch host\"]\n      UI[\"客户端 UI\"] --> CFG[\"配置（任务目录+安全限制）\"]\n      TOOLS[\"tool 封装<br>read_file/grep/write_file/edit\"]\n    end\n    CFG -- \"唯一配置入口\" --> CLI[\"clutch-workspace CLI\"]\n    TOOLS -- \"只传调用，不能配置\" --> CLI\n```\n\nCareful with <br> in mermaid labels — mermaid supports <br/> in some renderers; to be safe avoid it. Keep labels simple ASCII/中文.\n\nMaybe:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph host[\"clutch host\"]\n        UI[\"客户端 UI（人）\"] --> CFG[\"配置：任务目录 + 安全限制\"]\n        TOOLS[\"tool 封装（四个工具函数）\"]\n    end\n    CFG -->|\"唯一的配置通道\"| CLI[\"clutch-workspace CLI\"]\n    TOOLS -->|\"只发起调用，不能配置\"| CLI\n    CLI --> TASK[\"在任务目录围栏内执行\"]\n```\n\nThat's clean. No quotes-in-quotes issues.\n\nThen decision points. Let me formulate crisp questions with recommendations:\n\n**Q-A「安全限制」具体搬哪些进来？**（host 的 Workspace 里现有的安保）\n1. 越界拒绝（containment）— 必搬（这是核心）\n2. protected 文件名单（对工具隐藏/不可写的路径）— 搬不搬？\n3. 外部路径审批（allowed escapes，host 权限引擎批过的例外）— 搬不搬？\n4. snapshot/undo 安全网 — 搬不搬？\n推荐：1 必搬；2 搬（参数形式）；3 搬（否则审批流程没法落地）；4 先不搬（host 保留，属于「后悔药」不属于「围栏」）。\n\n**Q-B 任务目录参数的形态与默认值**\n- `--workspace <dir>` 全局参数（与 --json 并列、在子命令前）？\n- 不传时：默认 cwd（向后兼容、测试不炸）还是直接报错要求必填？\n推荐：全局 `--workspace`，缺省 cwd。host 永远显式传。\n\n**Q-C 配置怎么流到 CLI**（UI 的配置权落地点）\n- 方案 1：host 传输层每次 spawn 把 UI 配置翻译成命令行参数（--workspace X --protect y ...）。CLI 无状态零配置文件，保持「v1 零配置文件」精神。\n- 方案 2：CLI 自己读配置文件（如任务目录下 .clutch.json），UI 只写文件。CLI 有状态。\n推荐方案 1：CLI 保持无知，「tool封装没有配置入口」落实为「传输层只允许转发 UI 配置、不得自行增删」——但这条要写死在哪一层？（host 的 transport 是代码约束，靠纪律；配置文件则结构上就做不到被 tool 层篡改）\nHmm wait — actually if the tool封装 (agent-facing tool functions) pass flags, they COULD sneak config. The user explicitly says tool封装没有配置入口. In方案 1, the spawn happens in transport.py (host side, not the LLM-facing tool functions). The LLM-facing tool functions (filesystem.py wrappers) just produce tool calls; transport spawns. So \"tool封装\" = the host's agent/tools layer including transport? If config lives in host Config/UI settings, and transport reads Config (not tool args), then tool functions have no entry — they never see or pass workspace config. That matches. 方案 2 alternative: CLI reads a config file the UI wrote; then even host code can't misconfigure it; strongest structural guarantee, but adds config-file semantics to a repo frozen as \"zero config\".\n\nPresent both, recommend 方案 1 (flags, CLI stays pure; entry = transport reads UI config; tool functions never touch it). Note that this CHANGES the frozen \"zero config\" not at all (flags aren't config files) — good.\n\n**Q-D 违反围栏的退出码**\n- 新增 77 EX_NOPERM（sysexits 标准成员，语义正好是 permission denied），还是复用 65/66？\n推荐：新增 77。信封/文档同步。\n\n**Q-E host 侧旧围栏去留**\n- Workspace 里的 resolve/protected/escapes 逻辑：整体删除（信任下放后的唯一围栏在 CLI），还是暂时保留双保险？\n冻结的 Q21 是「full-cut + tag 回滚」。推荐：按 Q21 精神 full-cut，tag 兜底。\n但这是个更大的动作（host 改动），也许本轮只动 clutch-workspace，host 集成仍按原计划后置。ask.\n\n**Q-F 远程工作区（RemoteWorkspace/SSH bridge）**\n本轮 scope 只覆盖本地 CLI？推荐：是，远程后续再说。\n\nHmm, that's 6 questions — a lot. The user said \"你清楚我的意思吗\" — maybe they want confirmation first, THEN we enumerate. But per their standing instruction, batching the decision points now with recommendations is exactly what they want (\"让我决策而不是静默执行\"). Last time (M1) the same pattern worked well: confirm + decision list with recommendations + \"都按推荐\" option.\n\nAlso should I mention what happens to frozen decisions? Yes — explicitly state: this reverses \"pure capability / security stays in host\" and touches \"cwd 语义\". The memory must be updated after ratification. Also update the memory files AFTER user rules (or now note pending). I'll note in the reply that upon confirmation I'll update the frozen-decisions memory.\n\nShould I do any reading to verify host-side specifics (e.g., Config object, ui settings shape)? For THIS reply (understanding + questions), current context suffices. After rulings, during implementation, I'd read host config/transport more carefully.\n\nOne more consideration on \"任务目录\": in clutch UI there may already be a concept of \"workspace dir\" selection when starting a session (the UI picks a folder). Likely yes (agent works in a chosen directory). So \"任务目录可配置\" aligns with existing UI behavior; what changes is that the CLI takes it as a parameter instead of inheriting spawn cwd. Fine.\n\nAlso subtle: with --workspace, do paths still resolve relative to cwd too? E.g. --workspace X but user passes relative path \"a.txt\" — resolve against workspace X (the fence root), not process cwd. Yes: task dir becomes the anchor root. And cwd becomes irrelevant (except when --workspace omitted, default cwd). Spell this out in Q-B.\n\nAlso grep's rel display should anchor to the workspace root now (not cwd) — consistent.\n\nEdge: should --workspace accept multiple roots (fences)? No — keep single. Not worth asking.\n\nLet me also reflect: \"把安全限制也下放到workspace\" — \"也\" might reference the earlier下放 of file tools to clutch-workspace (the CLI). So: \"as we already moved the file tools down to the workspace CLI, now ALSO move the security restrictions down\". Yes, that supports Reading A (security into clutch-workspace). And \"任务目录\" = the workspace root = \"可配置的参数\" = CLI parameter. 客户端 UI has config rights over it (the UI decides task dir + security policy), tool封装 no config entry (host tool layer can't override/confuse it). My understanding stands.\n\nStructure of reply:\n1. 「清楚」+ 一句复述 + mermaid 图\n2. 这会推翻/修改哪两条冻结决策（点明）\n3. 新契约草案一句话：UI 定围栏，CLI 执行围栏，tool 封装只调用不配置\n4. 六个拍板点（合并成 5-6 个，带推荐）\n5. 提示：拍板后我会更新冻结记忆、改 CLI + 测试；host 侧集成按原计划另行执行\n\nKeep explanations plain per user preference. Reasonable length — this is an architecture discussion; be thorough but not bloated.\n\nNo tool calls needed. Answer directly."}
{"type": "final", "timestamp": 1789896392.5264397, "id": "b9b4c161ceb8", "status": "completed", "summary": "清楚，而且这是一次方向性反转——先把我的理解复述给你验货，再说要你拍板的点。\n\n## 我理解的架构\n\n「安全围栏」从 host 搬进 clutch-workspace：任务目录从「隐式的进程 cwd」变成**显式可配置的参数**；配置权只在客户端 UI（人）手里；host 的 tool 封装层只能发起调用，**没有任何配置入口**——它既不能指定任务目录，也不能改安全策略，从结构上杜绝「被服务的 agent 自己给自己挪围栏」。\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph host[\"clutch host\"]\n        UI[\"客户端 UI（人）\"] --> CFG[\"配置：任务目录 + 安全限制\"]\n        TOOLS[\"tool 封装（read/grep/write/edit）\"]\n    end\n    CFG -->|\"唯一的配置通道\"| CLI[\"clutch-workspace CLI\"]\n    TOOLS -->|\"只发起调用，不能配置\"| CLI\n    CLI --> TASK[\"在任务目录围栏内执行四个命令\"]\n```\n\n一句话：**UI 定围栏，CLI 执围栏，tool 封装只敲门口禁上的铃。**\n\n## 这会推翻两条已冻结决策\n\n1. 「纯能力、无越界保护、cwd 即 workspace」→ 变成「CLI 内置围栏 + `--workspace` 参数」\n2. 相关的「安全/分类留在 host」→ 变成「围栏下放，host 只剩配置转发」\n\n拍板后我会同步更新冻结记忆，避免以后的会话拿旧契约说事。\n\n## 要你拍板的 6 个点（都带推荐）\n\n**① 「安全限制」具体搬哪些进来？** host 的 Workspace 现有四样安保：\n- 越界拒绝（路径解析后必须落在任务目录内）——**必搬**（核心）\n- protected 名单（对工具隐身/禁写的路径）——推荐**搬**，以参数形式传入\n- 外部路径白名单（host 权限引擎批过的例外路径）——推荐**搬**，否则审批流程无处落地\n- snapshot/undo 后悔药——推荐**不搬**（它是撤销机制不是围栏，先留在 host）\n\n**② 任务目录参数长什么样？**\n推荐：全局 `--workspace <dir>`（和 `--json` 并列、放子命令前）。**不传时默认 cwd**（向后兼容，已写的 64 个测试不炸）；host 将来永远显式传。传了之后，相对路径就锚在任务目录而不是 cwd。\n\n**③ UI 的配置怎么流到 CLI？** 两个方案：\n- **方案 A（推荐）**：CLI 保持零配置文件、无状态；host 传输层每次启动时把 UI 配置翻译成命令行参数（`--workspace X --protect y ...`）。「tool 封装无配置入口」= 配置只存在于 host 的 UI 设置层，tool 函数碰不到、传不了，传输层只做忠实转发。\n- 方案 B：CLI 自己读配置文件（如任务目录下的 `.clutch.json`），UI 只负责写文件。结构上更强（host 代码想配也配不了），但 CLI 从「零配置」变「有状态」，和你之前拍的 v1 零配置相反。\n\n**④ 违反围栏的退出码？** 现在的表（0/64/65/66/70/74/130）没有「越权」档。推荐**新增 77**（sysexits 标准成员 EX_NOPERM，语义正好）。\n\n**⑤ host 侧旧围栏去留？** Workspace 里的 resolve/protected/escape 逻辑：按你之前 Q21 的精神 **full-cut 删除**（唯一围栏在 CLI，出问题靠 tag 回滚）——但这是 host 改动，可以本轮只动 CLI、host 集成维持原计划后置。推荐后者（本轮不碰 host）。\n\n**⑥ 远程工作区（SSH bridge 那套）本轮管吗？** 推荐**不管**，围栏下放先只做本地 CLI，远程桥的安全语义单独一轮。\n\n---\n\n你可以逐条回（比如「①全搬除了undo ②同意 ③A ④77 ⑤本轮不碰host ⑥不管远程」），也可以「都按推荐」。拍板后我：改 CLI（围栏实现 + `--workspace` + 77 码）→ 补测试跑绿 → 提交 → 更新冻结记忆。"}
{"type": "user_message", "timestamp": 1789896437.2343524, "id": "a3192917ddbf", "content": "为什么撤销机制还留在host内？它不应该是workspace的功能之一吗"}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789896575.257445, "id": "6cdb7ecc1499", "content": "问得好——先让我把 host 里撤销机制的真实现状摸一遍再回答，避免凭印象说错：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_40e9b959d8a04917b49f5026", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"agent\",\"pattern\":\"snapshot|undo|Undo\"}"}, {"id": "call_efb45475720540d595c29768", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"ui\",\"pattern\":\"undo|Undo\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user asks: \"为什么撤销机制还留在host内？它不应该是workspace的功能之一吗\" — \"Why is the undo mechanism still in the host? Shouldn't it be one of the workspace's features?\"\n\nContext: In my previous message, in decision point ①, I recommended NOT moving snapshot/undo into clutch-workspace (\"推荐不搬（它是撤销机制不是围栏，先留在 host）\"). The user is now questioning that recommendation — they're arguing the undo mechanism should also be part of the workspace component.\n\nNote the task title says: \"为什么撤销机制还留在host内？它不应该是workspace的功能之一吗\" — this is the question to answer. It's a design-philosophy question. I should:\n\n1. First, actually explain WHY I recommended keeping it in host (my reasoning at the time)\n2. Then genuinely evaluate their counter-argument — is it right? Actually, let me think deeply about this.\n\nTheir point: the workspace component is the thing that touches files. Undo (restore previous content) is fundamentally a file-operations concern. If the workspace owns write/edit, it should own undo. That's a coherent \"workspace = the file layer, with all its lifecycle\" argument.\n\nMy original reasoning for keeping undo in host:\n- The user framed the下放 as moving the SECURITY FENCE (围栏). Snapshot/undo is not a fence — it's a safety net. But wait, is that distinction actually sound?\n\nLet me think about what the undo mechanism IS in the host currently:\n- From workspace.py: `_MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE = 5` — \"undo stack depth per file (snapshots before each overwrite)\"\n- filesystem.py tool code: `workspace.snapshot(p, old)` before write/edit overwrites\n- LocalWorkspace has snapshot() storing old content in memory (presumably), and presumably an undo() method\n- The undo is per-file, in-memory snapshots (up to 5 per file), keyed by realpath\n\nAlso from the system context: the UI has an Undo button — \"If you corrupt a file, tell the user to use the Undo button next to the change\". So the undo feature is exposed in the clutch UI for file changes made by the agent.\n\nNow the architecture question: where should undo live?\n\nArguments for moving into clutch-workspace:\n1. **Cohesion/ownership**: The component that writes files should own the ability to restore them. If the CLI is \"the workspace\" (the file layer), undo is a file-layer lifecycle feature, not an agent-orchestration feature.\n2. **Host integration direction**: The host is heading toward full cut-over (Q21) — tools become subprocess calls to clutch-workspace. If host keeps undo, host must still READ files independently (to snapshot) — wait, no: currently host snapshots by reading old content before calling the tool. In the full cut-over world, host tool wrappers call the CLI via transport; the host wouldn't naturally have the file content in hand to snapshot (the CLI does the write internally). Actually host COULD read-before-write via its own workspace... but in the target architecture where the CLI owns file ops, the host would need to duplicate reads just to snapshot. That's awkward and racy. So in the cut-over end-state, undo almost HAS to live in the writing component = the CLI.\n\nHmm wait — actually let me check: currently in host, filesystem.py write_file does `workspace.snapshot(p, old)` where old was read via workspace.read. The host tool wrapper reads the old content itself. In the future transport-based integration (host spawns CLI subprocess per tool call), the host wrapper would have to read the file first (another subprocess call or direct read) to snapshot, then call CLI write — doubling I/O and introducing a race window. If the CLI does snapshot internally before write, it's atomic-ish and single-component.\n\n3. **The user's new architecture logic**: \"UI 定围栏，CLI 执围栏\" — the UI is the human's control point. The Undo button in the UI is also a human control point! If the UI has configuration rights over the workspace, the UI could equally have INVOCATION rights over undo (call `clutch-workspace undo` / `restore`). Actually the user explicitly said earlier: \"客户端ui对其有配置权，但tool封装没有配置入口\" — configuration rights for UI, no entry for tools. An undo COMMAND would be a tool exposed... hmm, to whom? If undo becomes a CLI subcommand, then the agent (via tool封装) could potentially call undo too — is that desirable? Maybe! The agent corrupting a file and self-correcting via undo is useful. Or restrict it.\n\nWait, but should undo be exposed to the agent? In the host today, is undo exposed as an agent tool? Let me recall... The system prompt for clutch (this environment) doesn't show an \"undo\" tool in my toolset. The UI has an Undo button — so undo is a UI/human feature, not an agent tool. The snapshots are taken by the tool layer automatically (before each overwrite), and the UI can roll back.\n\nSo undo = automatic snapshotting + UI-triggered restore.\n\nIf snapshots move into the CLI: the CLI takes snapshots internally on write/edit (zero API change for callers), and restore is either a subcommand (`clutch-workspace undo --path x`) or a `restore_file` command the UI/host invokes. The host UI calls it via transport/bridge. The agent doesn't get an undo tool unless host chooses to expose one — host controls which subcommands its tool封装 maps to. That's clean: tool封装 maps only the 4 commands to the agent; the UI's undo button calls the undo subcommand directly. \"tool封装没有配置入口\" remains true; and the undo surface stays human-controlled while the CLI owns the data path.\n\nArguments for keeping undo in host (my original reasoning):\n1. Scope discipline: v1 CLI was frozen minimal (4 commands); adding undo expands scope, adds state.\n2. Statefulness: snapshots in memory die with the process. The CLI is spawned per-call (stateless, short-lived)! THIS is the big technical wrinkle: host transport spawns the tool subprocess PER TOOL CALL (subprocess black-box model). In-memory snapshots in the CLI die when the process exits → snapshots would need to live ON DISK (e.g., .clutch/snapshots/ or hidden dir in the workspace). That's a design consequence the user must decide: persistent on-disk snapshot store inside the task dir (hidden folder), or CLI gains a server/daemon mode (no), or... On-disk is the obvious answer: snapshots stored under e.g. `<workspace>/.clutch/undo/` — which conflicts with \"dotfiles invisible to grep\" (fine, grep already skips dot-entries) and must be excluded from grep results and probably protected from the agent's own write/edit (the fence should protect the snapshot store from the agent — recursive protection question!).\n3. Where does host undo go across... currently host keeps snapshots in memory across tool calls because the host app is long-running. Moving to CLI means persistence = disk.\n\nSo the real answer to \"why did I keep it in host\": (a) it wasn't part of the fence the user asked to下放; (b) the per-call stateless CLI model makes undo require on-disk state, which is a bigger design step; (c) scope discipline on the frozen 4-command contract.\n\nBut the user's counter-argument is actually architecturally sound, especially in the end-state:\n- The writer owns the restore. Splitting them means host needs its own read path + racy double-read.\n- In full cut-over, host loses direct file access idioms anyway (it goes through the CLI).\n- The UI already treats workspace ops as workspace features; undo naturally belongs next to them.\n- It also strengthens the trust story: snapshots taken by the same fence that guards writes → the snapshot store can be fenced automatically (agent can't tamper with its own undo history — host-side snapshots are equally tamperable via file writes actually! If agent writes to .clutch/undo via relative path... host fence would need protected paths; CLI fence can natively protect its own store).\n\nWait, actually there's a subtlety: currently host snapshots live in MEMORY of the host process — the agent CANNOT tamper with them at all (they're not on disk). Moving snapshots on-disk inside the workspace makes them potentially agent-reachable (it can write files in the workspace!). The fence must protect `<workspace>/.clutch/` from read (maybe ok to hide) and write/edit. That's a new protection requirement — but under the new architecture the CLI owns protection, so it can hard-protect its own store natively. That's actually an argument FOR moving undo in: the CLI can make the undo store untamperable by construction, whereas host-side on-disk stores would need... no wait host-side store is in memory, untamperable. Host in-memory undo is actually SAFER from tampering. But it only works while host lives in-process with the tools; in the subprocess cut-over model host no longer intercepts content, so host in-memory undo dies anyway unless host does read-before-call.\n\nOK so the honest technical answer:\n\n**Why it's in host TODAY**: historical — host tool layer does read-modify-write in-process, so grabbing old content for the snapshot was free; undo is wired to the UI's Undo button through the host app's memory. There was never a deep principle; it's a byproduct of the monolithic host.\n\n**Why my recommendation said keep in host for now**: (1) 你这轮下放的是围栏，undo 不是围栏是后悔药 — scope containment for this round; (2) the CLI is spawned per call and stateless — undo needs durable state, which forces an on-disk snapshot store design (where? format? retention? how hidden from grep? how protected from the agent?) — that's its own decision batch; (3) frozen 4-command contract.\n\n**Why the user is actually right in the end-state**: (1) cut-over means host stops touching file bytes; snapshot-then-write must be atomic in the writer; (2) UI's Undo button is the same \"human control point\" family as UI configuration — natural to route through the workspace component; (3) tamper-resistance: the fence owner can protect its own undo store by construction; (4) consistency: \"workspace = the file layer\" — read/grep/write/edit/undo is a coherent file-lifecycle API.\n\nSo my answer: acknowledge the user's point is architecturally correct for the end-state, explain why I'd parked it (not a fence + statefulness problem + scope), and lay out the design decisions that moving it in entails. Then let them rule. Because their standing instruction: decision points go to them. This is a genuine decision point: undo migration NOW (this round) or later round.\n\nIf they move it in this round, new decision points:\n1. **存储介质**: on-disk snapshot store (per-call stateless CLI forces this). Location: `<workspace>/.clutch/undo/`? Or alongside a global location outside task dir (e.g., user config dir)? In-task-dir makes it self-contained & travels with the folder, but pollutes the task dir & must be fenced/hidden; outside keeps task dir clean but decouples (deleting workspace folder loses undo — actually fine) and crosses the fence boundary (CLI writing outside its own fence = principle violation!). So recommend inside task dir, dot-prefixed, fenced + grep-invisible by construction.\n\nWait, do we need to ask this or recommend? Recommend inside; ask as confirmation.\n\n2. **接口形态**: subcommand(s). `clutch-workspace undo --path f.txt` (restore latest) + maybe `--list`? And `history`? Minimal: `undo_file` command restoring most recent snapshot; maybe `--depth N`? Keep minimal: one command `undo_file --path x` restores the most recent snapshot (pop). Or non-destructive `restore --version n`? Host currently: depth 5 per file, UI undo pops... I don't know exact host undo semantics (pop vs peek). There's agent/tools/workspace.py snapshot() and presumably undo()/restore. I should check host implementation to mirror semantics faithfully if we move it. That's implementation homework — mention it.\n\n3. **自动快照触发点**: write_file/edit_file take a snapshot before overwrite automatically (same as host today) — no caller change. Retention depth: 5 (host parity) or configurable? Recommend fixed 5, no knob (zero-config spirit).\n\n4. **谁有权调 undo**: agent via tool封装? or UI only? Recommend: subcommand exists; host chooses exposure — UI wired to it; agent NOT given an undo tool initially (host tool registry simply doesn't map it). This keeps \"tool封装没有配置入口\" and adds \"tool封装决定暴露面\". Actually that's host-side detail, default fine.\n\n5. **编码/保真**: snapshots store exact bytes (pre-write content), restore writes back verbatim with same newline law.\n\n6. **退出码/错误**: undo with no snapshots → 66? empty → brief \"no snapshot for path\".\n\nAlso interplay with this round's fence work: snapshot store must be (a) excluded from grep results, (b) rejected as a write/edit/read target for the agent?? If CLI protects it absolutely, then even host can't restore via write — but undo command writes it internally (same process, below the fence — implementation detail: internal writes bypass fence). And read_file on .clutch/ — hide or show? Recommend: fully invisible to read/grep/write/edit (like protected), only undo internals touch it.\n\nHmm, but careful: this adds ANOTHER security-ish feature. But it's coherent: the fence protects the fence's own bookkeeping.\n\nAlso: does undo apply to deletes? There's no delete command in CLI, so undo only covers overwrite (write on existing) and edit. Host same. Fine.\n\nSo the response structure:\n\n1. Direct answer: 你说得对（在终局架构下），我先把「为什么它今天在 host、为什么我上一轮建议先不搬」讲清楚——不是原则，是历史包袱 + 本轮范围控制 + 一个真实的技术约束。\n2. 三个理由展开：\n   a. 今天在 host 是历史：host 工具层进程内 read-modify-write，抓旧内容零成本；Undo 按钮走 host 内存。不是设计原则。\n   b. 我建议缓搬的真正原因：CLI 是「每次调用 spawn 一个进程」的无状态模型——快照放内存随进程死，必须落盘，这就带出一串新设计（存哪/格式/保留几份/怎么对 agent 隐身），值得单独一轮而不是搭围栏的车。\n   c. 但你的论点在 cut-over 终局里成立：host 不再碰文件字节后，host 侧快照要么消失要么变成「写前再读一遍」（双读 + 竞态）；「谁写谁管恢复」才是内聚的；而且围栏的持有者可以**结构性保护自己的undo 账本**（agent 改不了自己的后悔药）——这反而是下放的加分项。\n3. 所以修正立场：同意搬，但作为独立决策批次。给出若本轮就搬需要拍板的点（推荐已附）：\n   - 存储位置：任务目录内 `.clutch/undo/`（隐身 + 被围栏硬保护 + 随目录自包含）vs 任务目录外（破坏「CLI 不写围栏外」原则）→ 推荐前者\n   - 接口：新增 `undo_file --path x` 子命令（恢复最近一份），快照仍由 write/edit 自动拍摄，无需调用方改变 → 推荐就一个命令，深度固定 5 份（host 对齐）\n   - 暴露面：子命令存在于 CLI，host 的 agent 工具表**不映射**它（只有 UI 的 Undo 按钮调）→ agent 不能自己偷吃后悔药（或者你想要 agent 也能undo？）\n   - 范围：只覆盖覆盖型写入（write 已有文件/edit），同 host 现状\n4. 请求拍板：撤销机制本轮搬 or 下一轮搬？若搬，上面推荐是否照单？\n\nWait — I should also double check what host's undo actually is, to not misstate. Let me grep the host for snapshot/undo quickly to ground my claims (read-only, cheap). Files: agent/tools/workspace.py (has snapshot mention at line ~445 \"_MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE = 5\"), and probably an undo method + UI wiring. Let me grep \"snapshot|undo\" in agent/ and ui/.\n\nThat's worth doing — my answer should be grounded in the actual current implementation (e.g., whether undo is pop-on-restore, whether UI calls host API). Also the memory \"MCP web-service feature fully removed\" and \"Tool subprocess execution refactor shipped (pilot: read_file)\" are relevant: the tool subprocess refactor — read_file pilot — suggests host tools are being migrated to subprocess calls one by one! That strengthens the cut-over narrative: currently maybe only read_file goes through the subprocess, write/edit still in-process? \"pilot: read_file\" — yes, pilot only. So TODAY write/edit still run in-process in host (snapshot free), and the CLI only mirrors semantics for future cut-over. Undo in host works because host still does writes in-process.\n\nLet me verify with greps:\n1. grep \"snapshot\" in agent/\n2. grep \"undo\" in agent/ and ui/\n\nThen compose. Keep the reply focused on answering WHY + the corrected stance + decision points. Plain language like last time, maybe a small mermaid or just text. No mermaid needed really; text is fine. Keep it moderately compact.\n\nAlso remember the standing rule: user asked a \"why\" question — answer it honestly (including admitting my park-it recommendation was a scoping call, and their instinct is right for the end state). Don't be sycophantic; be technical.\n\nLet me grep."}
