{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789896646.5395486, "id": "1a05430b5654", "content": "问得对，而且查完现状我可以给你一个诚实的回答：**撤销机制留在 host 不是设计原则，是单体时代的历史产物**。你的方向在终局架构下是成立的——我上一轮建议「缓搬」有其真实原因，但那个原因值得摊开讲。\n\n## 它今天在 host 的完整链条\n\n```\nUI 的 ↶ undo 按钮 (ui/app.js:1321)\n  → server.py:485 HTTP 端点（\"User-side undo\"）\n    → Workspace._snapshots（workspace.py:118，纯内存 dict，每文件最多 5 份）\n      → undo() 弹出最近一份写回（workspace.py:137）\n```\n\n快照的拍摄点是 host 的 write_file/edit_file（filesystem.py:96、141），因为 **host 工具层是进程内 read-modify-write**——旧内容本来就在手上，顺手存一份零成本。仅此而已。\n\n## 我上轮说「缓搬」的真实原因（不是「该留」）\n\n一个硬约束：**现在的 clutch-workspace 是「每次调用 spawn 一个进程、干完就退」的无状态模型**。快照放 CLI 内存里，进程一退就蒸发——下次调用根本接不上。所以撤销下放 ≠ 搬个函数，而是必须先回答「快照持久化到哪、什么格式、存几份、怎么对 agent 隐身」。这是一整批设计决策，我不想让它搭围栏的顺风车悄悄混过去——按你的规矩，这得拿出来拍板，所以我先按「不动」报了。\n\n## 但你的论点在 cut-over 终局里是对的\n\n1. **谁写谁管恢复**：host 向 subprocess 化迁移已经在做（read_file 已是试点）。到 write/edit 也走 CLI 那天，host 手里没有旧内容了——想快照就得「自己先读一遍 → 再让 CLI 写」，双读之间有竞态窗口。快照放写入方内部是原子又内聚的。\n2. **围栏持有者能结构性保护自己的账本**：快照落盘后最怕 agent 改自己的后悔药。今天内存栈靠「碰不到」天然安全；落盘后就必须有围栏——而新架构里**围栏正好在 CLI 手里**，它可以把快照库对自己隐身+禁写，矛盾闭环。\n3. **信任层级自洽**：undo 本来就是**用户侧**控制（↶ 按钮从来不暴露给 agent）。你刚定的原则「UI 有配置权、tool 封装无入口」——撤销权和配置权是同一层级的东西，都该走「UI → workspace」这条线，而不是藏在 host 工具层。\n\n## 所以：修正立场，同意下放——但有 5 个点要你拍板\n\n| # | 决策点 | 推荐 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 快照存哪 | 任务目录内 `.clutch/undo/`：点开头（grep 的隐藏规则现成生效）、被新围栏硬保护、随任务目录自包含。存目录外会破坏「CLI 不写围栏外」原则，排除 |\n| 2 | 接口形态 | 新增 `undo_file --path x` 子命令（弹最近一份、字节保真写回、输出摘要+diff）；write/edit 覆盖前**自动**拍快照，调用方零改动；深度固定 5 份，不加旋钮 |\n| 3 | 暴露面 | 子命令存在于 CLI，但 host 的 agent 工具表**不映射**它——只有 server.py→UI 按钮调它。要不要给 agent 也开 undo 工具？（我不推荐） |\n| 4 | 语义细节 | 弹出式（undo 一次消耗一份，同 host 现状）；无快照时退 66 + `no snapshot for path`；**附带行为变化**：落盘后 undo 能活过 host 重启（今天是内存栈，重启即失——这是升级不是退化） |\n| 5 | 节奏 | 和围栏下放**同轮做**（快照库的隐身/禁写本来就要围栏就位才有意义），还是围栏先落、undo 紧随第二轮？推荐同轮 |\n\n你上一轮对「①undo 不搬」的裁决被这一问推翻了，以本轮为准。逐条回或「都按推荐」，我就把它并进围栏那批改动一起实现。", "tool_calls": [], "reasoning": "Now I have the full picture of the current undo mechanism:\n\n- `agent/tools/workspace.py:117-140`: `self._snapshots: dict[str, list[str]] = {}` — per-file undo stack **in host memory**, depth 5 (`_MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE = 5`). `snapshot()` records previous content before overwrite; `undo()` (line ~137) pops the last snapshot and writes it back, returns None when no snapshot.\n- `agent/tools/filesystem.py:96,141`: write_file/edit_file call `workspace.snapshot(p, old/text)` before overwriting.\n- `agent/server.py:485-502`: HTTP endpoint \"User-side undo: restore the last snapshot of a file in the workspace... Backs the UI's ↶ undo button\"; 404 \"no snapshot to restore\".\n- `ui/app.js:1321-1345`: the Undo button per diff calls the server endpoint; \"↶ undone\" / \"↶ no snapshot\" / \"↶ failed\".\n\nSo the current chain: **UI button → server.py HTTP endpoint → Workspace.undo() (in-memory stack) → write back**. Snapshots taken by the host tool layer at write/edit time. The AGENT has no undo tool (matches: undo is user-facing only).\n\nKey architectural facts for answering \"why is undo in host\":\n1. It's in host because the host tool layer does read-modify-write **in-process** — snapshotting was free there. It's a byproduct of the monolith, not a design principle.\n2. It's in-memory: `dict[str, list[str]]` in the long-running host process. That's WHY it can't simply move to the current clutch-workspace CLI: the CLI is spawned **per tool call** and exits — an in-memory stack would evaporate between calls. Moving undo into the CLI forces durable on-disk snapshots.\n3. My \"keep in host\" recommendation last round was a scoping call (this round = fence下放), plus the stateful-store consequence above.\n\nAnd the user's argument, evaluated honestly:\n- In the cut-over end state (host tools become subprocess calls — already piloted with read_file per the memory \"Tool subprocess execution refactor shipped (pilot: read_file)\"), the host no longer holds the bytes at write time... wait, actually in the current pilot architecture, how does host call the CLI? The transport spawns the CLI with cwd=workspace; the host tool wrapper receives the CLI's result. For write/edit, host wouldn't have read the old content — the CLI does the write internally. So for host to snapshot, it would have to do its own read BEFORE calling the CLI (double read + race window where the file could change between host-read and CLI-write). That's exactly the awkwardness that makes \"the writer owns the snapshot\" the right call.\n- Also tamper-resistance: in-memory host snapshots are untamperable by the agent but die with the process and don't survive host restarts; on-disk snapshots survive restarts but must be protected. Under the new architecture, the CLI owns the fence → it can structurally protect its own snapshot store (hide from grep/read, reject write/edit, only its undo internals touch it). That's a genuine synergy argument for moving undo in.\n- Consistency: the user's stated principle — \"UI 有配置权，tool 封装无配置入口\" — extends naturally: undo is a USER-side control (the ↶ button), so it belongs to the same trust tier as UI configuration, not to the agent tool surface. Routing it through the workspace component as a subcommand (exposed to host server → UI, NOT mapped into the agent's tool registry) fits the model perfectly.\n\nSo my answer: \n1. Why it's in host TODAY: historical accident of the monolith (in-process read-modify-write) + it's wired to server.py's HTTP endpoint for the UI button. Not a principle.\n2. Why I parked it last round: (a) this round's scope was the fence; (b) the real blocker — the CLI is per-call stateless, so undo migration = designing an on-disk snapshot store, which is its own decision batch.\n3. Why the user is right for the end state: writer-owns-restore cohesion; no double-read race; fence-protected undo ledger; undo is a user control → same tier as UI config.\n4. Revised position: agree to move it in — as its own batch (can be same round as the fence work if they want).\n5. Decision points if moved in now (with recommendations):\n   - Storage: on-disk under `<workspace>/.clutch/undo/` (hidden from grep by the dot rule already; hard-protected by the new fence; travels with task dir). Alternative outside-task-dir violates \"CLI stays inside the fence\".\n   - Command surface: `undo_file --path x` (pop latest, restore verbatim, print summary+diff like host's undo). Snapshots auto-taken by write_file/edit_file before overwrite — no caller change. Depth fixed at 5 (host parity), zero knobs.\n   - Exposure: subcommand exists in CLI; host's agent-facing tool registry does NOT map it (agent can't eat its own后悔药); only server.py→UI button calls it. (Or does the user want the agent to have undo too? Ask.)\n   - Semantics parity: pop-on-undo (destructive), 404-equivalent = exit 66 + \"no snapshot for path\"; restore writes bytes verbatim with the LF law.\n   - Restart survival: on-disk store means undo survives host restarts (behavior change vs today — improvement, worth noting).\n   - Fence interplay: `.clutch/` becomes invisible+write-protected for ALL four commands (read_file listing too? host hides protected from listings). Recommend fully invisible.\n   - Also: multiple workspaces/sessions pointing at same task dir — snapshot store is per task dir, fine.\n\nAlso note: this changes scope of the previous \"① 不搬 undo\" ruling — so I should ask them to supersede that item.\n\nFormat: plain-language answer like the previous explanation style. Structure:\n- 一句直接回答：它今天在 host 不是设计原则，是单体时代的历史产物；你的方向在终局架构下是对的，我上轮建议「缓搬」的真正原因是一个技术约束（无状态进程 vs 内存栈）。\n- 现状链条（简短，带 file:line 证据）\n- 三个理由展开（历史 / 我缓搬的真实原因 / 你的论点为何在终局成立）\n- 修正后的立场 + 若本轮搬，要拍板的 5 个点（带推荐）\n- 收尾：等裁决\n\nKeep it tight. No new tool calls needed. Chinese. Maybe a small table for the decision points. No mermaid needed."}
