{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789897749.7889507, "id": "9e6c8071981d", "tool_call_id": "call_a36d7344dbdf4b3397570c05", "content": "1: \"\"\"Per-machine supervisor: spawns and manages session subprocesses.\n2: \n3: Every UI window is a SESSION: the supervisor spawns one agent.server child\n4: (--port 0, random port parsed from its stdout banner) per session and reports\n5: the port back. The window then talks to its child DIRECTLY (locally, or over\n6: its own tunnel forward) — the supervisor never proxies traffic, it only owns\n7: the lifecycle:\n8: \n9:     POST /api/session/start      -> {session_id, port}\n10:     POST /api/session/stop       -> kill the session child\n11:     POST /api/session/heartbeat  -> keep the session alive (stale ones die)\n12:     GET  /api/health             -> ok\n13: \n14: Lifecycle (per product decision):\n15:   - the FIRST window starts the supervisor (Electron probes /api/health and\n16:     spawns it when down); the LAST window's exit ends it — each window stops\n17:     its session on close, and the supervisor self-exits after an idle grace\n18:     once no sessions remain. A stale-session reaper also clears sessions whose\n19:     window crashed (heartbeat stops).\n20:   - on Windows each session child is assigned to a kill-on-close Job\n21:     (_job_assign): the stale reaper needs THIS process alive, so a supervisor\n22:     that dies without reaping (crash, TerminateProcess, Electron's tree going\n23:     down with it) would orphan the session — the Job makes the kernel kill the\n24:     whole session tree the moment this process's Job handles die with it. An\n25:     orphaned session is not cosmetic: it keeps holding its .clc write lock,\n26:     and reopening that project then lands read-only with no visible other\n27:     window.\n28: \n29: Locking: each session child is an independent process, so the .clc write lock\n30: is a kernel file lock (flock / LockFileEx) on the machine's tmp dir —\n31: process-level mutual exclusion, released by the OS on exit, no TTL needed.\n32: (noclobber+TTL remains only for\n33: the execBridge-degraded path, where the agent process and the project files\n34: live on different machines.)\n35: \"\"\"\n36: \n37: from __future__ import annotations\n38: \n39: import argparse\n40: import ctypes\n41: import json\n42: import os\n43: import queue\n44: import re\n45: import signal\n46: import subprocess\n47: import sys\n48: import threading\n49: import time\n50: import uuid\n51: from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n52: from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer\n53: \n54: DEFAULT_PORT = 8890\n55: PORT_BANNER_RE = re.compile(r\"\\[clutch-server\\] http://127\\.0\\.0\\.1:(\\d+)\")\n56: SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S = 30.0  # child port banner: onefile extraction is slow on a remote\n57: SESSION_STALE_S = 10.0  # no heartbeat for this long -> reap the session (heartbeat interval is 8s)\n58: REAP_INTERVAL_S = 2.0\n59: IDLE_TIMEOUT_S = 8.0  # no sessions for this long -> self-exit\n60: KILL_GRACE_S = 3.0\n61: \n62: \n63: def _log(msg: str) -> None:\n64:     \"\"\"Log to stdout without EVER blocking or crashing the caller.\n65: \n66:     When the process that spawned us dies, our stdout is a dead socket; once its\n67:     buffer fills, a plain ``print`` blocks forever (the handler thread stuck in\n68:     the post-session ``print`` is exactly the orphaned-supervisor \"Empty reply\"\n69:     hang). _make_stdout_nonblocking marks the fd non-blocking so the write\n70:     raises BlockingIOError instead of blocking; we swallow it and drop the line.\n71:     \"\"\"\n72:     try:\n73:         print(msg, flush=True)\n74:     except (OSError, ValueError):  # dead/full stdout: drop the log line\n75:         pass\n76: \n77: \n78: def _make_stdout_nonblocking() -> None:\n79:     \"\"\"Turn a full write to stdout/stderr from a BLOCK into an exception, so the\n80:     supervisor's own logging (and the _wait_port forwarder) can never hang.\"\"\"\n81:     try:\n82:         import fcntl\n83:     except ImportError:\n84:         return\n85:     for fd in (1, 2):\n86:         try:\n87:             flags = fcntl.fcntl(fd, fcntl.F_GETFL)\n88:             fcntl.fcntl(fd, fcntl.F_SETFL, flags | os.O_NONBLOCK)\n89:         except OSError:\n90:             pass\n91: \n92: \n93: class _SafeStdStream:\n94:     \"\"\"stdout/stderr that keeps serving when the parent is gone. The supervisor\n95:     is often spawned by Electron and orphaned (re-parented to init/systemd) with\n96:     its stdio socketpair dangling; a print to that dead socket then raises\n97:     BrokenPipeError, which previously killed the request handler mid-response\n98:     AND the reaper loop (both die on their first print) — the port stays\n99:     squatted forever, leaking session children and blocking every later\n100:     session/start. Swallow stream errors so a dead parent can never take the\n101:     server down.\n102: \n103:     Windows has no fcntl, so _make_stdout_nonblocking skips it there and a FULL\n104:     pipe would block the printing thread forever (the same orphaned-supervisor\n105:     hang, reached without an exception). When fcntl is unavailable the stream\n106:     therefore decouples writes through a bounded queue drained by one daemon\n107:     thread: write() enqueues (or drops, never blocks) and the drain thread\n108:     absorbs however long the dead pipe stalls.\"\"\"\n109: \n110:     _QUEUE_MAX = 1000\n111: \n112:     def __init__(self, inner, async_when_no_fcntl: bool | None = None) -> None:\n113:         self._inner = inner\n114:         self._queue: queue.Queue | None = None\n115:         if inner is None:  # pythonw: no console streams at all; no-ops below\n116:             return\n117:         try:\n118:             import fcntl  # noqa: F401\n119:             has_fcntl = True\n120:         except ImportError:\n121:             has_fcntl = False\n122:         if async_when_no_fcntl or (async_when_no_fcntl is None and not has_fcntl):\n123:             self._queue = queue.Queue(maxsize=self._QUEUE_MAX)\n124:             threading.Thread(target=self._drain, daemon=True, name=\"clutch-stdio-drain\").start()\n125: \n126:     def _drain(self) -> None:\n127:         assert self._inner is not None and self._queue is not None\n128:         while True:\n129:             data = self._queue.get()\n130:             try:\n131:                 self._inner.write(data)\n132:                 self._inner.flush()\n133:             except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 -- dead/full stream: drop the line\n134:                 pass\n135: \n136:     def write(self, data):\n137:         if self._queue is not None:  # async mode: never block, drop on overflow\n138:             try:\n139:                 self._queue.put_nowait(data)\n140:             except queue.Full:\n141:                 pass\n142:             return len(data)\n143:         if self._inner is None:\n144:             return len(data)\n145:         try:\n146:             return self._inner.write(data)\n147:         except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError, AttributeError):\n148:             return len(data)\n149: \n150:     def flush(self):\n151:         if self._queue is not None or self._inner is None:\n152:             return\n153:         try:\n154:             self._inner.flush()\n155:         except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):\n156:             pass\n157: \n158: \n159: @dataclass\n160: class Session:\n161:     session_id: str\n162:     proc: subprocess.Popen\n163:     port: int\n164:     last_beat: float = field(default_factory=time.time)\n165:     # Windows: kill-on-close Job holding the whole session tree; its handle\n166:     # dies with this supervisor, and so does the session (None = unavailable)\n167:     job: int | None = None\n168: \n169: \n170: def _signal_soft(proc: subprocess.Popen) -> None:\n171:     \"\"\"Ask a session child to stop: SIGTERM to its process group (POSIX), or\n172:     CTRL_BREAK_EVENT on Windows (the child is spawned with\n173:     CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP).\n174: \n175:     Best effort, and on Windows normally unavailable: CTRL_BREAK reaches only a\n176:     process group that shares OUR console, while a packaged supervisor is\n177:     spawned by Electron with windowsHide and has no console at all — so this\n178:     raises OSError(WinError 6) instead of delivering anything. Every OSError\n179:     (that one, ProcessLookupError, PermissionError) is left to _kill_hard.\n180:     \"\"\"\n181:     try:\n182:         if os.name == \"posix\":\n183:             os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGTERM)\n184:         else:\n185:             proc.send_signal(signal.CTRL_BREAK_EVENT)\n186:     except OSError:\n187:         return\n188: \n189: \n190: def _kill_hard(proc: subprocess.Popen) -> None:\n191:     \"\"\"Stop of last resort, when the child ignored (or never got) the soft\n192:     signal: SIGKILL to the group (POSIX), or taskkill /T on Windows.\n193: \n194:     taskkill rather than proc.kill(): TerminateProcess reaps only the direct\n195:     child, so the shell it ran commands in — and whatever that shell started —\n196:     stays behind as an orphan holding its port. /T walks the tree.\n197:     \"\"\"\n198:     if os.name == \"posix\":\n199:         try:\n200:             os.killpg(os.getpgid(proc.pid), signal.SIGKILL)\n201:         except OSError:\n202:             pass\n203:         return\n204:     try:\n205:         subprocess.run(\n206:             [\"taskkill\", \"/T\", \"/F\", \"/PID\", str(proc.pid)],\n207:             capture_output=True,\n208:             timeout=10,\n209:             creationflags=subprocess.CREATE_NO_WINDOW,\n210:         )\n211:     except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):\n212:         pass\n213:     try:\n214:         proc.kill()  # taskkill missing or denied: still drop the direct child\n215:     except OSError:\n216:         pass\n217: \n218: \n219: # ---- Windows: a session child must not outlive this supervisor ----\n220: #\n221: # POSIX kills a session through its process group, and the group's reaper (this\n222: # supervisor) sits in the UI's process tree, so a dead window means a dead\n223: # reaper AND a dead group. Windows has no process group to lean on: a\n224: # supervisor that dies without walking its children (crash, TerminateProcess,\n225: # Electron's tree dying first) leaves the session child alive forever — an\n226: # invisible zombie that still holds the .clc write lock, so every later open of\n227: # that project lands read-only with no visible other window (observed in the\n228: # wild: two leftover `python.exe` from a dev supervisor kept a project locked\n229: # for good). The kernel answer is a Job with JOB_OBJECT_LIMIT_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE:\n230: # the session belongs to the Job, and when THIS process dies its handles close\n231: # with it, so the kernel terminates the whole session tree — the venv/onefile\n232: # launcher AND the real interpreter it spawned, plus anything they start. That\n233: # also covers the kill path: TerminateProcess on a launcher reaps only the\n234: # launcher, while the interpreter inside the Job dies with the Job's handle.\n235: \n236: _JOB_KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE = 0x00002000\n237: _JOB_EXTENDED_LIMIT_INFO = 9  # JobObjectExtendedLimitInformation\n238: _JOB_BASIC_ACCOUNTING_INFO = 1  # JobObjectBasicAccountingInformation\n239: _PROCESS_SET_QUOTA = 0x0100\n240: _PROCESS_TERMINATE = 0x0001\n... (showing lines 1-240 of 786; use offset=241 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
