{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789897807.9222498, "id": "00a52dc56887", "tool_call_id": "call_9a31834e3e344783992b4c3c", "content": "tests\\supervisor_test.py:\n  Line 53:     sup = Supervisor(\n  Line 120:         # heartbeat unknown session -> 404\n  Line 121:         st, _ = http_post(f\"{base}/api/session/heartbeat\", {\"session_id\": \"nope\"})\n  Line 122:         check(st == 404, \"heartbeat for unknown session rejected\")\n  Line 129:     # ---- 3. stale-session reaping (crashed window: heartbeat stops) ----\n  Line 135:     # no heartbeats: the reaper must kill it\n  Line 137:     check(ok, \"stale session reaped without heartbeat\")\n  Line 140:     # ---- 4. heartbeat keeps a session alive ----\n  Line 146:         st, _ = http_post(f\"{base3}/api/session/heartbeat\", {\"session_id\": sid4})\n  Line 147:         check(st == 200, \"heartbeat accepted\")\n  Line 149:     check(sid4 in sup3.sessions, \"heartbeated session survives past stale window\")\n  Line 318:     # holding the port. The grandchild's heartbeat is the witness: it must stop\n  Line 347:         check(wait_until(hb.exists, 10.0, \"grandchild heartbeat\"), \"the shell's child is running\")\n  Line 351:         time.sleep(0.8)  # let the last in-flight heartbeat land\n  Line 406:             sup9 = Supervisor(\n  Line 412:             sess9 = sup9.start_session()\n  Line 479:     # either guaranteed killable (Job) or is never started. _job_assign is\n  Line 481:     # banner, which also proves start_session returns BEFORE _wait_port.\n  Line 494:             sup14 = Supervisor(\n  Line 500:             orig_assign = _svmod._job_assign\n  Line 501:             _svmod._job_assign = lambda pid: None  # simulate an unavailable Job\n  Line 503:                 sess14 = sup14.start_session()\n  Line 505:                 _svmod._job_assign = orig_assign", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789897927.4811184, "id": "27e05bf5a87b", "content": "最后确认一下 import 面（谁直接 import `agent.supervisor`，entry script 长什么样），避免拆包时漏掉引用点。", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_85993e5d4b674245abc490e9", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"agent\\\\.supervisor|agent import supervisor|supervisor_entry\",\"path\":\"scripts\"}"}, {"id": "call_b8d261dfdb0647ab9f144080", "name": "grep", "arguments": "{\"pattern\":\"import supervisor|from \\\\.supervisor|from agent\\\\.supervisor|_svmod|from \\\\.\\\\.agent\",\"path\":\"tests/supervisor_test.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Now I have the complete picture. Let me organize the design.\n\n**Current state of `agent/supervisor.py` (786 lines), mixed concerns:**\n\n1. **Stdio hardening** (lines 63–157): `_log`, `_make_stdout_nonblocking`, `_SafeStdStream` — survival machinery for a daemon orphaned from its parent. NOT session-specific. Any long-lived clutch process (workspace daemon from cli-srv plan, llm-proxy) needs this.\n\n2. **Process-kill primitives** (lines 170–216): `_signal_soft`, `_kill_hard` — graceful-then-hard tree kill, cross-platform (killpg / CTRL_BREAK / taskkill /T). Generic.\n\n3. **Windows Job Object machinery** (lines 219–434): constants, ctypes structures, `_descendant_pids`, `_job_assign`, `_job_close` — kernel-level \"child must not outlive me\" guarantee. Generic, valuable for ANY child (workspace daemon too! actually the workspace daemon is exactly the case where you want kill-on-close… hmm, actually the workspace daemon should survive? No — per the cli-srv plan, daemon idle-times-out itself; but if the daemon must die with the host session, Job is the guarantee).\n\n4. **Port banner discovery** (`_wait_port`, lines 576–611): spawn child with `--port 0`, read stdout banner `[clutch-server] http://127.0.0.1:(\\d+)`, forward lines. Generic mechanism (any clutch server child), with a clutch-server-specific regex.\n\n5. **ProcessRegistry / lifecycle core** (`Supervisor` class, lines 437–645): sessions dict + lock, start/stop/heartbeat/shutdown_all, stale reaper + idle self-exit, `_kill` soft→hard→Job-close ladder. Mostly generic EXCEPT:\n   - \"session\" naming and heartbeat semantics (UI-window liveness)\n   - `agent_cmd` / `--base-url` forwarding\n   - session_id = uuid\n\n6. **HTTP lifecycle API** (lines 648–726): `/api/health`, `/api/session/start|stop|heartbeat`, `/api/shutdown`. Session-specific surface.\n\n7. **Entry point** (lines 729–786): argparse (`--port`, `--idle-timeout`, `--agent-cmd`, `--cwd`), frozen-vs-dev command default, SIGTERM handler, exit watcher.\n\n**Consumers (external contract that must not change):**\n- `ui/server-bootstrap.js`: probes `/api/health` on 8890, spawns `-m agent.supervisor --port 8890 --idle-timeout ...`, `supervisorSessionStart/Stop/heartbeat` on `/api/session/*`.\n- `ui/main.js`: local + remote (tunnel) supervisors, `/api/shutdown` on quit.\n- `ui/ssh-tunnel.js`: remote bootstrap runs `agent-supervisor --agent-cmd .../agent-server` on 8890.\n- `tests/supervisor_test.py`: pins Supervisor in-process behavior (stale reaping, heartbeat, Job refusal, _wait_port).\n- `ui/electron-builder.yml` / `server-bundle.js`: PyInstaller entries `scripts/supervisor_entry.py`, dist artifacts `agent-supervisor`, `agent-server`.\n\n**Future tenants** (per the approved cli-srv decision + other processes):\n- clutch-workspace daemon (per-workspace, discovery file, token, lazy start, idle self-exit 10min, undo stack in memory)\n- llm-proxy (ui/llm-proxy.js is node — maybe out of scope; but remote reverse-forward expects :8892 local llm-proxy)\n- agent-server sessions (current)\n- possibly remote supervisor itself\n\n**The user's ask**: extract the process-management abstraction into layers: a general \"process supervision\" layer that will broadly manage ALL clutch-related processes; specialize on top for session management.\n\n**Design — layered:**\n\nLayer 0 (leaf, no deps): `procenv` / stdio survival — `_log`, `_SafeStdStream`, `_make_stdout_nonblocking`. Name: `agent/procsurvive.py`? Better to group.\n\nLayer 1 (platform kill guarantees): `agent/prockill.py` — `_signal_soft`, `_kill_hard`, Job machinery (`_job_assign`/`_job_close`/`_descendant_pids` + ctypes structs). Expose a clean facade: `KillGuarantee` — e.g. `ensure_killable(pid) -> handle | None` + `finalize(handle)` + `kill_tree(proc, handle)`.\n\nLayer 2 (generic supervised child): `agent/procwatch.py` or `procsup.py` — `ManagedProcess` dataclass (id, proc, port, meta, guarantee handle, last_beat optional), `ProcessSupervisor` class: register/spawn (cmd template + banner spec), stop (soft→hard→finalize ladder), reaper (stale-by-heartbeat optional + idle self-exit), thread-safe. Hmm — but careful with the refactor skill's anti-over-engineering: don't propose abstractions with only one implementation... BUT here there ARE concrete imminent second tenants: the workspace daemon (approved direction, M-next) — the user explicitly states the purpose (\"以后广泛地管理所有和clutch有关的进程\"). The abstraction is the user's explicit requirement, so it's justified by direct request + at least one concrete second consumer on the roadmap.\n\nLayer 3 (specialization): `SessionSupervisor(ProcessSupervisor)` — adds: heartbeat semantics, agent_cmd default resolution (frozen/dev), `--base-url` forwarding, session HTTP API mapping, killable-refusal policy (Windows: refuse unkillable session — note: is this policy generic or session-specific? I'd argue generic policy but *configured*: `require_kill_guarantee: bool`. For workspace daemon, lazy-start from terminal, a refusal policy would be different — daemon can fall back to direct execution. So make it a policy knob).\n\nLayer 4: process identity & discovery — fixed port 8890 for session supervisor vs per-workspace discovery file for daemon. That's the cli-srv decision table #2/#3. The general layer should offer \"endpoint advertisement\" as a pluggable: `advertise(port, token?) -> discovery artifact`. But careful about YAGNI: the discovery file is for the daemon (not yet built). Should the general layer include it now? The user said \"这部分以后广泛地管理所有和clutch有关的进程\" — the refactor NOW is to layer out what EXISTS; the daemon specifics (discovery file, token) come when the daemon lands. So: keep layer 2 free of endpoint assumptions (it already returns port); leave discovery/token to the daemon specialization when it's built. Mention it as the seam.\n\nKey subtlety: what's generic vs special?\n- Generic: spawn+banner-port discovery, kill ladder (soft→hard→Job close), orphan protection (Job), idle self-exit, stale reaping (heartbeat is optional), safe stdout, HTTP lifecycle skeleton (health + shutdown are generic; session endpoints are not).\n- Session-specific: heartbeat contract with UI windows (8s interval / 10s stale), agent_cmd resolution + base_url, refusal policy tied to .clc write lock story, `/api/session/*` endpoints, fixed port 8890 machine-wide single instance.\n\nAlso generic candidate: `_wait_port`'s banner regex is `PORT_BANNER_RE` (clutch-server). Generalize as parameter `banner_re` with the current one as default.\n\n**Module layout proposal** (keep it modest — 3 new modules max, not a package sprawl):\n\n```\nagent/procmon/\n  __init__.py        (facade exports)\n  stdio.py           — _log, _SafeStdStream, make_stdout_nonblocking   (Layer 0)\n  kill.py            — soft/hard kill ladder + Windows Job guarantee      (Layer 1)\n  supervise.py       — ManagedProcess + ProcessSupervisor (spawn/port/reap/idle) (Layer 2)\nagent/supervisor.py  — SessionSupervisor(ProcessSupervisor) + HTTP API + main (Layer 3+4, shrunk ~300 lines)\n```\n\nHmm, or flat files: `agent/proc_stdio.py`, `agent/proc_kill.py`, `agent/proc_supervise.py`. The repo's agent/ package seems flat (agent/base.py, agent/config.py, agent/supervisor.py, agent/tools/...). Let me check agent/ layout quickly... I haven't listed agent/. From greps: agent/base.py, agent/config.py, agent/supervisor.py, agent/server.py, agent/core/project_lock.py, agent/tools/cli.py, agent/tools/transport.py. There's an agent/core/ subpackage. So flat with a subpackage is fine. Three flat modules or one `agent/procmgr/` package. I'd propose `agent/procmgr/` package with stdio.py/kill.py/supervise.py — keeps the namespace story \"procmgr = the general layer\" and supervisor.py remains the session specialization. Actually simpler: since kill.py machinery is Windows-ctypes heavy (~220 lines), stdio ~100 lines, supervise ~250 lines — one package of 3 modules is reasonable. Alternatively keep it flat: `agent/proc_stdio.py`, `agent/proc_kill.py`, `agent/proc_core.py`. Flat matches existing style better (agent/core/ exists for project_lock). I'll propose `agent/procmgr/` package — it reads as a product boundary (\"the general process-management layer\"), which is exactly what the user asked to carve out. Either is fine; recommend package, note flat alternative.\n\nNaming: existing log tags say `[supervisor]`; keep session module importable as before for the test (`_svmod._job_assign` monkeypatch in supervisor_test!). Important: tests/supervisor_test.py monkeypatches `_svmod._job_assign` and reads `_svmod` — the refactor must preserve that seam (either re-export `_job_assign` in supervisor.py or update the test). This is a key compatibility detail to call out.\n\n**Supervise.py API sketch:**\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass ManagedProcess:\n    pid_tag: str            # caller's identity key (session_id, workspace hash, ...)\n    proc: subprocess.Popen\n    port: int | None\n    last_beat: float        # refreshed by beat(); reaper only enforces if beat_required\n    beat_required: bool\n    guarantee: object | None  # kill-layer handle (Windows Job)\n\nclass SpawnSpec:\n    cmd: list[str]          # argv template\n    cwd: str | None\n    banner_re: re.Pattern | None   # port discovery from stdout\n    start_timeout_s: float\n    env_extra: dict | None\n\nclass ProcessSupervisor:\n    def __init__(self, *, stale_s, idle_timeout_s, reap_interval_s, require_kill_guarantee=None): ...\n    def spawn(self, key_hint..., spec) -> ManagedProcess | None   # job-assign first, banner wait, refuse if unkillable\n    def stop(self, key) -> bool          # soft -> grace -> hard -> guarantee close\n    def beat(self, key) -> bool\n    def reap_loop(self)                  # stale + idle self-exit, never raises\n    def shutdown_all(self)\n    # exit_event, exit_when_idle, last_activity\n```\n\nWait — one design decision: heartbeat/stale is session-specific-ish. The supervisor class has stale_s=10 and heartbeat; is stale-reaping generic? For a workspace daemon there are no heartbeats (daemon idles out itself; clients are stateless). So: `beat()` exists in the generic layer but reaping by staleness only applies to entries registered with `beat_required=True`. That keeps ONE reaper loop, parameterized. Good — avoids two timers.\n\nKill-refusal policy: currently Windows-only hard requirement (`job is None → refuse`). Generic layer: `require_kill_guarantee: bool | None` (None = platform default: required on nt, n/a on posix). Session sets True (must keep current behavior); daemon later may choose False with fallback-to-direct semantics.\n\nPort banner: generic `_wait_port(proc, banner_re, timeout, forward=True)`.\n\nHTTP layer: session endpoints stay in supervisor.py. The generic `/api/health` + `/api/shutdown` shape is duplicated later by the daemon; could lift a tiny `LifecycleApi` mixin later — but YAGNI: leave HTTP in the specialization now; note that the daemon will reuse `build_server`-style helper. Hmm — build_server is generic already (takes sup with `supervisor` attr...). Actually `_Handler` routes session paths; health/shutdown reference `sup.sessions`/`sup.exit_when_idle` — those are generic-layer fields. Minor: keep in place.\n\n**What stays where (mapping of current 786 lines):**\n\n| current lines | content | new home |\n|---|---|---|\n| 63–157 | _log, _make_stdout_nonblocking, _SafeStdStream | procmgr/stdio.py |\n| 170–216 | _signal_soft, _kill_hard | procmgr/kill.py |\n| 219–434 | Job machinery + _descendant_pids | procmgr/kill.py |\n| 576–611 | _wait_port | procmgr/supervise.py (generic, banner_re param) |\n| 613–645 | _kill ladder | procmgr/supervise.py or kill.py (stop(process)) |\n| 159–167 + 437–645 | Session dataclass + Supervisor | Session dataclass+SessionSupervisor stay in supervisor.py, delegating to procmgr core |\n| 648–726 | HTTP handler | supervisor.py (unchanged behavior) |\n| 707–786 | main/entry | supervisor.py (unchanged) |\n\nTwo implementation strategies:\n(A) Full inversion: `ProcessSupervisor` generic; `SessionSupervisor(ProcessSupervisor)` thin subclass mapping start_session→spawn etc. HTTP and tests keep working via delegation methods.\n(B) Composition: session module holds a `ProcessSupervisor` internally.\n\nSubclass is fine here — the session IS-A process supervisor with extra policy; but careful: Liskov — start_session(base_url) maps to spawn(spec). I'd do thin subclass or delegator; recommend delegator/composition if the method signatures would otherwise distort; but honestly subclass keeps tests (`sup.sessions` access in tests! `sid4 in sup3.sessions`) intact. tests use `sup.sessions`, `sup.start_session()`, `sup14 = Supervisor(...)`, `_svmod._job_assign` monkeypatch. Keeping the public name `Supervisor` + `Session` in agent/supervisor.py preserves ALL tests untouched; internal delegation to procmgr. The monkeypatch `_svmod._job_assign = lambda pid: None` — if start logic moves to procmgr/supervise.py, patching `_svmod._job_assign` no longer intercepts. Options: keep a module-level indirection point, or update the test to patch `procmgr.kill.job_assign`. Updating the test is fine (it's white-box anyway); OR keep the patch seam by having supervisor.py pass a `job_assign=job_assign` hook into the core. Simpler: update test to patch new location; it's our test. But behavior-preservation principle: prefer minimal test edits, and note exactly which lines change.\n\n**Also frozen builds**: `_agent_cmd_default` stays in supervisor.py (session-specific: agent-server binary). PyInstaller entries (`scripts/supervisor_entry.py`) unchanged — entry still `agent.supervisor:main`.\n\n**Benefits narrative:**\n- The next tenants land by writing a specialization, not by copying 400 lines of kill/stdio machinery: workspace daemon (cli-srv decisions ratified direction) = `ProcessSupervisor` + discovery file + no-heartbeat + its own HTTP surface; remote bootstrap unchanged.\n- Test surface: existing supervisor_test keeps pinning session behavior; add small unit tests for kill ladder/banner wait against the new modules (or reuse existing ones since behavior unchanged).\n- The platform footguns (orphaned stdio hang, taskkill-only-child, Job escape race) become single-sourced: today they're documented in one file but entangled with session naming; future daemon authors won't re-derive them.\n\n**Risks/notes:**\n- Windows Job + spawn race ordering must be preserved EXACTLY: assign before _wait_port; refuse-on-None on nt. The generic layer must make this ordering intrinsic (spawn does assign→banner), not caller-arranged.\n- `_kill` never raises + Job close in `finally` — preserve.\n- reap_loop never raises; log-and-continue — preserve.\n- stdout wrapping order in main(): wrap streams BEFORE first print, nonblocking after Supervisor construction — preserve.\n- No behavior change: same endpoints, same log lines (`[supervisor] session ... on port ...`), same defaults.\n\n**Plan steps (for work mode):**\n1. Create agent/procmgr/{__init__,stdio,kill,supervise}.py — move code verbatim (names can lose the leading underscore at module boundary: job_assign, job_close, signal_soft, kill_hard, descendant_pids, wait_port, KillGuarantee?).\n2. Rewrite supervisor.py: Session dataclass (keeps session_id/proc/port/last_beat/job? → job now inside ManagedProcess.guarantee; Session becomes a thin view or is replaced by ManagedProcess + session_id key). Decide: core returns ManagedProcess; SessionSupervisor wraps into Session for API compatibility.\n3. Update tests: only the `_svmod._job_assign` monkeypatch target (+ maybe import of private names). Run tests/supervisor_test.py + server_test full + toolcli (unaffected) etc.\n4. Commit (two commits: pure-move commit + specialization commit? Given repo convention of focused commits: commit 1 \"refactor: extract general process-management layer (procmgr) from session supervisor\", commit 2 test-touch).\n\nWait — am I supposed to implement? I'm in CHAT MODE. read-only. So I deliver this as the design/plan and tell the user to switch to work mode for implementation. Per instructions: \"If the task requires writing or editing files... tell the user clearly that the task needs work mode.\"\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider: the refactor skill Phase 1 says write REFACTORING.md and wait for approval. But I can't write files in chat mode. And the user's request here isn't a general \"audit and recommend\" — it's a specific, well-defined extraction with clear scope. The skill workflow would have me produce recommendations; my response IS the research + recommendation (the layering). Given chat mode, I'll present the full plan inline and ask them to switch to work mode, where I'd implement directly (the scope is already user-specified, so REFACTORING.md round-trip adds little — I'll present numbered recommendations so they can approve selectively, satisfying the skill's spirit).\n\nLet me also double check: is there an existing agent/procmgr or similar? No — agent/ has base, config, supervisor, server, core/, tools/. Fine.\n\nOne more check — scripts/supervisor_entry.py to confirm entry point import path. Quick grep. Also check pyproject entry points. Let me do one quick grep to be thorough about entry points that import agent.supervisor.\n\nActually let me verify who imports agent.supervisor:\n- scripts/supervisor_entry.py\n- tests/supervisor_test.py\n- ssh-tunnel.js spawns `-m agent.supervisor` (subprocess, not import)\n- server-bootstrap.js `-m agent.supervisor`\n\nA quick grep for \"agent.supervisor|from agent import supervisor|import supervisor\" in py/scripts."}
