r with spawn/stop/beat/reap_loop/shutdown_all, wait_port, stop_process (the kill ladder).\n\nDesign decisions per the approved plan:\n- `require_kill_guarantee: bool | None = None` — None = platform default (nt: required).\n- `spawn(key, spec, *, beat_required) -> ManagedProcess | None`\n- `stop(key) -> bool`\n- `beat(key) -> bool`\n- `reap_loop()` — never raises\n- `shutdown_all()`\n- fields: `processes: dict[str, ManagedProcess]`, `_lock`, `last_activity`, `exit_event`, `exit_when_idle`\n\nBehavior preservation details for spawn:\n```python\ndef spawn(self, key, spec, *, beat_required=True) -> ManagedProcess | None:\n    with self._lock:\n        self.last_activity = time.time()\n        self.exit_when_idle = False\n    try:\n        env = dict(os.environ)\n        if spec.env_extra:\n            env.update(spec.env_extra)\n        spawn_kwargs: dict = {\"start_new_session\": True} if os.name == \"posix\" else {}\n        if os.name == \"nt\":\n            spawn_kwargs[\"creationflags\"] = subprocess.CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP\n        proc = subprocess.Popen(spec.cmd, cwd=spec.cwd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, env=env, **spawn_kwargs)\n    except OSError as e:\n        log(f\"[procmgr] spawn failed: {e}\")\n        return None\n    guarantee = job_assign(proc.pid)\n    if os.name == \"nt\" and require_kill_guarantee and guarantee is None:\n        log(\"[procmgr] kill-on-close Job unavailable - refusing an unkillable child\")\n        self._kill_process(proc, None)\n        return None\n    port = None\n    if spec.banner_re is not None:\n        port = wait_port(proc, spec.banner_re, spec.start_timeout_s)\n        if port is None:\n            log(\"[procmgr] child never printed its port\")\n            self._kill_process(proc, guarantee)\n            return None\n    mp = ManagedProcess(key=key, proc=proc, port=port, guarantee=guarantee, last_beat=time.time(), beat_required=beat_required)\n    with self._lock:\n        self.processes[key] = mp\n        self.last_activity = time.time()\n    return mp\n```\n\nHmm wait — log messages. Original: `\"[supervisor] spawn failed: {e}\"`, `\"[supervisor] kill-on-close Job unavailable - refusing an unkillable session\"`, `\"[supervisor] session child never printed its port\"`, `\"[supervisor] session {sid} on port {port}\"`, `\"[supervisor] session {session_id} stopped\"`, `\"[supervisor] reaping stale session {sid}\"`, `\"[supervisor] idle, exiting\"`, `\"[supervisor] reap_loop error: {e}\"`.\n\nThe compat contract said log lines preserved. But the generic layer doesn't know \"session\". Design: ProcessSupervisor takes a `log_prefix` or label? Cleaner: the generic layer logs with a neutral prefix like `[procmgr]`, and SessionSupervisor logs its session-specific lines (`[supervisor] session {sid} on port {port}`, `[supervisor] session stopped`, `[supervisor] reaping stale session`). Hmm, but \"reaping stale session\" is logged inside the reaper loop...\n\nLet me check what the tests actually assert about logs. From my earlier grep of supervisor_test.py I saw checks about status codes and session membership, grandchild heartbeat, etc. I didn't see log assertions. Do tests grep the log output? Let me check the test file to be careful. The tests spawn real children (they use a stub child that prints a port banner? Let me look at the test file to understand what the tests need — especially `_wait_port` usage with real child processes, `PORT_BANNER_RE` (do tests import it?), `Supervisor(...)` constructor args used in tests (agent_cmd, cwd, stale_s, idle_timeout_s, reap_interval_s, start_timeout_s).\n\nImportant: tests construct `Supervisor(agent_cmd=..., cwd=..., stale_s=..., ...)` — my SessionSupervisor must keep that constructor signature.\n\nAlso test line 23: `from agent.supervisor import Supervisor, _SafeStdStream, build_server`. Line 195: `from agent.supervisor import _agent_cmd_default`. Line 483-505: `import agent.supervisor as _svmod; _svmod._job_assign`.\n\nDoes the test import `PORT_BANNER_RE` or reference `_svmod.PORT_BANNER_RE`? I need to read the full test file to know exactly what seams exist. Let me read tests/supervisor_test.py fully (~510 lines based on line 505 mention).\n\nAlso need to check: does anything else import from agent.supervisor? ui spawns it as subprocess (`-m agent.supervisor`), scripts/supervisor_entry.py imports main. agent/server.py? Let me grep for \"supervisor\" in agent/*.py — earlier grep showed agent/tools/transport.py mentions supervisor only in a comment. Fine.\n\nPlan:\n1. Read tests/supervisor_test.py fully.\n2. Check git log for commit message conventions.\n3. Write the three procmgr modules + __init__.\n4. Rewrite agent/supervisor.py.\n5. Fix the test monkeypatch seam.\n6. Run tests: supervisor_test.py, server_test, and a broader sweep (selfcheck? local_shell_test? The M1 memory said toolcli_test, local_shell_test, selfcheck, server_test, websearch_test were the green set).\n7. Commit (two commits).\n\nLet me read the test file first — it's the safety net, I must know exactly what it pins.\n\nThe test file is ~510 lines. Let me read it in two chunks. Actually let me read the whole thing with max_chars high enough — 510 lines * ~60 chars avg = ~30K chars. Default max_chars is 20000, might truncate. Read in 2 chunks of ~260 lines.\n\nAlso check git log briefly for conventions."}
