ne 309: `Supervisor._kill(_NoConsole(grace))` → could become `stop_process(_NoConsole(grace))` from procmgr, or keep `Supervisor._kill` alias. Keep the alias to minimize test churn: `Supervisor._kill` staticmethod that calls procmgr's `stop_process`.\n\nLet me design procmgr.kill:\n\n```python\n# agent/procmgr/kill.py\nKILL_GRACE_S = 3.0\n\ndef signal_soft(proc) -> None: ...\ndef kill_hard(proc) -> None: ...\ndef stop_process(proc, guarantee=None, grace_s: float | None = None) -> None:\n    # the ladder; grace_s defaults to module KILL_GRACE_S\n```\n\nHmm — if `stop_process` takes `grace_s=None` and looks up `KILL_GRACE_S` at call time (`grace = KILL_GRACE_S if grace_s is None else grace_s`), then patching `procmgr.kill.KILL_GRACE_S` works. Test 11 update: `patch.object(_proc_kill_module, \"KILL_GRACE_S\", 0.5)` and call `stop_process(_NoConsole(grace))`. Or keep `Supervisor._kill` in supervisor.py delegating: `Supervisor._kill = staticmethod(lambda proc, job=None: stop_process(proc, job))`. Test line 309 `Supervisor._kill(_NoConsole(grace))` stays untouched. Only the patch target line changes to procmgr.kill.KILL_GRACE_S. \n\n10. Test 13 uses `sess9.job` — an int handle (Windows). `ManagedProcess.guarantee` holds it. `Session` dataclass in supervisor.py: keep `session_id, proc, port, last_beat, job` fields exactly (test accesses `.proc`, `.port`, `.job`). So Session stays a real dataclass in supervisor.py, constructed by SessionSupervisor from ManagedProcess. \n\n11. Test 14: `not sup14.sessions` after refusal; `sup14.start_session()` returns None; child killed. The refusal logic: on nt, `require_kill_guarantee` platform default → required. The stub: `_svmod._job_assign = lambda pid: None`. New seam: patch `agent.procmgr.kill.job_assign`. But WAIT — if `supervise.py` does `from agent.procmgr.kill import job_assign` at import time, then patching `procmgr.kill.job_assign` would NOT affect the already-bound name in supervise.py. The test must patch where it's USED: `agent.procmgr.supervise.job_assign`. So supervise.py should `from agent.procmgr import kill` and call `kill.job_assign(pid)` — then patching `agent.procmgr.kill.job_assign` works (attribute lookup through module reference at call time). I'll use module-qualified calls in supervise.py for the patchable seams: `kill.job_assign`, `kill.stop_process`. And in kill.py, `_descendant_pids` → `descendant_pids` internal call is same-module, fine.\n\nSimilarly test 11's patch of KILL_GRACE_S: `stop_process` reads `KILL_GRACE_S` as a module global inside kill.py — patching `agent.procmgr.kill.KILL_GRACE_S` works.\n\n12. `build_server` and `_Handler` reference `sup.sessions` and `sup.exit_when_idle` (line 699-701: `with sup._lock: if not sup.sessions: sup.exit_when_idle = True`). Generic core will have `processes` dict — the handler is session-specific and stays in supervisor.py; it can use the SessionSupervisor's own `sessions` dict. Design decision: SessionSupervisor keeps its own `sessions: dict[str, Session]` and delegates spawn/kill to core? OR SessionSupervisor subclasses ProcessSupervisor and exposes `sessions` as a property mapping key→Session?\n\nHmm. Subclass design: ProcessSupervisor holds `self.processes: dict[str, ManagedProcess]`. SessionSupervisor adds `self.sessions: dict[str, Session]`... then two dicts to keep in sync — bad. Alternative: SessionSupervisor holds ONLY `sessions: dict[str, Session]` and overrides the core's bookkeeping? The core's reaper iterates its own dict...\n\nCleaner composition: ProcessSupervisor is the machinery (lock, processes dict, reaper, exit_event). SessionSupervisor(ProcessSupervisor):\n- `start_session(base_url=None)`: builds SpawnSpec, calls `self.spawn(key=uuid, spec, beat_required=True)`; on success constructs `Session(session_id=key, proc=mp.proc, port=mp.port, last_beat=mp.last_beat, job=mp.guarantee)` and stores in `self.sessions`. \n- `sessions` is a real dict owned by SessionSupervisor, kept in lockstep with core `processes`