Process**? No — make `ManagedProcess` carry a generic `meta` field, and `Session = ManagedProcess` with session-specific FIELD NAMES via a thin subclass:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass Session(ManagedProcess):\n    @property\n    def session_id(self): return self.key\n    @property\n    def job(self): return self.guarantee\n    @job.setter\n    def job(self, v): self.guarantee = v\n```\n\nHmm, that keeps tests working: `sess9.job is not None` ✓, `sess9.job = None` ✓ (setter), `sess9.proc.pid` ✓, `sess9.port` ✓. And `sup.sessions` — SessionSupervisor keeps `self.sessions: dict[str, Session]` as THE core dict? I.e., generic core has `self.processes` but subclass overrides with `self.sessions`?\n\nActually — why not let the GENERIC core's dict be named generically but allow subclass to alias it:\n\n```python\nclass ProcessSupervisor:\n    def __init__(...):\n        self.processes: dict[str, ManagedProcess] = {}\n\nclass SessionSupervisor(ProcessSupervisor):\n    def __init__(self, agent_cmd, cwd=None, ...):\n        super().__init__(...)\n        self.agent_cmd = ...\n        self.sessions = self.processes  # same dict, session-named view\n```\n\n`self.sessions = self.processes` — same dict object, session-named. Tests work: `sid3 in sup2.sessions` ✓, `sup5.sessions[sid].proc.args` ✓, `not sup14.sessions` ✓. And `start_session` returns a `Session(...)` instance that it ALSO stores: `self.processes[key] = sess`? But core `spawn` already stores ManagedProcess into `self.processes`. If SessionSupervisor wants the stored value to BE a Session, spawn must construct it... \n\nMake the core's spawn construct the record via an overridable factory: `self._make_record(key, proc, port, guarantee, beat_required) -> ManagedProcess` — base returns ManagedProcess; SessionSupervisor overrides to return Session (subclass of ManagedProcess). That's clean, one dict, no sync. Session subclasses ManagedProcess adding session_id/job property views. \n\nHmm wait — but then ManagedProcess needs beat_required as a field? Reaper logic: for records with beat_required=False, skip staleness check. Fine.\n\nBut dataclass inheritance with defaults: ManagedProcess fields all have defaults or none? `@dataclass class ManagedProcess: key: str; proc: Popen; port: int | None; guarantee: ...; last_beat: float; beat_required: bool` — non-default fields fine. Session adds only properties → no field issues. But `@dataclass` subclass with no new fields is fine. Actually with properties, don't make Session a dataclass at all — just `class Session(ManagedProcess)` with two properties. And original Session had `field(default_factory=time.time)` for last_beat — construction site handles that.\n\nWait, do I even want Session to subclass? `Session(session_id=..., proc=..., port=..., job=...)` in the old constructor signature — I'm rewriting start_session anyway. Properties are fine.\n\nNow the reaper loop in core iterates `self.processes.items()`, checks `beat_required and now - last_beat > stale_s` → stale keys → for each, `self.stop(key)` (SessionSupervisor wants to log \"[supervisor] reaping stale session {sid}\"). Log line preservation: the core logs `[procmgr] reaping stale process {key}`? Contract said log lines preserved. The logs go to supervisor stdout which the UI forwards to its console; remote diagnostics tail /tmp/clutch-server.log. Are specific log strings load-bearing? ui/server-bootstrap.js just pipes through. ssh-tunnel.js diagnostic tails last 40 lines generically. So changing `[supervisor] session ... on port ...` wording is *cosmetically* visible but nothing parses it. Still — keep session-relevant log lines emitted from SessionSupervisor overrides to be safe:\n\nDesign: core hooks for logging:\n- `spawn()` logs `[procmgr] spawn failed: {e}` and `[procmgr] child never printed its port` — hmm, original: `[supervisor] session child never printed its port`. I'll parameterize: core has `self.label = \"procmgr\"` and logs `[{self.label}] ...`. SessionSupervisor sets `self.label = \"supervisor\"` — then ALL log lines stay byte-identical: `[supervisor] spawn failed`, `[supervisor] kill-on-close Job unavailable...`, `[supervisor] session child never printed its port`, `[supervisor] reaping stale session {key}`, `[supervisor] idle, exiting`, `[supervisor] reap_loop error`. Only the \"reaping stale session\" wording uses \"session\" — core shouldn't say \"session\"... Compromise: core logs `reaping stale {key}` with label prefix → `[supervisor] reaping stale <sid>` — slightly different wording (\"session\" dropped). To keep byte-identical, give core an overridable `_log_reaped(key)` hook? That's over-engineering. Honest assessment: these log lines are diagnostics, nothing parses them; I'll keep `[supervisor] reaping stale session {sid}` via a tiny format string on the core: `self.reap_log = \"reaping stale {key}\"`? Ugh.\n\nSimpler: core method `_stale_keys()` returns keys; the REAP+LOG+stop loop lives in the core with log message `f\"[{self.label}] reaping stale {key}\"` and SessionSupervisor sets `label=\"supervisor\"`... \"reaping stale 0428\" vs \"reaping stale session 0428\". I'll just make SessionSupervisor override the small `_on_stale(key)` hook:\n\n```python\n# core reap_loop inner:\nfor key in stale:\n    self._log_stale(key)\n    self.stop(key)\n\n# base:\ndef _log_stale(self, key): log(f\"[{self.label}] reaping stale {key}\")\n# session override:\ndef _log_stale(self, key): log(f\"[supervisor] reaping stale session {key}\")\n```\n\nOne tiny hook, preserves logs exactly. Acceptable — not over-engineering, it's the seam between generic and specific messaging.\n\nSimilarly \"session {sid} on port {port}\" and \"session {sid} stopped\" logs are in start_session/stop_session which are SESSION methods (not core) — they stay in supervisor.py verbatim. Core spawn logs \"child never printed its port\" → session wants \"session child never printed its port\". Same hook pattern? That's 2 hooks... Or: session's start_session could do the banner-wait logging itself? No — spawn is atomic in core (refusal logic inside).\n\nLet me reconsider: hooks `_log_spawn_failure(e)`, `_log_no_banner()`, `_log_refused()`, `_log_stale(key)` — 4 one-liner hooks for byte-identical logs. Hmm, that's hook sprawl. Alternative: accept minor wording drift in core logs and note it. OR simplest: core takes optional `log_context` strings... \n\nYou know what — simplest honest solution: the core logs with label only:\n- `[supervisor] spawn failed: ...` (identical already, label=supervisor)\n- refusal: original `[supervisor] kill-on-close Job unavailable - refusing an unkillable session` — core would say `...refusing an unkillable child`. One word drift.\n- no banner: original `session child never printed its port` → core `child never printed its port`. \n- stale: `reaping stale session {sid}` → `reaping stale {key}`.\n\nNothing parses these (verified: ui pipes/tails only). I'll add ONE hook `_log_stale` — no wait. Decision: keep TWO tiny hooks is fine, but honestly let me just parameterize with an f-string template per event? No.\n\nFINAL decision: single `label` field (\"supervisor\" for sessions, \"procmgr\" default) + accept the three tiny wording drifts (\"session\" dropped). They're diagnostics; I'll note the drift explicitly in the commit message and to the user. Actually hold on — is even THAT worth it? Let me check if supervisor_test asserts any log content... From my full read: NO log assertions in tests. UI: pipes to stdout, tails generically, greps nothing. Confirmed safe. I'll do label-only, note drift.\n\nHmm, actually one more idea — even simpler and zero drift: since SessionSupervisor.start_session wraps core spawn, the \"no banner\"/\"refusal\" logs fire INSIDE core spawn. What if core spawn returns a reason enum instead of logging those two? `spawn(key, spec, beat_required) -> ManagedProcess | None` plus... no, tests need start_session()→None and core refusal kills the child. Core must do the kill. Logging in core. OK going with label + drift. DONE deliberating.\n\n13. `_agent_cmd_default` stays in supervisor.py ✓. `build_server` stays ✓. `main()` stays ✓ — but `main` uses `_make_stdout_nonblocking()` and `_SafeStdStream` — import from procmgr.stdio, re-export `_SafeStdStream` and `_make_stdout_nonblocking` names for compat (test imports `_SafeStdStream` from agent.supervisor; nothing imports `_make_stdout_nonblocking` — but keep alias anyway? test line 23 imports Supervisor, _SafeStdStream, build_server. Keep `_SafeStdStream` re-export; `_make_stdout_nonblocking` used internally by main — re-export not needed but harmless. I'll re-export `_SafeStdStream` only, since that's the only one with an external consumer).\n\n14. `Supervisor._kill` staticmethod — keep as compat alias in supervisor.py:\n```python\n_stop_process = kill.stop_process  # from procmgr\nclass SessionSupervisor(...):\n    ...\nSupervisor = SessionSupervisor\n```\nTest calls `Supervisor._kill(_NoConsole(grace))` and patches `_sv.KILL_GRACE_S`. So supervisor.py must still HAVE a module-level `KILL_GRACE_S` that `_kill` reads... If I keep `_kill` in supervisor.py as a staticmethod:\n```python\n@staticmethod\ndef _kill(proc, job=None):\n    kill.stop_process(proc, job)\n```\n→ patching `_sv.KILL_GRACE_S` does nothing (grace read from kill module). Test 11: with patch ineffective, took ≈ 3.05s < 5.0 ✓ passes, but the patch's PURPOSE (make the test fast) dies and the test slows by ~2.5s. Not acceptable — update the test patch target to `agent.procmgr.kill.KILL_GRACE_S` and call... test line 309 calls `Supervisor._kill(_NoConsole(grace))` — keep working (alias to stop_process). Update line 307 only:\n```python\nimport agent.procmgr.kill as _pkill\nwith _m.patch.object(_pkill, \"KILL_GRACE_S\", 0.5):\n```\nThat's the honest minimal change. So supervisor.py: `from agent.procmgr import kill` and `Supervisor._kill = staticmethod(kill.stop_process)`? As a class attribute: `_kill = staticmethod(kill.stop_process)` inside class body. stop_process signature `(proc, guarantee=None)` — call `Supervisor._kill(_NoConsole(grace))` → guarantee=None ✓. And internally SessionSupervisor uses `kill.stop_process(mp.proc, mp.guarantee)`.\n\nAlso keep module-level `KILL_GRACE_S = kill.KILL_GRACE_S` in supervisor.py? Not needed after test update; but SESSION constants (SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S etc.) stay in supervisor.py. PORT_BANNER_RE stays in supervisor.py (clutch-server banner is session-specific — well, it's the agent-server banner; daemon will have its own). ✓.\n\n15. Session constructor signature: `Supervisor(agent_cmd, cwd=None, stale_s=SESSION_STALE_S, idle_timeout_s=IDLE_TIMEOUT_S, reap_interval_s=REAP_INTERVAL_S, start_timeout_s=SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S)`. Core: `ProcessSupervisor(stale_s, idle_timeout_s, reap_interval_s, require_kill_guarantee=None, label=\"procmgr\")`. SessionSupervisor.__init__ keeps old signature, maps: `super().__init__(stale_s=stale_s, idle_timeout_s=idle_timeout_s, reap_interval_s=reap_interval_s, require_kill_guarantee=None, label=\"supervisor\")` — on nt platform default = required ✓, posix = not required ✓ (posix gets guarantee=None from job_assign and `os.name == \"nt\" and job is None` check → never refuses on posix ✓ — preserve exactly: `if os.name == \"nt\" and self.require_guarantee and guarantee is None`).\n\nWait, careful: `require_kill_guarantee=None` means platform default: effective = `(os.name == \"nt\")` when None. Core: \n```python\nneed = self.require_guarantee if self.require_guarantee is not None else (os.name == \"nt\")\nif need and guarantee is None: refuse\n```\nOn posix job_assign returns None; need=False → no refuse ✓. On nt default need=True → refuse ✓. Session doesn't pass anything → current behavior ✓. Daemon later passes require_kill_guarantee=False explicitly.\n\n16. Constants placement: SESSION_STALE_S=10, REAP_INTERVAL_S=2, IDLE_TIMEOUT_S=8, SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S=30, KILL_GRACE_S=3, DEFAULT_PORT=8890, PORT_BANNER_RE — split: KILL_GRACE_S → procmgr/kill.py. stale/reap/idle defaults → procmgr/supervise.py as STALE_S/REAP_INTERVAL_S/IDLE_TIMEOUT_S. supervisor.py keeps DEFAULT_PORT, PORT_BANNER_RE, SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S, and re-exports the others for... does anything import SESSION_STALE_S etc. from supervisor? grep showed only supervisor_entry imports main; test imports Supervisor/_SafeStdStream/build_server/_agent_cmd_default/patch KILL_GRACE_S. ui/server-bootstrap reads HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS locally (\"8000 < supervisor stale timeout (30s)\" — comment says 30s?? stale is 10s... comment drift, not mine to fix now). So supervisor.py defines its own copies or imports: I'll have supervisor.py import from procmgr and define `SESSION_STALE_S = STALE_S` etc.? Minimal: supervisor.py passes explicit defaults? SessionSupervisor signature: `stale_s: float = 10.0, idle_timeout_s: float = 8.0, reap_interval_s: float = 2.0` — importing the core constants. Keep supervisor.py names for greppability:\n```python\nfrom agent.procmgr.kill import KILL_GRACE_S\nfrom agent.procmgr.supervise import IDLE_TIMEOUT_S, REAP_INTERVAL_S, STALE_S as SESSION_STALE_S\n```\nCareful: `from x import y as z` re-export style is fine. But KILL_GRACE_S re-export in supervisor is now unused by code — the TEST patches procmgr.kill.KILL_GRACE_S. Re-export could CONFUSE future patchers... but harmless. I'll re-export with a comment \"compat alias\".\n\n17. `_Handler` /api/shutdown does `with sup._lock: if not sup.sessions: sup.exit_when_idle = True`. Core exposes `_lock`, `processes`/`sessions`, `exit_when_idle` — keep. Better: core gets a method `request_shutdown_when_idle()` encapsulating that — and handler calls it. Cleaner API, same behavior (arm only when empty, non-sticky when in-use). ✓ I'll add `request_idle_exit()` to core.\n\n18. `start_session` original behavior detail: it refreshes last_activity and CLEARS exit_when_idle BEFORE spawning (`with self._lock: self.last_activity=now; self.exit_when_idle=False`). Core spawn must do the same ✓ (in spawn start).\n\n19. stop_session: pops from sessions FIRST (under lock), updates last_activity, then kills outside lock. Core `stop(key)`: pop under lock + last_activity update, then `kill.stop_process(proc, guarantee)` outside lock, return bool ✓. SessionSupervisor.stop_session(sid) → calls core stop(sid) + logs `[supervisor] session {sid} stopped` when True ✓.\n\n20. heartbeat: core beat(key) → updates last_beat, returns bool. SessionSupervisor.heartbeat(sid) → core.beat ✓.\n\n21. shutdown_all: core iterates keys → stop each ✓. Original: `with lock: sids = list(keys)` then stop each. ✓.\n\n22. reap_loop: core — while not exit_event: compute now; stale = [keys where beat_required and now-last_beat > stale_s]; n = len(processes); exit_when_idle snapshot — under ONE lock pass ✓ (original did exactly that); stop stale (with _log_stale hook); idle check `n == 0 and (exit_when_idle or now - self.last_activity > idle_timeout_s)` → set exit_event, break; wait(reap_interval_s) via exit_event.wait ✓; try/except around body logging reap_loop error ✓.\n\nNote the original reads `self.last_activity` OUTSIDE the lock (line 566) — keep semantics same-ish; I'll read it inside the lock (harmless improvement? No — behavior preservation: reading inside lock is strictly more correct, no observable drift). Fine.\n\n23. `_wait_port` → core `wait_port(proc, banner_re, timeout_s)`: reader thread + forwarder thread + q.get(timeout) ✓. Original logs nothing on timeout (start_session logs). Forwarder writes to sys.stdout... uses raw sys.stdout.write with flush, swallowing OSError/ValueError ✓.\n\nReader decodes utf-8 replace, searches banner_re, q.put(int), sink.put(line) ✓.\n\n24. ManagedProcess dataclass fields: key, proc, port, guarantee, last_beat, beat_required. Session(ManagedProcess) with session_id/job properties. Dataclass inheritance: ManagedProcess is @dataclass; Session extends with no new dataclass fields, only properties — plain class subclass of a dataclass works: `class Session(ManagedProcess):` + @property methods. Instances construct via `Session(key=..., proc=..., port=..., guarantee=..., last_beat=..., beat_required=...)` — dataclass __init__ inherited ✓.\n\nTest 13: `sess9.job = None` → needs setter ✓ (property + setter). `sess9.job` returns guarantee ✓. Test accesses `sess9.proc.pid` ✓, `sess9.port` ✓.\n\nCore spawn constructs record via `self._make_record(...)`: base: `return ManagedProcess(...)`. SessionSupervisor overrides: `return Session(...)`. \n\n25. `start_session` return type: Session | None ✓; core spawn returns the record (Session instance) — SessionSupervisor.start_session:\n```python\ndef start_session(self, base_url=None):\n    spec = SpawnSpec(\n        cmd=[*self.agent_cmd, \"--port\", \"0\"] + ([\"--base-url\", base_url] if base_url else []),\n        cwd=self.cwd,\n        banner_re=PORT_BANNER_RE,\n        start_timeout_s=self.start_timeout_s,\n    )\n    key = uuid.uuid4().hex[:12]\n    rec = self.spawn(key, spec, beat_required=True)\n    if rec is None: return None\n    log(f\"[supervisor] session {rec.key} on port {rec.port}\")\n    return rec  # IS a Session\n```\n✓ session_id property = key = uuid hex[:12] ✓ same format.\n\n26. Env: original `env = dict(os.environ)` — no extra env used. SpawnSpec.env_extra optional, unused by session. Keep in core for daemon? YAGNI said no... but harmless & plausibly needed (PYTHONUTF8 etc.). Hmm — skill says never propose abstractions without concrete use. Drop env_extra. Actually agent/server child might need... current code passes plain os.environ copy. Drop it. (Can add when daemon needs it.)\n\n27. procmgr/__init__.py: re-export ProcessSupervisor, ManagedProcess, SpawnSpec, stop_process, job_assign, SafeStdStream, log, make_stdout_nonblocking + __all__. Keep it lean.\n\n28. stdio.py naming: public `log`, `make_stdout_nonblocking`, `SafeStdStream`. supervisor.py: `from agent.procmgr.stdio import SafeStdStream, log, make_stdout_nonblocking` + `_SafeStdStream = SafeStdStream` compat re-export (test). All internal log() calls in supervisor.py use `log(...)` — and docstrings inside stdio.py mention `_make_stdout_nonblocking`/`_SafeStdStream` — update name references in docstrings to public names.\n\n29. kill.py docstrings reference `_signal_soft`/`_kill_hard`/`_job_assign` — rename references consistently.\n\n30. supervise.py: ProcessSupervisor with `_lock`, `processes`, `last_activity`, `exit_event`, `exit_when_idle`, `label`, `require_guarantee`, plus constants STALE_S/REAP_INTERVAL_S/IDLE_TIMEOUT_S, `wait_port`, hooks `_log_stale`, `_make_record`, `request_idle_exit`.\n\nAlso — careful with reap_loop logging idle exit: `[supervisor] idle, exiting` → `f\"[{self.label}] idle, exiting\"` → sessions keep byte-identical ✓. reap error: `[supervisor] reap_loop error: {e}` ✓ same pattern. spawn failed: `[supervisor] spawn failed: {e}` ✓. refusal: drift \"unkillable child\" vs \"unkillable session\"... with label=supervisor the line becomes `[supervisor] kill-on-close Job unavailable - refusing an unkillable child`. Hmm — one-word drift on THE line a future debugger greps for from old docs... Fine, noted in commit.\n\nActually wait — should core log prefix come from label? `[procmgr] ...` for daemon. Yes.\n\n31. main() unchanged except imports; `_agent_cmd_default` unchanged; `build_server` unchanged; `_Handler` unchanged except `/api/shutdown` calls `sup.request_idle_exit()` — wait, original inline: `with sup._lock: if not sup.sessions: sup.exit_when_idle = True`. Replace with `sup.request_idle_exit()` — same behavior (arm only when n==0). ✓\n\n32. signals: `signal_soft` uses os.killpg / CTRL_BREAK_EVENT; on posix with start_new_session ✓ unchanged.\n\n33. Windows Job structures — verbatim copy, rename `_descendant_pids`→`descendant_pids`, `_job_assign`→`job_assign`, `_job_close`→`job_close`, constants keep leading underscore (private fine) — but they're module-private in kill.py, fine.\n\nNow — one subtle thing about spawn ordering: original assigns job BEFORE _wait_port ✓ preserved. And on refusal, original `self._kill(proc, None)` → core: `kill.stop_process(proc, None)` ✓.\n\nAnd `stop_process(proc, guarantee)` ladder — copy _kill body, `finally: job_close(guarantee)`. Grace read: `grace = KILL_GRACE_S` — must read module global AT CALL TIME for the test patch: function body references `KILL_GRACE_S` directly (module global lookup at runtime) ✓ patchable.\n\nEdge: `stop_process` signature `(proc, guarantee=None)`; docstring preserved.\n\n34. supervisor.py `Supervisor = SessionSupervisor` alias + `Session`, `_job_assign` alias? Test 14 patch point UPDATED to procmgr.kill.job_assign, so no `_job_assign` alias needed. But leave `_SafeStdStream` alias (test imports it). Also tests import `_agent_cmd_default` ✓ stays.\n\nAlso: supervisor_test imports `from agent.supervisor import Supervisor, _SafeStdStream, build_server` ✓ all present.\n\nTest changes (only 2 spots):\n- line 307: `with _m.patch.object(_sv, \"KILL_GRACE_S\", 0.5):` → patch procmgr.kill. Need import: line 277 has `import agent.supervisor as _sv` — add `import agent.procmgr.kill as _pkill`. Change 307 to `with _m.patch.object(_pkill, \"KILL_GRACE_S\", 0.5):`. Keep line 309 `Supervisor._kill(...)` ✓ (alias exists).\n- lines 500-505: `_svmod._job_assign` → `import agent.procmgr.kill as _svmod` patch `_svmod.job_assign`.\n\nComment at 479-480 mentions `_job_assign` — update name in comment.\n\n35. Docstring at top of supervisor.py — trim the moved parts, keep session lifecycle narrative + note the layering.\n\nNow line counts: stdio.py ~110, kill.py ~270, supervise.py ~230, __init__ ~30, supervisor.py ~380.\n\nThen: syntax check, run supervisor_test, run server_test + broader green set (toolcli_test, local_shell_test, selfcheck, websearch_test — the M1 green set minus clutch-workspace stuff). M1 memory said: \"Regression green: toolcli_test, local_shell_test, selfcheck, server_test full, websearch_test\". supervisor_test isn't in that list? It IS a test file... the memory listed the set run during toolcli work. I'll run supervisor_test + server_test + toolcli + transport-related, and quick: tests dir listing to know runner style: \"uv run python -m tests.supervisor_test\". Uses uv. Check uv available — .venv exists. Use `uv run python -m tests.supervisor_test` per docstring; fallback `.venv/Scripts/python -m tests.supervisor_test`.\n\nCommits: two focused:\n1. `refactor(supervisor): extract the general process-management layer (agent/procmgr)` — modules + supervisor rewrite (behavior-preserving).\n2. `test(supervisor): retarget white-box seams to agent.procmgr` — test seam updates.\nActually commit 1 without test changes breaks green-at-each-commit (test patches _svmod._job_assign which no longer exists → test would ERROR at line 500 attr access? `_svmod._job_assign` where _svmod=agent.supervisor — attribute missing → AttributeError → test crashes). So single commit? Repo convention = focused commits, but atomicity matters more. Alternative: commit 1 = procmgr modules ONLY (new files, no behavior change), commit 2 = supervisor.py rewrite + test seams together. That keeps commit 1 green (supervisor untouched, new modules unused-but-importable) and commit 2 atomic. ✓ That's the right split. Hmm, but then commit 2 is big-ish. Fine — \"the cut\" is one logical change.\n\nWait, actually reconsider: maybe simpler = everything in ONE commit `refactor(supervisor): extract generic process layer (agent/procmgr)` including test seams. M1 style had feat+test split but those were different concerns. Here test-seam change is PART of the refactor. I'll do the two-commit split as planned (pure addition, then cut-over) — cleaner bisect story.\n\nLet me now check the exact lines of supervisor.py once more where I need precision — I have the full file in context (read across 3 reads). Good.\n\nOne more consumer check: does agent/server.py import anything from supervisor? Earlier grep on server.py for \"supervisor|import|def main\" (in fix.clc log) showed server.py is a plain server without supervisor imports. Let me verify quickly with grep to be safe — `from .supervisor|from agent.supervisor|import supervisor` across agent/. Also check scratch files reference supervisor_test? scratch-launch-tests.py? Those are scratch, gitignored (db6d3e8 \"chore: ignore local scratch test scripts and logs\"). Skip.\n\nAlso scripts/supervisor_entry.py: `from agent.supervisor import main` ✓ unchanged.\n\nPyInstaller build script references module? build-server-bundle.sh line 46 lists supervisor_entry.py as entry ✓ unchanged.\n\nNow write the files.\n\n**agent/procmgr/stdio.py** — copy lines 63-157, renames:\n- `_log` → `log`\n- `_make_stdout_nonblocking` → `make_stdout_nonblocking`\n- `_SafeStdStream` → `SafeStdStream`\nDocstring tweaks: \"supervisor's own logging\" → keep general (\"the process's own logging\"). The _SafeStdStream docstring mentions supervisor-specific orphan story — generalize lightly: \"A Clutch daemon is often spawned by a parent that dies first...\" Keep the substance.\n\nHeader docstring for stdio.py: explain purpose.\n\n**agent/procmgr/kill.py** — lines 170-434:\n- KILL_GRACE_S = 3.0 (from supervisor line 60) — placed here with its own comment.\n- signal_soft (docstring adapted: \"Ask a managed child to stop...\")\n- kill_hard (docstring: taskkill rationale — mentions \"the shell it ran commands in\" — generic enough, keep)\n- Job block comment (lines 219-234) — mentions \"session child\" several times → generalize to \"managed child\"/\"supervised child\".\n- structures + descendant_pids + job_assign + job_close (docstrings mention session — generalize).\n- stop_process(proc, guarantee=None) — the ladder from _kill with full docstring (generalize \"session child\" → \"child\").\n\n**agent/procmgr/supervise.py**:\n- constants STALE_S=10 (comment: heartbeat interval is 8s), REAP_INTERVAL_S=2, IDLE_TIMEOUT_S=8.\n- wait_port(proc, banner_re, timeout_s) — from _wait_port, parametrized banner.\n- ManagedProcess + Session-compatible record... no, ManagedProcess here; Session subclass lives in supervisor.py (session-specific view).\n- SpawnSpec dataclass.\n- ProcessSupervisor class: __init__(stale_s=STALE_S, idle_timeout_s=IDLE_TIMEOUT_S, reap_interval_s=REAP_INTERVAL_S, require_kill_guarantee=None, label=\"procmgr\").\n  - fields: label, stale_s, idle_timeout_s, reap_interval_s, require_kill_guarantee, processes, _lock, last_activity, exit_event, exit_when_idle.\n  - spawn(key, spec, *, beat_required) -> ManagedProcess | None\n  - stop(key) -> bool\n  - beat(key) -> bool\n  - request_idle_exit() -> None\n  - shutdown_all()\n  - reap_loop()\n  - _make_record hook\n  - _log_stale hook\n\nDocstrings preserve the crucial comments (idle refresh before spawn; assign before banner; refusal rationale; reaper never raises).\n\nspawn failure logs: `log(f\"[{self.label}] spawn failed: {e}\")`; banner timeout: `log(f\"[{self.label}] child never printed its port\")` — session drift: was \"session child never printed its port\". Hmm label=supervisor gives `[supervisor] child never printed its port`. Accept.\n\nRefusal: `log(f\"[{self.label}] kill-on-close Job unavailable - refusing an unkillable child\")`. Accept drift (was \"session\").\n\n**agent/procmgr/__init__.py**: docstring + re-exports.\n\n**agent/supervisor.py** rewrite (~380 lines):\n- module docstring: keep session architecture narrative (lines 1-35) but reference procmgr for the moved machinery; keep Locking paragraph.\n- imports: argparse, json, os, re, signal, subprocess(for type hints? Session.proc typed via ManagedProcess), sys, threading, time, uuid; from dataclasses import dataclass? Session is a plain subclass now. from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer; from agent.procmgr import kill; from agent.procmgr.stdio import SafeStdStream, log, make_stdout_nonblocking; from agent.procmgr.supervise import IDLE_TIMEOUT_S, ProcessSupervisor, REAP_INTERVAL_S, STALE_S, SpawnSpec, wait_port? (session doesn't call wait_port directly — core does. No import needed.)\n- DEFAULT_PORT=8890, PORT_BANNER_RE, SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S=30.0 (comment), re-export KILL_GRACE_S? I'll re-export SESSION_STALE_S etc.:\n  ```python\n  # compat names (tests/external greps): same values, owned by procmgr now\n  SESSION_STALE_S = STALE_S\n  SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S = 30.0\n  ```\n  KILL_GRACE_S: not needed in supervisor after test update. But `_kill` alias… no. Skip; test patches procmgr.kill. Hmm — leave `KILL_GRACE_S` OUT to avoid stale second source. OK.\n- class Session(ManagedProcess): properties session_id, job(+setter). Dataclass subclass: ManagedProcess is @dataclass with fields (key, proc, port, guarantee, last_beat, beat_required). Session adds properties only.\n- class SessionSupervisor(ProcessSupervisor):\n  - __init__(agent_cmd, cwd=None, stale_s=SESSION_STALE_S, idle_timeout_s=IDLE_TIMEOUT_S, reap_interval_s=REAP_INTERVAL_S, start_timeout_s=SESSION_START_TIMEOUT_S)\n  - agent_cmd=list(agent_cmd), cwd, start_timeout_s\n  - super().__init__(stale_s=stale_s, idle_timeout_s=idle_timeout_s, reap_interval_s=reap_interval_s, label=\"supervisor\")\n  - self.sessions = self.processes (session-named alias, with comment)\n  - _make_record override → Session(...)\n  - _log_stale override → \"[supervisor] reaping stale session {key}\"\n  - start_session(base_url=None)\n  - stop_session(session_id) → bool + log\n  - heartbeat(session_id) → bool\n  - _kill = staticmethod(kill.stop_process)  (compat: tests call Supervisor._kill(proc))\n- Supervisor = SessionSupervisor\n- _SafeStdStream = SafeStdStream\n- _Handler (unchanged, shutdown → sup.request_idle_exit())\n- _agent_cmd_default unchanged\n- build_server unchanged\n- main unchanged (uses make_stdout_nonblocking; log)\n- SIGTERM handler, exit watch — unchanged.\n\nOne nit: `_Handler` type hint `supervisor: Supervisor = None` — keep with alias.\n\n`uuid` import needed in 