{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789899384.329287, "id": "a9554e0ffc2d", "content": "开工。daemon 属于 clutch-workspace 独立仓（零依赖承诺必须守住，不能 import 主仓的 procmgr）。先把 M1 的现有代码全部读透——信封形状、Result 结构、CLI 分发层、测试姿势，一个都不能凭记忆写：", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_1478250920ab4dadaab70b45", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/cli.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_2cce5cf43cf3483dafb17def", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/envelope.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The user wants me to implement the workspace daemon now. This is the cli-srv model we ratified earlier in this conversation:\n\nDecision table (all ratified with \"都按推荐\" earlier for the supervisor layering, and the cli-srv decisions were presented... wait, actually let me re-check. In the conversation:\n\n1. First turn: user asked about daemon terminal invocation vs cli-srv. I answered with a decision table (6 decisions) recommending cli-srv.\n2. User said \"先这样，把supervisor管理进程那套抽象分层出来...\" — \"先这样\" means \"OK, that's fine for now\" — arguably approving the cli-srv direction, then asked for the supervisor layering.\n3. I implemented the supervisor layering, and the memory notes \"the future workspace daemon (cli-srv, ratified direction)\".\n4. Now: \"ok，现在实现workspace daemon\" — implement the workspace daemon per the ratified design.\n\nThe ratified design from my first answer (decision table):\n1. 模型定型: cli-srv (daemon + thin client + fallback direct execution), abandon stdio resident approach\n2. 协议: 127.0.0.1 + random port + token + discovery file; NO reconfigure endpoint (fence changes = daemon restart)\n3. daemon 粒度: one per workspace (discovery file addressed by workspace path hash); idle timeout suicide (~10 min) + pid liveness check cleanup of stale discovery files\n4. 懒启动: terminal first call auto-starts daemon, failure silently falls back to direct execution\n5. undo 存储: daemon memory stack (dies with daemon); disk-based .clutch/undo/ scheme formally abandoned; undo_file in HTTP API but NOT mapped to agent tool table\n6. carry-over: fence items ①which to move ②--workspace ④exit code 77 ⑥remote — still pending\n\nHmm, but wait. Decision 6 says carry-over items are undecided. And in the previous turn's design (the layering), the daemon would subclass ProcessSupervisor with beat_required=False, require_kill_guarantee=False, own discovery file/token, own HTTP surface.\n\nBut hold on — there's an important question: WHERE does the workspace daemon live? The clutch-workspace repo! The memories say:\n\n\"clutch-workspace（Python 独立仓，位于 clutch 工作区子文件夹 clutch-workspace/，自有 git）M1 完成并已提交：main 分支两笔聚焦提交 33e5f98(feat: 四命令 CLI + --json 信封 + sysexits) + b97185a(test: 64 黑盒用例)，工作树 clean。\"\n\nSo clutch-workspace is an independent repo (clutch-workspace/ inside the main clutch workspace) with:\n- clutch_workspace/cli.py — argparse wiring: build_parser → _dispatch → Result\n- clutch_workspace/envelope.py — emit_result/emit_error\n- clutch_workspace/filesystem.py — read_file, write_file, edit_file, grep\n- tests/ — 64 blackbox tests\n- Exit codes: 64 usage, 74 io, 70 bug, 130 interrupt\n\nThe daemon should be implemented in the clutch-workspace repo — it's the workspace tool CLI. The daemon adds:\n- server mode (`clutch-workspace serve`? or hidden command)\n- daemon: listens on 127.0.0.1 random port, token auth, discovery file\n- fence config at startup (only entry point)\n- undo stack in memory\n- CLI thin client: on invocation, read discovery file → if daemon alive, forward HTTP; else lazy-start daemon or fall back to direct execution\n- --workspace flag (decision: carry-over ②? Actually --workspace was listed in carry-over as undecided... hmm, item ② --workspace was a carry-over from a previous session's fence discussion)\n\nWait, the carry-over items: \"围栏上轮的 ①搬哪些 ②--workspace ④退出码77 ⑥远程不管——不受本轮影响，仍待裁\" — these were pending. The user said \"先这样\" which approved the 5 main recommendations... and arguably \"都按推荐\" in the third turn was for the supervisor layering decisions. So the carry-overs are still open questions.\n\nBut now the user says \"实现workspace daemon\" — I need to make sensible default decisions for the carry-overs or implement without them where possible. Let me think about what's needed:\n\nFrom my first answer's design:\n- Discovery file: user-level directory, named by hash of workspace path, contains port + token + pid\n- CLI: on startup, compute discovery file path from --workspace (or cwd); if daemon alive → forward HTTP, map {code,error} back to original exit codes and envelope, UX byte-identical; if not running → lazy-start daemon (failure → silent fallback to direct execution); --no-server forces local direct execution\n- Daemon: binds 127.0.0.1 random port, token in discovery file, fence in startup args only (no reconfigure endpoint), undo stack in memory, idle timeout suicide ~10 min, pid liveness check cleans stale discovery files\n- undo_file in HTTP API, not in agent tool table\n\nAnd from the layering turn: the daemon's supervision uses agent/procmgr? Wait — careful. The clutch-workspace is an INDEPENDENT repo with zero dependencies (zero-dependency promise: \"零依赖仍然守住\"). The agent/procmgr layer is in the main clutch repo. The clutch-workspace repo can't import agent.procmgr.\n\nHmm. So how do these relate? The procmgr layer manages PROCESSES (spawn/supervise children). The workspace daemon is itself a long-lived process in clutch-workspace. Two aspects:\n\n1. The daemon itself (clutch-workspace repo): `clutch-workspace daemon` command — an HTTP server with token auth, undo stack, fence. This needs its own stdio-safety (SafeStdStream-like) since it's spawned detached... Actually wait, who spawns the daemon?\n\n2. The lazy-start: the CLI (thin client) spawns the daemon. That's a spawn of a detached long-lived process. The host session (agent server) may also spawn it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the architecture. From the mermaid diagram in my first answer:\n\n```\n终端 / 脚本 → C[clutch-workspace CLI 薄客户端]\nhost:\n  TOOLS[tool 封装（进程内 HTTP 调用）] → C\n  SERVER[server.py 会话] -->|spawn + 围栏配置（唯一入口）| D[daemon]\nC -->|HTTP 127.0.0.1 + token| D\nC -.->|daemon 缺席 → 本地直执| DIR\nD → DIR[任务目录]\n```\n\nSo the daemon lives in clutch-workspace. The host (main repo) would later wire its tool wrappers to call the daemon via HTTP instead of spawning per-call — but that's host-side wiring (Q21/31-33 postponed per M1 memory).\n\nFor THIS task (\"实现workspace daemon\"), the scope should be the clutch-workspace side:\n1. Daemon server module (HTTP on 127.0.0.1, token, discovery file, fence, undo stack, idle suicide, pid liveness)\n2. CLI thin client: transparent forwarding + lazy start + --no-server fallback\n3. undo/undo_file commands? Wait — undo was mentioned: \"undo 栈 daemon 内存栈；undo_file 进 HTTP API，仍不映射给 agent 工具表\"\n\nHmm wait, does clutch-workspace currently HAVE undo? M1 was 4 commands (read_file, grep, write_file, edit_file). The undo discussion: \"undo 栈全局一致\" — the daemon shares one undo stack across all clients. Was undo part of the plan for clutch-workspace M2? The carry-over mentioned \".clutch/undo/ 落盘方案正式作废\" — so there WAS a previous undo disk scheme (from an earlier session, maybe in the main repo's workspace.py? The main repo has agent/tools/workspace.py with undo?).\n\nLet me check what exists. Actually the main repo memory \"Tool subprocess execution refactor shipped (pilot: read_file)\" and the workspace tools (read_file, write_file, edit_file, grep as agent tools). Is there an undo mechanism in the main repo's workspace tools? There was talk of \"undo 栈\" — the daemon sharing one in-memory undo stack. And \"undo_file 进 HTTP API，仍不映射给 agent 工具表\" — meaning there's an undo_file operation planned but only exposed via HTTP, not as an agent tool.\n\nI should explore both repos before implementing:\n1. clutch-workspace/ — the independent repo: cli.py, envelope.py, filesystem.py, tests/\n2. Maybe agent/tools/workspace.py in main repo for undo semantics reference\n\nLet me plan the implementation:\n\n**clutch-workspace M2: workspace daemon**\n\nNew modules in clutch_workspace/:\n- `daemon.py` — the daemon: HTTP server (ThreadingHTTPServer), token auth, discovery file write/cleanup, idle suicide, fence, undo stack, request handling mapping to filesystem commands\n- `client.py` — the thin client: discovery read, liveness check, HTTP forward, lazy start, fallback\n- `undo.py` — in-memory undo stack (per daemon), undo_file operation\n- cli.py changes: wire dispatch through client (unless --no-server), add daemon subcommand? How is the daemon started?\n\nDesign details:\n\n**Discovery file**: \n- Location: user-level directory. Windows: %LOCALAPPDATA%/clutch-workspace/ or ~/.clutch-workspace/? For cross-platform: `~/.clutch-workspace/daemons/<hash>.json`. Hash of resolved workspace path (sha256 of str(resolve()) — case handling on Windows: lowercase drive? Use os.path.normcase + resolve for stable keys).\n- Content: {port, token, pid, workspace, version?} — JSON.\n- Staleness: pid liveness check (os.kill(pid, 0) on POSIX; on Windows: use OpenProcess? or psutil? Zero deps... On Windows, checking pid liveness without psutil: ctypes OpenProcess + GetExitCodeProcess, or just try connecting to the port — the real liveness test is \"can I authenticate over HTTP\". Simpler robust approach: read discovery file → try HTTP health with token → if fails, try to lazy-start. PID check is an optimization to decide whether to overwrite a stale file. Actually simplest: on daemon start, it writes/overwrites the discovery file; client on connect failure treats daemon as dead and starts a new one (which overwrites the file). But two racers could both start daemons → port conflict? No — random port each; last writer wins the file; the loser daemon idles out eventually (idle suicide). That's acceptable but wasteful; a simple pid-liveness check before starting reduces duplicate daemons. Implement pid check: POSIX os.kill(pid,0); Windows: ctypes kernel32.OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION) + GetExitCodeProcess == STILL_ACTIVE. Keep it best-effort in a small helper with fallbacks.\n\nHmm — but careful about over-engineering. The M1 repo is minimal and clean. The daemon needs to be pragmatic and testable. Let me think about what the MINIMAL correct implementation is:\n\n**Daemon lifecycle**:\n- `clutch-workspace daemon --workspace <dir> [--fence ...] [--idle-timeout 600]` — long-running process. Hidden from help? No — it's a legitimate subcommand (scripts/terminal lazy-start calls it).\n- On start: resolve workspace, apply fence (startup args = the ONLY fence entry — no HTTP reconfigure), bind 127.0.0.1:0 (random), write discovery file, print banner? (the CLI waits for...? The client can poll the discovery file for port+token — race: daemon writes file after binding. Client starts daemon with `--discovery <path>`? The discovery path is derived from workspace hash, deterministic on both sides — daemon writes it, client polls for it up to N seconds, then connects.)\n- Idle suicide: no requests for --idle-timeout (default 600s) → clean exit (remove discovery file, exit 0).\n- On clean shutdown (SIGTERM): remove discovery file.\n\n**Protocol (HTTP)**:\n- POST /read_file {path, offset, limit, max_chars} → Result envelope {ok, content/error...}. What does the Result envelope look like in clutch-workspace? Need to read envelope.py/filesystem.py. The CLI's --json envelope: {content, error, diff}? From M1 memory: \"--json 信封\", \"信封成功形状\" tests in test_cli: `test_envelope_success_shape` — need to read the actual code.\n- Token auth: header `Authorization: Bearer <token>` or custom header `X-Clutch-Token`. 403/404 on mismatch → client treats as \"not our daemon\" (stale file) → overwrite by starting fresh.\n- Endpoints: /read_file, /grep, /write_file, /edit_file, /undo (or /undo_file), /health, /shutdown? (shutdown endpoint — useful for tests; token-gated. And \"不提供 reconfigure 端点\" — only shutdown/undo/lifecycle beyond the four commands.)\n- Response: the JSON envelope of the Result + HTTP status 200 always? Or map exit codes? The client maps the envelope back to stdout/stderr/exit code locally. Keep HTTP simple: 200 + envelope JSON for command results; 403 bad token; 400 bad JSON body; 404 unknown path.\n\n**Fence**: what fences exist? From previous discussions: 围栏 = protected paths (protect() from the main repo's workspace). In clutch-workspace M1, is there a protected concept? The toolcli memory mentions \"re-applies protect() from the parent's workspace.protected() snapshot\". For clutch-workspace CLI, fence might be: forbid paths outside workspace root? Restrict write/edit to certain subpaths? M1's filesystem._resolve probably confines to root already? Need to read filesystem.py.\n\nThe carry-over said \"围栏上轮的 ①搬哪些\" — which fences to move was undecided. So for M2 I should implement the fence MECHANISM (config at daemon startup, no reconfigure endpoint) with a minimal default (e.g. protected path patterns deny-list for write/edit), leaving the exact fence set configurable via CLI flags at daemon start. Keep it simple: `--protect <glob>` repeatable, and maybe `--deny-write` outside certain dirs? Actually — minimal: the daemon accepts the same flags the CLI would accept for protection. But M1 CLI has NO protection flags at all (4 commands with basic flags). Hmm.\n\nLet me not invent too much: implement the daemon with a `Fence` config object (read-only after startup) that currently supports: workspace root confinement (already implicit in filesystem) + protected path globs for write/edit (+undo refuses to touch protected). CLI daemon flag: `--protect GLOB` repeatable. Thin client passes nothing (fence is daemon-startup-only). When host spawns daemon later, it injects the fence flags — the auditable entry point.\n\n**Undo stack**: in-memory, per daemon. Each write_file/edit_file that CHANGES a file pushes an undo entry (path, old content or reverse patch). `undo` endpoint: pop last entry, restore, return what it did. Also \"undo_file\"? The ratification said \"undo_file 进 HTTP API\" — maybe undo_file = undo of a specific file? Hmm, in the main repo there may be an undo_file tool. Actually re-reading: \"`undo_file` 进 HTTP API，仍不映射给 agent 工具表\" — so there's an operation named undo_file. It might just be the endpoint name for undo. I'll name the endpoint /undo and keep semantics: undo the most recent write/edit (global stack). Hmm — but without knowing the previous design... Let me search the main repo for undo_file to see if it exists (agent/tools/workspace.py). That will tell me the exact semantics to port.\n\n**Thin client in cli.py**:\n- New global flag `--no-server` (before subcommand, like --json): skip daemon entirely, direct execution (current behavior).\n- Default flow for write/edit (mutating commands; read-only commands too? The design says all commands forward for consistent state. But read/grep are stateless — forwarding them buys undo-stack coherence? No — undo only matters for write/edit. Forwarding read/grep adds latency for no benefit... BUT: shared daemon = one process touching the files; read consistency isn't affected either way. However: fence! If the daemon has a fence, direct-execution reads bypass nothing (reads aren't fenced). Hmm, but what about \"daemon 缺席 → 本地直执\" fallback — that's for ALL commands. And forward-everything keeps it simple and uniform: one code path. Also the daemon's CWD is the workspace — path resolution identical.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: if write/edit go to the daemon but read/grep run locally, that's fine for correctness (same files). But uniform forwarding = simpler mental model + the daemon's undo stack sees all mutations. Read/grep forwarding costs ~1ms on loopback. I'll forward everything (except --no-server / daemon-unreachable fallback).\n\nWait — but lazy-start: should read/grep also lazy-start a daemon? That would spawn a daemon on every bare read when absent — heavy for scripting (`clutch-workspace read_file x` in a loop when daemon dead → each call tries to spawn). Mitigation: lazy-start only for mutating commands (write/edit)? But then a read-before-any-write doesn't start the daemon, and the first write starts it. Terminal interactive use mostly does reads... Hmm, the ratified decision #4: \"终端首次调用自动拉起 daemon，失败静默回退直执\" — first invocation (any command) auto-starts. I'll implement: all commands attempt lazy-start (spawn is cheap-ish: ~200-400ms Python startup... actually spawning a python daemon takes ~300ms+. For scripts doing one read, that's overhead every time UNLESS the daemon persists — after the first spawn, subsequent calls hit the alive daemon at ~1-3ms. The 10-min idle timeout keeps it alive across a scripting session. And --no-server exists for scripts that want zero overhead + no side effects. That's the ratified design; keep it.)\n\n- Forwarding: read discovery file → daemon alive? POST → map response to envelope + exit code. Dead/stale → lazy-start (spawn `sys.executable -m clutch_workspace.daemon`? or re-exec sys.argv[0] daemon? Use [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace.daemon\", ...] — hmm, but frozen? clutch-workspace isn't frozen; it's run via entry point script `clutch-workspace` (console script). Best: re-invoke the same console script: [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace\", \"daemon\", ...]? Is there a __main__? M1: `python -m clutch_workspace`? conftest runs... need to check pyproject entry points. Re-exec via sys.executable -m clutch_workspace.cli or the console script shim. Simplest robust: [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace.daemon\", \"--workspace\", root, ...] — module with __main__ guard. Detached spawn: POSIX start_new_session=True; Windows: creationflags=DETACHED_PROCESS|CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP? The daemon must survive the CLI exiting. stdin/stdout/stderr → DEVNULL (daemon logs nowhere / or to a log file in the discovery dir?). Keep logs off by default; --verbose flag for debugging to stderr... but stderr DEVNULL'd when detached. Fine — debugging via foreground `daemon` command in another terminal.\n\n- Lazy-start failure (spawn fails, timeout waiting for discovery): silently fall back to local direct execution. Exit codes preserved.\n\n- Also: envelope rendering must be byte-identical. The daemon returns the Result as JSON; client calls the same emit_result/emit_error. So envelope.py needs a function to emit from a dict. The Result dataclass — reconstruct Result from JSON dict.\n\n**Idle suicide**: daemon tracks last-request time; a watchdog thread checks every few seconds; on idle timeout: remove discovery file (if still ours — compare token), exit. Also pid written into discovery file for liveness checks.\n\n**Race: two clients lazy-start simultaneously** → two daemons. Both write discovery (different ports). One file wins. The loser gets no requests and idles out in 10 min. Acceptable; note in docs. Could add a lock but YAGNI — actually a simple mitigation: before starting, re-read discovery and re-check liveness (double-check). Still racy but narrows. Keep simple.\n\n**Windows Job / kill-on-close**: daemon is detached and MEANT to outlive the CLI. But should it die with the host session? Ratified: daemon is per-workspace machine-wide resident with idle suicide. The host (agent server) that spawns it: when the host dies, daemon persists until idle timeout — that's by design (terminal and scripts share it). OK. But the layering memory said \"the future workspace daemon subclasses ProcessSupervisor with beat_required=False, require_kill_guarantee=False\" — that's from the MAIN repo's perspective (host managing daemons). For THIS implementation in clutch-workspace (zero-dep repo), we don't import procmgr. The main-repo wiring (host tool → HTTP) is a separate later task (host 接线 was explicitly postponed in M1: \"host 接线（Q21/31-33 冻结后置）\").\n\nHmm wait — actually, should I reconsider? Maybe the user wants the daemon IN the main repo? No — clutch-workspace is the workspace tool repo; the daemon IS the workspace daemon; it belongs there. The M1 decisions and 64 tests live there. Definitely clutch-workspace/.\n\n**Testing**: the repo has blackbox tests via conftest run() helper (runs CLI as subprocess? Let me read conftest). Tests for daemon mode:\n- daemon lifecycle: start daemon (subprocess or in-process thread), discovery file written, health ok, token required (403 without), commands round-trip identical to local execution\n- CLI forwarding: with daemon up, CLI output identical to direct execution (byte-identical envelope/exit codes)\n- lazy start: no daemon → CLI starts one (and subsequent call reuses), fallback on spawn failure (e.g. impossible python?) — fallback test: make daemon unstartable, CLI still succeeds via direct path\n- --no-server: no discovery read, direct execution\n- undo: write, undo → content restored; edit, undo → restored; undo on empty stack → dataerr-ish envelope (exit 64? or 74? decide: undo with nothing to undo = usage-ish? It's a state error → I'd say exit 64 dataerr like edit zero-occurrences... actually envelope error codes: dataerr=64, noinput=74(exists=NOINPUT), io=74, bug=70. undo empty → \"nothing to undo\" → 64? Hmm. Let me look at the sysexits mapping in cli.py: 64 = usage/data errors, 74 = IO. \"nothing to undo\" is a data/state condition → 64.)\n- idle suicide: short timeout (0.5s) → daemon exits, discovery removed\n- stale discovery: file with dead pid/garbage → CLI recovers (starts fresh)\n- token mismatch: discovery with wrong token → CLI treats as stale, restarts fresh daemon\n- fence: --protect glob → write to protected path refused by daemon; direct execution unaffected? (fence is daemon-only config; direct exec has no fence — that's the documented semantics: fence lives in daemon startup args.)\n\nWait, one important semantic: when fence refuses a write at the DAEMON, the client shows the refusal envelope with the mapped exit code. The refusal comes as the envelope error (not HTTP-level) so the envelope shape stays uniform. Protected write → dataerr (64)? It's a permission-ish error → EX_PERM? sysexits: 77 = EX_NOPERM. Carry-over ④ was \"退出码77\" pending decision — permission errors → 77. Since carry-over is undecided, I'll implement protected-path refusal as dataerr 64 for now? Hmm — 77 NOPERM exists in sysexits and the carry-over item ④ specifically anticipated \"退出码77\". Since we're now BUILDING the thing that would use it, choosing 77 for fence refusals is the natural reading of the earlier direction. But it was explicitly \"仍待裁\"... The user said \"都按推荐\" twice covering my recommendations; for the daemon I should make a decision and flag it. I'll implement fence refusal = 77 (EX_NOPERM) and note it as a default that can be flipped. Rationale: it's semantically a permission denial, distinct from bad-usage 64.\n\nAlso `--workspace` flag: carry-over ② undecided but the daemon NEEDS a workspace root (per-workspace daemon keyed by path hash). The CLI needs --workspace too (to compute which daemon). Implement `--workspace DIR` global flag (default: cwd). This is forced by the daemon design; flag it.\n\nNow, envelope shape — I need to read the actual code. Let me read clutch-workspace files:\n- clutch_workspace/__init__.py? __main__.py?\n- cli.py (already have line numbers; read fully)\n- envelope.py\n- filesystem.py\n- pyproject.toml (entry points, python version)\n- tests/conftest.py (how tests run the CLI)\n- Maybe one test file for style (already have names)\n\nAlso check main repo agent/tools/workspace.py for undo semantics (undo_file!). Let me grep for undo in agent/.\n\nPlan of reads (parallel batch 1):\n1. read clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/cli.py (full, 133+ lines)\n2. read clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/envelope.py\n3. read clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/filesystem.py\n4. read clutch-workspace/pyproject.toml\n5. read clutch-workspace/tests/conftest.py\n6. grep undo in agent/ (main repo)\n\nThen design precisely, implement, test, commit in clutch-workspace repo (it has its own git).\n\nThis is a big implementation. Let me get to it.\n\nActually — also consider: should the daemon be a NEW file `daemon.py` in clutch_workspace/ + `client.py`? And modify cli.py to route through client. Keep envelope.py untouched mostly (maybe add Result-from-dict helper if Result is a dataclass — put that in envelope.py or client.py).\n\nUndo stack design (in-memory, daemon-lifetime):\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass UndoEntry:\n    path: str       # as-given? absolute?\n    before: str | None  # None = file did not exist\n    existed: bool\n```\nOn write_file: read old bytes (may not exist) BEFORE writing; push after successful write. On edit_file: same (edit = write of modified content; capture before). Undo: pop; if before is None → delete file (and created parents? M1 write auto-creates parents; undo should remove created file; leave parents — document). Else restore verbatim with newline=\"\" binary write (same as filesystem write semantics — need to check filesystem.py's write: newline=\"\\n\" text mode... I'll reuse the internal write helper).\n\nCap stack depth (e.g. 100) — drop oldest.\n\nundo endpoint returns envelope describing the restoration (like write's summary/diff?) — simplest: return the same Result shape as write_file (unified diff of undo applied? that'd be nice: undo produces a diff showing the reversal). Keep simple: Result with content = summary line + diff like write_file does. If filesystem.py has _unified_diff helper, reuse.\n\nEdge: undo across fence: restoring a file now protected? Fence check applies on undo too (it's a write).\n\nConcurrency: ThreadingHTTPServer → requests on threads; lock around stack + mutations? File ops in M1 are single-threaded CLI; daemon multi-threaded. Add a global lock around command execution in the daemon (serialize all filesystem commands — simplest correctness; throughput irrelevant for this use). One lock, done.\n\nSecurity recap: token = secrets.token_hex(32)? stdlib secrets ✓ zero-dep. Discovery file perms: chmod 600 on POSIX (best-effort; Windows ignore). Token check via header; constant-time compare (hmac.compare_digest).\n\nTimeouts: client HTTP timeout ~30s? Commands are fast; daemon start wait: poll discovery up to ~10s (onefile not involved here; python spawn ~300ms; 10s generous), health check with small timeout.\n\nHTTP server: ThreadingHTTPServer with HTTP/1.1? BaseHTTPRequestHandler default protocol_version HTTP/1.0 — set protocol_version = \"HTTP/1.1\" + Content-Length always (we do). keep-alive benefit minimal; but client using http.client per request... fine.\n\nClient HTTP: urllib.request (stdlib) or http.client. urllib is simpler: POST json, read response, status. Timeouts via urllib open(timeout=). Use urllib.request with Request(headers={token}).\n\nWhat about GET /health for liveness probe: return {\"status\":\"ok\",\"workspace\":...,\"pid\":...}? Client probe: GET /health with token header; 200+ok = alive. On connection refused/timeout → dead.\n\nDiscovery file format:\n```json\n{\"version\": 1, \"workspace\": \"C:\\\\...\", \"port\": 51234, \"token\": \"hex\", \"pid\": 12345, \"fence\": {...}?}\n```\nClient doesn't need fence (read-only informational). Keep minimal: version, workspace, port, token, pid, started (timestamp, informational).\n\nDiscovery path: user dir:\n- Windows: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\clutch-workspace (fallback ~/.clutch-workspace)\n- POSIX: ~/.clutch-workspace (or XDG_CACHE_HOME? keep ~/.clutch-workspace/daemons)\n- filename: sha256(normcase(resolved workspace path))[:24].json? Use full hex[:32]. Fine: \"d-\" + hex[:32] + \".json\".\n\nnormcase on Windows lowercases and converts / to \\ — stable across cwd spellings ✓. On POSIX normcase is identity ✓.\n\nAlso: workspace root must EXIST for daemon start? CLI resolves workspace: if dir doesn't exist → fall back to direct execution (which will produce the proper noinput error envelope!). Important: preserve error semantics — don't fail before the command would. E.g. `read_file --path missing.txt` with no daemon: direct exec gives noinput/74. If lazy-start requires an existing dir, missing dir → skip daemon, direct exec → correct error ✓. Also grep --path can be a subdirectory or FILE (single-file search) — workspace default = cwd always exists normally.\n\nHmm wait — workspace root resolution: `--workspace` given → that dir; else cwd. For daemon keying: resolve(). If it doesn't exist → direct exec only.\n\nDaemon spawn env: pass through env; add nothing special. Detach flags as discussed. stdout/stderr → DEVNULL.\n\nShutdown endpoint: POST /shutdown with token → daemon removes discovery + exits (after responding). Useful for tests + ops. \n\nAlso SIGTERM handler → clean exit (remove discovery file if ours).\n\nEdge: daemon holds no locks on files; it's just a resident executor — .clc-style write locks are NOT part of clutch-workspace (that's main-repo project locks). ✓ no interaction.\n\nIdle timeout default: 600s (10 min, ratified \"如 10 分钟\").\n\nNow the \"banner\" — daemon prints to stdout when foreground: `[clutch-workspace-daemon] http://127.0.0.1:PORT workspace=<...>` — for foreground debugging. When detached (spawned by client), stdout is DEVNULL; client discovers via file. No banner parsing needed (discovery file is the channel) ✓ simpler than supervisor's banner approach.\n\nNow — cli.py wiring. Current: main(argv) parses with parse_known? `--json` must precede subcommand; global flags parsed by main parser. Add `--no-server` and `--workspace` as global (pre-subcommand) flags. The dispatch: `_dispatch(args) -> Result` currently calls filesystem functions directly. New flow:\n\n```python\ndef _dispatch(args) -> Result:\n    if args.no_server:\n        return _dispatch_local(args)\n    outcome = client.try_daemon(workspace=args.workspace)  # -> Daemonic | None\n    ...\n```\n\nDesign client API:\n\n```python\n# client.py\ndef execute(workspace: str | None, command: str, payload: dict, *, allow_spawn: bool = True) -> Result | None:\n    \"\"\"Run via daemon; None -> caller must fall back to local.\"\"\"\n```\nReturns Result (success or error-Result) — errors from daemon mapped into Result with exit code so envelope.py can emit uniformly. So Result needs to carry exit codes? Let me see how Result/CommandError work in envelope.py — CommandError(code, message)? M1: \"目录+range 退出码=64\", \"sysexits\" — CommandError likely has an .exit_code or the envelope maps error kind→code. Need to read envelope.py + filesystem.py Result definition (Result is probably in filesystem.py or a types module).\n\nLet me now do the reads. Batch:\n1. cli.py full\n2. envelope.py full\n3. filesystem.py full (241+ lines)\n4. pyproject.toml\n5. conftest.py\n6. grep -n \"undo\" agent/ (main repo) — for undo_file semantics\n7. __init__/__main__ in clutch_workspace\n\nThen write:\n- clutch_workspace/fencing.py (Fence + matching) — or inside daemon.py? Small; separate keeps daemon.py lean. Name: fencing.py? M1 style is flat simple: filesystem.py, envelope.py, cli.py. I'll do: daemon.py (server + fence + undo + discovery write), client.py (discovery read/forward/lazy-start), undo inside daemon.py (it's ~60 lines). Hmm — separation of concerns: \n  - `daemon.py`: main(), Fence, UndoStack, handler, discovery write, idle watch\n  - `client.py`: discovery read, liveness, forward, lazy_start, execute()\n  - shared discovery path helper: `discovery.py`? Both need: discovery_path(workspace) + pid liveness. Put in client.py and daemon imports from client? Daemon needs write+remove; client needs read+probe. A tiny `discovery.py` module with path+read+write+remove+liveness is cleanest shared ground. 3 new modules then: discovery.py, daemon.py, client.py. Plus tests/test_daemon.py (and maybe test_client parts folded in).\n\npyproject: add [project.scripts]? Already has clutch-workspace script? Check pyproject. Module run: does clutch_workspace have __main__.py? conftest probably invokes cli.main in-process (M1 memory said tests are blackbox via subprocess? \"64 黑盒用例\" — conftest _run with cwd/stdin — looks like in-process capture? It takes cwd + stdin and returns... need to read).\n\nFor the daemon tests, spawn real daemon subprocess (blackbox) + in-process for speed? Mixed: use subprocess for lifecycle tests (a couple), in-process server (thread) for command round-trips. conftest style will guide.\n\nAlright — reads first. Then implement. Also remember: commit conventions in clutch-workspace repo: \"feat: ...\", \"test: ...\" (M1 used feat/test prefixes without scope). Follow that.\n\nOne more thing: the user's phrase \"现在实现workspace daemon\" — full implementation with tests green + committed, as I promised earlier (\"裁完我一次性落地：daemon + 薄客户端 + 围栏 + --workspace + undo 栈 + 双模式测试全绿 + 提交\"). That promise listed: daemon + thin client + fence + --workspace + undo stack + dual-mode tests all green + commit. That's the scope. \n\nLet me go. First batch of reads."}
