{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789899532.771821, "id": "03ac3e146c71", "tool_call_id": "call_d1a5fb29a857498b99b9acde", "content": "100: \n101: class Workspace(ABC):\n102:     def __init__(self, root: str | None = None, transport: Transport | None = None) -> None:\n103:         # _root_path picks the path FLAVOR: the host's Path locally, PurePosixPath\n104:         # remotely (a Windows client's Path would re-separator remote /-paths\n105:         # with backslashes before they were ever sent over the bridge)\n106:         self.root: PurePath = self._root_path(root) if root else Path(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"clutch-\"))\n107:         # No local mkdir here on purpose: this base also serves RemoteWorkspace,\n108:         # whose root lives on another HOST. Creating it locally broke macOS\n109:         # (/home is an autofs mount: mkdir -> [Errno 45] ENOTSUP -> \"cannot\n110:         # create project\"). LocalWorkspace ensures its root in its own\n111:         # __init__; the remote side creates what it needs (mkdir -p on write).\n112:         self._transport = transport or LocalTransport(str(self.root))\n113:         self._protected: set[Path] = set()\n114:         # absolute paths outside the root the CURRENT tool call may touch;\n115:         # granted by the permission gate, cleared after every tool call\n116:         self._allowed_escapes: set[Path] = set()\n117:         # per-file undo stack: previous contents recorded before every overwrite\n118:         self._snapshots: dict[str, list[str]] = {}\n119: \n120:     def allow(self, paths) -> None:\n121:         \"\"\"Record user-approved external paths for the current tool call.\"\"\"\n122:         self._allowed_escapes |= {self.realpath(p) for p in paths}\n123: \n124:     def clear_allowed(self) -> None:\n125:         \"\"\"Drop the per-call escape allowance (called after every tool call).\"\"\"\n126:         self._allowed_escapes.clear()\n127: \n128:     def snapshot(self, path: Path, content: str) -> None:\n129:         \"\"\"Record the previous content of a file before an overwrite (undo stack).\"\"\"\n130:         key = str(self.realpath(path))\n131:         stack = self._snapshots.setdefault(key, [])\n132:         stack.append(content)\n133:         if len(stack) > _MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE:\n134:             stack.pop(0)\n135: \n136:     def restore(self, path: Path) -> str | None:\n137:         \"\"\"Pop the last snapshot and write it back; returns the restored content\n138:         (None when there is no snapshot for this file).\"\"\"\n139:         key = str(self.realpath(path))\n140:         stack = self._snapshots.get(key)\n141:         if not stack:\n142:             return None\n143:         content = stack.pop()\n144:         self.write(str(path), content)\n145:         return content\n146: \n147:     def protect(self, path: Path) -> None:\n148:         \"\"\"Mark a file as invisible/unusable to the agent (e.g. the .clc project file).\"\"\"\n149:         self._protected.add(self.realpath(path))\n... (showing lines 100-149 of 715; use offset=150 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789899532.7820609, "id": "59c68b5db673", "tool_call_id": "call_804f6c6f55f94fbaa9d387ab", "content": "440: \n441: # generous per-op timeout for internal remote file ops (read/write/list/append)\n442: _REMOTE_IO_TIMEOUT = 60.0\n443: # how much of a failed exec's stderr/stdout to show in the raised error\n444: _ERR_SNIPPET = 300\n445: # undo stack depth per file (snapshots before each overwrite)\n446: _MAX_SNAPSHOTS_PER_FILE = 5\n447: \n448: \n449: class RemoteWorkspace(Workspace):\n450:     \"\"\"Workspace whose files live on a remote host reached over the exec bridge.\n451: \n452:     Every exec starts a fresh shell, so read/write/list use absolute paths and\n453:     run() prefixes `cd '<root>' &&` for the same cwd semantics as LocalWorkspace.\n454:     File operations are plain POSIX sh — cat, ls, and `printf '%s'` appends (no\n... (showing lines 440-454 of 715; use offset=455 to continue)", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789900023.7062602, "id": "ba36cc02b290", "content": "M1 source is fully read now. Let me check the repo root, test style, and git state before writing code.", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_dbeb63e8526445c7bc7e3c92", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"clutch-workspace\"}"}, {"id": "call_827018f0adb949f4b9fd0b3c", "name": "read_file", "arguments": "{\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/tests/test_write.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_dd58459f9eff48f59ee835ed", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cd clutch-workspace && git log --oneline -5 && git status --short\"}"}], "reasoning": "I have all I need now. Let me design the daemon implementation.\n\n## Design recap (ratified decisions)\n\n**Discovery file** (`discovery.py`):\n- path = sha256 of `os.path.normcase(os.path.abspath(workspace))` — actually ratified: \"path = sha256 of `os.path.normcase`+resolved workspace, filename `d-<hex32>.json`\"\n- user-level dir: `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\clutch-workspace` fallback `~/.clutch-workspace`\n- JSON `{version, workspace, port, token, pid, started}`\n- pid liveness check\n\n**Daemon** (`daemon.py`):\n- `--workspace DIR` required arg, `--protect GLOB` repeatable, `--idle` (600s default), `--port` (default 0=random)\n- ThreadingHTTPServer, 127.0.0.1\n- token via secrets.token_hex\n- Writes discovery file, foreground banner print\n- HTTP: GET /health, POST /read_file /grep /write_file /edit_file /undo /shutdown\n- Fence via --protect globs (fnmatch)\n- In-memory UndoStack `{path, before: str|None, existed}` cap ~100\n- Idle watchdog: last-request timestamp; if now - last > idle → suicide\n- SIGTERM cleanup: remove discovery file\n- One lock serializes command execution\n- Exit code 77 for fence refusal\n\nWait — fence refusal exit 77. That's a client-side mapping: the daemon returns envelope with a special code, and the client exits 77. Hmm, how do we map envelope → exit codes byte-identically? The envelope only has {content, error, diff}. The exit code needs to be conveyed. Options: HTTP status carries the sysexits code! That's clean: daemon returns HTTP status = exit code (64/65/66/77/74/70...) and body = envelope JSON. Client maps HTTP status → process exit code, body → emit. For 77 specifically: daemon rejects fenced path → HTTP 77 with envelope error.\n\nBut careful: for byte-identical output, client just calls emit_result/emit_error on the envelope parsed from the body. The exit code comes from HTTP status.\n\nActually — the existing tests run `python -m clutch_workspace` in a tmp cwd, no daemon. The default path: lazy start? Ratified decision: \"lazy start on first invocation with silent fallback to direct execution\". Hmm wait, let me re-read: \"daemon + thin client + fallback direct execution; ... lazy start on first invocation with silent fallback to direct execution\".\n\nSo the CLI default behavior: try to connect to daemon (probe discovery file for the CWD workspace); if daemon not there, lazily START one (spawn detached), wait up to ~10s polling discovery file, connect; if anything fails (spawn fails, timeout, workspace dir missing), fall back to direct execution. `--no-server` skips all of that and executes directly.\n\nHmm, but \"lazy start with silent fallback to direct execution\" — meaning if the daemon can't start, we silently just run directly. OK.\n\nImportant subtlety: **the daemon's CWD must be the workspace dir** since filesystem.py resolves relative paths against `Path.cwd()`. When the daemon starts, it should `os.chdir(workspace)`. Then all commands resolve against the workspace.\n\n**Client** (`client.py`):\n- `connect(workspace)` → probe discovery file → GET /health with token → returns (port, token) or None\n- lazy start: spawn `[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace.daemon\", \"--workspace\", ws, ...]` detached, then poll discovery file ~10s\n- `forward(cmd, args)` → POST JSON → (status, envelope)\n\n**Undo in HTTP but not agent tool table**: undo_file is in the HTTP API (POST /undo) but not added as a CLI subcommand? Hmm. Wait — \"undo_file in HTTP API but NOT mapped to agent tool table\". The agent tool table is in the main repo. But what about the clutch-workspace CLI? Hmm. The task says the HTTP surface includes POST /undo. Does the CLI get an `undo` subcommand?\n\nLet me think. The ratified decision list says: \"undo_file in HTTP API but NOT mapped to agent tool table\". The agent tool table = main repo's tool registry. The clutch-workspace CLI is separate. For the CLI to be usable with undo... Actually the main repo's agent would use undo through its own tool mapping. But since it's \"not mapped to agent tool table\", the undo is only reachable via HTTP directly? That seems odd but it's the ratified decision — probably because the host agent's undo goes through its own snapshot mechanism, and clutch-workspace's undo is for the daemon session.\n\nHmm, but for testability and CLI usability, I think adding a CLI `undo` subcommand makes sense... but wait — byte-identical UX contract: \"CLI surface `clutch-workspace [--json] <command> [flags]`\". The four commands. Adding `undo` to the CLI would extend the surface. The decisions don't say to add undo to the CLI. And \"undo_file in HTTP API but NOT mapped to agent tool table\" strongly suggests: HTTP-only. The CLI stays 4 commands.\n\nHmm, but then how do users call undo? Via HTTP. That's fine — it's for the host agent's future use... but the host agent maps tools to CLI calls... whatever. I'll follow the ratified decision strictly: HTTP surface has /undo; CLI surface stays the 4 commands. Actually wait, let me reconsider — maybe I should give the CLI a hidden way to test undo. The tests can hit HTTP directly via urllib. Fine.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider once more. A daemon-managed undo stack that can only be triggered by raw HTTP is a bit weird but it's exactly what was ratified (\"undo_file in HTTP API but NOT mapped to agent tool table\"). Keep it. Tests will exercise it over HTTP.\n\n**Exit code 77**: fence refusal. Daemon: when write/edit/undo targets a protected path (matching a --protect glob) → CommandError with code 77. I need to add EX_PROTOCOL-ish... 77 = EX_NOPERM in sysexits (EX_NOPERM=77 \"permission denied\"). Actually sysexits.h: EX_NOPERM = 77. So fence refusal = EX_NOPERM. I'll add `EX_NOPERM = 77` to exitcodes.py with a comment.\n\nWait, but the ratified plan says \"fence refusal = exit 77 (flagged as flippable default)\". OK, 77.\n\nWhere does the fence check happen? In filesystem.write_file/edit_file there's no fence. The daemon wraps commands: before executing write_file/edit_file/undo, check the target path against the protected globs → raise CommandError(77, \"...\"). Reads (read_file/grep) — protected means \"invisible/unusable to the agent\" in the host; here --protect = fence for writes? The decision says \"Fence via repeatable --protect GLOB\". In the host, protect makes files invisible AND unusable. Hmm. For our daemon: fenced = refuse writes/edits/undo (mutation). Should read also refuse? In host Workspace.protect: \"Mark a file as invisible/unusable to the agent\". So reads too. Hmm, but for the workspace daemon, the fence's purpose is to protect e.g. `.clc` project files from agent mutation...\n\nLet me keep it simple and match the host spirit: protect = refuse to read, grep through, write, edit, undo? That could be annoying for grep (needs filtering). Hmm.\n\nActually let me look at the frozen decision more carefully: \"Fence via repeatable `--protect GLOB`\" — from the design summary. It doesn't specify mutation-only. The carry-over note: \"①which fences to move\" is still pending. So the precise fence semantics were NOT fully ratified — pending decision ①. I should implement something reasonable and document it.\n\nGiven pending decision ① is \"which fences to move\", the minimal viable fence for the daemon is mutation-protection: write_file/edit_file/undo refuse protected paths with exit 77. Reads/greps pass through. I'll document that in the module docstring and keep it flippable. Actually hmm — \"protect\" semantics from the host include invisibility. But being conservative: implement write-fence only, clearly documented. Since ① is pending, any choice is provisional; mutation-fence is the smallest useful one. Let me note it in the commit/message as pending ①.\n\n**Undo stack**: cap ~100 entries total (per daemon). Entry: `{path: str, before: str|None}` — before=None means file didn't exist before → undo deletes it. Push after successful write/edit. Undo: pop last; fence check on the path first (refuse 77 if protected); if before is None → delete file (unlink, missing_ok? if missing already, treat as ok or error?); else write back before-content. Return Result describing the restore.\n\nUndo errors: empty stack → \"nothing to undo\" error (~exit 64 per summary — \"(~exit 64)\"). Hmm, \"empty stack → 'nothing to undo' error (~exit 64)\". 64 = EX_USAGE. Fine.\n\nUndo restore write should be newline=\"\\n\" utf-8, same as filesystem.\n\n**Discovery module** (`discovery.py`):\n\n```python\ndef discovery_dir() -> Path\ndef key(workspace: str) -> str  # hex32\ndef path_for(workspace) -> Path  # dir / d-<hex32>.json\ndef read(workspace) -> dict | None  # None if missing/corrupt/dead-pid (cleanup stale)\ndef write(workspace, port, token, pid) -> Path\ndef remove(workspace)\ndef pid_alive(pid) -> bool\ndef workspace_key_input(workspace) -> str  # normcase(abspath(resolved))\n```\n\nRatified: \"path = sha256 of `os.path.normcase`+resolved workspace\". So input = os.path.normcase(str(Path(workspace).resolve()))? resolve() on a path that may not exist — Path.resolve() in py3.10+ works with strict=False default, resolves symlinks where possible. Use `os.path.normcase(os.path.abspath(workspace))`? The ratified wording says normcase + resolved. I'll do `os.path.normcase(str(Path(workspace).resolve()))`.\n\npid liveness: on Windows, `psutil` not available; stdlib way: try `os.kill(pid, 0)`? On Windows os.kill with sig 0... Actually on Windows os.kill(pid, 0) calls TerminateProcess? No wait: Python docs — on Windows, os.kill only supports CTRL_C_EVENT/CTRL_BREAK_EVENT and otherwise calls TerminateProcess with the sig as exit code! `os.kill(pid, 0)` on Windows would TERMINATE the process with exit code 0. Dangerous! So must not use os.kill on Windows.\n\nRobust cross-platform stdlib pid liveness:\n- Windows: `ctypes.windll.kernel32.OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION=0x1000, False, pid)` → if handle, check `GetExitCodeProcess` == STILL_ACTIVE. Or simpler: OpenProcess returns NULL if process doesn't exist (ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER 87). Also could use `ctypes.windll.kernel32.GetProcessTimes`.\n- POSIX: os.kill(pid, 0) → OSError ESRCH = dead; EPERM = alive.\n\nI'll implement `_pid_alive` with platform branch. On Windows use ctypes (zero-dependency, stdlib).\n\nEdge: pid reuse — acceptable (documented).\n\n**Daemon** (`daemon.py`):\n\n```python\npython -m clutch_workspace.daemon --workspace DIR [--protect GLOB ...] [--idle SECONDS] [--port N] [--discovery-dir DIR?]\n```\n\n- Banner: print one line to stdout like `clutch-workspace daemon: workspace=... port=... pid=...` then flush. Foreground banner per decision (\"foreground banner print\"). Hmm — \"client spawns it detached ... DEVNULL stdio\". Banner goes nowhere when detached, but if you run the daemon manually in a foreground terminal, you see it. Also maybe write a log? Keep minimal: banner to stdout.\n\n- Handler: BaseHTTPRequestHandler subclass.\n  - do_GET: /health → 200 {\"ok\": true, \"workspace\": ..., \"pid\": ...}. Unknown → 404 envelope error.\n  - do_POST: routes /read_file /grep /write_file /edit_file /undo /shutdown.\n  - Token check on ALL requests (health too? If health requires token, the probe needs the token — the client gets the token from the discovery file, so fine. But health check with bad token → 403; that also validates token. Ratified: \"403 bad token\". I'll require token on everything.)\n  - Read body: Content-Length; parse JSON → 400 on bad JSON.\n  - Execute under lock; map CommandError → HTTP status = err.code with error envelope; success → 200 with result envelope. Unexpected exception → 70 + envelope.\n  - /shutdown: respond 200 first ({\"ok\": true}), then trigger shutdown of the server loop (set a flag / call server.shutdown() from a thread to avoid deadlock).\n  - After each request: update last activity, push undo entries as commands execute (the undo capture happens in the command wrappers).\n\nHow do undo captures integrate with filesystem.write_file/edit_file? Those functions don't expose old content. Options:\n1. Daemon wraps: before calling filesystem.write_file(path,...), read old content itself (same _read_text logic), then after success push {path, before}.\n2. Monkeypatch.\n\nCleaner: daemon reads old content pre-call via filesystem internals. `_read_text` is private but same package — acceptable? I could add a tiny public helper in filesystem.py: `snapshot_for(path) -> str | None` … but modifying filesystem.py adds surface. Hmm, but there's a subtle race: daemon reads old, then write_file reads old again — serialized by the lock, so no race. Two reads of old content is fine.\n\nBut careful: for edit_file, \"before\" = the file content before the edit (full text). For write_file, before = old text or None if it didn't exist. filesystem._read_text raises FileNotFoundError for missing → None.\n\nI'll add to filesystem.py a small public function `read_before(path: str) -> str | None`? Hmm — wait. There's a subtlety about existed: for write_file with `old=\"\"` — if the file existed but was empty, `old = \"\"` and existed=True. The decision: entry `{path, before: str|None, existed}`. Why `existed` separate from before=None? If file existed and was empty: before=\"\" (not None) and existed=True. If missing: before=None, existed=False. Undo restores: if existed → write before (even empty); else delete. So before=\"\" works to distinguish empty-existing. Then why `existed` at all? Maybe for clarity in the JSON. I'll store all three fields as ratified: `{path, before, existed}`. That matches the ratified discovery JSON spec... well, that's in-memory anyway.\n\nFence check: which path do we check? The path AS GIVEN? Or resolved? Globs like `*.env` or `.clc*` or `secrets/*` — matching against the CWD-relative display path AND the basename AND the raw given path? Keep it simple and predictable: fence matches if the glob matches (fnmatch) the path as given, its basename, or its CWD-relative form. Hmm, that's three-way matching. Simpler: match against CWD-relative resolved path and the given path and basename. I'll implement `_fenced(p: Path, given: str) -> bool` checking fnmatch over: given, basename, rel. Use fnmatch.fnmatch (case-insensitive on Windows per os.path.normcase — actually fnmatch.fnmatch normalizes case on case-insensitive platforms; fnmatchcase doesn't). Use fnmatch.fnmatch (platform-flavored) — good for Windows `*.ENV` matching.\n\n**Idle watchdog**: thread daemon=True; loop sleep(1s... or 5s); if time.monotonic() - last_activity > idle and no request in flight: shutdown. Implement with a threading.Event and server.shutdown() called from the watchdog thread (shutdown() must not be called from a handler thread or serve_forever thread — calling from watchdog thread is fine).\n\nCareful: `HTTPServer.shutdown()` blocks until serve_forever exits. Watchdog thread calling shutdown() while main thread is in serve_forever() — fine.\n\n**Startup flow (daemon)**:\n1. Parse args.\n2. Resolve workspace abs; if missing → error exit? Daemon started lazily only when client saw the dir exists; if missing, daemon prints error and exits 66 (EX_NOINPUT).\n3. os.chdir(workspace).\n4. Bind server (\"127.0.0.1\", port).\n5. token = secrets.token_hex(32)?? token_hex(16)=32 chars. Fine: token_hex(16).\n6. Write discovery file (with pid).\n7. Print banner, flush.\n8. Start watchdog, install SIGTERM/SIGINT handlers → cleanup discovery + exit.\n9. serve_forever.\n\nSignal handling: signal.signal(SIGTERM, handler) where handler calls cleanup and raises SystemExit — but handler in main thread during serve_forever... serve_forever runs in main thread; signal handler runs in main thread; calling server.shutdown() from the same thread that runs serve_forever deadlocks! Instead the handler should set a flag / call server.server_close + os._exit? Cleanest: handler does cleanup (remove discovery file) then `os._exit(0)`? That's abrupt but works for a daemon. Hmm, better: handler sets stop event and calls... Actually per Python docs: you CAN call shutdown() from a signal handler if it runs in a different thread than serve_forever — it doesn't. Use the watchdog approach: signal handler sets `self._stop_requested` event; watchdog loop notices and calls shutdown() (watchdog is a different thread). That's clean.\n\nActually simpler: signal handler just does discovery cleanup + os._exit(0)? Then socket stays half-open but process dies — OS closes sockets. Discovery removal is the important cleanup (ratified: \"SIGTERM cleanup\"). But graceful is nicer: use the event. I'll do: handler sets stop_event; watchdog checks stop_event every 0.5s and calls server.shutdown(); main thread then does cleanup and returns. But if watchdog already exited... watchdog loop: while not stop_event: sleep; check idle. When stop_event set → shutdown(). Watchdog is daemon thread and always running while serve_forever runs. OK.\n\nHmm wait, one more subtlety: ThreadingHTTPServer with daemon_threads=True so hung client connections don't block exit. Also allow_reuse_address. In Python 3.10 ThreadingHTTPServer has daemon_threads = True already? ThreadingHTTPServer sets daemon_threads = True. Yes (http.server: `class ThreadingHTTPServer(socketserver.ThreadingMixIn, HTTPServer): daemon_threads = True`).\n\n**Client** (`client.py`):\n\n```python\ndef resolve_daemon(workspace: str, timeout: float = 10.0) -> tuple[dict, ...] | None\n```\n\nFlow used by cli.main:\n1. If args.no_server → return None (direct).\n2. workspace = args.workspace or os.getcwd().\n   - If not os.path.isdir(workspace) → return None (skip daemon; preserves noinput/74 semantics — direct exec handles missing files).\n\n   Wait — but if --workspace points elsewhere and CWD is different... hmm. When using a daemon for workspace W, the daemon chdir'd to W, so path resolution happens against W. But then a direct-exec fallback runs against process CWD ≠ W! Consistency: with `--workspace DIR`, direct execution must ALSO happen in DIR. So cli must chdir to workspace before direct execution. That's carry-over ②: `--workspace DIR` global flag. When set (or when CWD is the workspace), execution is relative to that dir.\n\n   Design: `--workspace DIR` (default: CWD). If we run directly, os.chdir(workspace) first (fail → error exit). If we forward to daemon, daemon is chdir'd there.\n\n   Hmm wait, but then with default (no --workspace), workspace = CWD anyway. Simple: always effective_workspace = args.workspace or CWD; if direct path chosen → chdir(effective_workspace) before dispatch (this also makes tests that pass --workspace work).\n\n   Note: tests run with cwd=tmp_path already; adding chdir to same dir is a no-op. Good.\n\n3. Probe: discovery read for effective workspace → if file exists and pid alive → try GET /health with token (short timeout ~2s). If success → daemon available.\n4. If not available: lazy start — spawn daemon detached; poll discovery up to ~10s for a live entry; then health check. If still nothing → fall back direct (silent).\n5. Forward: POST with JSON body of the command args, timeout (long-ish, commands are quick; but grep over big trees could take a while — use, say, 120s? Client transport timeout: give 300s to be safe? Hmm. grep on huge workspace could exceed. I'll use 600s = idle window, well beyond normal ops).\n\n   Hmm, actually there's a wrinkle: if the daemon dies mid-request or the request fails (URLError, timeout) → fall back to direct execution silently? Silent fallback on forward failure could double-apply a mutation... no wait: if forward failed at transport level, the command may or may not have been applied (e.g. response lost after write). Falling back and re-executing write_file is idempotent (overwrite with same content) — fine. edit_file also idempotent (after successful edit, old_string no longer appears → second attempt errors \"not found\" — NOT idempotent!). Hmm. Risk: response lost after successful edit → fallback direct → edit fails with 65 \"old_string not found\". That's confusing but rare and arguably correct (edit was applied). What should we do? On transport failure after connect succeeded, DON'T silently fall back for mutations; surface an error? Ratified decision says lazy start falls back silently when daemon is ABSENT. Mid-request failure is different.\n\n   Decision: connection failures during the probe/lazy-start phase → silent direct fallback. Transport failure during a forward (daemon was healthy, then request failed) → emit error exit 74 \"daemon connection lost\" (no silent re-execution — avoids the non-idempotent double-apply problem). Hmm, but is that over-engineering? It's the safe choice; document it.\n\n6. Envelope mapping: HTTP 200 → emit_result(envelope) exit 0. HTTP != 200: if body is a valid error envelope → emit_error(CommandError(status, content), json) exit status... but careful: exit codes must be the CLI's contract codes. Daemon returns status = the sysexits code. For 403/400/404 (HTTP-level), map to... these shouldn't happen in normal ops (token mismatch → 403 → treat as daemon unusable, fallback direct? If token mismatched, discovery is stale/foreign — safest: remove discovery file, fallback direct). Map: 403/404 → treat as no-daemon → fallback direct (with discovery cleanup). 400 → usage error 64 locally? The daemon's 400 = malformed request JSON, which would be a client bug. Map 400 → EX_SOFTWARE-ish? Actually since the client builds requests itself, 400 = bug → exit 70. 5xx/other → 70. But 70 requires traceback? No: emit_error with \"internal error: ...\" prose. Keep it: for statuses not in the contract table, exit 70 with the envelope content.\n\nSo client.forward returns (status, envelope_dict). CLI maps:\n- status == 200 and not error → Result(content, diff) → emit_result → 0\n- status == 200 and error → can't happen (daemon only sends error envelopes on non-200). Defensive: emit error.\n- status in contract codes (64,65,66,70,74,77,130?) → emit_error(CommandError(status, content)) → exit status.\n- else → exit 70 with envelope content prefixed.\n\n7. `undo` — HTTP only, not a CLI subcommand (ratified). So client.py has `undo()` function for HTTP use / tests.\n\nHmm wait — really? Let me reconsider once more whether CLI gets `undo`. \"undo_file in HTTP API but NOT mapped to agent tool table\" — the agent tool table lives in the MAIN repo (agent/tools/*). The clutch-workspace CLI is a separate surface. If the main repo agent wanted daemon undo, it would map undo_file → HTTP call directly, not through the 4-command CLI. So CLI stays 4 commands. But then who tests undo? Tests hit HTTP directly. OK — but hmm, users of the CLI... The CLI is a thin client; its undo would be natural but the ratified surface says 4 commands. I'll add CLI `undo` — no wait. Don't. Stick to ratified decisions; deviations need user sign-off. HTTP-only undo it is.\n\nHmm, hold on. But the client spawns daemon per workspace keyed on CWD; a CLI user doing two write_file invocations hits the same daemon (discovery file), so the undo stack persists across invocations. With HTTP-only undo, a user can still curl it. Fine.\n\n**Byte-identical envelope**: daemon responses are envelope JSON; client re-emits via emit_result/emit_error → byte-identical to direct execution.\n\nOne nuance: direct execution with stdin payload (`-`): the CLIENT reads stdin before forwarding (payload goes in the JSON body). Keep `_payload` reading in CLI before forwarding. Good — no stdin passing over HTTP.\n\n**Windows detached spawn**: subprocess.Popen with:\n- creationflags: `DETACHED_PROCESS = 0x00000008` and `CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP = 0x200`, or use `CREATE_NO_WINDOW = 0x08000000`. Ratified: Windows DETACHED_PROCESS. Use DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP. stdout/stderr/in = DEVNULL. close_fds=True.\n- POSIX: start_new_session=True, DEVNULL stdio.\n\nDetached + DEVNULL means banner invisible when spawned — fine.\n\nHmm wait: on Windows, DETACHED_PROCESS means no console; python writes to DEVNULL anyway.\n\nOne catch on Windows: `sys.executable` when running under pytest — fine, points to python.exe.\n\n**Daemon module name in spawn**: `[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace.daemon\", \"--workspace\", ws, ...]`. For `-m` to work, the package must be importable. In dev/test, PYTHONPATH set to repo root — the client must pass its env along (Popen inherits env by default — conftest sets PYTHONPATH for the CLI subprocess, which inherits to the daemon spawn).\n\nBut in production (installed package), -m works. OK.\n\nAlso: if the CLI is frozen/py2exe... not our problem.\n\n**Discovery staleness cleanup**: read() checks pid liveness; if dead → remove file, return None. Ratified: \"idle suicide ~10 min + pid liveness cleanup of stale discovery files\".\n\n**Concurrency lock**: one lock around command execution in daemon (ratified: \"daemon serializes command execution with one lock\").\n\n**/health response**: include workspace + pid, {\"ok\": true, ...}. Envelope? Health isn't a command; plain JSON ok.\n\nNow — idle default 600s ratifié. Watchdog checks every ~1s. Idle timer resets on ANY request (including health? If a monitor polls health forever, daemon never idles — acceptable; reset on any request).\n\nHmm, one question: should /shutdown also be token-gated? Yes, everything token-gated.\n\n**Where do commands get their args?** The POST body JSON: e.g. {\"path\": \"a.txt\", \"offset\": 1, \"limit\": 5, \"max_chars\": 20000} / {\"pattern\": ..., \"path\": ..., \"include\": ...} / {\"path\": ..., \"content\": ...} / {\"path\": ..., \"old_string\": ..., \"new_string\": ...} / {} for undo.\n\nDaemon maps to filesystem functions directly. Missing required keys → 400.\n\nNow about **testing**. Test plan:\n1. Unit-ish blackbox via CLI with a live daemon: spawn daemon manually (subprocess in fixture, wait for banner/health), then run CLI commands with --workspace pointing at it → same outputs as direct. But wait: CLI probes discovery by workspace key — daemon fixture writes discovery to the default user dir... that pollutes the user's real discovery dir during tests! Need a `--discovery-dir` override (env var or flag) for tests: `CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR` env var? Or a daemon `--discovery-dir` flag that the CLI also needs to know... The CLI computes the discovery path itself, so the override must be readable by the CLI too → env var is right: `CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR`. Tests set env for both CLI subprocess and daemon subprocess. Hmm, but conftest's `_run` builds env from os.environ — I can set env vars in test via monkeypatch.setenv (os.environ propagates to subprocess). Good.\n\n   Also discovery dir default: %LOCALAPPDATA%\\clutch-workspace fallback ~/.clutch-workspace. Use `os.environ.get(\"LOCALAPPDATA\")` then home.\n\n2. End-to-end lazy start test: run CLI in tmp workspace with discovery-dir override; CLI spawns daemon itself; run write_file; then read_file hits same daemon (undo stack persists? test undo via HTTP). Then stop daemon via HTTP /shutdown and assert discovery file gone. This validates the whole flow including detached spawn. On Windows detached spawn from pytest works.\n\n3. Fence test: daemon with --protect \"secret*\" → write_file --path secret.txt over HTTP → exit 77.\n\n4. Idle suicide: daemon with --idle 1 → wait ~2.5s → discovery gone, daemon dead. (Keep test timeouts generous.)\n\n5. Stale discovery cleanup: write discovery file with pid of dead process (e.g. spawn+kill a python, or use a pid unlikely to exist — finding a definitely-dead pid is tricky; spawn `python -c pass` and wait, its pid is dead afterwards; on Windows pid reuse possible but unlikely) → CLI probe treats as absent → direct exec still works.\n\n6. Regression: existing 64 tests must stay green (fallback path unchanged). The default path now ATTEMPTS lazy start for tmp_path workspaces! Uh oh — existing tests run CLI in tmp_path with default discovery dir (user real dir). Lazy start would spawn REAL daemons during the existing test suite, keyed on tmp_path workspaces. That changes behavior of 64 tests: each CLI invocation would try to start a daemon (~10s poll on failure? No — spawn succeeds, so a daemon starts; tests then forward through it). Two issues: (a) slows tests, (b) leaks daemons (600s idle!) writing discovery files into the user's real LOCALAPPDATA, (c) subtle behavior changes (e.g. OSError mapping could differ).\n\n   That's unacceptable. Existing tests must exercise DIRECT execution by default. Options:\n   - Env kill switch: `CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER=1` disables daemon entirely (like --no-server). conftest sets it in _run env → all existing tests run direct, unchanged. New daemon tests explicitly unset it / override to 0.\n   - Default remains lazy-start-on for real users.\n\n   That's the clean solution: conftest._run adds CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER=1 (and I'll allow per-test override by passing env var). Wait — \"override\": _run builds env = dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH=...) — I'll make it: start from os.environ, set PYTHONPATH, PYTHONUTF8, and set NO_SERVER=1 only if not already present in os.environ (so a daemon test can monkeypatch.setenv(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"0\") to re-enable). Simpler: env.setdefault semantics:\n\n   ```python\n   env = dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH=str(ROOT), PYTHONUTF8=\"1\")\n   env.setdefault(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"1\")\n   ```\n\n   Daemon tests do `monkeypatch.setenv(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"0\")` before calling run → env dict copies os.environ which has \"0\" → setdefault no-ops.\n\n   And daemon unit tests spawn the daemon directly (not via CLI), also with env overrides.\n\nAlso the CLIENT must consult CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER (treated like --no-server). Document in --help.\n\n**Docs**: update README? There's a README? Let me check the repo root. Also update cli.py docstring, exitcodes.py table (77). pyproject — no new deps.\n\nAlso: version bump? 0.1.0 → 0.2.0? The M1 release was v0.1.14 in the MAIN repo — different project. clutch-workspace is at 0.1.0. This is a feature → 0.2.0? Hmm, memory says \"Clutch push/release conventions\" — the version here is independent. I'll bump to 0.2.0 in `__init__.py` + pyproject? The commit style `feat: ...`. Minor bump appropriate. Let me check memory for release conventions... Memory \"Clutch push/release conventions + auth reality\" — but that's for the main repo. For clutch-workspace, M1 didn't release. I'll bump to 0.2.0 and mention it. Actually — safer to not do a release, just commit. Bumping version in-tree is fine.\n\nLet me check whether there's a README in clutch-workspace and what tests look like (to match style). Let me look at test_cli.py briefly for style, and check repo root contents + git log.\n\nNow, implementation files:\n\n### `clutch_workspace/exitcodes.py` — add EX_NOPERM = 77\n\n### `clutch_workspace/discovery.py`\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Discovery files: how clients find the daemon for a workspace.\n\nOne daemon per workspace; the rendezvous is a JSON file in a user-level\ndirectory, named by a hash of the (normalized, resolved) workspace path:\n\n    <discovery_dir>/d-<sha256[:32]>.json\n    {\"version\": 1, \"workspace\": ..., \"port\": int, \"token\": hex, \"pid\": int, \"started\": iso}\n\nThe default directory is %LOCALAPPDATA%/clutch-workspace, falling back to\n~/.clutch-workspace where LOCALAPPDATA is not set (POSIX). Tests (and\nparanoid users) can repoint it with CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR.\n\nStale entries (dead pid) are removed on read: the daemon idles out after\n~10 minutes, so discovery files outlive their daemons routinely.\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nFunctions:\n- `discovery_dir() -> Path`\n- `workspace_key(workspace) -> str` — `os.path.normcase(str(Path(workspace).resolve()))`; sha256 → hex[:32]\n- `discovery_file(workspace) -> Path`\n- `read(workspace) -> dict | None` — parse; validate keys; pid alive? else remove + None. Corrupt JSON → remove + None.\n- `write(workspace, port, token, pid) -> Path` — mkdir parents; write JSON (newline \\n).\n- `remove(workspace) -> None` — missing_ok.\n- `pid_alive(pid) -> bool`\n\npid_alive:\n```python\nif pid <= 0: return False\nif os.name == \"nt\":\n    import ctypes\n    PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION = 0x1000\n    STILL_ACTIVE = 259\n    k32 = ctypes.windll.kernel32\n    h = k32.OpenProcess(PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION, False, pid)\n    if not h: return False\n    try:\n        code = ctypes.c_ulong()\n        if not k32.GetExitCodeProcess(h, ctypes.byref(code)): return False\n        return code.value == STILL_ACTIVE\n    finally:\n        k32.CloseHandle(h)\ntry:\n    os.kill(pid, 0)\nexcept ProcessLookupError: return False  # hmm, on POSIX: ESRCH\nexcept PermissionError: return True      # exists but not ours\nexcept OSError: return False\nreturn True\n```\n\nCareful: on Windows os.kill(pid, 0) terminates — we don't call it there.\n\nNote ProcessLookupError is a subclass of OSError; order matters. On some POSIX, os.kill(pid,0) with a zombie returns success — fine, alive-ish.\n\n### `clutch_workspace/daemon.py`\n\n```python\n\"\"\"The workspace daemon: one long-lived process per workspace.\n...\npython -m clutch_workspace.daemon --workspace DIR [--protect GLOB ...] [--idle SECONDS] [--port N]\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nStructure:\n- `class Daemon`: holds server, token, protect globs, undo stack, lock, last_activity.\n- Handler class created via closure or with reference to daemon instance (set as class attr on a per-instance subclass — standard trick: `Handler.daemon = self`).\n\nRoutes:\n- `_cmd_read_file(args)` → filesystem.read_file(path, offset, limit, max_chars)\n- `_cmd_grep` → filesystem.grep(pattern, path, include)\n- `_cmd_write_file` → capture before; check fence; filesystem.write_file; push undo\n- `_cmd_edit_file` → same\n- `_cmd_undo` → fence check on entry path; pop; restore\n- `/shutdown`\n\nFence check BEFORE mutation (and for undo, check the recorded path).\n\nWait, order for write: fence-check FIRST (before reading old content, before writing). And capture `before` via filesystem._read_text... using _resolve? I need the same path. I'll do `before = _read_before(path)` in daemon using `filesystem._read_text(filesystem._resolve(path))` wrapped in try FileNotFoundError → None. Slight duplication of resolution — acceptable within the same package (documented). Alternatively add a public helper to filesystem.py: `previous_content(path) -> str | None`. Cleaner for a same-package consumer. Let me add:\n\n```python\ndef previous_content(path: str) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"The file's current text, or None when it does not exist (undo support:\n    the daemon records this before a write/edit and restores it on undo).\"\"\"\n    try:\n        return _read_text(_resolve(path))\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        return None\n```\n\nThat keeps `_resolve`/`_read_text` private and gives the daemon a tiny public seam. And for undo-restore, the daemon needs to WRITE content back — needs a write helper with the same newline discipline. Add:\n\n```python\ndef restore_content(path: str, content: str | None) -> Result-ish?\n```\n\nHmm, restore = write content back (or delete file). Let me give filesystem.py a `restore(path, content)` that: if content is None → unlink (missing_ok); else mkdir parent + write_text(newline=\"\\n\"). Return a summary string? The undo Result content: e.g. `OK: undid <path> (restored N chars)` / `(deleted)`. Let the daemon compose prose; filesystem.restore returns str summary of what it did:\n\n```python\ndef restore(path: str, content: str | None) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Inverse of a recorded write/edit (undo): content None deletes the file.\n    Shares write discipline (utf-8, newline='\\\\n') with write_file.\"\"\"\n    p = _resolve(path)\n    if content is None:\n        p.unlink(missing_ok=True)   # py3.8+: ok, requires-python >= 3.10\n        return f\"OK: undid {path} (file removed)\"\n    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    p.write_text(content, encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")\n    return f\"OK: undid {path} (restored {len(content)} chars)\"\n```\n\nWait — for a path whose parent doesn't exist anymore, unlink raises FileNotFoundError even with missing_ok? No: missing_ok handles it (it catches FileNotFoundError). Yes, `Path.unlink(missing_ok=True)` suppresses FileNotFoundError. Fine.\n\nUndo empty stack: CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"nothing to undo\").\n\nUndo fence: check entry[\"path\"] against fence → 77. Then restore. Push nothing on undo (no redo).\n\nNow, HTTP layer:\n\n```python\nclass _Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    protocol_version = \"HTTP/1.1\"  # keep-alive; must set Content-Length on all responses (we do)\n```\n\nHmm, HTTP/1.1 with Content-Length set → keep-alive fine. urllib.request sends Connection: close by default? urllib sends `Connection: close`. Then server closes anyway. protocol_version 1.1 is fine. Actually with BaseHTTPRequestHandler default (HTTP/1.0), each request closes the connection — simpler and plenty. Keep default to avoid subtle keep-alive hangs. Default it is.\n\n- `do_GET`: path == \"/health\" → token check → 200 JSON `{\"ok\": true, \"workspace\": ws, \"pid\": pid, \"idle\": idle}`. else 404 envelope.\n- `do_POST`: routes table; token check; read body; json.loads (400 on error); execute under daemon.lock; respond.\n\nToken check: `hmac.compare_digest(self.headers.get(\"X-Clutch-Token\", \"\"), daemon.token)`. Need `import hmac, secrets`.\n\nResponse helper:\n```python\ndef _respond(self, status: int, payload: dict) -> None:\n    body = json.dumps(payload, ensure_ascii=False).encode(\"utf-8\")\n    self.send_response(status)\n    self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json; charset=utf-8\")\n    self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))\n    self.end_headers()\n    self.wfile.write(body)\n```\n\nError envelope shape = {\"content\": msg, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\"} — matches client emit mapping.\n\nUnexpected exception in command → 70 + {\"content\": f\"internal error: {err}\", ...}; also print traceback to stderr (daemon stderr is DEVNULL when spawned, visible when foreground — helpful).\n\nlog_message override: silence per-request logging (or write to stderr; foreground diagnostics nice). Keep a quiet default: silence to avoid noise; banner is enough. Hmm — foreground debugging benefits from logs... but spawned daemons have DEVNULL stderr anyway. Silence.\n\n`/shutdown` handling: respond 200 `{\"ok\": true, \"bye\": true}` then request shutdown from a thread:\n```python\nthreading.Thread(target=self.server.shutdown, daemon=True).start()\n```\nCalling server.shutdown() from another thread stops serve_forever. Also clear undo stack (it dies with process anyway).\n\nIdle watchdog:\n```python\ndef _watchdog(self):\n    deadline_granularity = 0.5\n    while not self._stop.is_set():\n        self._stop.wait(0.5)\n        if self._stop.is_set(): break\n        with self._lock:\n            idle_for = time.monotonic() - self._last_activity\n            busy = self._busy > 0\n        if not busy and idle_for >= self.idle:\n            self.server.shutdown()   # different thread than serve_forever: safe\n            break\n```\n\nWait: server.shutdown() stops serve_forever loop; then main proceeds to cleanup. Note _last_activity init at startup.\n\nBusy counter: increment around command execution (with lock) so idle check doesn't kill mid-request (idle 600 anyway, but --idle 1 in tests could race a slow grep). Good.\n\nRequest handling updates `daemon.touch()` (sets _last_activity = monotonic) at start of each request.\n\nSignal handling:\n```python\ndef _term(self, signum, frame):\n    self._stop.set()   # watchdog notices and shuts the server down\n```\nRegister SIGTERM and SIGINT. On SIGINT default behavior would raise KeyboardInterrupt in serve_forever — with our handler, we just stop. Watchdog poll 0.5s → responsive enough.\n\nHmm, edge: watchdog calls server.shutdown() then breaks; main thread after serve_forever: server.server_close(); discovery.remove(workspace); print bye to stdout.\n\nMain:\n```python\ndef main(argv=None) -> int:\n    parser ... --workspace required, --protect action=append default=[], --idle type=float default=600.0, --port type=int default=0\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\n    ws = Path(args.workspace)\n    if not ws.is_dir(): print error to stderr; return EX_NOINPUT\n    os.chdir(ws)\n    ...\n    httpd = ThreadingHTTPServer((\"127.0.0.1\", args.port), handler)\n    port = httpd.server_address[1]\n    token = secrets.token_hex(16)\n    daemon = Daemon(...)\n    Handler.daemon_ref = daemon\n    discovery.write(ws, port, token, os.getpid())\n    print(banner, flush=True)\n    start watchdog thread\n    try: httpd.serve_forever()\n    finally: httpd.server_close(); discovery.remove(ws)\n    return 0\n```\n\nThe banner: `clutch-workspace daemon ready: workspace=<ws> port=<port> pid=<pid> idle=<n>s` then when stopping: `clutch-workspace daemon stopped: workspace=<ws>`.\n\nDiscovery file contains absolute workspace (resolved). Note discovery.write needs the resolved workspace string; use str(ws.resolve()).\n\nOne race: two daemons started concurrently for same workspace (double lazy-start) — \"last-writer-wins discovery, loser idles out\" ratified. Fine.\n\n**Fence implementation in daemon**:\n\n```python\ndef _fence_violation(self, path: str) -> str | None:\n    \"\"\"The --protect glob that fences this path, or None. Matched against the\n    path as given, its basename, and its CWD-relative form (so 'secret*',\n    '*.env' and 'secrets/*' all behave as written). Mutation-only for now:\n    write_file / edit_file / undo refuse; reads still see fenced files\n    (which fences move is carry-over ①, still pending).\"\"\"\n    if not self.protect: return None\n    p = filesystem._resolve(path)  # hmm private...\n```\n\nUgh, need rel form. `_rel` is private too. I now have previous_content/restore as public seams; for fence I need resolve + rel. Options: make fence matching in filesystem.py too? A `is_fenced(path, globs)`? Fence is daemon policy; filesystem is pure capability (frozen \"pure capability\" cuts: \"no protected set\"). So fence lives in daemon. I'll add public helpers to filesystem... that erodes the pure-capability freeze? previous_content/restore are capability-neutral IO helpers, fine. resolve/rel are also generic.\n\nCleaner: daemon computes rel via `Path.cwd()` (daemon chdir'd to workspace):\n```python\ngiven = path\nbase = os.path.normcase(given)\nname = os.path.basename(given.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\"))\ntry: rel = os.path.relpath(resolved, os.getcwd())\n```\nHmm, getting messy with separators. Windows paths: given could be \"secret.txt\", \"sub\\\\x.txt\", absolute. Globs written unix-style \"secrets/*\". fnmatch on Windows normalizes case but not separators: fnmatch(\"secrets\\\\x.txt\", \"secrets/*\") → False on Windows (fnmatch translates * to .* and / stays /; normcase lowercases but keeps backslash). So match against multiple candidate strings with BOTH separator flavors:\n\n```python\ncandidates = {given, name}\ntry:\n    r = str(p.relative_to(Path.cwd().resolve()))  # like filesystem._rel\nexcept ValueError:\n    r = str(p)\ncandidates |= {r, r.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\"), r.replace(\"/\", \"\\\\\")}\nfor g in self.protect:\n    if any(fnmatch.fnmatch(c, g) for c in candidates): return g\n```\n\nHmm wait, `fnmatch.fnmatch` on POSIX is case-sensitive, on Windows case-insensitive (normcase). Good — platform-flavored, matches host fnmatch use in grep --include.\n\nTo avoid reimplementing rel, just import the private helpers? Same package, module-private convention... I said filesystem keeps `_resolve`/`_rel` private; the daemon importing `filesystem._rel` would work but private cross-module import within a package is acceptable-ish. I'd rather keep daemon self-contained with os.path logic. Let me write in daemon:\n\n```python\ndef _candidates(path: str) -> list[str]:\n    p = filesystem._resolve(path)\n```\n\nOK decision: I'll add ONE more tiny public function to filesystem.py? No — keep it simple: in daemon, use `Path(path).resolve()`… wait that differs from _resolve (which keeps final component unresolved, resolves parent). For FENCE matching, resolving fully is fine (even better: matches the real target). Fence matching doesn't need _resolve's exact semantics. Daemon:\n\n```python\ndef _match_candidates(path: str) -> set[str]:\n    \"\"\"Every string form a --protect glob may reasonably match: the path as\n    given, its basename, and the CWD-relative form in both separator flavors.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        resolved = Path(path).resolve()\n    except OSError:\n        resolved = Path(os.path.abspath(path))\n    out = {path, os.path.basename(path.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\"))}\n    try:\n        rel = str(resolved.relative_to(Path.cwd().resolve()))\n    except ValueError:\n        rel = str(resolved)\n    out |= {rel, rel.replace(\"\\\\\", \"/\"), rel.replace(\"/\", \"\\\\\")}\n    return out\n```\n\nHmm, Path.cwd() when daemon chdir'd = workspace. Good.\n\nBasename of \"secrets/\" → \"\" — harmless.\n\n### `clutch_workspace/client.py`\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Thin-client plumbing: find (or lazily start) the workspace daemon, forward\nthe command over HTTP, and map the response back onto the CLI's contract.\n...\nFallback policy (ratified): if no daemon is reachable when the command\narrives, start one once and wait up to LAZY_START_SECONDS for it; if that\nfails — no workspace dir, spawn failed, port lost, whatever — run the\ncommand directly in this process and nobody notices. A daemon that dies\nMID-command is different: the command may have half-happened, so re-running\nit locally is not safe (edit_file is not idempotent); that surfaces as 74.\n\"\"\"\n```\n\nConstants: LAZY_START_SECONDS = 10.0, PROBE_TIMEOUT = 2.0, COMMAND_TIMEOUT = 600.0? Hmm idle is 600; command timeout should exceed any command: grep on huge tree... give 3600? A hung daemon would hang the CLI for an hour — bad. 120s? Direct exec has no timeout at all, so a forward timeout that falls back... we DON'T fall back mid-command (not safe). So timeout → error 74. A legit long grep (>120s) would then fail where direct exec would succeed. Compromise: 300s. Document. Hmm... Actually socket timeout applies per socket op, not total; a slow-but-progressing grep keeps sending data... with HTTP/1.0 single response, urllib read() blocks until complete — timeout applies to individual blocking calls. grep computes the WHOLE result then responds, so no bytes flow during compute → timeout kills it. 300s covers reasonable greps. OK: COMMAND_TIMEOUT = 300.0.\n\nFunctions:\n\n```python\nclass DaemonDown(Exception): ...  # internal? maybe not needed\n\ndef enabled(explicit_no: bool) -> bool  # --no-server or env kill switch\n```\n\nActually simpler: CLI computes `use_server = not args.no_server and os.environ.get(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"\") not in (\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\")`.\n\n```python\ndef endpoint(workspace: str, lazy: bool = True) -> tuple[str, str] | None:\n    \"\"\"(base_url, token) for this workspace's daemon, or None.\n\n    Probe first; with lazy=True start one and poll when absent.\"\"\"\n    rec = discovery.read(workspace)\n    if rec and _healthy(rec): return _base(rec), rec[\"token\"]\n    if not lazy: return None\n    return _start_and_wait(workspace)\n\ndef _healthy(rec) -> bool: try GET /health token timeout PROBE → json ok\n```\n\nHmm, if discovery exists, pid alive, but health fails (crashed between write and bind? daemon binds before writing discovery — actually write discovery AFTER bind, so if discovery exists, port is live) → then health should succeed; if not (firewall? token mismatch?), remove stale file? If health fails while pid alive: possibly a DIFFERENT process reused the pid. Clean up: remove discovery, then try lazy start.\n\n_start_and_wait:\n```python\ndef _start_and_wait(workspace: str) -> tuple[str, str] | None:\n    try:\n        _spawn(workspace)\n    except OSError:\n        return None\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + LAZY_START_SECONDS\n    while time.monotonic() < deadline:\n        rec = discovery.read(workspace)\n        if rec and _healthy(rec):\n            return _base(rec), rec[\"token\"]\n        if not _spawning_is_alive...  # can't know child; just poll\n        time.sleep(0.1)\n    return None\n```\n\nHmm — keep the Popen handle to detect early death? If daemon dies instantly (bad args), polling full 10s wastes time. Keep handle: child = Popen(...); poll: if child.poll() is not None → return None immediately. But we must ensure the child is reaped. Popen object garbage-collected... keeping the handle in the CLI process is fine; process exits after command. But wait — if CLI exits while daemon child still running: on POSIX, daemon is in new session; fine. On Windows DETACHED_PROCESS; fine. But Popen handle with __del__ warns about \"subprocess still running\"? No warning for that; only ResourceWarning if not waited on GC... Actually Popen.__del__ warns ResourceWarning if process still running when GC'd (\"subprocess is still running\"). In a CLI that exits immediately — harmless (warnings off by default... ResourceWarning shown only with -W). Tests might turn warnings on? pytest shows ResourceWarnings! The CLI runs as subprocess — its warnings don't reach pytest. Fine.\n\nBut hold on: waiting vs not — if we DON'T wait and CLI exits, on Windows the child is detached, no issue. OK keep handle, and to be tidy `child.returncode`... skip. Keep simple: store handle locally; when polling loop exits with success, just leave it (daemon must keep running — do NOT wait()).\n\nHmm, ResourceWarning inside the daemon-spawning CLI process at exit could print \"ResourceWarning: subprocess is still running\" to stderr with -X dev? Our conftest runs `python -m clutch_workspace` without -W dev → hidden. OK.\n\n_spawn:\n```python\ndef _spawn(workspace: str):\n    kwargs = dict(\n        stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,\n        cwd=workspace,   # daemon also chdir's; belt and braces\n    )\n    cmd = [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace.daemon\", \"--workspace\", workspace]\n    if os.name == \"nt\":\n        flags = subprocess.DETACHED_PROCESS | subprocess.CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP\n        kwargs[\"creationflags\"] = flags\n    else:\n        kwargs[\"start_new_session\"] = True\n    return subprocess.Popen(cmd, **kwargs)\n```\n\nDETACHED_PROCESS exists in subprocess module? `subprocess.DETACHED_PROCESS` — yes, Windows-only attr (3.7+). CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP yes.\n\nNote: env inherited → CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR + NO_SERVER propagate to daemon (daemon doesn't care about NO_SERVER; discovery dir matters only for client... daemon writes discovery via discovery module too! So the env var must match between CLI and daemon — inherited env guarantees it. Tests set it in os.environ before spawning CLI → CLI passes to daemon.\n\nforward:\n```python\ndef forward(base, token, command: str, payload: dict) -> tuple[int, dict]:\n    \"\"\"POST one command; (http_status, envelope_dict).\"\"\"\n    req = urllib.request.Request(\n        f\"{base}{command}\",\n        data=json.dumps(payload).encode(\"utf-8\"),\n        headers={\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\", \"X-Clutch-Token\": token},\n        method=\"POST\",\n    )\n    try:\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=COMMAND_TIMEOUT) as resp:\n            return resp.status, json.loads(resp.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:\n        body = e.read().decode(\"utf-8\", \"replace\")\n        try: env = json.loads(body)\n        except ValueError: env = {\"content\": body or e.reason, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\"}\n        return e.code, env\n```\n\nAnd a `shutdown(base, token)` helper for tests: POST /shutdown ignore errors.\n\nAlso `stop(workspace)` convenience? For tests, direct HTTP is fine.\n\nCLI mapping — in cli.py:\n\n```python\ndef main(argv=None):\n    ...parse...\n    try:\n        _check_args(args)\n        result, code = _route(args)   # ← replaces direct _dispatch\n        ...\n```\n\nRestructure: build payload + command name from args; if server enabled → try client:\n\n```python\ndef _run(args) -> Result:\n    \"\"\"One command, through the daemon when there is one (or can be one),\n    direct otherwise. Byte-identical output either way: the daemon answers\n    in envelopes and the client re-emits through the same emit path.\"\"\"\n    payload, fn = _prepare(args)  # reads stdin payloads, validates combos\n    ws = args.workspace or os.getcwd()\n    if _server_enabled(args):\n        ep = client.endpoint(ws, lazy=True)\n        if ep is not None:\n            base, token = ep\n            status, env = client.forward(base, token, \"/\" + args.command, payload)\n            if status == 200 and not env.get(\"error\"):\n                return Result(env.get(\"content\", \"\"), env.get(\"diff\", \"\"))\n            if status not in (400, 403, 404, 500, 502, 503):  # contract code from the daemon\n                raise CommandError(status, env.get(\"content\", \"daemon error\"))\n            # 400/403/404/5xx: protocol-level trouble, not a command verdict\n            # → fall through to direct execution (403/404 also cleans discovery)\n            client.drop(ws)  # hmm — only for 403/404\n    os.chdir(ws)   # direct execution honors --workspace too\n    return _dispatch_payload(fn, payload)  # or keep _dispatch(args)\n```\n\nHmm wait, careful — payload reading (stdin `-`) must happen ONCE and before forwarding. Current `_dispatch` calls `_payload(...)` inline. If I forward first and fall back to `_dispatch(args)`, `_payload` reads stdin again — drained → empty! So refactor: read payloads once in _prepare, then either forward or call filesystem directly with values.\n\nLet me restructure cleanly:\n\n```python\ndef _prepare(args) -> tuple[str, dict]:\n    \"\"\"Command name + HTTP payload, with stdin payloads drained exactly once.\"\"\"\n    if args.command == \"read_file\":\n        return \"read_file\", {\"path\": args.path, \"offset\": args.offset, \"limit\": args.limit, \"max_chars\": args.max_chars}\n    if args.command == \"grep\":\n        return \"grep\", {\"pattern\": args.pattern, \"path\": args.path, \"include\": args.include}\n    if args.command == \"write_file\":\n        return \"write_file\", {\"path\": args.path, \"content\": _payload(args.content)}\n    if args.command == \"edit_file\":\n        if args.old_string == \"-\" and args.new_string == \"-\":\n            raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"only one of --old-string/--new-string may read stdin ('-')\")\n        return \"edit_file\", {\"path\": args.path, \"old_string\": _payload(args.old_string), \"new_string\": _payload(args.new_string)}\n    raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, f\"unknown command: {args.command}\")\n\n_EXECUTE = {\n    \"read_file\": lambda a: filesystem.read_file(a[\"path\"], offset=a[\"offset\"], limit=a[\"limit\"], max_chars=a[\"max_chars\"]),\n    \"grep\": lambda a: filesystem.grep(a[\"pattern\"], path=a[\"path\"], include=a[\"include\"]),\n    \"write_file\": lambda a: filesystem.write_file(a[\"path\"], content=a[\"content\"]),\n    \"edit_file\": lambda a: filesystem.edit_file(a[\"path\"], old_string=a[\"old_string\"], new_string=a[\"new_string\"]),\n}\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```python\ndef _execute(args) -> Result:\n    command, payload = _prepare(args)\n    if _server_enabled(args):\n        served = _via_daemon(args, command, payload)\n        if served is not None:\n            return served\n    _to_workspace(args)          # os.chdir, direct path honors --workspace too\n    return _EXECUTE[command](payload)\n```\n\n_via_daemon:\n```python\ndef _via_daemon(args, command, payload) -> Result | None:\n    ws = args.workspace or os.getcwd()\n    if not os.path.isdir(ws):\n        return None                      # no workspace, no daemon: keep direct semantics (74 etc.)\n    ep = client.endpoint(ws)\n    if ep is None:\n        return None                      # silent fallback (ratified)\n    base, token = ep\n    status, env = client.forward(base, token, command, payload)\n    if status == 200 and not env.get(\"error\"):\n        return Result(env.get(\"content\", \"\"), env.get(\"diff\", \"\"))\n    if status in _CONTRACT_CODES:        # the daemon's verdict for THIS command\n        raise CommandError(status, env.get(\"content\", \"command failed\"))\n    # protocol-level trouble (403 bad token, 404, 5xx): the daemon is not\n    # usable — forget it and run the command directly, like its absence.\n    client.forget(ws)\n    return None\n```\n\n_CONTRACT_CODES = {EX_USAGE, EX_DATAERR, EX_NOINPUT, EX_SOFTWARE, EX_IOERR, EX_NOPERM(77), 130?}. Daemon never returns 130. Just: `if status in (64, 65, 66, 70, 74, 77): raise CommandError(...)`. But EX_SOFTWARE(70) from daemon → re-raise locally as CommandError(70, content) → exit 70. Good — bug stays 70.\n\nHmm wait, what about status==200 with env error=true? Defensive: treat as CommandError(EX_SOFTWARE, content).\n\n forget(ws): remove discovery file (stale/wrong token). But careful — 403 could ALSO mean a foreign daemon we shouldn't kill... we just remove OUR discovery read — discovery.read is keyed by workspace hash, so removing it makes the next invocation lazy-start a fresh daemon. The running foreign daemon keeps serving its real clients (which have the right token... they read the same discovery file — hmm, if token mismatches, NOBODY has the right token; file is corrupt/stale → removing is right).\n\nBUT careful: if 403 happens because another daemon for the same workspace was restarted with a new token and our CLI read a cached... no caching; each invocation reads fresh. OK.\n\nAlso 5xx: daemon bug — forget + direct? A 500 means daemon-side crash for THIS command; direct re-execution is safe (command didn't complete... unless partial write happened then crashed — write_file crashing after write_text? Then undo push didn't happen; direct re-exec of write_file is idempotent → fine; edit_file: if the crash happened after write_text, direct re-exec errors \"not found\"... edge of an edge. Accept: forget + direct). Hmm, actually simpler and safer for 5xx: raise CommandError(70)? Let me think about which is better UX. A daemon 500 = daemon bug; falling back to direct gives the user their result (good) and the daemon stays broken for future calls... every call pays connect+fail+fallback cost until idle-out. forget() removes discovery → next call lazy-starts a fresh daemon. That self-heals. OK: 5xx → forget + direct fallback.\n\nNow --workspace flag + chdir. Where? In `_execute` direct path: os.chdir(ws). But ALSO when forwarding with --workspace: the daemon chdir'd itself; client process doesn't need chdir. But diff headers show CWD-relative paths — daemon's CWD = workspace → rel paths relative to workspace. Direct exec with --workspace: chdir first → same. Consistent.\n\nWait, but existing behavior: no --workspace → ws = CWD → chdir(CWD) = no-op. Existing tests unaffected.\n\nEdge: os.chdir(ws) failing (permissions) → OSError → caught in main → EX_IOERR(74). Reasonable.\n\nWhere does `_check_args` fit? Before _prepare. Keep.\n\nNow argparse additions:\n\n```python\nparser.add_argument(\"--workspace\", default=\"\", metavar=\"DIR\", help=\"run against DIR instead of the CWD (the daemon is keyed on it; direct execution chdirs there)\")\nparser.add_argument(\"--no-server\", action=\"store_true\", help=\"skip the workspace daemon entirely (always execute in-process; also CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER=1)\")\n```\n\nMust be on the TOP-level parser (before subcommand) per contract \"global flags precede the subcommand\".\n\n--version output: \"clutch-workspace 0.1.0\" → bump to 0.2.0? Both __init__ and pyproject. Also update version help. Minor bump.\n\nenv kill switch helper — put in client.py:\n\n```python\ndef disabled_by_env() -> bool:\n    return os.environ.get(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"\").strip().lower() in (\"1\", \"true\", \"yes\", \"on\")\n```\n\ncli._server_enabled(args) = not args.no_server and not client.disabled_by_env().\n\n**daemon.py command mapping** — payload → filesystem:\n\n```python\n_ROUTES = {\"read_file\": ..., \"grep\": ..., \"write_file\": ..., \"edit_file\": ...}\n```\n\nFor write/edit with undo:\n\n```python\ndef _write(self, a):\n    self._fence(a[\"path\"])\n    before = filesystem.previous_content(a[\"path\"])\n    res = filesystem.write_file(a[\"path\"], content=a[\"content\"])\n    self._remember(a[\"path\"], before)\n    return res\n\ndef _edit(self, a):\n    self._fence(a[\"path\"])\n    before = filesystem.previous_content(a[\"path\"])\n    res = filesystem.edit_file(a[\"path\"], old_string=a[\"old_string\"], new_string=a[\"new_string\"])\n    self._remember(a[\"path\"], before)\n    return res\n\ndef _remember(self, path, before):\n    existed = before is not None\n    self.undo.append({\"path\": path, \"before\": before, \"existed\": existed})\n    if len(self.undo) > _UNDO_MAX: self.undo.pop(0)\n```\n\nHmm wait — edit_file on missing file raises CommandError(NOINPUT) → no push (correct: push only after success; `_remember` after the call = only on success).\n\nundo:\n```python\ndef _undo(self, a):\n    if not self.undo:\n        raise CommandError(EX_USAGE, \"nothing to undo\")\n    entry = self.undo.pop()\n    self._fence(entry[\"path\"])\n    summary = filesystem.restore(entry[\"path\"], entry[\"before\"])\n    return Result(summary)\n```\n\nundo route payload: ignore (empty dict). Client's forward for undo: `client.undo(base, token)`.\n\nAlso undo of an edit where file was deleted by user meanwhile: restore writes it back — fine.\n\nUndo of write that created parents: undo deletes only the file, not parents — fine.\n\n_fence:\n```python\ndef _fence(self, path: str) -> None:\n    if not self.protect: return\n    for cand in _candidates(path):\n        for g in self.protect:\n            if fnmatch.fnmatch(cand, g):\n                raise CommandError(EX_NOPERM, f\"path is protected (--protect {g}): {path}\")\n```\n\nNow daemon HTTP handler with lock + busy counter + touch:\n\n```python\nclass _Handler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    daemon: \"Daemon | None\" = None   # wired in Daemon.__init__\n\n    def do_GET(self):\n        d = self.daemon\n        d.touch()\n        if self.path != \"/health\":\n            return self._respond(404, _err(f\"unknown path: {self.path}\"))\n        if not self._authorized(): return\n        self._respond(200, {\"ok\": True, \"workspace\": d.workspace, \"pid\": d.pid, \"undo\": len(d.undo)})\n\n    def do_POST(self):\n        d = self.daemon\n        d.touch()\n        if not self._authorized(): return\n        route = d.route(self.path)  # None → 404\n        ...\n        try: length = int(self.headers.get(\"Content-Length\", 0)); body = self.rfile.read(length)\n        except: 400\n        try: payload = json.loads(body or b\"{}\")\n        except ValueError: return 400\n        if not isinstance(payload, dict): 400\n        with d.lock:\n            d.busy += 1\n        try:\n            res = route(payload)   # may raise CommandError\n        except CommandError as e:\n            return self._respond(e.code, _err(e.message))\n        except Exception as e:\n            traceback.print_exc()\n            return self._respond(EX_SOFTWARE, _err(f\"internal error: {e}\"))\n        finally:\n            with d.lock: d.busy -= 1\n        self._respond(200, _ok(res))\n```\n\nHmm — `d.touch()` inside handler thread; fine (monotonic assignment atomic-ish; GIL).\n\nWait, order: authorize BEFORE reading body for 403? If unauthorized we respond 403 without reading body — then connection closes with unread body → client gets 403 fine. OK.\n\n_authorized:\n```python\ndef _authorized(self) -> bool:\n    token = self.headers.get(\"X-Clutch-Token\", \"\")\n    if hmac.compare_digest(token, self.daemon.token): return True\n    self._respond(403, _err(\"bad or missing token\"))\n    return False\n```\n\nhmac.compare_digest with str args: both must be ASCII str — header could contain non-ASCII → TypeError! compare_digest accepts str only if both ASCII. Header values are latin-1 decoded; non-ASCII possible → wrap in try. Safer: encode both to bytes:\n```python\nhmac.compare_digest(token.encode(\"utf-8\", \"replace\"), self.daemon.token.encode())\n```\nGood.\n\nRoute map: {\"/read_file\": d.read_cmd, \"/grep\": ..., \"/write_file\": ..., \"/edit_file\": ..., \"/undo\": ..., \"/shutdown\": d._shutdown_cmd}. Unknown → 404.\n\nShutdown route executed under lock? It responds then triggers shutdown from a thread — don't hold the lock while calling server.shutdown (deadlock risk: shutdown waits for serve_forever loop, not handlers... ThreadingHTTPServer: serve_forever loop just accepts; shutdown() stops the accept loop; in-flight handler threads keep going (daemon_threads). So shutdown from within a handler thread is SAFE (the docs' warning is about calling shutdown() from the same thread that runs serve_forever). Actually docs: \"shutdown() must be called while serve_forever() is running in another thread, or it will deadlock\" — meaning calling it FROM the serve_forever thread deadlocks. A handler thread is a different thread → safe. So:\n\n```python\ndef _shutdown_cmd(self, a):\n    threading.Thread(target=self.server.shutdown, daemon=True).start()\n    return Result(\"OK: shutting down\")\n```\n\nBut wait — after self.server.shutdown() stops the accept loop, can we still _respond? Respond happens AFTER route returns: sequence: route spawns shutdown-thread (async), returns Result, handler responds 200. The shutdown thread stops accept loop — in-flight response unaffected (socket already accepted). Good. Race: shutdown thread wins before response written? Response write goes to the already-accepted connection socket — server_close() closes the LISTENING socket, not accepted connections. But main thread after serve_forever calls server_close() then exits process → process exit kills in-flight handler responses possibly! Add a small join: main after server_close should wait briefly for the shutdown case? Simplify: in main, after serve_forever returns: `httpd.server_close()`, discovery.remove, print stopped. The client that called /shutdown doesn't care about the response body much, but our own client.shutdown() does urlopen which may get connection-reset → we ignore errors in shutdown helper. For tests, tolerate. Better: make /shutdown respond FIRST, then trigger shutdown — can't easily with the route-return shape. Alternative: make shutdown handler special-cased in do_POST: respond 200 immediately, then spawn thread. Let me special-case:\n\n```python\nif self.path == \"/shutdown\":\n    self._respond(200, {\"ok\": True})\n    threading.Thread(target=self.server.shutdown, daemon=True).start()\n    return\n```\nRespond then shutdown — clean. (Still token-gated, still under... no lock needed.)\n\nEdge: daemon_threads=True means process exit won't hang on handler threads. Good.\n\n**Watchdog and shutdown**: after serve_forever, set self._stop so watchdog exits.\n\n**Test plan concretely**:\n\nNew test file `tests/test_daemon.py`. Needs:\n- env overrides: CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR → tmp_path/\"disc\"; CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER removed/0.\n- helper to spawn daemon: `subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"clutch_workspace.daemon\", \"--workspace\", str(ws), \"--idle\", \"600\", *protect_flags], env=..., stdout=PIPE, stderr=PIPE)` — foreground mode for tests so we can read banner? Banner on stdout — read line to know readiness (or poll discovery file). Poll discovery_file for existence + health (up to 10s). Then teardown: POST /shutdown, wait with timeout, kill fallback.\n- fixture `daemon(tmp_path, monkeypatch)` → returns namespace(ws, base, token, ...).\n\nTests:\n1. `test_daemon_forwards_four_commands_identically` — run each command via CLI with server enabled and compare envelope to direct run (direct = with --no-server). Compare stdout bytes + exit codes. This is the byte-identical guarantee test.\n2. `test_lazy_start_and_reuse` — no daemon running; run CLI write_file with NO_SERVER=0 → succeeds; discovery file exists; health says pid X; run read_file → hits same daemon (assert health pid unchanged and undo count 1 — proves same daemon). Then POST /shutdown → discovery file gone.\n3. `test_no_server_flag_runs_direct` — with NO_SERVER unset(=1 via conftest default) works (already covered by 64 tests) — plus `--no-server` with NO_SERVER=0 still direct: assert discovery file NOT created.\n4. `test_fence_blocks_mutation_with_77` — daemon with --protect \"secret*\" \"secrets/*\"; CLI write_file --path secret.txt → exit 77, ERROR mentions protect; write other.txt → 0. Also edit_file on fenced file → 77. (edit needs existing file: create via direct first.)\n5. `test_undo_over_http` — via client functions (import clutch_workspace.client in test — conftest allows imports for plumbing): write a.txt (v1), overwrite (v2), POST /undo → content back to v1; undo again → file deleted; undo again → exit 64 \"nothing to undo\" (HTTP 64). Fence: undo of fenced path → 77? e.g. write secret.txt twice then undo → 77 and file stays v2. Nice.\n6. `test_idle_suicide` — daemon --idle 1; wait for discovery; sleep ~2.5s; discovery file gone; process exited (poll() not None).\n7. `test_stale_discovery_cleanup` — create discovery file manually with dead pid (spawn `python -c \"import time;time.sleep(0)\"`? just take pid of a finished subprocess) + fake port/token; CLI with NO_SERVER=0 → runs direct, discovery file removed. Assert file gone + command output correct.\n8. `test_workspace_flag_direct` — CLI --workspace subdir with NO_SERVER=1: write_file --path f.txt lands in subdir. And with daemon too? Lazy-start on subdir then read via second CLI call with --workspace subdir → sees file. Good coverage of workspace keying.\n9. `test_corrupt_discovery_falls_back` — discovery file with garbage bytes → CLI direct works, file replaced?? read() removes corrupt file → lazy start spawns new daemon... With NO_SERVER=0: lazy start happens → daemon starts, discovery rewritten. Assert command OK (either way) — and maybe just assert direct with --no-server + corrupt file works.\n\nAlso client unit tests? Keep to blackbox + a couple of client-module tests (discovery key stability, pid_alive on own pid = True).\n\nHow do tests call HTTP? Via `clutch_workspace.client` helpers (forward/shutdown) — but tests are \"black-box, no imports of the module under test for behavior, only constants\". Using client.forward for SHUTDOWN plumbing is infrastructure, acceptable; but test assertions should go through CLI output. For undo (no CLI), HTTP via client.forward is the only way — fine, it's the documented HTTP surface. I'll import client + discovery in tests for plumbing and assertions about discovery state.\n\nByte-identical check details: CLI direct vs forwarded:\n- read_file output identical.\n- write_file: diff header paths CWD-relative → daemon CWD = workspace = tmp_path; CLI direct CWD = tmp_path → same.\n- grep same.\n- Exit codes same.\nUse run(..., ) with and without --no-server and compare .stdout/.returncode exactly.\n\nOne wrinkle: when server is ENABLED for tmp_path, lazy start happens on first command — that's what we want (test 2). For test 1, first run enables + starts daemon; subsequent runs reuse.\n\nAlso need `--workspace` NOT passed (default CWD=tmp_path) so discovery key matches CWD — run() fixture already uses cwd=tmp_path.\n\nTiming: lazy start poll 10s; CI slow? Daemon starts in ~100-300ms. Fine.\n\nWindows specifics: subprocess.Popen of daemon from pytest with stdout=PIPE — reading banner could block; I'll poll discovery file instead of reading pipes (avoid pipe deadlock). Teardown: shutdown via HTTP, then `proc.wait(timeout=10)`, if still alive → terminate/kill. Also pytest tmp_path cleanup with running daemon on Windows → file locks! Must ensure daemon dead before tmp teardown: fixture teardown does shutdown + wait + kill. Also the IDLE watchdog (600s) keeps it alive until killed. And discovery dir also under tmp_path → cleaned with it.\n\nBut test 2's lazy-started daemon (spawned BY the CLI subprocess, detached): CLI exits, daemon lingers (idle 600) with discovery in tmp discovery dir and CWD in tmp workspace → pytest tmp cleanup on Windows fails to remove locked/attached dirs? The daemon's CWD inside tmp_path → Windows can't delete a directory that is a process's CWD! pytest tmp_path cleanup... does pytest clean tmp dirs eagerly? It retains last 3 runs, garbage-cleans older ones LATER (not during the run, mostly). Risk of unraisable cleanup errors in FUTURE runs, not this one. Hmm, but retained dirs from THIS run with live daemons holding CWD → next pytest run's cleanup could error.\n\nMitigation: daemon tests that lazy-start should shut the daemon down at the end via HTTP /shutdown (test does POST /shutdown and asserts discovery gone). But if the test FAILS midway, daemon leaks. Add a safety: session-scoped autouse fixture that at session end sweeps the test discovery dir: read all d-*.json in the test discovery dir, POST /shutdown to each. Plus try/except around everything. Also set the daemons' idle low? Can't control lazy-start flags... CLI lazy start uses DEFAULT idle (600) — no flag pass-through. So sweep fixture it is. Also on Windows, daemon holding CWD in tmp_path: after /shutdown it exits → released. Sweep at session end handles leaks.\n\nHmm — should CLI expose lazy-start idle config? No, keep minimal.\n\nAlso: daemon spawned detached inherits env including CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR=tmp discovery dir — good.\n\nOne more: sweep fixture needs the same discovery dir as tests used → monkeypatch.setenv per test; sweep fixture can't know... Make a session-scoped env: set CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR to a session tmp dir via monkeypatch in a session fixture (monkeypatch is function-scoped; use `pytest.MonkeyPatch` manually in session fixture with `.context()`? Simpler: session autouse fixture using os.environ directly + finally restore). Then all daemon tests share one discovery dir; conftest._run copies os.environ → children see it; sweep reads the same dir.\n\nAnd NO_SERVER: session fixture must NOT set it globally (existing tests rely on conftest._run's setdefault=1). Daemon tests: `monkeypatch.delenv(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", raising=False)` or set \"0\".\n\nCareful — conftest._run: `env.setdefault(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"1\")` — daemon tests set os.environ value \"0\" → setdefault won't override \"0\" → server enabled. Existing tests: os.environ unset → \"1\" → direct.\n\nNow the conftest edit: add `env.setdefault(\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_NO_SERVER\", \"1\")` — 1-line change, existing 64 tests unaffected behaviorally (they were direct before; now still direct).\n\n**Version**: bump __init__ to 0.2.0, pyproject version 0.2.0. Also description mention daemon? pyproject description: \"Standalone file-inspection CLI: read_file / grep / write_file / edit_file as plain flags-in, text-out commands.\" → append \"; optional per-workspace daemon\". Minor. Also add `[project.urls]`? No.\n\nIs there a README in clutch-workspace? Let me check root listing. If there's a README, update a section (the readme-crafter skill — should I load it? The task is implementing a daemon, not writing a README. If a README exists, a small doc update is fine without the full skill. Let me check).\n\n**Commit plan** (ratified style):\n1. `feat: workspace daemon + thin client (lazy start, --no-server, undo, fence)` — code.\n2. `test: daemon dual-mode suite` — tests + conftest env line. Hmm, conftest env line is test infra → goes with test commit. But feat commit without test commit would FAIL the 64 tests? No: with conftest unchanged (no NO_SERVER default), existing tests would lazy-start daemons during CI → slow/flaky. Order matters: put conftest change IN the feat commit? Then commit 1 is green and commit 2 adds tests. Hmm, style from M1: separate feat/test commits. To keep every commit green: feat commit includes the conftest kill-switch line (infra, not tests). I'll do that and note it. Actually — wait: is feat-commit-green a rule here? Memory says M1 committed feat then test. To be safe, keep both commits green: feat commit includes conftest one-liner. Fine.\n\nLet me also double check `_payload` reading order vs `_check_args`: unchanged.\n\nPotential gotcha — `client.endpoint` probe uses urllib GET /health with token header. Build:\n\n```python\ndef _health(base, token):\n    req = urllib.request.Request(f\"{base}/health\", headers={\"X-Clutch-Token\": token})\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=PROBE_TIMEOUT) as resp:\n        return json.loads(resp.read().decode(\"utf-8\"))\n```\nHTTPError (403) raises — catch in _healthy → False.\n\nbase url: `http://127.0.0.1:{port}`.\n\nNow about `discovery.read` validating: ensure keys present and types sane: version==1, port int 1-65535, token str non-empty, pid int. If malformed → remove + None. workspace field: could verify it matches ours (hash collision impossible practically) — skip.\n\n`started`: datetime isoformat. Unused by client; informational.\n\nEdge — `discovery.write` atomicity: write to temp + os.replace to avoid a reader seeing partial JSON. Nice touch, cheap:\n```python\ntmp = final.with_suffix(\".json.tmp\")  # hmm same dir\ntmp.write_text(json.dumps(...), encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\\n\")\nos.replace(tmp, final)\n```\nConcurrent writers same tmp name → race; add pid+rand suffix: `final.with_name(final.name + f\".{os.getpid()}.tmp\")`. Then replace. Cleanup temp on failure (try/finally unlink).\n\npid_alive on own pid → True (OpenProcess succeeds, exit code STILL_ACTIVE). Good for tests.\n\nNow — daemon's `--protect` glob strings on Windows: fnmatch case-insensitive. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about **grep/read through daemon when workspace arg given to CLI**: payload path is as-given; daemon CWD = workspace → resolves under workspace. Direct: CLI chdir'd to workspace → same. Consistent.\n\nAnd `--workspace` with relative DIR: resolved by daemon: Path(args.workspace).resolve() then chdir. Client: `ws = args.workspace or os.getcwd()`; `os.path.isdir(ws)`; discovery key from normcase(resolve). Daemon gets same string → same key. Consistent.\n\nBUT: lazy start spawn with cwd=workspace and `--workspace workspace` where workspace is RELATIVE — daemon chdir'd to same cwd? Spawn cwd=ws (relative resolved against CLI's cwd) → daemon's initial cwd = intended dir; then it resolves Path(args.workspace).resolve() — relative to ITS cwd = same dir. Consistent. Still, pass ABSOLUTE workspace to spawn to be safe: `os.path.abspath(ws)`.\n\nSimilarly client.endpoint should abspath the workspace before hashing? discovery.workspace_key does resolve() internally — relative resolve against client cwd; daemon resolves same string against ITS cwd (== client cwd via spawn cwd=abspath). OK — just pass abspath everywhere from client.\n\nNow the daemon key: daemon computes discovery path from `--workspace` value it received (absolute). Client computes from abspath(ws). Same input string → same normcase+resolve → same hash. Windows normcase handles drive-letter case differences (C:\\ vs c:\\). resolve() may yield `\\\\?\\C:\\...`? Path.resolve() on py3.10 Windows returns normal form (no \\\\?\\ prefix unless long path). OK.\n\nOne more consideration: **tmp_path on Windows** = `C:\\Users\\...\\AppData\\Local\\Temp\\pytest-...` — resolve() fine. 8.3 short names (RUNNER~1) could differ between cwd string and abspath... both go through Path.resolve() → canonical. Good.\n\n**Daemon `__main__` guard** and `if __name__ == \"__main__\": raise SystemExit(main())`.\n\nAlso the daemon needs EX_NOPERM import etc.\n\nLet me now also add EX_NOPERM to exitcodes.py table comment.\n\n**README**: check existence.\n\nNow let me also double-check `filesystem.previous_content` + `restore` don't break the \"pure capability\" freeze — they're pure IO helpers, no policy. Document in module docstring? The docstring says deltas are exactly the frozen cuts; adding two helpers for undo is a small extension — mention in the commit message. The docstring itself I'll leave mostly, maybe not touch. Hmm — the file docstring says \"no protected set / snapshots / containment\". Adding previous_content/restore contradicts \"no snapshots\" literally! Update that docstring line: snapshots live in the DAEMON's memory; filesystem just exposes the primitives. Edit the docstring bullet to say so. Actually simpler: put previous_content/restore in daemon.py? They need _resolve/_read_text + write discipline... duplicating write discipline in daemon is worse. Keep in filesystem.py, adjust docstring: \"- no protected set / containment: every path is servable — the undo stack and the fence live in the daemon (daemon.py), which uses the tiny snapshot primitives at the bottom of this module\". Good.\n\nLet me write the code now. Also check tests dir file style — look at test_write.py quickly for conventions, and check README existence, git status/log. Then write:\n\n1. exitcodes.py edit (add 77)\n2. filesystem.py edit (docstring line + previous_content + restore)\n3. discovery.py (new)\n4. daemon.py (new)\n5. client.py (new)\n6. cli.py edit (flags + routing)\n7. conftest.py edit (env default)\n8. __init__/pyproject version bump\n9. tests/test_daemon.py (new)\n10. Run suite, ruff if available\n11. Commit\n\nLet me look at the repo root + test_write.py style + git log."}
