{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789900591.1723921, "id": "45fa2c64cb64", "content": "One test I just wrote is dishonest — on a fenced daemon a write can never land, so an undo entry for a fenced path can't exist. Rewriting it as the real guarantee: a refused write pushes no undo entry:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_ea4221a70d174f3cb359f07a", "name": "edit_file", "arguments": "{\"new_string\":\"def test_fenced_refusal_pushes_nothing_onto_undo(spawn, run, tmp_path):\\n    d = spawn(tmp_path, \\\"--protect\\\", \\\"fenced.txt\\\")\\n    (tmp_path / \\\"open.txt\\\").write_text(\\\"v1\\\\n\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n    proc = run(\\\"--json\\\", \\\"write_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"open.txt\\\", \\\"--content\\\", \\\"v2\\\\n\\\")\\n    assert proc.returncode == 0\\n\\n    code, _ = _forward_json(run, \\\"write_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"fenced.txt\\\", \\\"--content\\\", \\\"x\\\")\\n    assert code == EX_NOPERM, \\\"the fenced write is refused\\\"\\n\\n    status, _ = client.forward(d.base, d.token, \\\"undo\\\", {})\\n    assert status == 200\\n    assert (tmp_path / \\\"open.txt\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") == \\\"v1\\\\n\\\", \\\"undo pops the last SUCCESSFUL write\\\"\\n\\n    status, env = client.forward(d.base, d.token, \\\"undo\\\", {})\\n    assert status == EX_USAGE and \\\"nothing to undo\\\" in env[\\\"content\\\"], \\\"a refused write must leave no entry\\\"\",\"old_string\":\"def test_undo_refuses_fenced_path(spawn, run, tmp_path):\\n    d = spawn(tmp_path, \\\"--protect\\\", \\\"fenced.txt\\\")\\n    (tmp_path / \\\"fenced.txt\\\").write_text(\\\"v1\\\\n\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n    proc = run(\\\"--json\\\", \\\"write_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"fenced.txt\\\", \\\"--content\\\", \\\"v2\\\\n\\\")\\n    assert proc.returncode == 0, \\\"this daemon does not fence the path, so the write lands\\\"\\n\\n    status, env = client.forward(d.base, d.token, \\\"undo\\\", {})\\n    assert status == EX_NOPERM, \\\"the FENCED daemon's undo must refuse\\\"\\n    assert (tmp_path / \\\"fenced.txt\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") == \\\"v2\\\\n\\\", \\\"refused undo must not touch the file\\\"\",\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/tests/test_daemon.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Wait — in `test_undo_refuses_fenced_path` I spawn a daemon WITH `--protect fenced.txt`, then assert that a write via CLI lands? That's wrong! The daemon has the fence, so `run(\"--json\", \"write_file\", \"--path\", \"fenced.txt\", ...)` → 77, not 0. The scenario I want: daemon fenced → write refused 77... but then undo can't be tested because nothing was pushed.\n\nCorrect scenario for \"undo respects fence\": daemon WITHOUT fence pushed an undo entry, but then... no — the fence is static per daemon. Hmm. How can an undo entry exist for a path that's now fenced? Only if the SAME daemon fenced it at start and the write succeeded — impossible.\n\nThe real value of fencing undo: /undo targeting a fenced path must be refused even if the entry exists. To create that state, the entry must have been pushed by a daemon that wasn't fenced... different daemon, same workspace+discovery → new daemon would have own empty stack. So within one daemon it's impossible to have a fenced entry legitimately... UNLESS the fence test constructs the state differently: the fence check happens at undo time against the daemon's CURRENT globs. Since globs never change (no reconfigure endpoint), a fenced daemon can never hold an entry for a fenced path. So the fence-at-undo branch is defense-in-depth (guards a future reconfigure endpoint).\n\nSo the honest test: daemon WITH --protect fenced.txt; write via daemon → 77 (already covered by test_fence). For undo+fence, craft the state via HTTP? There's no way to push an entry without a successful write. \n\nAlternative honest test: daemon fenced; attempt /undo when stack is empty → \"nothing to undo\" (64) — not the fence branch. Hmm.\n\nOK simplest correction: the test asserts that on a FENCED daemon, after a refused write, /undo is refused/no-op and file untouched. I'll rewrite: daemon with --protect fenced.txt; write → 77 (entry NOT pushed); /undo → 64 nothing to undo; and separately, to exercise the fence branch at undo-time directly... can't without reconfigure. \n\nOption: construct via TWO daemons? Daemon A (no fence) serves, push entry by writing v2. Then /shutdown A. Start daemon B (fenced) — its stack is empty. No.\n\nSo the fence-at-undo branch is unreachable through the public surface — fine, keep the code as defense-in-depth but test what IS reachable: on a fenced daemon, a refused write leaves no entry, and undo says \"nothing to undo\" (64), file untouched. Let me rewrite that test accordingly with an honest comment.\n\nActually wait — there IS one more reachable path: the daemon is fenced with a glob that matches at UNDO time but not at WRITE time? Globs are static, but the FILESYSTEM can change: write to \"fenced.txt\" — matches. What about a path that changes identity? E.g. protect \"fenced.txt\", write to a path via a symlink that resolves differently? Overkill.\n\nRewrite:\n\n```python\ndef test_fenced_write_pushes_nothing_onto_undo(spawn, run, tmp_path):\n    d = spawn(tmp_path, \"--protect\", \"fenced.txt\")\n    (tmp_path / \"open.txt\").write_text(\"v1\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n    assert run(\"--json\", \"write_file\", \"--path\", \"open.txt\", \"--content\", \"v2\\n\").returncode == 0\n    code, env = _forward_json(run, \"write_file\", \"--path\", \"fenced.txt\", \"--content\", \"x\")\n    assert code == EX_NOPERM\n\n    status, env = client.forward(d.base, d.token, \"undo\", {})\n    assert status == 200\n    assert (tmp_path / \"open.txt\").read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\") == \"v1\\n\", \"undo pops the OPEN write, not the refused one\"\n\n    status, env = client.forward(d.base, d.token, \"undo\", {})\n    assert status == EX_USAGE and \"nothing to undo\" in env[\"content\"], \"a refused write must not leave an undo entry\"\n```\n\nThat's honest and covers the interaction. Let me replace."}
