{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789901041.771583, "id": "2a8f03960d37", "content": "Test-design bug in my `both()` helper — it deleted the file *between* the two runs instead of seeding both identically. Rewriting with explicit seeds:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_43784635ed9a417bbbc59ca1", "name": "edit_file", "arguments": "{\"new_string\":\"def test_forwarded_output_is_byte_identical_to_direct(daemon, run, tmp_path):\\n    f = tmp_path / \\\"f.txt\\\"\\n\\n    def seed_absent():\\n        f.unlink(missing_ok=True)\\n\\n    def seed_v1():\\n        f.write_text(\\\"v1\\\\n\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n\\n    def seed_v2():\\n        f.write_text(\\\"v2\\\\n\\\", encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n\\n    def both(seed, *args: str):\\n        seed()  # identical state before each leg: only the transport differs\\n        via_daemon = run(\\\"--json\\\", *args)\\n        seed()\\n        direct = run(\\\"--no-server\\\", \\\"--json\\\", *args)\\n        assert (via_daemon.returncode, via_daemon.stdout) == (direct.returncode, direct.stdout), args\\n\\n    both(seed_absent, \\\"write_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\", \\\"--content\\\", \\\"v1\\\\n\\\")\\n    assert f.read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") == \\\"v1\\\\n\\\"\\n    both(seed_v1, \\\"edit_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\", \\\"--old-string\\\", \\\"v1\\\", \\\"--new-string\\\", \\\"v2\\\")\\n    both(seed_v2, \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\")\\n    both(seed_v2, \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\", \\\"--offset\\\", \\\"1\\\", \\\"--limit\\\", \\\"1\\\")\\n    both(seed_v2, \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\".\\\")  # directory listing\\n    both(seed_v2, \\\"grep\\\", \\\"--pattern\\\", \\\"v2\\\", \\\"--include\\\", \\\"*.txt\\\")\\n    both(seed_absent, \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\")  # error verdict: 66 both ways\\n\\n    # human mode too: stdout AND the error prose on stderr must match byte-for-byte\\n    seed_absent()\\n    a = run(\\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\")\\n    b = run(\\\"--no-server\\\", \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\")\\n    assert (a.returncode, a.stdout, a.stderr) == (b.returncode, b.stdout, b.stderr)\",\"old_string\":\"def test_forwarded_output_is_byte_identical_to_direct(daemon, run, tmp_path):\\n    pairs = []\\n\\n    def both(*args: str):\\n        via_daemon = run(\\\"--json\\\", *args)\\n        reset()\\n        direct = run(\\\"--no-server\\\", \\\"--json\\\", *args)\\n        assert (via_daemon.returncode, via_daemon.stdout) == (direct.returncode, direct.stdout), args\\n        pairs.append((via_daemon.returncode, via_daemon.stdout))\\n\\n    def reset():\\n        f = tmp_path / \\\"f.txt\\\"\\n        if f.exists():\\n            f.unlink()\\n\\n    both(\\\"write_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\", \\\"--content\\\", \\\"v1\\\\n\\\")\\n    assert (tmp_path / \\\"f.txt\\\").read_text(encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\") == \\\"v1\\\\n\\\"\\n\\n    both(\\\"edit_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\", \\\"--old-string\\\", \\\"v1\\\", \\\"--new-string\\\", \\\"v2\\\")\\n    both(\\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\")\\n    both(\\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"f.txt\\\", \\\"--offset\\\", \\\"1\\\", \\\"--limit\\\", \\\"1\\\")\\n    both(\\\"grep\\\", \\\"--pattern\\\", \\\"v2\\\", \\\"--include\\\", \\\"*.txt\\\")\\n    both(\\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\")  # error verdict: 66 in both paths\\n    assert pairs[-1][0] == EX_NOINPUT\\n\\n    # human mode too: error prose on stderr must match byte-for-byte\\n    a = run(\\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\")\\n    b = run(\\\"--no-server\\\", \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\")\\n    assert (a.returncode, a.stdout, a.stderr) == (b.returncode, b.stdout, b.stderr)\",\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/tests/test_daemon.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Test-design bug, not code bug: my `both()` calls `reset()` AFTER the daemon run — which deletes f.txt — then the direct run edits a missing file → 66. The reset is meant to equalize state BEFORE each run, but for edit I need the file PRESENT with \"v1\". My reset() deletes the file unconditionally — wrong for edit/read/grep which need the file.\n\nRedesign `both()`: reset BEFORE the daemon run to a known state, then reset to the SAME state before the direct run:\n\n```python\ndef both(*args, seed=None):\n    seed()                      # state A\n    via = run(\"--json\", *args)\n    seed()                      # same state A\n    direct = run(\"--no-server\", \"--json\", *args)\n    assert equal\n```\n\nSeeds:\n- write: no file (delete f.txt)\n- edit: f.txt = \"v1\\n\"\n- read/grep: f.txt = \"v1\\nv2\\n\" — wait, after edit the content is v2. For read: seed f.txt = \"v2\\n\" then read. grep \"v2\" with seed \"v2\\n\".\n- read missing: no file.\n\nLet me rewrite the test cleanly:\n\n```python\ndef test_forwarded_output_is_byte_identical_to_direct(daemon, run, tmp_path):\n    f = tmp_path / \"f.txt\"\n\n    def seed_absent():\n        f.unlink(missing_ok=True)\n\n    def seed_v1():\n        f.write_text(\"v1\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n\n    def seed_v2():\n        f.write_text(\"v2\\n\", encoding=\"utf-8\")\n\n    def both(seed, *args):\n        seed()\n        via_daemon = run(\"--json\", *args)\n        seed()\n        direct = run(\"--no-server\", \"--json\", *args)\n        assert (via_daemon.returncode, via_daemon.stdout) == (direct.returncode, direct.stdout), args\n\n    both(seed_absent, \"write_file\", \"--path\", \"f.txt\", \"--content\", \"v1\\n\")\n    assert f.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\") == \"v1\\n\"\n    both(seed_v1, \"edit_file\", \"--path\", \"f.txt\", \"--old-string\", \"v1\", \"--new-string\", \"v2\")\n    both(seed_v2, \"read_file\", \"--path\", \"f.txt\")\n    both(seed_v2, \"read_file\", \"--path\", \"f.txt\", \"--offset\", \"1\", \"--limit\", \"1\")\n    both(seed_v2, \"grep\", \"--pattern\", \"v2\", \"--include\", \"*.txt\")\n    both(seed_absent, \"read_file\", \"--path\", \"missing.txt\")\n    ...\n```\n\nAlso directory listing: both(seed_v2, \"read_file\", \"--path\", \".\") — lists dir; tmp has f.txt only? daemon_env discovery dir is under tmp_path too! So listing includes discovery/ entry in BOTH paths — same output, fine. But wait — is discovery dir created at that point? Lazy daemon started during first write; discovery dir exists with the d-*.json → listed as \"discovery/\" in both runs → identical. OK but flaky ordering? sorted both sides — deterministic. Include it.\n\nThen human-mode + error stderr comparison at the end."}
