{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789901117.612313, "id": "f9e7bb315885", "content": "The forwarded leg returns 70 — an internal error somewhere in the daemon path. Reproducing in isolation to see the envelope:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_74049c74c90041efbd8dec6d", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cd clutch-workspace && cat > /tmp/diag_missing.py <<'EOF'\\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, tempfile\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\".\\\")\\nfrom clutch_workspace import discovery, client\\n\\nws = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\\\"diag-miss-\\\")\\nos.environ[\\\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR\\\"] = os.path.join(ws, \\\"disc\\\")\\nenv = dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH=os.getcwd())\\nproc = subprocess.run(\\n    [sys.executable, \\\"-m\\\", \\\"clutch_workspace\\\", \\\"--json\\\", \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\"],\\n    cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\", env=env,\\n)\\nprint(\\\"rc:\\\", proc.returncode)\\nprint(\\\"stdout:\\\", proc.stdout)\\nprint(\\\"stderr:\\\", proc.stderr[-2000:])\\nrecord = discovery.read(ws)\\nprint(\\\"record:\\\", record)\\nif record:\\n    client.shutdown(f\\\"http://127.0.0.1:{record['port']}\\\", record[\\\"token\\\"])\\nEOF\\n./.venv/Scripts/python /tmp/diag_missing.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "The forwarded \"read_file missing.txt\" returns 70 while direct returns 66. Let me see the content of the daemon's envelope. Something in the daemon path raises an unexpected exception → 70. What could fail for a MISSING file via the daemon... but the same request succeeded earlier in the sequence? Earlier \"both(seed_v2, read f.txt)\" passed. So the daemon is alive.\n\nHmm wait — is it the ENVELOPE content? Let me get full stdout of both. The failing assert shows content beginning '{\"conte...' truncated. Need more of the output. Let me run with -v and print. Actually quicker: reproduce via HTTP directly: daemon running? Not now. Let me add a debug run: run the CLI with server enabled reading a missing file, print stdout.\n\nHypothesis: the `both()` order — the read-missing case is the last `both()`. Before it, grep ran with seed_v2. So daemon is fine. Then read missing → daemon → filesystem.read_file raises CommandError(EX_NOINPUT) → daemon responds 66 with envelope... client maps status 66 → _CONTRACT_CODES contains 66 → CommandError(66, content) → exit 66. Why 70?\n\nUnless... EX_NOINPUT isn't in _CONTRACT_CODES! I imported: EX_USAGE, EX_DATAERR, EX_NOINPUT, EX_SOFTWARE, EX_IOERR, EX_NOPERM. Yes it's there.\n\nHmm, 70 = EX_SOFTWARE — from main's catch-all: some exception in the CLI. Which? Traceback prints to stderr! The test captured stderr. Let me run that single case manually and look at stderr.\n\nActually — I bet it's the OSError catch: main catches CommandError first... Let me think: _via_daemon raises CommandError(66, \"file not found: missing.txt\") — caught by `except CommandError as err:` → emit_error → return 66. Fine...\n\nUnless the daemon returned a DIFFERENT status for the missing read — e.g. 70 with content \"internal error: ...\" — mapped through _CONTRACT_CODES (70 in set) → CommandError(70, content) → exit 70. That matches: the DAEMON errored internally. Why would read_file of a missing file 70 inside the daemon? filesystem.read_file: p.is_dir() False → _read_text raises FileNotFoundError → CommandError(NOINPUT). Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the envelope CONTENT will tell. Run manually: spawn daemon via client (lazy) with discovery override in a temp dir, then read missing file, print status/content."}
{"type": "tool_call", "timestamp": 1789901117.612313, "id": "5ca59c9b35fe", "name": "run_command", "arguments": "{\"command\":\"cd clutch-workspace && cat > /tmp/diag_missing.py <<'EOF'\\nimport json, os, subprocess, sys, tempfile\\nsys.path.insert(0, \\\".\\\")\\nfrom clutch_workspace import discovery, client\\n\\nws = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\\\"diag-miss-\\\")\\nos.environ[\\\"CLUTCH_WORKSPACE_DISCOVERY_DIR\\\"] = os.path.join(ws, \\\"disc\\\")\\nenv = dict(os.environ, PYTHONPATH=os.getcwd())\\nproc = subprocess.run(\\n    [sys.executable, \\\"-m\\\", \\\"clutch_workspace\\\", \\\"--json\\\", \\\"read_file\\\", \\\"--path\\\", \\\"missing.txt\\\"],\\n    cwd=ws, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding=\\\"utf-8\\\", env=env,\\n)\\nprint(\\\"rc:\\\", proc.returncode)\\nprint(\\\"stdout:\\\", proc.stdout)\\nprint(\\\"stderr:\\\", proc.stderr[-2000:])\\nrecord = discovery.read(ws)\\nprint(\\\"record:\\\", record)\\nif record:\\n    client.shutdown(f\\\"http://127.0.0.1:{record['port']}\\\", record[\\\"token\\\"])\\nEOF\\n./.venv/Scripts/python /tmp/diag_missing.py\"}", "tool_call_id": "call_74049c74c90041efbd8dec6d"}
{"type": "tool_result", "timestamp": 1789901144.8228292, "id": "536ed74ac296", "tool_call_id": "call_74049c74c90041efbd8dec6d", "content": "OK: command succeeded\nstdout:\nrc: 70\r\nstdout: {\"content\": \"internal error: HTTP/1.0 66 \\r\\n\", \"error\": true, \"diff\": \"\"}\r\n\r\nstderr: t):\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\��ʱ�ļ�\\Clutch\\clutch-workspace\\clutch_workspace\\cli.py\", line 182, in main\r\n    result = _execute(args)\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\��ʱ�ļ�\\Clutch\\clutch-workspace\\clutch_workspace\\cli.py\", line 135, in _execute\r\n    served = _via_daemon(workspace, command, payload)\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\��ʱ�ļ�\\Clutch\\clutch-workspace\\clutch_workspace\\cli.py\", line 158, in _via_daemon\r\n    status, env = client.forward(base, token, command, payload)\r\n                  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n  File \"C:\\Users\\12741\\Desktop\\��ʱ�ļ�\\Clutch\\clutch-workspace\\clutch_workspace\\client.py\", line 76, in forward\r\n    with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=COMMAND_TIMEOUT) as response:\r\n         ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\urllib\\request.py\", line 187, in urlopen\r\n    return opener.open(url, data, timeout)\r\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\urllib\\request.py\", line 487, in open\r\n    response = self._open(req, data)\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\urllib\\request.py\", line 504, in _open\r\n    result = self._call_chain(self.handle_open, protocol, protocol +\r\n                              '_open', req)\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\urllib\\request.py\", line 464, in _call_chain\r\n    result = func(*args)\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\urllib\\request.py\", line 1350, in http_open\r\n    return self.do_open(http.client.HTTPConnection, req)\r\n           ~~~~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\urllib\\request.py\", line 1325, in do_open\r\n    r = h.getresponse()\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\http\\client.py\", line 1450, in getresponse\r\n    response.begin()\r\n    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\http\\client.py\", line 336, in begin\r\n    version, status, reason = self._read_status()\r\n                              ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~^^\r\n  File \"C:\\Python314\\Lib\\http\\client.py\", line 324, in _read_status\r\n    raise BadStatusLine(line)\r\nhttp.client.BadStatusLine: HTTP/1.0 66 \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nrecord: {'version': 1, 'workspace': 'C:\\\\Users\\\\12741\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Temp\\\\diag-miss-fe1g7cox', 'port': 5558, 'token': '6ad0164bd8f69f9e8077421672dac635', 'pid': 7552, 'started': '2026-09-20T10:45:44+00:00'}\r\n", "is_error": false, "diff": ""}
{"type": "assistant_message", "timestamp": 1789901214.7922273, "id": "bea2d979fe5c", "content": "Real protocol bug, and it was mine: sysexits codes are 2-digit (64/66/70/74/77), which `http.client` rejects as an HTTP status line (`BadStatusLine`). The ratified shape was right all along — **verdicts ride HTTP 200 inside the envelope** with the exit code as a transport-only field. Fixing daemon, client mapping, and the two affected tests:", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_4f02ee8977364dea85d5d7cd", "name": "edit_file", "arguments": "{\"new_string\":\"Protocol — all JSON, every request token-gated via the X-Clutch-Token\\nheader (anything else is 403):\\n\\n    GET  /health      → {\\\"ok\\\": true, \\\"workspace\\\", \\\"pid\\\", \\\"undo\\\"}\\n    POST /read_file /grep /write_file /edit_file /undo /shutdown\\n                      → the command response\\n\\nEvery command response rides HTTP 200; the VERDICT is the envelope's\\n\\\"code\\\" field: 0 ok, else the BSD sysexits code the CLI contract uses\\n(64/65/66/74; 77 when a --protect glob fences the path; 70 for a daemon\\nbug). The sysexits numbers are NOT HTTP statuses — 2-digit codes are\\nillegal status lines — so they travel in the body. The other three fields\\nare exactly the CLI's {\\\"content\\\", \\\"error\\\", \\\"diff\\\"} envelope, so the thin\\nclient re-emits byte-identically and just maps code → exit status. The\\ntransport statuses (403 bad token, 400 bad JSON, 404 unknown path) carry\\nno verdict: the client treats them as \\\"no usable daemon\\\" and falls back.\",\"old_string\":\"Protocol — all JSON, every request token-gated via the X-Clutch-Token\\nheader (anything else is 403):\\n\\n    GET  /health      → {\\\"ok\\\": true, \\\"workspace\\\", \\\"pid\\\", \\\"undo\\\"}\\n    POST /read_file /grep /write_file /edit_file /undo\\n                      → the command verdict, envelope out\\n    POST /shutdown    → 200, then the daemon unpublishes and exits\\n\\nThe HTTP status IS the command verdict: 200 ok, otherwise the BSD sysexits\\ncode the CLI contract uses (64/65/66/74; 77 when a --protect glob fences\\nthe path; 70 for a daemon bug). The body is the same {\\\"content\\\", \\\"error\\\",\\n\\\"diff\\\"} envelope the CLI emits, so the thin client re-emits it byte-\\nidentically. /health and /shutdown answer plain {\\\"ok\\\": true, ...}.\",\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/daemon.py\"}"}, {"id": "call_38865bb86ac04d88beed124a", "name": "edit_file", "arguments": "{\"new_string\":\"def _verdict(result: Result) -> dict:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"The CLI's envelope plus the verdict code — \\\"code\\\" is transport-only:\\n    sysexits numbers are illegal HTTP statuses, so they ride in the body.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return {\\\"content\\\": result.content, \\\"error\\\": False, \\\"diff\\\": result.diff, \\\"code\\\": 0}\\n\\n\\ndef _verdict_err(code: int, message: str) -> dict:\\n    return {\\\"content\\\": message, \\\"error\\\": True, \\\"diff\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"code\\\": code}\\n\\n\\ndef _transport_err(message: str) -> dict:\\n    return {\\\"content\\\": message, \\\"error\\\": True, \\\"diff\\\": \\\"\\\"}\",\"old_string\":\"def _err_envelope(message: str) -> dict:\\n    return {\\\"content\\\": message, \\\"error\\\": True, \\\"diff\\\": \\\"\\\"}\",\"path\":\"clutch-workspace/clutch_workspace/daemon.py\"}"}], "reasoning": "Excellent — found a real protocol bug: `BaseHTTPRequestHandler.send_response(66)` emits an HTTP status line \"HTTP/1.0 66 \" — but 66 is not a valid HTTP status code, and http.client raises BadStatusLine when parsing it! Non-standard status codes break urllib's parser? Actually http.client accepts any 3-digit code... but here it got \"HTTP/1.0 66 \" with TWO digits? No — \"66\" is 2 digits → BadStatusLine. Wait, send_response(66) should produce \"HTTP/1.0 66 \\r\\n\" — status formatted as str(code) = \"66\" (2 digits). http.client requires exactly 3 digits → BadStatusLine. That's the bug: sysexits codes 64/65/66/70/74/77 — most are 2-digit!\n\nSo sending the exit code as the raw HTTP status doesn't work for 2-digit codes. Redesign the verdict channel:\n\nOption A: HTTP status = 200 always for command responses, and put the verdict code INSIDE the envelope: {\"content\", \"error\", \"diff\", \"code\": 66}. The envelope is \"the same shape the CLI emits\" — adding a \"code\" field changes the shape... but this envelope is transport-internal; the CLI re-emits only the three contract fields. The client maps envelope[\"code\"] → exit. For byte-identity of OUTPUT (stdout/stderr), only content/error/diff matter. Adding \"code\" to the daemon's HTTP envelope is invisible to the user.\n\nBut the ratified design said \"command results = HTTP 200 + envelope JSON\" — wait, actually the summary says exactly that: \"command results = HTTP 200 + envelope JSON, client maps envelope→stdout/exit codes byte-identically via existing emit\". Hmm! It says HTTP 200 + envelope, and client maps envelope → exit codes. So the verdict code was ALWAYS meant to ride in the envelope. My HTTP-status-as-verdict was a deviation. Good — return to the ratified shape:\n\n- 200 + {\"content\", \"error\", \"diff\", \"code\": int} — hmm, do I add \"code\" for success too? Keep symmetric: every command response carries \"code\" (0 on success). The client maps: error? code : 0.\n\nActually cleaner: keep the daemon's HTTP envelope EXACTLY the 3 contract fields, and use HTTP status for the TRANSPORT-level verdicts only (403/404/400). Then how does the client know the exit code for error envelopes? Add \"code\". Fine — decision: envelope gets an extra transport-only field \"code\". Client strips it when re-emitting (emit uses content/error/diff only — Result(content, diff) — so extra key is naturally dropped).\n\nAlso with 200 always, urllib won't raise HTTPError for command verdicts — simpler forward(). /health stays {\"ok\": true}.\n\nUpdated daemon changes:\n- _post command execution: on CommandError → respond(200, {\"content\": msg, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\", \"code\": err.code}). On success → 200 + {..., \"code\": 0}.\n- Unexpected → 200 + {..., \"code\": 70, content \"internal error: ...\"}? Hmm — should an internal daemon bug be transport-70 or envelope-code-70? Keep envelope code 70 (verdict semantics), status 200. Simpler.\n\nWait — but there's a wrinkle: daemon bugs producing envelope code 70 → client raises CommandError(70) → exit 70. Good.\n\n- client.forward: returns (status, env); _via_daemon now:\n  - status 200: env has \"code\". If env.get(\"error\"): raise CommandError(env[\"code\"], env[\"content\"]) — with code guaranteed in contract set (daemon only sends verdict codes; defensive: if code not in _CONTRACT_CODES → EX_SOFTWARE).\n  - else Result(content, diff).\n  - 403/404/400/5xx → forget + direct fallback (unchanged).\n\nHealth stays 200 {\"ok\"...}; 403 on bad token.\n\nAlso update daemon docstring protocol section: status line explanation changes: \"All command responses ride HTTP 200; the verdict is the envelope's code field (0 ok, else the sysexits code: 64/65/66/74; 77 fenced; 70 bug). The three contract fields stay exactly the CLI's envelope; code is transport-only, dropped on re-emit.\"\n\nAnd _err_envelope gains code param: `_envelope(result_or_error)`.\n\nUpdate tests: the guards test (404/403/400) unchanged (transport statuses still used there). The undo/forward flows unchanged (client.forward handles). byte-identical test unchanged.\n\nLet me rewrite the daemon _post and envelope helpers, client comment, cli _via_daemon mapping.\n\ndaemon.py changes:\n\n```python\ndef _ok_envelope(result: Result) -> dict:\n    return {\"content\": result.content, \"error\": False, \"diff\": result.diff, \"code\": 0}\n\ndef _err_envelope(message: str, code: int = 0) -> dict:\n    return {\"content\": message, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\", \"code\": code}\n```\n\nHmm wait, for /health 404 path etc the envelope has code 0? Those are transport errors with error=True; give them a code too? For 403/404/400 transport responses the client ignores body codes (falls back direct). Set code=0 default fine... but error=True with code=0 is odd. Make _err_envelope require code and pass 70 for transport? No — cleanest: transport errors use plain {\"content\",\"error\",\"diff\"} without code... inconsistent shape. Let me give _err_envelope a mandatory code param and use:\n- 403 → code 0? no...\n\nHmm, honestly: for transport-level responses, put a diagnostic code that is NOT a verdict: use EX_SOFTWARE? That'd confuse the fallback logic? Client only reads env[\"code\"] on status 200. Transport responses (403/404/400) → body code never read. So any value is fine; use 0. But error=true + code=0 reads weird in raw curl debugging. Alternative: transport errors keep code absent — dict without \"code\" key. Shapes: verdict envelopes always have code; transport error envelopes have error+content only. Client reading env.get(\"code\", 0). Fine. Hmm, but \"400: request body must be one JSON object\" is arguably a client bug worth surfacing as 70... ratified mapping says 400 → forget+direct → command runs fine locally. OK.\n\nDecision:\n- verdict envelopes (always HTTP 200): {\"content\", \"error\", \"diff\", \"code\"}\n- transport errors (403/404/400): {\"content\", \"error\": true, \"diff\": \"\"} (no code)\n\nImplement:\n\n```python\ndef _verdict(result: Result) -> dict:\n    return {\"content\": result.content, \"error\": False, \"diff\": result.diff, \"code\": 0}\n\ndef _verdict_err(code: int, message: str) -> dict:\n    return {\"content\": message, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\", \"code\": code}\n\ndef _transport_err(message: str) -> dict:\n    return {\"content\": message, \"error\": True, \"diff\": \"\"}\n```\n\n_post:\n```python\n        try:\n            length = int(self.headers.get(\"Content-Length\") or 0)\n            payload = json.loads(self.rfile.read(length) or b\"{}\")\n        except (OSError, ValueError):\n            return self._respond(400, _transport_err(\"request body must be one JSON object\"))\n        if not isinstance(payload, dict):\n            return self._respond(400, _transport_err(\"request body must be one JSON object\"))\n        try:\n            result = d.route(self.path, payload)\n        except _BadRequest as err:\n            return self._respond(200, _verdict_err(EX_USAGE, str(err)))  # hmm — 400 transport or verdict 64?\n```\n\n_BadRequest = protocol misuse (missing fields — our own client's bug). Since client builds payloads, missing field = bug → verdict 70? or 64? It's \"bad flags\" category → 64 usage. I'll make it verdict EX_USAGE (64). That also means if it ever happened to a user, they'd see a 64 error rather than silent fallback — acceptable. Actually wait: verdict 64 → client raises CommandError(64) → exit 64 with the message. Debuggable. Good.\n\n- CommandError → 200 + _verdict_err(err.code, err.message)\n- Exception → 200 + _verdict_err(70, f\"internal error: {err}\")\n- success → 200 + _verdict(result)\n\nclient.forward comment update: all command verdicts ride 200; non-200 = transport.\n\n_via_daemon:\n```python\n    try:\n        status, env = client.forward(base, token, command, payload)\n    except client.DaemonUnreachable as err:\n        raise CommandError(EX_IOERR, str(err)) from None\n    if status != 200:\n        # transport-level trouble (403/404/400/5xx): not a verdict — forget\n        # this daemon and run directly, exactly as if it had never been there\n        client.forget(workspace)\n        return None\n    content, diff = env.get(\"content\", \"\"), env.get(\"diff\", \"\")\n    if not env.get(\"error\"):\n        return Result(content, diff)\n    code = env.get(\"code\")\n    if code in _CONTRACT_CODES:\n        raise CommandError(code, content)\n    raise CommandError(EX_SOFTWARE, content or \"malformed daemon response\")\n```\n\nUpdate daemon module docstring protocol text accordingly.\n\nThen the corrupted second failure: test_stale_and_corrupt... FileNotFoundError writing the stale discovery file — because `discovery.discovery_file(...).write_text(...)` — parent dir doesn't exist yet (no daemon ran in that test). Fix test: mkdir parents first. In the test: `disc` dir — the fixture yields `tmp_path/\"discovery\"` without creating. Add `daemon_env.mkdir()` usage or `disc.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` before write. The fixture has the disc var; test does: `discovery.discovery_file(str(tmp_path)).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` then write. Simpler: in fixture, `disc.mkdir(exist_ok=True)` at setup. Then all daemon tests have it. Do that.\n\nAlso EX_NOINPUT import in test file still used (yes, line 392).\n\nAlso update test_http_guards: 404 for /nope and bad-token 403, 400 bad json — unchanged. But my guards test asserted POST /write_file {\"nope\": 1} → 404? I wrote `(\"/write_file\", {\"nope\": 1})` in the 404 loop — that's a valid path with missing field → NOW verdict 64 not 404! Fix: the 404 loop should only use /nope. The missing-field case → new assert: status 200 + code 64. Let me restructure that test."}
